window.NUR258_QBANK={"1": [{"q": "Four clients arrive in the eye clinic. Which does the nurse see first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["A client with gradual painless loss of side vision over 2 years", "A client with sudden severe eye pain, halos, vomiting, a fixed dilated pupil and a hazy cornea", "A client with a clouded lens and worsening night glare", "A client with wavy lines on an Amsler grid at home"], "ans": [1], "why": "Acute closed-angle glaucoma presents as fixed dilated pupil, hazy cornea and pain with vomiting, and it is a <b>surgical emergency</b> - pressure must come down or vision is lost. The gradual painless peripheral loss is chronic open-angle glaucoma: serious, but managed with lifelong drops, not tonight."}, {"q": "A client with labyrinthitis has vomited 4 times in 3 hours and is dizzy. Which assessment is the priority?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Fluid and electrolyte status", "Effectiveness of the vestibular suppressant", "Tympanic membrane appearance", "Ability to ambulate to the bathroom"], "ans": [0], "why": "Once vomiting is repetitive the problem stops being vestibular and becomes volume: <b>fluid and electrolyte assessment is the priority</b>. Reaching for meclizine treats the dizziness the client came in with, not the dehydration she has developed since."}, {"q": "A client is prescribed two different ophthalmic drops in the same eye. Which instruction is correct?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Instill both drops back to back, then blink several times", "Wait 5 to 10 minutes between the two drops and press the inner canthus for 2 minutes", "Wait 30 seconds between drops and wipe the eye from outer to inner canthus", "Wait 30 minutes between drops and keep the eye closed for 10 minutes"], "ans": [1], "why": "Different drops need <b>5 to 10 minutes</b> apart so the first is not washed out, and occluding the inner canthus for <b>2 minutes</b> blocks systemic absorption through the nasolacrimal duct. Back-to-back instillation simply flushes the first drug away."}, {"q": "Which findings are risk factors for glaucoma? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Long-term corticosteroid use", "Wearing corrective lenses", "Thin central cornea", "Daily vitamin A supplement", "High myopia"], "ans": [0, 2, 4], "why": "The risk list is family history, age over 60, diabetes, hypertension, <b>corticosteroid use, thin central cornea and high myopia</b>. Corrective lenses, vitamin A and sunglasses were named explicitly as non-risks - refraction and nutrition do not raise intraocular pressure."}, {"q": "A client is receiving IV gentamicin along with scheduled furosemide. What is the nurse's priority action?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Increase oral fluid intake to protect the kidneys", "Monitor for early hearing loss and report it so the dose can be adjusted", "Teach the client to avoid loud environments during therapy", "Request that the furosemide be given at bedtime"], "ans": [1], "why": "Aminoglycoside plus loop diuretic is the ototoxic pairing to memorize, and the priority is to <b>detect early hearing loss and report it so the dose can change</b>. Pushing fluids and avoiding noise sound protective but do nothing about drug-induced cochlear damage."}, {"q": "A client reports a painless curtain moving across her visual field that started this morning. A new nurse offers her a routine appointment next week. How should the nurse respond?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Agree, since painless vision changes are not urgent", "Arrange immediate evaluation; retinal detachment is an emergency despite being painless", "Instruct her to patch the eye and rest at home for 24 hours", "Tell her this is expected with cataract formation"], "ans": [1], "why": "A painless curtain across the vision is <b>retinal detachment - an emergency</b>, and it is the one eye condition that may require face-down (prone) positioning for about 2 weeks post-op. The trap is equating absence of pain with absence of urgency."}, {"type": "sata", "week": "1", "step": "action", "topic": "Sensory Disorders - Eye & Ear", "q": "A client with Meniere's disease is having an acute attack of vertigo with nausea and nystagmus. Which actions should the nurse take right now? (Select all that apply.)", "opts": ["Dim the lights and lower the noise level in the room.", "Raise the side rails and keep the client in bed until the attack passes.", "Walk the client to the bathroom now so the client does not become incontinent.", "Give the prescribed meclizine and antiemetic.", "Offer strong coffee to help the client stay alert.", "Coach the client to turn the head slowly and avoid sudden position changes."], "ans": [0, 1, 3, 5], "why": "During an attack the nurse controls the environment and prevents falls, so dim quiet surroundings, raised rails, bed rest, and slow head movement are the immediate actions. Meclizine settles the vestibular symptoms and an antiemetic treats the vomiting. Walking a client who has active vertigo and nystagmus is how falls happen, so elimination needs are met at the bedside. Caffeine is a trigger that worsens inner-ear symptoms rather than helping.", "src": "ATI ch 14 Middle and Inner Ear Disorders", "icon": "👁️", "topicFull": "Sensory Disorders - Eye & Ear", "pharm": 1}, {"q": "A client is scheduled for cataract surgery on the right eye. Which statement by the client tells the nurse the teaching was understood?", "type": "single", "opts": ["\"I will bend at the waist to pick things up so I do not strain my back.\"", "\"I will avoid lifting anything heavier than about 10 pounds and will not strain on the toilet.\"", "\"I should expect sharp pain in the eye for the first two days.\"", "\"I can sleep on my right side as long as I use a soft pillow.\""], "ans": [1], "why": "Everything after cataract surgery is about <b>keeping intraocular pressure down</b>: no heavy lifting, no straining, no bending at the waist, and sleeping on the <b>unaffected</b> side. Bending and side-lying on the operative eye both raise pressure. <b>Sharp pain is never expected</b> - it suggests hemorrhage or increased pressure and must be reported at once.", "topic": "Sensory Disorders - Eye & Ear", "icon": "👁️", "topicFull": "Sensory Disorders - Eye & Ear"}, {"q": "A nurse instils two different eye drops for the same eye. Which actions are correct? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Wait at least 5 minutes between the two drops", "Place the drop in the conjunctival sac, not on the cornea", "Apply punctal occlusion for 1 to 2 minutes after a beta blocker drop", "Let the dropper tip rest on the lower lash to steady the hand", "Give the suspension (milky) drop before the clear solution"], "ans": [0, 1, 2], "why": "Spacing the drops stops the second one washing the first away, and the <b>conjunctival sac</b> is the target because the cornea is exquisitely sensitive. <b>Punctal occlusion</b> matters most with timolol and the other beta blockers - it keeps the drug out of the systemic circulation, where it can drop the heart rate and trigger bronchospasm. Touching the dropper to the lash contaminates it. The order is reversed: <b>solutions first, suspensions last</b>.", "topic": "Sensory Disorders - Eye & Ear", "icon": "👁️", "topicFull": "Sensory Disorders - Eye & Ear"}, {"q": "An older client with presbycusis is admitted. Which nursing actions support communication? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Face the client and make sure your mouth is visible", "Lower the pitch of your voice", "Shout to be sure you are heard", "Reduce background noise before starting", "Rephrase rather than simply repeating a sentence that was not understood"], "ans": [0, 1, 3, 4], "why": "Presbycusis takes the <b>high frequencies first</b>, so lowering pitch helps and shouting does not - shouting raises pitch and distorts the face, destroying the lip-reading cues the client is relying on. Facing the client, cutting background noise and rephrasing all give the brain a second route to the meaning.", "topic": "Sensory Disorders - Eye & Ear", "icon": "👁️", "topicFull": "Sensory Disorders - Eye & Ear"}, {"q": "A client reports vertigo, tinnitus, and a sense of fullness in one ear, with episodes lasting hours. Which instruction is the priority for safety?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Increase dietary sodium to maintain fluid balance", "Sit or lie down immediately when an attack begins and do not drive", "Perform vigorous head-turning exercises during an attack", "Irrigate the ear canal with warm water twice daily"], "ans": [1], "why": "Ménière's disease brings on vertigo without warning; the danger is <b>falling or crashing a car</b>, so the client stops moving and does not drive. Sodium is <b>restricted</b>, not increased, because it worsens endolymph volume. Head movement makes an attack worse, and irrigation has no role.", "topic": "Sensory Disorders - Eye & Ear", "icon": "👁️", "topicFull": "Sensory Disorders - Eye & Ear"}, {"q": "Which findings in a client with open-angle glaucoma should the nurse expect? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Gradual loss of peripheral vision", "Sudden severe eye pain with vomiting", "Intraocular pressure above 21 mm Hg", "Increased cup-to-disc ratio on examination", "Central vision loss with straight lines appearing wavy"], "ans": [0, 2, 3], "why": "Open-angle glaucoma is the <b>silent</b> one: pressure creeps up, the optic disc cups, and <b>peripheral</b> vision goes first, which is why it is often found on a routine exam. Sudden pain with vomiting is <b>closed-angle</b> glaucoma, an emergency. Central loss with wavy lines is <b>macular degeneration</b>.", "topic": "Sensory Disorders - Eye & Ear", "icon": "👁️", "topicFull": "Sensory Disorders - Eye & Ear"}, {"q": "A client with a history of closed-angle glaucoma is admitted for hip surgery. The pre-op orders include scopolamine for nausea. What is the nurse's priority action?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Hold the dose and contact the prescriber before it is given", "Give the dose and recheck the pupils in one hour", "Give the dose along with the client's beta-blocker eye drops", "Hold the dose only if the client begins to report eye pain"], "ans": [0], "why": "Scopolamine is an <b>anticholinergic</b>, and anticholinergics dilate the pupil, which jams the drainage angle shut and spikes the pressure, so they are contraindicated in closed-angle glaucoma. Waiting for eye pain is tempting but that pain <b>is</b> the acute attack, and the drops do not protect against a drug that is already dilating the pupil."}, {"q": "One day after surgical repair of a detached retina with a gas bubble, a client is found lying flat on his back watching television. What should the nurse do first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Help him back into the face-down position and review that it continues about 2 weeks", "Raise the head of the bed to 30 degrees and let him rest", "Turn him onto the operative side to keep pressure off the other eye", "Apply a warm compress to the eye and notify the surgeon"], "ans": [0], "why": "Retinal detachment repair is the <b>one</b> eye surgery that gets face-down (prone) positioning, for roughly 2 weeks, so the gas bubble presses the retina back into place. Raising the head of the bed feels like a safe, comfortable compromise, but any position other than face-down lets the bubble float off the retina; warm compresses and operative-side positioning are not used."}, {"q": "A client with macular degeneration calls the clinic and reports that the straight lines on her home Amsler grid look wavy today, though her side vision is unchanged. Which response by the nurse is best?", "type": "single", "opts": ["\"Come in to be evaluated - new wavy lines can mean wet macular degeneration, which is treated with anti-VEGF injections.\"", "\"That is expected as central vision fades; keep checking the grid and call in a month.\"", "\"Stop using the grid; it only screens for rising eye pressure.\"", "\"Your peripheral vision is intact, so no follow-up is needed right now.\""], "ans": [0], "why": "The whole point of the home Amsler grid is that <b>wavy lines</b> catch a change early, and wet macular degeneration is treated with anti-VEGF injections. Calling the change expected is tempting because macular degeneration does take central vision, but intact peripheral vision is the normal pattern of this disease and is not a reason to delay evaluation."}, {"q": "A client struck on the side of the head reports severe ear pain that suddenly stopped, followed by drainage and muffled hearing on that side. Which instruction is most important?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Keep the ear dry and avoid swimming and flying until it has healed", "Irrigate the ear canal daily to flush out the drainage", "Expect that surgery will be scheduled to close the opening", "The pain stopping means the injury resolved, so no follow-up is needed"], "ans": [0], "why": "Pain that <b>suddenly relieves</b> is the classic sign of a ruptured tympanic membrane, and the care is to keep the ear dry with no swimming or flying while it heals on its own. Assuming the pain stopping means resolution is exactly backwards - the relief marks the rupture - and routine surgery is not the expected treatment."}, {"q": "A client with type 2 diabetes for 8 years tells the nurse he cancelled his eye appointment because his vision seems fine. Which teaching points should the nurse include? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["A dilated eye exam is needed every year even when vision seems normal", "Keeping the A1C at target is the real protection for the retinas", "Controlling blood pressure also protects vision", "An eye exam is only needed once blurred vision develops", "Daily eye drops are prescribed to prevent diabetic retinopathy"], "ans": [0, 1, 2], "why": "Every diabetic gets an <b>annual dilated exam regardless of symptoms</b>, and the real prevention is tight A1C and blood pressure control. Waiting for blurred vision is the trap the client already fell into, and daily drops belong to glaucoma, not to preventing diabetic retinopathy."}, {"q": "A 78-year-old client is admitted after finishing a long course of IV gentamicin. He worked 30 years in a factory without hearing protection and also has impacted cerumen and a history of otosclerosis. Which factors put him at risk for permanent hearing loss? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["The prolonged course of gentamicin", "Thirty years of loud occupational noise", "His age of 78", "The impacted cerumen in the canal", "The history of otosclerosis"], "ans": [0, 1, 2], "why": "Sensorineural loss damages the hair cells or nerve and is <b>usually permanent</b>; its causes are noise, age, and ototoxic drugs such as aminoglycosides like gentamicin. Cerumen and otosclerosis are tempting because they clearly affect hearing, but they are <b>conductive</b> problems - sound cannot get in - and conductive loss is often fixable."}], "2": [{"q": "A nurse sustains a needlestick from a client who is HIV positive. What does the nurse do first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Report the exposure to the charge nurse", "Wash the site with soap and water", "Draw a baseline HIV antibody test", "Begin post-exposure prophylaxis"], "ans": [1], "why": "The sequence taught is <b>wash with soap and water first</b>, then report, start PEP and draw baseline labs. Rushing to PEP feels more urgent, but the first act reduces the inoculum still on the skin."}, {"q": "Which action most effectively prevents both CLABSI and CAUTI?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Use full sterile technique at insertion", "Do not place the device unless it is truly necessary", "Change the dressing every 7 days", "Prefer the femoral site for central access"], "ans": [1], "why": "The single most effective action is <b>not placing the device at all unless it is necessary</b>; sterile insertion is the runner-up. Femoral lines infect far more than subclavian, so that option is wrong twice over."}, {"q": "A client is to receive acetaminophen 650 mg from a 160 mg/5 mL suspension, measured at home in teaspoons. How many teaspoons should the nurse teach?", "type": "single", "opts": ["2 teaspoons", "4 teaspoons", "10 teaspoons", "20 teaspoons"], "ans": [1], "why": "650 divided by 160, times 5 mL, equals 20.3 mL; at <b>5 mL per teaspoon that is about 4 teaspoons</b>. The classic miss is stopping at 20 and calling it 20 teaspoons - the units, not the math, are the trap."}, {"q": "Which measures belong in neutropenic precautions? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Private room with positive-pressure airflow", "N95 respirator for everyone entering", "Remove fresh flowers and potted plants from the room", "Dedicated equipment kept in the room", "Keep the door open so staff can observe the client"], "ans": [0, 2, 3], "why": "Neutropenic (reverse) isolation means a <b>positive-pressure private room, no fresh flowers or plants, dedicated equipment</b>, limited visitors and daily cleaning. N95 and negative pressure belong to airborne precautions, which protect everyone else from the client - the opposite direction of risk."}, {"q": "A client is admitted with varicella. Which precautions does the nurse initiate?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Contact precautions with a private room", "Airborne and contact precautions: negative-pressure room, door closed, N95, plus gown and gloves", "Droplet precautions with a surgical mask within 3 feet", "Standard precautions only until lesions crust"], "ans": [1], "why": "Varicella is one of the four airborne conditions along with TB, measles and disseminated herpes zoster, so it needs a <b>negative-pressure room, closed door and N95</b>, and because the lesions shed virus too, <b>contact precautions (gown and gloves) are added</b> until every lesion has crusted. Contact alone is the trap: it misses the airborne spread."}, {"q": "A nurse is exposed to HIV and asks about prophylaxis timing. Which response is correct?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Start within 72 hours at the latest and continue for 28 days", "Start within 7 days and continue for 14 days", "Start only if the baseline test is positive, then continue for 6 weeks", "Start immediately and continue until the viral load is undetectable"], "ans": [0], "why": "PEP is started as soon as possible, ideally within hours, and <b>never beyond 72 hours</b>, then continued a full <b>28 days</b>. Waiting for a baseline result wastes the window - the baseline draw documents pre-exposure status, it does not gate treatment."}, {"type": "sata", "week": "2", "step": "solutions", "topic": "Infectious Diseases & HIV", "q": "A nurse is teaching a client who has HIV about ways to reduce the risk of opportunistic infection. Which statements by the client indicate a correct understanding of the teaching? (Select all that apply.)", "opts": ["“I will wash my hands frequently throughout the day.”", "“I will avoid changing my cat's litter box.”", "“I will stop my antiretroviral therapy once my viral load is undetectable.”", "“I will keep my influenza and pneumococcal vaccinations up to date.”", "“I will eat soft-cooked eggs and rare beef for extra protein.”", "“I will avoid crowds and people who are currently sick.”"], "ans": [0, 1, 3, 5], "why": "Frequent hand hygiene, avoiding litter box duty (toxoplasmosis exposure), staying current on immunizations, and avoiding crowds and sick contacts all lower infection risk for a client with HIV. Antiretroviral therapy must be continued exactly as prescribed and never stopped, even when the viral load becomes undetectable, because stopping allows the virus to rebound and increases drug-resistance risk. Undercooked eggs and rare meat carry a foodborne-illness risk that an immunocompromised client should avoid, not seek out.", "src": "ATI Adult Med-Surg ch 86 (HIV/AIDS)", "icon": "🦠", "topicFull": "Infectious Diseases & HIV"}, {"q": "A client with HIV has a CD4 count of 180 cells/mm³. Which prophylaxis does the nurse anticipate?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole for Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia", "Isoniazid for latent tuberculosis regardless of skin test result", "Azithromycin for Mycobacterium avium complex", "No prophylaxis is needed until the CD4 count is below 50"], "ans": [0], "why": "The thresholds are worth memorizing: <b>PCP prophylaxis at CD4 under 200</b>, MAC prophylaxis at <b>under 50</b>. At 180 the client needs trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole. Isoniazid is only for a positive screen, not everyone.", "pharm": 1, "topic": "Infectious Diseases & HIV", "icon": "🦠", "topicFull": "Infectious Diseases & HIV"}, {"q": "Which statements about HIV transmission are accurate? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["A client with a sustained undetectable viral load does not transmit HIV sexually", "HIV is transmitted by sharing drinking glasses", "Breast milk can transmit HIV", "Standard precautions are used for all clients regardless of HIV status", "A negative antibody test three weeks after exposure rules out infection"], "ans": [0, 2, 3], "why": "<b>Undetectable equals untransmittable</b> for sexual transmission - a central teaching point and a powerful reason for adherence. Breast milk does transmit. Standard precautions apply to everyone, which is exactly why they are called standard. HIV is <b>not</b> spread by casual contact, and a test at three weeks is inside the window period, so it rules out nothing.", "topic": "Infectious Diseases & HIV", "icon": "🦠", "topicFull": "Infectious Diseases & HIV"}, {"q": "A nurse is caring for a client on airborne precautions for tuberculosis. Which actions are correct? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Place the client in a negative-pressure room", "Wear a fit-tested N95 respirator to enter", "Have the client wear a surgical mask when transported out of the room", "Wear a surgical mask instead of a respirator if the client is masked", "Keep the room door closed"], "ans": [0, 1, 2, 4], "why": "Airborne precautions are <b>negative pressure, door closed, N95 for staff</b>. The direction of the masks is the part people reverse: <b>staff wear the respirator, the client wears a surgical mask</b> when they must leave the room. A surgical mask does not protect the nurse from droplet nuclei.", "topic": "Infectious Diseases & HIV", "icon": "🦠", "topicFull": "Infectious Diseases & HIV", "card": "NG-364_tuberculosis-pharmacology.html", "cardLabel": "Tuberculosis — Pharmacology"}, {"q": "A client taking rifampin for tuberculosis reports orange-red urine. What is the nurse's best response?", "type": "single", "opts": ["\"Stop the medication and come in to be seen today.\"", "\"This is expected with rifampin. It also stains soft contact lenses permanently.\"", "\"That suggests kidney damage; we will check your creatinine.\"", "\"Drink more water and the color will clear within a day.\""], "ans": [1], "why": "Orange-red discoloration of urine, sweat and tears is a <b>harmless, expected</b> rifampin effect - but it <b>permanently stains soft contact lenses</b>, which is the part clients are never told. What does need reporting on rifampin is hepatotoxicity: jaundice, dark urine with pale stools, right upper quadrant pain.", "pharm": 1, "topic": "Infectious Diseases & HIV", "icon": "🦠", "topicFull": "Infectious Diseases & HIV", "card": "NG-364_tuberculosis-pharmacology.html", "cardLabel": "Tuberculosis — Pharmacology"}, {"q": "A client admitted with Clostridioides difficile has had profuse watery diarrhea for two days. Which problem does the nurse address first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Dehydration", "Electrolyte imbalance", "Perianal skin breakdown", "Poor nutritional intake"], "ans": [0], "why": "In C. diff the priority problem is <b>dehydration</b>, and electrolytes come second. Electrolyte imbalance is tempting because it comes from the same fluid loss, but it is ranked after dehydration, and hydration is assessed before antibiotics are given."}, {"q": "A nurse is caring for a mechanically ventilated client and is reviewing the unit's ventilator-associated pneumonia prevention measures. Which intervention is correct?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Elevate the head of the bed at least 30 degrees", "Suction the airway every 2 hours on a set schedule", "Keep the client flat while the endotracheal tube is in place", "Limit oral care to once every 24 hours"], "ans": [0], "why": "VAP prevention is <b>head of bed at least 30 degrees</b>. Scheduled <b>q2h suctioning</b> is the classic wrong answer — it looks proactive, but routine suctioning traumatizes the trachea rather than preventing pneumonia."}, {"q": "A client is admitted with fever and cough attributed to an emerging infectious disease whose route of transmission has not yet been confirmed. What does the nurse do first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Place the client in the most protective isolation available", "Notify the public health department before isolating the client", "Maintain standard precautions until the route is identified", "Start droplet precautions and leave the door open"], "ans": [0], "why": "Until the route is confirmed, isolate at the <b>most protective level available</b>, then report to public health. Notifying public health first is tempting because reporting is required, but it protects no one while the client sits unisolated with an unknown route."}, {"q": "A client with HIV has been taking antiretroviral therapy for three months and asks how the team will know the medication is working. Which laboratory value does the nurse identify?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Viral load", "White blood cell count", "Absolute neutrophil count", "CD4 count"], "ans": [0], "why": "<b>Viral load</b> is what tracks how well ART is working, and therapy lowers it. CD4 is the tempting choice because it is the monitoring lab for HIV itself and rises with treatment, but response to therapy is judged by viral load; WBC is not the HIV monitoring lab at all."}, {"q": "A client is admitted with Clostridioides difficile after a recent course of antibiotics. Which actions does the nurse take? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Perform hand hygiene with soap and water rather than an alcohol-based rub", "Clean the room and equipment with a bleach solution", "Initiate contact precautions in a private room", "Place the client in a negative-pressure room with the door closed", "Use an alcohol-based hand rub after removing gloves"], "ans": [0, 1, 2], "why": "C. diff calls for <b>soap and water</b>, <b>bleach</b> cleaning, and <b>contact precautions</b> in a private room, because alcohol gel does not kill C. diff spores. A negative-pressure room with the door closed belongs to airborne precautions (TB, measles, varicella); with contact precautions the door may stay open."}, {"q": "A nurse is caring for a client with a subclavian central line. Which actions reduce the client's risk of a central line-associated bloodstream infection? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Scrub the hub with friction for 15 seconds before every access", "Scrub the chlorhexidine prep for a full 2 minutes at each dressing change", "Change the transparent dressing every 7 days, or sooner if it is damp, loose or soiled", "Review the line every day and remove it as soon as it is no longer needed", "Avoid drawing blood from the central line, because drawing blood is what causes CLABSI", "Ask the provider for a femoral site instead of the subclavian site"], "ans": [0, 2, 3], "why": "<b>Scrub the hub 15 seconds</b> with friction, change the transparent dressing <b>every 7 days</b> (gauze every 2 days, either one sooner if damp, loose or soiled), and <b>pull the line the day it stops being needed</b> — that last one prevents more infections than any technique.<br><br><b>Why 2 minutes is wrong here:</b> the chlorhexidine–alcohol prep has two times, and the site decides which one. <b>Dry site</b> (chest, arm, abdomen — so a <b>subclavian</b> line) = <b>30 seconds</b> of back-and-forth friction. <b>Moist site</b> (groin, axilla — a <b>femoral</b> line) = <b>2 minutes</b>. Either way, let it <b>dry completely</b>; the drying is what kills, not the wiping. This client has a subclavian line, so 30 seconds.<br><br>Drawing blood from the line does not itself cause CLABSI — <b>poor technique at the hub</b> does. And femoral is the <b>highest</b>-infection site, not a request you would make."}, {"q": "A health care worker's tuberculin skin test, read at 48 hours, shows 14 mm of induration. Which statement by the nurse is accurate?", "type": "single", "opts": ["A positive test means you have been infected with TB; a chest x-ray is needed to check for active disease", "You have active TB and must start isolation at home", "The result is negative because it is under 15 mm", "The test must be read again in 2 weeks"], "ans": [0], "why": "For health care workers <b>10 mm or more is positive</b> (5 mm for HIV, immunosuppression or close contacts; 15 mm for people with no risk factors). A positive test shows <b>infection, not necessarily active disease</b>: a chest x-ray and, if needed, sputum cultures decide that.", "card": "nur258-module-02-infectious-diseases-hiv.html#dx-tuberculosis", "src": "SimpleNursing review video (Fall 2026, Wk 03): immunology - anaphylactic shock, HIV, lupus, RA, TB, scleroderma"}, {"q": "The nurse is collecting sputum specimens from a client with suspected active tuberculosis. Which instruction is correct?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Collect a specimen early in the morning on 3 consecutive days", "Collect one specimen at bedtime after rinsing with mouthwash", "Collect all three specimens within the same hour", "Collect saliva rather than sputum, because it is easier to obtain"], "ans": [0], "why": "Secretions pool overnight, so <b>early-morning sputum</b> gives the best yield, and <b>three specimens on consecutive days</b> are needed for AFB smear and culture. Three negative cultures are also how a client is shown to be no longer infectious. Mouthwash and saliva dilute the sample.", "card": "nur258-module-02-infectious-diseases-hiv.html#dx-tuberculosis", "src": "SimpleNursing review video (Fall 2026, Wk 03): immunology - anaphylactic shock, HIV, lupus, RA, TB, scleroderma"}], "3": [{"q": "Ten minutes into an IV infusion a client reports itching and dizziness. BP is 86/52, HR 118, RR 24. What does the nurse do first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Stop the infusion", "Obtain a set of vital signs and recheck in 5 minutes", "Administer the prescribed antihistamine", "Place the client in Trendelenburg"], "ans": [0], "why": "This is type I (IgE-mediated) hypersensitivity, and the first action is to <b>stop the offending infusion</b> before any drug is given. Reaching for diphenhydramine while the antigen keeps running lets the reaction progress to anaphylaxis."}, {"q": "A client's chart lists a penicillin allergy. What does the nurse do first before the prescribed antibiotic is given?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Hold the dose and document the allergy", "Ask the client to describe exactly what happened when she took penicillin", "Notify the provider that the client is allergic", "Give the dose slowly with epinephrine at the bedside"], "ans": [1], "why": "First <b>clarify what actually happened</b> - many reported allergies are GI upset, not immune reactions. If the answer is rash, itching or swelling, then notify the provider before giving the drug."}, {"q": "A client is admitted with an acute gout flare of the great toe. Which new prescription should the nurse question?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Naproxen", "Colchicine", "Allopurinol", "Prednisone"], "ans": [2], "why": "<b>Allopurinol is long-term prevention and can worsen an acute flare</b>, so starting it now should be questioned. Colchicine and NSAIDs plus corticosteroids treat the attack - reversing prevention and acute treatment is the classic miss."}, {"q": "Which findings support a type I hypersensitivity reaction? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Blood pressure 86/52", "Temperature 37.3 C", "Heart rate 118", "Generalized itching", "Oxygen saturation 96 percent on room air"], "ans": [0, 2, 3], "why": "The cues scored as relevant were <b>hypotension, tachycardia, itching, dizziness, diarrhea and RR 24</b>, and about 90 percent of anaphylaxis includes urticaria or rash. Temp 37.3 and SpO2 96 percent are inside normal limits and were scored as not indicative - normal values are distractors."}, {"q": "A client is to receive diphenhydramine 25 mg from a 12.5 mg/5 mL elixir at home. How much should the nurse teach her to take?", "type": "single", "opts": ["1 teaspoon", "2 teaspoons", "4 teaspoons", "10 teaspoons"], "ans": [1], "why": "25 mg divided by 12.5 mg, times 5 mL, is 10 mL, and at 5 mL per teaspoon that is <b>2 teaspoons</b>. Answering 10 means the student stopped at milliliters and never converted."}, {"q": "A client has widespread pain, fatigue and poor sleep. ESR, CRP and ANA are all normal. Which condition does this pattern support?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Rheumatoid arthritis", "Systemic lupus erythematosus", "Fibromyalgia", "Serum sickness"], "ans": [2], "why": "<b>Fibromyalgia is not inflammatory, so ESR, CRP and ANA are normal</b> - that is the fastest way to separate it from RA and lupus, which show abnormal labs. Management is low-impact exercise, sleep hygiene and duloxetine, pregabalin or milnacipran, not opioids."}, {"q": "A client is having an anaphylactic reaction. Which action does the nurse take first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Administer intramuscular epinephrine", "Start an IV of normal saline", "Administer IV diphenhydramine", "Obtain a set of vital signs"], "ans": [0], "why": "<b>Epinephrine first, intramuscular, into the vastus lateralis</b> - it is the only drug that reverses the airway and vascular collapse. Antihistamines and steroids are adjuncts that work far too slowly to save the airway, and stopping to take vital signs delays the one thing that matters.", "pharm": 1, "topic": "Allergic, Inflammatory…", "icon": "🦠", "topicFull": "Allergic, Inflammatory & Immunologic Disorders"}, {"q": "Which findings suggest a systemic lupus erythematosus flare rather than stable disease? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["New proteinuria", "Butterfly rash worsening after a beach day", "Weight loss with fever", "Blood pressure 118/74 mm Hg", "New joint pain and swelling"], "ans": [0, 1, 2, 4], "why": "A flare shows as <b>new organ involvement</b> - proteinuria means lupus nephritis, the complication that decides prognosis - plus fever, weight loss, worsening rash and arthritis. <b>Sun exposure is a classic trigger</b>, which is why sunscreen is a daily prescription, not a comfort measure. A normal blood pressure is reassuring, not a flare sign.", "topic": "Allergic, Inflammatory…", "icon": "🦠", "topicFull": "Allergic, Inflammatory & Immunologic Disorders"}, {"q": "A client with rheumatoid arthritis asks how it differs from osteoarthritis. Which features belong to rheumatoid arthritis? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Symmetric joint involvement", "Morning stiffness lasting more than an hour", "Fatigue, low-grade fever and weight loss", "Pain that worsens through the day with use", "Heberden's nodes at the distal finger joints"], "ans": [0, 1, 2], "why": "Rheumatoid arthritis is a <b>systemic autoimmune</b> disease: symmetric, prolonged morning stiffness, and whole-body symptoms. Osteoarthritis is <b>mechanical wear</b>: asymmetric, worse with use through the day, brief stiffness, and <b>Heberden's nodes</b> at the distal joints. Getting this pair straight answers a large number of exam items.", "topic": "Allergic, Inflammatory…", "icon": "🦠", "topicFull": "Allergic, Inflammatory & Immunologic Disorders", "card": "musc/NG-163_rheumatoid-arthritis.html", "cardLabel": "Rheumatoid Arthritis"}, {"q": "A client starting methotrexate for rheumatoid arthritis needs teaching. Which instructions are correct? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Take folic acid as prescribed", "Avoid alcohol", "Report sore throat, mouth ulcers or unusual bruising", "Take the dose every day at bedtime", "Use reliable contraception"], "ans": [0, 1, 2, 4], "why": "Methotrexate for arthritis is <b>weekly, not daily</b> - a daily dose is a well-documented fatal medication error. Folic acid reduces the side effects, alcohol compounds the liver toxicity, sore throat and ulcers signal bone-marrow suppression, and the drug is <b>teratogenic</b>.", "pharm": 1, "topic": "Allergic, Inflammatory…", "icon": "🦠", "topicFull": "Allergic, Inflammatory & Immunologic Disorders", "card": "musc/NG-163_rheumatoid-arthritis.html", "cardLabel": "Rheumatoid Arthritis"}, {"q": "A client admitted with widespread hives that appeared shortly after a meal tells the nurse it is \"just a rash.\" Which action does the nurse take first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Inspect the lips, tongue and airway", "Apply a cool compress to the hives", "Give the ordered oral antihistamine", "Document the size and location of the hives"], "ans": [0], "why": "Urticaria can be the <b>opening act of anaphylaxis</b>, so the lips, tongue and airway are assessed before the rash is called minor. The ordered antihistamine is the tempting choice, but antihistamines never rescue an airway and giving it first delays finding swelling."}, {"q": "A client with systemic lupus erythematosus who takes hydroxychloroquine calls the clinic to report new blurred vision. Which response by the nurse is correct?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Keep taking it and mention the change at your next routine visit", "Take each dose with food to settle the visual side effect", "Stop the drug and call the provider today, because vision changes can mean retinal damage", "This is expected during a flare and will clear when the flare settles"], "ans": [2], "why": "Any visual change on hydroxychloroquine may be <b>irreversible retinal toxicity</b>, so the drug is stopped and the provider notified; routine eye exams are required. Waiting for the next visit is tempting because the symptom sounds mild, but retinal damage allowed to progress does not reverse."}, {"q": "A client with systemic lupus erythematosus is seen for a follow-up visit. Which finding does the nurse report to the provider first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Butterfly rash across the cheeks and nose", "Rising BUN and creatinine with BP 160/98 and dark frothy urine", "Fatigue and joint pain between flares", "Skin that reddens quickly after an hour of gardening"], "ans": [1], "why": "Rising BUN and creatinine with hypertension and dark frothy urine point to <b>lupus nephritis</b>, the complication that determines survival. The butterfly rash is the tempting pick because it looks dramatic, but it is an expected finding in lupus and calls for sun-protection teaching, not an urgent report."}, {"q": "A client admitted with Raynaud's phenomenon is being taught how to limit attacks. Which statement shows correct understanding?", "type": "single", "opts": ["My fingers will turn red first, then blue, then white", "Heat brings these attacks on, so I will avoid hot water", "I will wear gloves in cold places, including the freezer aisle at the store", "When an attack starts I should run cold water over my hands"], "ans": [2], "why": "Cold triggers the vasospasm, so gloves belong in any cold environment, including the freezer aisle. The color-sequence option is tempting because the three colors are right, but the order is reversed: it is <b>white, then blue, then red</b>."}, {"q": "A client is discharged on allopurinol after repeated gout attacks. Which instructions does the nurse include? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Drink at least 2 liters of fluid a day", "Avoid organ meats and seafood", "Avoid alcohol", "Your kidney function will be monitored while you take this drug", "Start this drug at the first twinge of an acute attack to shorten it"], "ans": [0, 1, 2, 3], "why": "Allopurinol is the <b>long-term</b> gout drug: push 2 or more liters of fluid a day, monitor the kidneys, and avoid organ meats, seafood and alcohol. Using it to treat an acute attack is the classic miss — it can worsen the flare, which is treated with colchicine or naproxen plus corticosteroids."}, {"q": "A client admitted for surgery reports a latex allergy. Which statements does the nurse include in teaching? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Latex-free supplies will be used for your whole stay, not only in the operating room", "Tell us if bananas, avocados, kiwi or chestnuts have ever made you itch or swell", "Report itching, or any swelling of your lips or tongue, right away", "These precautions can stop once you are back in your room after surgery", "An antihistamine before contact makes latex exposure safe for you"], "ans": [0, 1, 2], "why": "Latex allergy calls for a <b>latex-free environment for the entire stay</b>, and latex cross-reacts with banana, avocado, kiwi and chestnut, so those foods are worth asking about. Limiting precautions to the procedure is the tempting error, and premedicating does not make exposure safe — antihistamines never rescue an airway."}, {"q": "A client with systemic lupus erythematosus asks what can bring on a flare. Which triggers should the nurse teach the client to avoid? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Sun and UV light exposure without protection", "Smoking", "Physical and emotional stress", "Infections", "Daily low-impact exercise such as walking"], "ans": [0, 1, 2, 3], "why": "Avoid the <b>S's of lupus</b>: <b>sun/UV light</b> (the most tested - teach sunscreen, hats and long sleeves), <b>smoking</b>, <b>stress</b> and <b>sepsis/infection</b>. Gentle regular exercise is encouraged to limit fatigue and joint stiffness, not avoided.", "card": "nur258-module-03-allergic-inflammatory-immunologic.html#dx-systemic-lupus-erythematosus", "src": "SimpleNursing review video (Fall 2026, Wk 3): lupus and immunosuppressants"}, {"q": "A client with rheumatoid arthritis is to start infliximab. Which result must the nurse confirm before the first dose?", "type": "single", "opts": ["A negative tuberculosis screening test", "A normal hemoglobin A1C", "A negative antinuclear antibody (ANA) test", "A normal serum potassium"], "ans": [0], "why": "TNF inhibitors (<b>infliximab, etanercept, adalimumab</b>) can <b>reactivate latent TB</b>, so a negative TB test is required before therapy starts and is repeated yearly. A positive test means TB treatment first. These drugs are also held during any active infection, and live vaccines are avoided.", "card": "nur258-module-03-allergic-inflammatory-immunologic.html#dx-rheumatoid-arthritis", "src": "SimpleNursing review video (Fall 2026, Wk 3): lupus and immunosuppressants"}, {"q": "A client with systemic lupus erythematosus takes azathioprine and prednisone. Which finding should the nurse report to the provider first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Temperature 38.3 &deg;C (100.9 &deg;F)", "C-reactive protein above the normal range", "Morning joint stiffness lasting 20 minutes", "A faint rash across the cheeks after gardening"], "ans": [0], "why": "On immunosuppressants a <b>fever may be the only sign of a serious infection</b>, because the usual response is blunted - report it right away (along with a rising WBC). An <b>elevated CRP is expected</b> with active autoimmune inflammation and is not specific; it was the most-chosen wrong answer in the video's question banks. Stiffness and a sun-triggered rash need follow-up, not first priority.", "card": "nur258-module-03-allergic-inflammatory-immunologic.html#dx-systemic-lupus-erythematosus", "src": "SimpleNursing review video (Fall 2026, Wk 3): lupus and immunosuppressants"}, {"q": "A client who received a kidney transplant is discharged on cyclosporine. Which statement indicates a need for further teaching?", "type": "single", "opts": ["\"I will take it with my morning glass of grapefruit juice.\"", "\"I will take it at the same time every day.\"", "\"I will need to take this medicine for the rest of my life.\"", "\"I will keep my appointments for blood work.\""], "ans": [0], "why": "<b>Grapefruit juice</b> blocks the enzyme that breaks cyclosporine down and can push levels into the toxic, kidney-damaging range. Lifelong use, the same time every day and regular drug-level and kidney-function labs are all correct.", "card": "nur258-module-03-allergic-inflammatory-immunologic.html#dx-immune", "src": "SimpleNursing review video (Fall 2026, Wk 3): lupus and immunosuppressants"}, {"q": "A client in anaphylactic shock has received IM epinephrine and an IV fluid bolus. Which finding indicates the treatment is working?", "type": "single", "opts": ["BP 112/70, apical pulse 99, capillary refill under 2 seconds", "BP 84/50 and pulse 128", "New hoarseness and a high-pitched sound on inspiration", "Urine output of 15 mL in the past hour"], "ans": [0], "why": "Anaphylactic shock is massive vasodilation and leaky capillaries. A <b>stable or rising blood pressure</b>, a <b>slowing heart rate</b> and <b>brisk capillary refill</b> show perfusion is returning. Hypotension with tachycardia, stridor or hoarseness (airway swelling) and low urine output mean it is not.", "card": "nur258-module-03-allergic-inflammatory-immunologic.html#dx-anaphylaxis", "src": "SimpleNursing review video (Fall 2026, Wk 03): immunology - anaphylactic shock, HIV, lupus, RA, TB, scleroderma"}, {"q": "A client with a known shellfish allergy develops a suspected allergic reaction after a meal. Which findings require immediate intervention?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Watery eyes and a diffuse rash", "Abdominal pain with vomiting", "Clammy skin and a persistent cough", "Runny nose and facial swelling"], "ans": [2], "why": "<b>Clammy skin and a persistent cough</b> mean the reaction has progressed to <b>anaphylactic shock</b>: the allergen meets IgE, histamine and other mediators flood out, and the result is hypotension and bronchoconstriction that can end in respiratory or cardiac failure. The immediate priority is <b>epinephrine</b>. Watery eyes, a rash, runny nose, nausea and vomiting, and mild facial swelling fit a mild-to-moderate allergic reaction; they need treatment, but not first. Other anaphylaxis signs to watch for: swelling of the tongue or throat, hypotension, palpitations and chest pain.", "topic": "Allergic, Inflammatory & Immunologic Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 practice question (24 Nov), reworded", "icon": "🦠", "topicFull": "Allergic, Inflammatory & Immunologic Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-03-allergic-inflammatory-immunologic.html#dx-anaphylaxis", "cardLabel": "Anaphylaxis"}, {"q": "A client used an epinephrine auto-injector at home after a bee sting and now feels better. What should the nurse teach?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Go to the emergency department right away, because symptoms can come back as the epinephrine wears off", "No further care is needed once the symptoms resolve", "Take an oral antihistamine at home instead of going to the hospital", "Use the second auto-injector only if hives appear"], "ans": [0], "why": "Epinephrine lasts only about <b>10 to 20 minutes</b>, and a <b>biphasic (second) reaction</b> can follow hours later, so emergency care is needed after every use. Antihistamines are add-ons; they do not treat airway swelling or shock.", "card": "nur258-module-03-allergic-inflammatory-immunologic.html#dx-anaphylaxis", "src": "SimpleNursing review video (Fall 2026, Wk 03): immunology - anaphylactic shock, HIV, lupus, RA, TB, scleroderma"}, {"q": "A client with rheumatoid arthritis is starting methotrexate and asks about vaccines. Which response is correct?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Inactivated vaccines, such as the flu shot, are recommended; live vaccines, such as MMR and the nasal flu spray, must be avoided", "All vaccines must be avoided while you take methotrexate", "Live vaccines are preferred because they give stronger immunity", "Vaccines can be given only after methotrexate has been stopped for a year"], "ans": [0], "why": "Methotrexate suppresses the immune system, so a <b>live vaccine can cause the disease</b> it is meant to prevent. <b>Inactivated</b> vaccines (injected flu, pneumococcal) are safe and encouraged, because infection is the bigger risk on immunosuppressants.", "card": "nur258-module-03-allergic-inflammatory-immunologic.html#dx-rheumatoid-arthritis", "src": "SimpleNursing review video (Fall 2026, Wk 03): immunology - anaphylactic shock, HIV, lupus, RA, TB, scleroderma"}, {"q": "Which tests does the nurse expect to support a diagnosis of rheumatoid arthritis? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Rheumatoid factor (RF)", "Anti-CCP antibodies", "Erythrocyte sedimentation rate (ESR)", "Synovial fluid analysis", "Hemoglobin A1C", "Serum amylase"], "ans": [0, 1, 2, 3], "why": "<b>RF</b> and <b>anti-CCP</b> are the antibody markers, <b>ESR and CRP</b> show the inflammation, and <b>synovial fluid</b> from an affected joint is cloudy with a high WBC count. A1C and amylase have nothing to do with RA.", "card": "nur258-module-03-allergic-inflammatory-immunologic.html#dx-rheumatoid-arthritis", "src": "SimpleNursing review video (Fall 2026, Wk 03): immunology - anaphylactic shock, HIV, lupus, RA, TB, scleroderma"}, {"q": "A client with rheumatoid arthritis is due for an infliximab infusion and is taking an antibiotic for a urinary tract infection. What does the nurse do?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Hold the infusion and notify the provider", "Give the infusion at a slower rate", "Premedicate with acetaminophen and give the infusion", "Give the infusion and repeat the urine culture tomorrow"], "ans": [0], "why": "TNF inhibitors such as infliximab blunt the response to infection, so they are <b>held during any active infection</b> and the provider decides when to resume. A slower rate or premedication does not remove that risk.", "card": "nur258-module-03-allergic-inflammatory-immunologic.html#dx-rheumatoid-arthritis", "src": "SimpleNursing review video (Fall 2026, Wk 03): immunology - anaphylactic shock, HIV, lupus, RA, TB, scleroderma"}, {"q": "A client with systemic sclerosis (scleroderma) has a sudden severe headache and a blood pressure of 210/118. Which medication does the nurse anticipate?", "type": "single", "opts": ["An ACE inhibitor, such as captopril", "A beta blocker, such as metoprolol", "A high-dose corticosteroid", "A loop diuretic, such as furosemide"], "ans": [0], "why": "Abrupt severe hypertension in scleroderma signals <b>scleroderma renal crisis</b>, which can destroy the kidneys within days. <b>ACE inhibitors</b> are the treatment of choice. High-dose steroids can actually <b>trigger</b> renal crisis.", "card": "musc/NG-316_scleroderma.html", "src": "SimpleNursing review video (Fall 2026, Wk 03): immunology - anaphylactic shock, HIV, lupus, RA, TB, scleroderma"}], "4": [{"q": "A client with burns to the face and anterior neck is hoarse and has singed nasal hair and carbonaceous sputum. What is the priority action?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Calculate the TBSA using the rule of nines", "Obtain intubation equipment now", "Start lactated Ringer's by the Parkland formula", "Apply silver sulfadiazine to the facial burns"], "ans": [1], "why": "Inhalation cues mean the <b>airway will swell shut within 1 to 2 hours</b>, so intubation equipment comes before TBSA estimation or fluids. Face, neck or chest burns make airway the priority; only waist-down burns put fluids first."}, {"q": "A client with 30 percent TBSA burns arrives in the ED. Which action does the nurse take first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Administer IV morphine for severe pain", "Insert two large-bore 18-gauge IV catheters", "Remove all clothing and jewelry", "Apply sterile dressings to the wounds"], "ans": [1], "why": "<b>Two large-bore (18 g) IVs come first</b> - without access there is no resuscitation and no analgesia. Pain is loud and tempting, but the line precedes the drug."}, {"q": "A 75 kg client has 54 percent TBSA partial- and full-thickness burns. Using the Parkland formula, what is the infusion rate for the first 8 hours?", "type": "single", "opts": ["506 mL/hr", "675 mL/hr", "1,013 mL/hr", "2,025 mL/hr"], "ans": [2], "why": "4 mL x 75 kg x 54 = 16,200 mL over 24 hours; half (8,100 mL) runs in the first 8 hours = <b>about 1,013 mL/hr</b> of lactated Ringer's. Halving 16,200 over 24 hours instead gives 675 - the error is forgetting the front-loaded first 8 hours, timed from injury, not arrival."}, {"q": "Which systemic effects does the nurse expect in the emergent phase of a major burn? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Hyperkalemia", "Metabolic alkalosis", "Paralytic ileus", "Hypoglycemia", "Hypothermia"], "ans": [0, 2, 4], "why": "Cell death and massive fluid shifts produce <b>hyperkalemia, hyponatremia, paralytic ileus, decreased cardiac output, hypothermia and Curling's ulcer</b>. Increased cardiac output, metabolic alkalosis and hypoglycemia were all scored wrong."}, {"q": "A client has superficial (first-degree) burns over both shoulders plus deep partial-thickness burns of the anterior trunk. Which areas are included in the Parkland calculation?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Both areas, since all burned skin loses fluid", "Only the anterior trunk", "Only the shoulders", "Neither, until TBSA exceeds 20 percent"], "ans": [1], "why": "<b>First-degree burns are never counted in TBSA or in Parkland</b> - only second- and third-degree burns are. Counting the red, dry, blister-free areas inflates the volume and over-resuscitates the client."}, {"q": "A client has a white, leathery, painless burn on the thigh surrounded by moist, blistered skin that she rates 9/10. How does the nurse interpret this?", "type": "single", "opts": ["The painless area is the least serious and needs no urgent care", "The painless area is full-thickness and the pain comes from surrounding partial-thickness burn", "The client is exaggerating the pain because the burn is insensate", "The painless area indicates a superficial burn that is already healing"], "ans": [1], "why": "<b>Full-thickness burns are painless because the nerve endings are destroyed</b>, while the partial-thickness skin around them hurts intensely. No pain does not mean not serious - that inversion is exactly what the item is testing."}, {"q": "An adult has deep partial-thickness burns to the entire anterior trunk and the whole of both arms. Using the rule of nines, what percentage of total body surface area is burned?", "type": "single", "opts": ["18%", "27%", "36%", "45%"], "ans": [2], "why": "Anterior trunk is <b>18%</b> and each whole arm is <b>9%</b>, so both arms add another 18%: <b>18 + 18 = 36%</b>. The adult rule of nines is worth committing to memory whole - head 9, each arm 9, each leg 18, anterior trunk 18, posterior trunk 18, perineum 1. Note that a burn of 36% is well past the 20% threshold at which formal fluid resuscitation and burn-center transfer are indicated.", "topic": "Burns", "icon": "🔥", "topicFull": "Burns", "card": "skin/NG-123_major-burns.html", "cardLabel": "Major Burns — Treatment"}, {"q": "Which findings during the emergent phase of a major burn need immediate intervention? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Singed nasal hair with a hoarse voice", "Urine output of 15 mL/hr", "Serum potassium 6.2 mEq/L", "Blood pressure 108/68 mm Hg", "Circumferential full-thickness burn of the chest"], "ans": [0, 1, 2, 4], "why": "Singed nasal hair and hoarseness mean an <b>inhalation injury</b> and a threatened airway - intubate before edema closes it. Urine under 30 mL/hr says the fluid resuscitation is failing. Potassium rises early as damaged cells release it. A <b>circumferential chest burn</b> stops the chest wall expanding and needs escharotomy. The blood pressure is acceptable.", "topic": "Burns", "icon": "🔥", "topicFull": "Burns", "card": "skin/NG-123_major-burns.html", "cardLabel": "Major Burns — Treatment"}, {"q": "A client is 36 hours post major burn and urine output suddenly rises sharply. What does the nurse recognize?", "type": "single", "opts": ["The onset of acute kidney injury", "The expected fluid remobilization of the acute phase", "A sign of inadequate fluid resuscitation", "The development of sepsis"], "ans": [1], "why": "After roughly 24 to 48 hours the capillary leak seals and fluid moves <b>back into the vasculature</b>, so diuresis begins. That is the shift from the emergent to the acute phase. The risks now flip to <b>fluid overload and hypokalemia</b>, the opposite of the first day.", "topic": "Burns", "icon": "🔥", "topicFull": "Burns", "card": "skin/NG-123_major-burns.html", "cardLabel": "Major Burns — Treatment"}, {"q": "A client is burned at home at 0800 and arrives in the emergency department at 1000, when lactated Ringer's is started. Using the Parkland formula, by what time should the first half of the 24-hour volume be infused?", "type": "single", "opts": ["By 1600", "By 1800", "By midnight", "By 0800 the next morning"], "ans": [0], "why": "The first half of the Parkland volume must be in within <b>8 hours of the injury</b>, not 8 hours after arrival, so the deadline is 1600. Choosing 1800 times the clock from the ED arrival and would leave the client under-resuscitated for two extra hours."}, {"q": "A client is admitted with multiple scattered, irregular grease-splash burns across the anterior trunk, both thighs and one forearm. The pattern does not fit the rule of nines. Which method should the nurse use to estimate the burned surface area?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Use the client's palm including the fingers as approximately 1% TBSA", "Use the nurse's palm without the fingers as approximately 1% TBSA", "Assign the full 18% for the anterior trunk and 18% for both thighs together", "Round the scattered areas up to the nearest 9% using the rule of nines"], "ans": [0], "why": "For scattered irregular burns the estimate is made with <b>the client's own palm including the fingers, about 1% TBSA</b>. The nurse's palm is the tempting wrong answer because hand size varies between people, which is exactly why the client's hand is the standard."}, {"q": "A client admitted with 45 percent TBSA partial- and full-thickness burns is being prepared for fluid resuscitation. A new graduate asks which solution to hang for the calculated volume. Which IV fluid should the nurse obtain?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Lactated Ringer's", "0.9% sodium chloride", "5% dextrose in water", "5% dextrose in 0.45% sodium chloride"], "ans": [0], "why": "Parkland resuscitation is calculated and given as <b>lactated Ringer's</b> over the first 24 hours. Normal saline is the tempting answer because it is the default resuscitation fluid on most units, but burn resuscitation as taught here is LR, not NS and not a dextrose solution."}, {"q": "A client is admitted after a burn involving both legs, the buttocks and the perineum. There are no burns to the face, neck or chest, the voice is clear and no soot or singed hair is present. Which nursing action takes priority?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Establish IV access and begin the calculated lactated Ringer's infusion", "Prepare for early intubation before airway swelling develops", "Apply silver sulfadiazine to all of the burned areas", "Premedicate the client for the first dressing change"], "ans": [0], "why": "Burns from the <b>waist down make fluid the priority</b>; airway takes precedence only when the face, neck or chest is involved. Early intubation is the correct answer for an inhalation picture, but this client has none of the airway red flags, so delaying fluids to manage an airway that is not threatened is the error."}, {"q": "A client with a major burn is now past the emergent phase and is receiving daily wound care. Which priorities does the nurse address during the acute phase of burn care? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Monitoring the wounds for infection", "Providing aggressive nutrition support", "Managing pain, including premedication before every dressing change", "Calculating and titrating the Parkland fluid volume", "Beginning long-term management of scarring and contractures"], "ans": [0, 1, 2], "why": "The acute phase is <b>wound care, infection, nutrition and pain</b>. Parkland belongs to the emergent phase (0-48 hours) and scar and contracture work belongs to the rehabilitative phase, so both feel right to a student who has not separated the three phases."}, {"q": "A client is admitted with burns over approximately 65 percent TBSA. Which complications should the nurse monitor for over the course of this client's care? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Hypothermia", "Compartment syndrome", "Hypernatremia caused by the burn injury", "Fluid overload after the first 24 hours", "Hypokalemia caused by cell death"], "ans": [0, 1, 3], "why": "Very large burns bring <b>infection, hypothermia, compartment syndrome and fluid overload once the first 24 hours have passed</b>. The two electrolyte options are reversed: cell death drives sodium <b>down</b> and potassium <b>up</b>, so hypernatremia and hypokalemia are the traps."}, {"q": "An adult client's clothes caught fire. Burns cover the anterior half of the head (the entire face), the upper half of the anterior torso, the lower half of both arms circumferentially, the posterior surface of the head, and the upper half of the posterior torso. Using the rule of nines, what is the estimated TBSA?", "type": "single", "opts": ["36 percent", "24 percent", "18 percent", "48 percent"], "ans": [0], "why": "Anterior head 4.5 + posterior head 4.5 = <b>9</b> for the whole head. Upper half of the anterior torso <b>9</b>, upper half of the posterior torso <b>9</b>. Each arm is 9 percent, so the lower half of one arm is 4.5 - <b>circumferential means all the way around that lower half, not the whole arm</b> - giving <b>9</b> for the pair. 9 + 9 + 9 + 9 = <b>36 percent</b>. Counting each burned arm as a full 9 is what inflates the answer.", "topic": "Burns", "icon": "🔥", "topicFull": "Burns", "src": "Your Parkland practice worksheet", "card": "skin/NG-146_burns-3-rule-of-9s-rehabilitation.html", "cardLabel": "Rule of 9's & Parkland Formula"}, {"q": "An adult client weighs 174.74 lb and has a TBSA of 36 percent. Using the Parkland formula, how much fluid should be infused over the first 24 hours after the burn injury?", "type": "single", "opts": ["11,438 mL", "5,719 mL", "22,875 mL", "25,163 mL"], "ans": [0], "why": "Convert the weight first: 174.74 lb divided by 2.2 = <b>79.4 kg</b>. Parkland is 4 mL x kg x percent TBSA = 4 x 79.4 x 36 = <b>11,437.5, about 11,438 mL over 24 hours</b>. 5,719 mL is the half that runs in the first 8 hours, not the 24-hour total. 25,163 mL is the classic trap - <b>multiplying by pounds instead of kilograms</b>.", "topic": "Burns", "icon": "🔥", "topicFull": "Burns", "src": "Your Parkland practice worksheet", "card": "skin/NG-146_burns-3-rule-of-9s-rehabilitation.html", "cardLabel": "Rule of 9's & Parkland Formula"}, {"q": "An adult burn victim weighs 81 kg and has a TBSA of 19 percent. Using the Parkland formula, what is the total fluid to be infused in the first 8 hours?", "type": "single", "opts": ["2,798 mL", "3,078 mL", "12,312 mL", "6,156 mL"], "ans": [1], "why": "4 x 81 x 19 = <b>6,156 mL over 24 hours</b>, and half of it - <b>3,078 mL</b> - runs in the first 8 hours. 6,156 mL is the whole day's volume and 12,312 mL is double it. Note the 8-hour clock starts at the <b>time of injury</b>, not the time of arrival, so any time already lost comes off the front.", "topic": "Burns", "icon": "🔥", "topicFull": "Burns", "src": "Your Parkland practice worksheet", "card": "skin/NG-146_burns-3-rule-of-9s-rehabilitation.html", "cardLabel": "Rule of 9's & Parkland Formula"}, {"q": "An adult brought to the ED after an explosion has third-degree burns over approximately 42 percent of his body and weighs 78 kg. Using the Parkland formula, how much IV fluid should he receive in the first 24 hours?", "type": "single", "opts": ["13,104 mL", "6,552 mL", "26,208 mL", "3,276 mL"], "ans": [0], "why": "4 mL x 78 kg x 42 = <b>13,104 mL in the first 24 hours</b>. 6,552 mL is half of that, which is the first 8 hours' volume rather than the day's.", "topic": "Burns", "icon": "🔥", "topicFull": "Burns", "src": "Your Parkland practice worksheet", "card": "skin/NG-146_burns-3-rule-of-9s-rehabilitation.html", "cardLabel": "Rule of 9's & Parkland Formula"}, {"q": "An adult weighing 78 kg has burns over 42 percent TBSA. Using the Parkland formula, what rate should the lactated Ringer's run at during the first 8 hours?", "type": "single", "opts": ["819 mL/hr", "546 mL/hr", "1,638 mL/hr", "410 mL/hr"], "ans": [0], "why": "Half of the 13,104 mL total - <b>6,552 mL</b> - runs in the first 8 hours: 6,552 divided by 8 = <b>819 mL/hr</b>. 546 mL/hr is 13,104 mL spread evenly across 24 hours, which is precisely the error Parkland exists to prevent: the first 8 hours are <b>front-loaded</b>, and the remaining half runs over the following 16.", "topic": "Burns", "icon": "🔥", "topicFull": "Burns", "src": "Your Parkland practice worksheet", "card": "skin/NG-146_burns-3-rule-of-9s-rehabilitation.html", "cardLabel": "Rule of 9's & Parkland Formula"}, {"q": "Using the rule of nines, estimate the extent of burn injury in an adult with burns to the anterior trunk, the anterior left arm and the posterior left leg.", "type": "single", "opts": ["31.5 percent", "36 percent", "28.5 percent", "30 percent"], "ans": [0], "why": "Anterior trunk <b>18</b>, anterior surface of one arm <b>4.5</b> (half of that arm's 9), posterior surface of one leg <b>9</b> (half of that leg's 18). 18 + 4.5 + 9 = <b>31.5 percent</b>. Counting the whole arm and the whole leg instead gives 45 - the rule of nines splits every limb into front and back.", "topic": "Burns", "icon": "🔥", "topicFull": "Burns", "src": "Your Parkland practice worksheet", "card": "skin/NG-146_burns-3-rule-of-9s-rehabilitation.html", "cardLabel": "Rule of 9's & Parkland Formula"}, {"q": "A 47-year-old man weighing 91 kg arrives in the ED with burns to his entire head, the upper anterior torso, the anterior surface of both arms, and the genitalia. Using the rule of nines, what is his TBSA?", "type": "single", "opts": ["28 percent", "27 percent", "31.5 percent", "36 percent"], "ans": [0], "why": "Entire head <b>9</b>, upper half of the anterior torso <b>9</b>, anterior surface of each arm 4.5 (<b>9</b> for the pair), genitalia <b>1</b>. 9 + 9 + 9 + 1 = <b>28 percent</b>. 27 percent is what you get by <b>forgetting the genitalia</b> - that lone 1 percent is the reason the rule of nines totals 100 and not 99.", "topic": "Burns", "icon": "🔥", "topicFull": "Burns", "src": "Your Parkland practice worksheet", "card": "skin/NG-146_burns-3-rule-of-9s-rehabilitation.html", "cardLabel": "Rule of 9's & Parkland Formula"}, {"q": "A 47-year-old man weighing 91 kg has burns totalling 28 percent TBSA. Using the Parkland formula, what rate in mL/hr should be infused during the first 8 hours?", "type": "single", "opts": ["637 mL/hr", "425 mL/hr", "1,274 mL/hr", "319 mL/hr"], "ans": [0], "why": "4 x 91 kg x 28 = <b>10,192 mL over 24 hours</b>. Half of it - 5,096 mL - runs in the first 8 hours: 5,096 divided by 8 = <b>637 mL/hr</b>. 425 mL/hr is the same total spread evenly over 24 hours, and 1,274 mL/hr is the first-8-hours volume crammed into 4.", "topic": "Burns", "icon": "🔥", "topicFull": "Burns", "src": "Your Parkland practice worksheet", "card": "skin/NG-146_burns-3-rule-of-9s-rehabilitation.html", "cardLabel": "Rule of 9's & Parkland Formula"}, {"q": "A worker splashed with a liquid chemical arrives at the emergency department. Wearing protective equipment, which action does the nurse take first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Remove all contaminated clothing, jewelry and shoes, then flush the skin with large amounts of water", "Apply a neutralizing agent to the burned skin", "Cover the area with a dry sterile dressing and calculate the TBSA", "Apply silver sulfadiazine cream"], "ans": [0], "why": "A chemical keeps burning as long as it touches the skin, so <b>everything contaminated comes off</b> (clothing, gloves, shoes, undergarments) and the area is <b>irrigated with copious water</b>. Neutralizers can generate heat and deepen the injury; dressings and topical agents come after decontamination.", "card": "nur258-module-04-burns.html#dx-electrical-and-chemical-burns", "src": "SimpleNursing review video (Fall 2026, Wk 4): burns"}, {"q": "A roofer arrives with hot tar stuck to the forearm. What is the immediate action?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Cool the tar and skin with cool water", "Peel the tar off before it hardens", "Apply ice directly to the tar", "Rub the area with alcohol to dissolve the tar"], "ans": [0], "why": "<b>Cooling with water</b> stops the burning process. The hardened tar is left in place and later loosened with a petroleum-based or mineral-oil product - peeling it off removes the skin underneath. Ice causes further tissue injury.", "card": "nur258-module-04-burns.html#dx-burns", "src": "SimpleNursing review video (Fall 2026, Wk 4): burns"}, {"q": "A client is discharged with partial-thickness burns of the right hand. Which instruction is appropriate?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Wrap each finger separately when dressing the hand", "Wrap the fingers together in one dressing to keep them still", "Keep the hand below the level of the heart", "Avoid moving the fingers until the burns heal"], "ans": [0], "why": "<b>Individual finger dressings</b> keep burned surfaces from sticking together (webbing) and allow movement. The hand is <b>elevated</b> to reduce edema and the fingers are <b>exercised</b> to prevent contractures.", "card": "nur258-module-04-burns.html#dx-acute-and-rehabilitative-phases", "src": "SimpleNursing review video (Fall 2026, Wk 4): burns"}, {"q": "A client has burns over 35 percent TBSA. Which laboratory finding does the nurse expect during the first 24 hours?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Hematocrit 60%", "Hematocrit 30%", "Serum potassium 3.0 mEq/L", "Serum sodium 150 mEq/L"], "ans": [0], "why": "Plasma leaks into the tissues as capillary permeability rises, so the blood left behind is concentrated (<b>hemoconcentration</b>) and the <b>hematocrit rises</b>. Potassium rises in the emergent phase from cell destruction, and sodium falls as it is trapped in the edema fluid.", "card": "nur258-module-04-burns.html#dx-emergent-resuscitative-phase", "src": "SimpleNursing review video (Fall 2026, Wk 4): burns"}, {"q": "Twelve hours after a 20 percent TBSA burn, a client receiving lactated Ringer's has BP 90/50, pulse 110 and a urine output of 20 mL in the past hour. Which action does the nurse anticipate?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Increase the lactated Ringer's rate per the resuscitation protocol", "Give furosemide to increase urine output", "Change the IV fluid to D5W", "Restrict oral and IV fluids until the edema decreases"], "ans": [0], "why": "<b>Urine output is the best guide</b> to resuscitation. Below 30 mL/hr (0.5 mL/kg/hr) with hypotension and tachycardia means the client is <b>under-resuscitated</b>, so the isotonic crystalloid rate goes up. A diuretic would worsen the hypovolemia, and D5W is hypotonic and will not stay in the vessels.", "card": "nur258-module-04-burns.html#dx-emergent-resuscitative-phase", "src": "SimpleNursing review video (Fall 2026, Wk 4): burns"}, {"q": "Using the rule of nines, what percentage of TBSA is burned in an adult with full-thickness burns of every posterior surface: the back of the head, the back of both arms, the back of the trunk including the buttocks, and the back of both legs?", "type": "single", "opts": ["49.5%", "36%", "45%", "54%"], "ans": [0], "why": "Posterior head <b>4.5</b> + posterior arms <b>4.5 + 4.5</b> + posterior trunk <b>18</b> + posterior legs <b>9 + 9</b> = <b>49.5%</b>, about half the body. The perineum's 1% is counted only if it is burned.", "card": "skin/NG-146_burns-3-rule-of-9s-rehabilitation.html#adult-rule-of-nines", "src": "SimpleNursing review video (Fall 2026, Wk 4): burns"}, {"q": "An adult has partial-thickness burns of the anterior surface of both legs and the perineum. Using the rule of nines, what is the TBSA?", "type": "single", "opts": ["19%", "18%", "27%", "37%"], "ans": [0], "why": "Anterior right leg <b>9</b> + anterior left leg <b>9</b> + perineum <b>1</b> = <b>19%</b>. Each whole leg is 18%, so the front of one leg is 9%.", "card": "skin/NG-146_burns-3-rule-of-9s-rehabilitation.html#adult-rule-of-nines", "src": "SimpleNursing review video (Fall 2026, Wk 4): burns"}, {"q": "A nurse is planning care for a client who has extensive burn injuries and is immunocompromised. Which precaution should the nurse include in the plan of care to prevent a Pseudomonas aeruginosa infection?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Avoid placing plants or flowers in the client's room", "Limit visitors to members of the client's immediate family", "Wear an N95 respirator mask when providing care to the client", "Encourage the client to eat raw fruits and vegetables"], "ans": [0], "why": "<i>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</i> lives in standing water and soil, so vase water and potting soil are reservoirs; keep plants and flowers out of the room of a client with burns who is immunocompromised. An N95 respirator is for airborne organisms such as TB. Raw fruits and vegetables carry organisms and are avoided, not encouraged. Restricting visitors to family does not remove the source; the rule is no visitors who are ill.", "topic": "Burns", "src": "Caroline's practice question, burn infection (27 Oct)", "icon": "🔥", "topicFull": "Burns"}], "5": [{"q": "A client in adrenal crisis has BP 78/44, confusion, potassium 6.1, severe abdominal pain, vomiting and diarrhea. Which action does the nurse take first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Give oral hydrocortisone with a sip of water", "Infuse a 0.9 percent sodium chloride bolus", "Administer oral potassium binder", "Obtain a dexamethasone suppression test"], "ans": [1], "why": "Crisis management starts with <b>fluids (and dextrose) before the definitive steroid</b>, and the instructors specifically wanted a 0.9 percent NS bolus. Every oral option is wrong here because she is vomiting and has diarrhea.", "card": "nur258-module-05-endocrine-disorders.html#dx-addison-disease-and-adrenal-crisis", "cardLabel": "Addison disease and adrenal crisis"}, {"q": "Four hours after a thyroidectomy, which finding does the nurse address first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["A hoarse, weakening voice", "Positive Chvostek sign", "Sore throat when swallowing", "Serosanguineous drainage on the dressing"], "ans": [0], "why": "<b>Voice changes signal recurrent laryngeal nerve involvement and airway risk</b>, so they rank first; hypocalcemia (Chvostek, Trousseau, carpopedal spasm) comes next, then neck swelling. Chvostek looks more dramatic on paper but the airway outranks it."}, {"q": "A client has 6 L of pale urine in 24 hours, urine specific gravity 1.002, serum sodium 152 and BP 88/54. Which condition does the nurse suspect?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Diabetes insipidus", "SIADH", "Cushing's syndrome", "Thyroid storm"], "ans": [0], "why": "Dilute high-volume urine with <b>low specific gravity and high serum sodium</b> is DI - dry inside, too little ADH. SIADH is the mirror: scant concentrated urine, high specific gravity, overload and low sodium."}, {"q": "A client with SIADH has a serum sodium of 120. Which interventions does the nurse include? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Fluid restriction", "Encourage 3 L of oral fluids daily", "Seizure precautions", "Neurologic checks", "Daily potassium supplementation"], "ans": [0, 2, 3], "why": "SIADH is treated with <b>fluid restriction plus diuretics and sodium</b>, and a sodium of 120 makes this a neuro problem - <b>seizure precautions and neuro checks</b> are required. Pushing fluids dilutes the sodium further; potassium is not the disordered electrolyte here."}, {"q": "Which laboratory pattern fits Addison's disease?", "type": "single", "opts": ["High sodium, high potassium, high glucose", "Low sodium, high potassium, low glucose", "Low sodium, low potassium, high glucose", "High sodium, low potassium, low glucose"], "ans": [1], "why": "Addison's is low cortisol and aldosterone: <b>low BP, low glucose, low sodium - but high potassium</b>. Cushing's is the mirror image on nearly every parameter except potassium, which inverts, and that single flip is usually the whole question."}, {"q": "A client taking levothyroxine has temp 104.9 F, HR 125, RR 42, restlessness and heat intolerance. Which intervention does the nurse anticipate?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Administer methimazole to block hormone synthesis", "Give a beta blocker, begin cooling, hold the next dose and notify the provider", "Increase the levothyroxine dose to stabilize the thyroid axis", "Start IV hydrocortisone and warming measures"], "ans": [1], "why": "This is iatrogenic hyperthyroidism from excess levothyroxine, so treatment is <b>beta blocker, cooling, low-stimulation room, hold the dose and notify</b>. Antithyroid drugs are useless because the excess T4 is already circulating, not being manufactured."}, {"q": "A client with Addison's disease is admitted with vomiting and hypotension. Which findings does the nurse expect? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Hyperkalemia", "Hyponatremia", "Hypoglycemia", "Hypertension", "Hyperpigmentation of skin creases"], "ans": [0, 1, 2, 4], "why": "Addison's is <b>too little</b> of everything adrenal: aldosterone falls so sodium is lost and potassium is retained; cortisol falls so glucose falls; ACTH rises trying to flog the gland and <b>pigments the skin</b>. Hypertension belongs to Cushing's, the mirror image.", "topic": "Endocrine Disorders", "icon": "⚗️", "topicFull": "Endocrine Disorders", "card": "endo/NG-287_addisons-vs-cushings.html", "cardLabel": "Addison’s vs Cushing’s"}, {"q": "Which client statement indicates correct understanding of lifelong corticosteroid replacement?", "type": "single", "opts": ["\"I will stop the steroid if I get a stomach upset.\"", "\"If I am ill or having surgery, my dose will need to go up, not down.\"", "\"I can stop once my energy returns.\"", "\"I should take it at bedtime to avoid daytime side effects.\""], "ans": [1], "why": "The single most important teaching in adrenal insufficiency is <b>stress dosing</b>: illness, injury and surgery all require more, never less. Abruptly stopping precipitates <b>addisonian crisis</b>, which kills. Replacement is taken in the <b>morning</b> to mimic the natural cortisol peak.", "pharm": 1, "topic": "Endocrine Disorders", "icon": "⚗️", "topicFull": "Endocrine Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-05-endocrine-disorders.html#dx-addison-disease-and-adrenal-crisis", "cardLabel": "Addison disease and adrenal crisis"}, {"q": "A client has SIADH. Which findings does the nurse expect? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Serum sodium 122 mEq/L", "Concentrated urine with high specific gravity", "Urine output of 6 L per day", "Weight gain without edema", "Serum osmolality below normal"], "ans": [0, 1, 3, 4], "why": "SIADH is <b>too much</b> ADH: water is held, so the client dilutes - low sodium, low serum osmolality, concentrated urine, and weight gain from water that stays intracellular rather than forming pitting edema. Six liters of urine a day is <b>diabetes insipidus</b>, the opposite condition.", "topic": "Endocrine Disorders", "icon": "⚗️", "topicFull": "Endocrine Disorders", "card": "endo/NG-282_siadh-vs-di-1.html", "cardLabel": "SIADH vs DI (1)"}, {"q": "A client with hypothyroidism is prescribed levothyroxine. Which instructions are correct? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Take it in the morning on an empty stomach", "Separate it from calcium and iron by at least 4 hours", "Report palpitations, weight loss or heat intolerance", "Expect full symptom relief within 48 hours", "Do not switch brands without telling the provider"], "ans": [0, 1, 2, 4], "why": "Levothyroxine needs an <b>empty stomach</b> and is blocked by calcium and iron. Palpitations and heat intolerance mean the dose has gone too high - the client has been made hyperthyroid. Brands differ in bioavailability. Relief takes <b>weeks</b>, not days, and expecting otherwise is why clients stop taking it.", "pharm": 1, "topic": "Endocrine Disorders", "icon": "⚗️", "topicFull": "Endocrine Disorders", "card": "endo/NG-285_hypothyroidism.html", "cardLabel": "Hypothyroidism"}, {"q": "A client with Hashimoto's hypothyroidism is admitted lethargic and difficult to arouse, hypothermic and bradycardic, with poor respiratory effort. Which action does the nurse take first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Support the client's airway and breathing", "Administer IV levothyroxine", "Begin an IV infusion of fluids with dextrose", "Apply warming blankets"], "ans": [0], "why": "In myxedema coma everything slows, and the module is explicit that <b>airway is the priority</b> when a hypothyroid client is lethargic with poor respiratory effort. Fluids with dextrose do come before IV levothyroxine, and warming is part of the treatment, but no drug helps a client who is not ventilating."}, {"q": "A client with pheochromocytoma has severe hypertension, headache and diaphoresis and is scheduled for adrenal surgery. The first dose of doxazosin is due. Which action is the priority?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Stay with the client and monitor for severe hypotension after the first dose", "Withhold the alpha blocker until after the adrenal tumor is removed", "Teach the client that the medication shrinks the tumor before surgery", "Expect the medication to raise the blood pressure toward normal"], "ans": [0], "why": "Alpha blockers — the <b>\"-osin\"</b> drugs such as doxazosin and terazosin — are started before surgery for this catecholamine-secreting tumor, and <b>severe first-dose hypotension</b> is the known risk to watch. Withholding until after surgery is the tempting wrong choice: the blockade must be established beforehand, not afterward."}, {"q": "A client reports months of fatigue, cold intolerance and constipation. Laboratory results show a low T4 with a low TSH. How does the nurse interpret this pattern?", "type": "single", "opts": ["The pituitary has failed, which is secondary hypothyroidism", "The thyroid gland itself has failed, which is primary hypothyroidism", "Hashimoto's thyroiditis is the most likely cause", "The client has been overtreated with thyroid replacement"], "ans": [0], "why": "Compare the gland hormone with its stimulating hormone: when both move the <b>same</b> direction, the problem is upstream, so low T4 with low TSH is pituitary, or secondary, failure. Primary hypothyroidism — including Hashimoto's — shows low T4 with a <b>high</b> TSH, because the pituitary is still pushing a thyroid that cannot answer."}, {"q": "A client who has taken high-dose oral steroids for months stopped them two days ago because he felt better. He is now weak, dizzy and hypotensive. Which explanation does the nurse give?", "type": "single", "opts": ["His adrenal glands have atrophied and cannot make cortisol, so the steroid must be tapered rather than stopped", "The dose was too high, and the symptoms will pass as the drug clears from his system", "He has developed Cushing's syndrome from the months of steroid therapy", "The symptoms are unrelated to the steroid and suggest an infection"], "ans": [0], "why": "Exogenous steroids suppress the body's own cortisol and the adrenals atrophy, which is why steroids are <b>always tapered, never stopped abruptly</b>; stopping leaves an acute cortisol deficit with weakness and hypotension. Waiting for the drug to clear is wrong — the problem is too <b>little</b> cortisol now, not too much."}, {"q": "A client admitted with Cushing's syndrome has moon face, a buffalo hump and central obesity with thin limbs. Which additional findings does the nurse expect? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Hypertension", "Hypokalemia", "Bronze hyperpigmentation of the skin", "Elevated blood glucose", "Hyponatremia"], "ans": [0, 1, 3], "why": "Cushing's is Addison's mirror image: too much cortisol raises blood pressure, glucose and sodium, and <b>potassium is the one value that inverts</b> and falls. Bronze skin and hyponatremia are the tempting picks, but both belong to Addison's, where cortisol is too low."}, {"q": "A client with Graves' disease is admitted with temp 103.8 F, HR 142, BP 168/88 and delirium. Which interventions does the nurse anticipate? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["IV fluids with dextrose", "A beta blocker for the cardiac symptoms", "External cooling measures", "IV levothyroxine", "Holding antithyroid medication until the fever resolves"], "ans": [0, 1, 2], "why": "This is thyroid storm: <b>fluids with dextrose come first</b>, then a beta blocker for the cardiac symptoms plus PTU or methimazole, plus cooling. IV levothyroxine is the trap — it treats the opposite crisis, myxedema coma — and the antithyroid drug is given, not held."}, {"q": "A 32-year-old client has BP 145/95, serum potassium 3.1 mEq/L, ST depression with prominent U waves on the monitor, and frequent urination. Which action is the nurse's priority?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Place the client on continuous cardiac monitoring", "Encourage foods high in sodium", "Administer the scheduled furosemide", "Obtain a 24-hour urine collection"], "ans": [0], "why": "Hypertension with hypokalemia suggests <b>primary hyperaldosteronism (Conn syndrome)</b>: aldosterone holds sodium and water and wastes potassium. A potassium of 3.1 with ST depression and U waves puts the client at risk of <b>dysrhythmias</b>, so monitoring comes first. Sodium is restricted, a potassium-wasting diuretic would worsen the hypokalemia (spironolactone is the potassium-sparing choice), and urine collection is diagnostic, not a priority.", "card": "nur258-module-05-endocrine-disorders.html#dx-endo", "src": "SimpleNursing review video (Fall 2026, Wk 5): hyperaldosteronism"}, {"q": "A client is being discharged after a bilateral adrenalectomy. Which statement indicates a need for further teaching?", "type": "single", "opts": ["\"Once my incision heals I can stop taking the hormone pills.\"", "\"I will wear a medical alert bracelet.\"", "\"I will call my provider if I get sick, because my dose may need to go up.\"", "\"I will take these hormones for the rest of my life.\""], "ans": [0], "why": "With both adrenal glands removed, <b>replacement is lifelong</b> - removing any endocrine gland means lifelong hormone replacement. Stopping abruptly can cause <b>adrenal crisis</b>. Medical ID and sick-day dose increases are correct.", "card": "nur258-module-05-endocrine-disorders.html#dx-addison-disease-and-adrenal-crisis", "src": "SimpleNursing review video (Fall 2026, Wk 5): Addison disease vs Cushing syndrome"}, {"q": "A client with Addison's disease is starting daily hydrocortisone replacement. Which instructions should the nurse include? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Wear a medical alert bracelet or carry ID", "Your dose will need to be increased during illness, surgery or major stress, as directed by your provider", "Never stop the medication suddenly", "Take the dose in the morning (the larger part in the morning if it is split)", "Follow a low-sodium diet"], "ans": [0, 1, 2, 3], "why": "Replacement is lifelong, and <b>stress raises cortisol needs</b>, so sick-day and surgery dose increases are taught. <b>Abrupt withdrawal can cause adrenal crisis.</b> Morning dosing mimics the body's normal cortisol rhythm. Clients with Addison's lose sodium, so salt is not restricted.", "card": "nur258-module-05-endocrine-disorders.html#dx-addison-disease-and-adrenal-crisis", "src": "SimpleNursing review video (Fall 2026, Wk 5): Addison disease vs Cushing syndrome"}, {"q": "A client with type 2 diabetes is starting long-term prednisone. What should the nurse anticipate?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Higher blood glucose readings that may need more insulin or a medication adjustment", "Lower blood glucose readings that may need less insulin", "No change, because prednisone does not affect glucose", "Glucose checks can stop while the client is on prednisone"], "ans": [0], "why": "Glucocorticoids <b>raise blood glucose</b> (more glucose production and insulin resistance), so glucose is checked more often and diabetes medicines often need increasing while on steroids, then adjusting back as the dose is tapered.", "card": "nur258-module-05-endocrine-disorders.html#dx-cushing-syndrome", "src": "SimpleNursing review video (Fall 2026, Wk 5): Addison disease vs Cushing syndrome"}, {"q": "A client has primary hyperparathyroidism with a serum calcium of 12.4 mg/dL. Which findings does the nurse expect? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Kidney stones", "Bone pain or fractures", "Constipation", "Decreased deep tendon reflexes and muscle weakness", "Positive Chvostek sign", "Tetany"], "ans": [0, 1, 2, 3], "why": "<b>Stones, bones and groans</b>: PTH pulls calcium out of bone (pain, fractures), the extra calcium forms <b>kidney stones</b>, and high calcium depresses nerve and muscle (<b>constipation, weakness, decreased reflexes</b>). Chvostek sign and tetany are signs of <b>low</b> calcium, as after a thyroidectomy.", "card": "nur258-module-05-endocrine-disorders.html#dx-endo", "src": "SimpleNursing review video (Fall 2026, Wk 5): hyper- and hypoparathyroidism"}, {"q": "A client with hypothyroidism has been taking levothyroxine. Which statements indicate a need for further teaching? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["\"It has been 18 days and I don't feel better, so I will stop it.\"", "\"This medicine will cure my thyroid.\"", "\"If I miss a dose, I will take two the next day.\"", "\"I take it in the morning on an empty stomach, 30 to 60 minutes before breakfast.\"", "\"I will keep taking it if I become pregnant.\""], "ans": [0, 1, 2], "why": "The full effect takes <b>several weeks</b>, so stopping at 18 days is premature. Levothyroxine <b>replaces</b> hormone for life; it does not cure. <b>Doubling doses</b> risks hyperthyroid symptoms. Morning, empty-stomach dosing is correct, and levothyroxine is <b>continued in pregnancy</b> (the dose often needs increasing).", "card": "nur258-module-05-endocrine-disorders.html#dx-hypothyroidism-and-myxedema-coma", "src": "SimpleNursing review video (Fall 2026, Wk 5): hyper vs hypothyroidism"}, {"q": "A client with Graves' disease asks about diet. Which plan does the nurse teach?", "type": "single", "opts": ["High-calorie, high-protein meals in six small feedings a day, avoiding caffeine", "Low-calorie, low-fat meals three times a day", "High-fiber meals with extra coffee for energy", "Low-protein meals to slow the metabolism"], "ans": [0], "why": "Hypermetabolism burns calories quickly, so <b>frequent high-calorie, high-protein meals</b> prevent weight loss. <b>Caffeine</b> worsens tachycardia, nervousness and insomnia, and high-fiber foods add to the diarrhea.", "card": "nur258-module-05-endocrine-disorders.html#dx-hyperthyroidism-and-thyroid-storm", "src": "SimpleNursing review video (Fall 2026, Wk 5): hyper vs hypothyroidism"}, {"q": "A client with untreated hypothyroidism is admitted with pneumonia. Which prescription should the nurse question?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Morphine 4 mg IV every 2 hours as needed", "Levothyroxine by mouth daily", "Acetaminophen for fever", "Incentive spirometry every hour while awake"], "ans": [0], "why": "Hypothyroid clients clear <b>opioids and sedatives</b> slowly and are very sensitive to them; respiratory depression can tip them into <b>myxedema coma</b>. These drugs are avoided or given at reduced doses with close monitoring, so a frequent IV opioid order is questioned.", "card": "nur258-module-05-endocrine-disorders.html#dx-hypothyroidism-and-myxedema-coma", "src": "SimpleNursing review video (Fall 2026, Wk 5): hyper vs hypothyroidism"}, {"q": "A client with newly diagnosed hypothyroidism also has signs of adrenal insufficiency. Which treatment does the nurse expect to be started first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["A corticosteroid such as hydrocortisone", "Levothyroxine", "Methimazole", "Potassium iodide"], "ans": [0], "why": "Thyroid hormone speeds metabolism, including the breakdown of cortisol, so starting it with untreated adrenal insufficiency can trigger <b>adrenal crisis</b>. <b>Steroids first, thyroid hormone second.</b>", "card": "nur258-module-05-endocrine-disorders.html#dx-hypothyroidism-and-myxedema-coma", "src": "SimpleNursing review video (Fall 2026, Wk 5): thyroid hormones and overview"}, {"q": "A client taking methimazole for Graves' disease calls with a sore throat and a temperature of 38.5 &deg;C (101.3 &deg;F). What should the nurse tell the client?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Hold the medication and come in today for a white blood cell count", "Take acetaminophen and continue the medication", "Double tomorrow's dose to control the symptoms", "Gargle with salt water; this is expected in the first month"], "ans": [0], "why": "Methimazole and PTU can cause <b>agranulocytosis</b>; <b>fever and sore throat</b> are its warning signs. The drug is held and a CBC with differential is checked right away.", "card": "nur258-module-05-endocrine-disorders.html#dx-hyperthyroidism-and-thyroid-storm", "src": "SimpleNursing review video (Fall 2026, Wk 5): thyroid hormones and overview"}, {"q": "Which findings does the nurse expect in a client with hyperthyroidism (Graves' disease)? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Heat intolerance and sweating", "Weight loss despite an increased appetite", "Tachycardia or palpitations", "Frequent loose stools", "Cold intolerance", "Constipation"], "ans": [0, 1, 2, 3], "why": "Hyperthyroid is <b>hot and fast</b>: heat intolerance, sweating, weight loss with a big appetite, tachycardia, frequent stools, anxiety and insomnia. Cold intolerance and constipation are the <b>cold and slow</b> picture of hypothyroidism.", "card": "nur258-module-05-endocrine-disorders.html#dx-hyperthyroidism-and-thyroid-storm", "src": "SimpleNursing review video (Fall 2026, Wk 5): hyper vs hypothyroidism"}, {"q": "A client with a history of Graves' disease has a temperature of 38.6 &deg;C (101.5 &deg;F), a heart rate of 160, agitation and confusion. Which finding most suggests thyroid storm rather than sepsis alone?", "type": "single", "opts": ["A heart rate far higher than the fever would explain, in a client with a thyroid history", "An elevated white blood cell count", "A low blood pressure", "A positive urine culture"], "ans": [0], "why": "Both cause fever, tachycardia and altered mental status. <b>Tachycardia out of proportion to the fever</b>, with a hyperthyroid history, points to <b>thyroid storm</b>. Infection is also a common trigger of storm, so the two can occur together.", "card": "nur258-module-05-endocrine-disorders.html#dx-hyperthyroidism-and-thyroid-storm", "src": "SimpleNursing review video (Fall 2026, Wk 5): thyroid hormones and overview"}, {"q": "A client has taken prednisone 40 mg daily for 6 months for Crohn's disease, which is now in remission. The provider plans to stop it. What is the nurse's priority?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Confirm a tapering schedule and teach the signs of adrenal insufficiency", "Stop the prednisone today because the disease is in remission", "Change to every-other-day dosing on the nurse's own judgment", "Teach a low-sodium diet"], "ans": [0], "why": "Months of steroids suppress the body's own cortisol production (the HPA axis). Stopping abruptly can cause <b>adrenal insufficiency or crisis</b>, so the dose is <b>tapered</b> and the client learns to report weakness, dizziness, nausea or low blood pressure.", "card": "nur258-module-05-endocrine-disorders.html#dx-addison-disease-and-adrenal-crisis", "src": "SimpleNursing review video (Fall 2026, Wk 5): Addison disease, Addisonian crisis and Cushing syndrome"}, {"q": "After an adrenalectomy for Cushing syndrome, the nurse monitors closely for which complication?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Adrenal insufficiency: hypotension, weakness, hyponatremia and hypoglycemia", "Thyroid storm", "Hypercalcemia", "Hyperkalemia from excess aldosterone"], "ans": [0], "why": "Once the source of excess cortisol is removed, the remaining adrenal tissue has been suppressed and cannot make enough, so the client can swing into <b>adrenal insufficiency</b>. Glucocorticoid replacement is given and blood pressure, electrolytes and glucose are watched closely.", "card": "nur258-module-05-endocrine-disorders.html#dx-cushing-syndrome", "src": "SimpleNursing review video (Fall 2026, Wk 5): Addison disease, Addisonian crisis and Cushing syndrome"}, {"q": "Which finding points to primary adrenal insufficiency (Addison's disease) rather than secondary insufficiency from long-term steroid use?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Bronze hyperpigmentation of the skin with a high ACTH level", "A low ACTH level", "A history of long-term prednisone use", "Normal serum potassium"], "ans": [0], "why": "In <b>primary</b> disease the adrenal glands fail, so the pituitary pours out <b>ACTH</b> - which darkens the skin - and aldosterone is lost too (high potassium). In <b>secondary</b> insufficiency ACTH is low, there is no bronzing, and aldosterone is preserved.", "card": "nur258-module-05-endocrine-disorders.html#dx-addison-disease-and-adrenal-crisis", "src": "SimpleNursing review video (Fall 2026, Wk 5): Addison disease, Addisonian crisis and Cushing syndrome"}, {"q": "Which test does the nurse expect to confirm a diagnosis of Addison's disease?", "type": "single", "opts": ["ACTH (cosyntropin) stimulation test", "Dexamethasone suppression test", "24-hour urine for catecholamines", "Water deprivation test"], "ans": [0], "why": "In the <b>cosyntropin stimulation test</b>, synthetic ACTH is given and cortisol fails to rise when the adrenal glands have failed. Dexamethasone suppression tests for Cushing syndrome, urine catecholamines for pheochromocytoma, and water deprivation for diabetes insipidus.", "card": "nur258-module-05-endocrine-disorders.html#dx-addison-disease-and-adrenal-crisis", "src": "SimpleNursing review video (Fall 2026, Wk 5): Addison disease, Addisonian crisis and Cushing syndrome"}], "6": [{"q": "A client arrives with glucose 640, dry mucous membranes and confusion. Which assessment does the nurse perform first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Level of consciousness", "Hydration and circulatory status", "Repeat fingerstick glucose", "Urine ketones"], "ans": [1], "why": "In severe hyperglycemia the first assessment is <b>dehydration and circulation</b> - it sits in the ABCs, ahead of a fingerstick and ahead of LOC, because glucose is not A-B-C-D. Both crises start with IV fluids, then a regular insulin drip."}, {"q": "Which client does the nurse assess first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["A client with glucose 386 and 2+ urine ketones ordered for an insulin drip", "A client with glucose 58 who is shaky, sweaty and confused", "A client with an A1C of 9.1 percent asking about diet", "A client with glucose 240 who missed a morning snack"], "ans": [1], "why": "<b>Hypoglycemia outranks a very high glucose in acuity</b> because she can lose consciousness within minutes. Awake at 58, she needs 15 g of fast carbohydrate (4 to 6 oz of juice) and a recheck in 15 minutes."}, {"q": "An insulin drip is ordered at 0.1 units/kg/hr for a client weighing 165 lb. The bag is 100 units in 100 mL. What rate does the nurse set?", "type": "single", "opts": ["4 mL/hr", "8 mL/hr", "15 mL/hr", "17 mL/hr"], "ans": [1], "why": "165 divided by 2.2 = 75 kg; 75 x 0.1 = 7.5 units/hr, and at 1 unit/mL that is 7.5, rounded to <b>8 mL/hr</b>. Answering 17 means the pounds were never converted - 2.2 always goes with the pounds."}, {"q": "A client is on a DKA insulin drip. Which actions does the nurse include? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Check glucose hourly", "Switch to a dextrose-containing fluid when glucose reaches 250", "Give sodium bicarbonate once glucose falls below 300", "Replace potassium as it normalizes and begins to fall", "Change to subcutaneous insulin as soon as ketones clear"], "ans": [0, 1, 3], "why": "The drip set is <b>hourly glucose, D5-containing fluid at 250 to avoid cerebral edema, and potassium replacement</b> because insulin drives K into cells. Bicarbonate is only for pH below 7.0, and subcutaneous insulin is never used during a crisis."}, {"q": "A client takes NPH insulin at 0700. When should the nurse teach her that a snack is most important?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Around 0715, when the insulin peaks", "Around 0900, 2 hours after the dose", "Around 1500, about 8 hours after the dose", "At bedtime only"], "ans": [2], "why": "NPH is the only intermediate insulin: onset 1 to 2 hours, <b>peak 6 to 8 hours</b>, so a snack belongs around the 8-hour mark. The 15-minute answer belongs to lispro and aspart, which are given immediately before a meal, never 30 minutes ahead."}, {"q": "A client with type 1 diabetes has influenza and is eating almost nothing. Which instruction is correct?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Hold the insulin until she is eating normally again", "Take half the usual insulin dose while appetite is poor", "Keep taking insulin, check glucose every 2 to 4 hours and check ketones", "Stop insulin but check glucose every 2 hours"], "ans": [2], "why": "The sick-day rule is <b>keep taking insulin even when not eating</b>, monitor glucose every 2 to 4 hours, check ketones, hydrate, and call for glucose over 250 that is not falling or illness beyond 2 days. Illness raises glucose through stress hormones, so skipping or halving the dose drives her toward DKA."}, {"type": "sata", "week": "6", "step": "recognize", "topic": "Diabetes", "q": "A nurse is caring for a client who has type 1 diabetes and is admitted with diabetic ketoacidosis. Which findings should the nurse expect? (Select all that apply.)", "opts": ["Fruity breath odor", "Kussmaul respirations", "Blood glucose less than 70 mg/dL", "Abdominal pain and vomiting", "Serum bicarbonate above 24 mEq/L", "Polyuria and polydipsia"], "ans": [0, 1, 3, 5], "why": "DKA classically produces a fruity breath odor from ketone buildup, Kussmaul respirations that blow off excess CO2 and acid, GI symptoms from ketosis and acidosis, and polyuria/polydipsia from osmotic diuresis. DKA causes hyperglycemia, not hypoglycemia, so a glucose under 70 mg/dL would not be expected. DKA causes metabolic acidosis with a low, not elevated, bicarbonate level, so a bicarbonate above 24 mEq/L is inconsistent with the diagnosis.", "src": "ATI Adult Med-Surg ch 83 (Complications of Diabetes Mellitus - DKA and HHS)", "icon": "🍬", "topicFull": "Diabetes", "card": "endo/NG-291_dka-vs-hhs.html", "cardLabel": "DKA vs HHS"}, {"q": "A client with type 1 diabetes has a blood glucose of 480 mg/dL, ketones in the urine, and deep rapid respirations. Which is the priority action?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Give the prescribed IV regular insulin bolus", "Begin IV 0.9% sodium chloride", "Administer sodium bicarbonate", "Give the client orange juice"], "ans": [1], "why": "In diabetic ketoacidosis the client is profoundly <b>volume depleted</b> from osmotic diuresis, and fluid comes first - it lowers glucose on its own and protects perfusion. Insulin follows, and only once <b>potassium is known</b>, because insulin drives potassium into cells and can stop the heart. Bicarbonate is reserved for severe acidosis.", "topic": "Diabetes", "icon": "🍬", "topicFull": "Diabetes", "card": "endo/NG-290_diabetes.html", "cardLabel": "Diabetes"}, {"q": "Which findings distinguish hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state from diabetic ketoacidosis? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Glucose often above 600 mg/dL", "Absent or minimal ketones", "Kussmaul respirations", "Usually occurs in type 2 diabetes", "Profound neurological changes"], "ans": [0, 1, 3, 4], "why": "HHS is the <b>type 2</b> emergency: enough insulin to block ketone production but not enough to control glucose, so sugars go higher, ketones stay absent, and the picture is dominated by <b>dehydration and altered mental status</b>. <b>Kussmaul respirations belong to DKA</b> - they are the lungs blowing off acid, and without ketoacidosis there is no acid to blow off.", "topic": "Diabetes", "icon": "🍬", "topicFull": "Diabetes", "card": "endo/NG-291_dka-vs-hhs.html", "cardLabel": "DKA vs HHS"}, {"q": "A client takes NPH insulin at 0730. When is hypoglycemia most likely?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Around 0800", "Between 1200 and 1900", "Around 2300", "Immediately after the injection"], "ans": [1], "why": "NPH is <b>intermediate-acting</b>: onset 1 to 2 hours, <b>peak 4 to 12 hours</b>, duration 12 to 18. A 0730 dose peaks across the afternoon and early evening, so that is when the client is at risk - typically before dinner. Knowing peaks is how these questions are answered, not memorizing times.", "pharm": 1, "topic": "Diabetes", "icon": "🍬", "topicFull": "Diabetes", "card": "endo/NG-290_diabetes.html", "cardLabel": "Diabetes"}, {"q": "Which instructions are correct for a client with diabetes about sick days? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Continue taking insulin even if unable to eat", "Check blood glucose every 3 to 4 hours", "Check urine or blood ketones when glucose is above 240 mg/dL", "Hold all insulin until eating resumes", "Drink sugar-free fluids if glucose is high and sugar-containing fluids if it is low"], "ans": [0, 1, 2, 4], "why": "Illness raises counter-regulatory hormones, so <b>glucose rises even when nothing is eaten</b>. Holding insulin during illness is how people arrive in DKA. Monitoring goes up, ketones get checked above 240, and fluid choice follows the glucose.", "topic": "Diabetes", "icon": "🍬", "topicFull": "Diabetes", "card": "endo/NG-290_diabetes.html", "cardLabel": "Diabetes"}, {"q": "A client with type 1 diabetes becomes shaky, diaphoretic and confused about an hour after her lispro dose. She is alert and able to swallow, and a fingerstick reads 58 mg/dL. Which action does the nurse take first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Give 4 oz of orange juice", "Administer 50 mL of D50 IV", "Recheck the glucose in 15 minutes before treating", "Hold the next insulin dose and notify the provider"], "ans": [0], "why": "For a hypoglycemic client who is awake and can swallow, the treatment is <b>15 g of fast carbohydrate</b> such as 4 oz of juice, followed by a recheck in 15 minutes. IV dextrose is reserved for the client who is unconscious or cannot swallow, and waiting 15 minutes before treating spends the exact time hypoglycemia does not give you."}, {"q": "A nurse finds a client with type 1 diabetes unresponsive and diaphoretic. The glucose is 42 mg/dL and the client has no IV access. Which action does the nurse take?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Administer IM glucagon", "Hold a cup of juice to the client's lips", "Place glucose tablets between the cheek and gum", "Recheck the glucose in 15 minutes"], "ans": [0], "why": "When the client is unconscious or cannot swallow, <b>nothing goes in the mouth</b> — the treatment is glucagon or IV dextrose, and with no IV access glucagon is the available route. Juice or tablets placed in the mouth of an unresponsive client risk aspiration, no matter how automatic the Rule of 15 feels."}, {"q": "A client with type 1 diabetes calls before a planned run and reports a glucose of 268 mg/dL with ketones in the urine. Which instruction does the nurse give?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Hold the run until the glucose is lower and the ketones have cleared", "Skip the insulin dose before the run to prevent hypoglycemia", "Run as planned, but carry a fast-acting carbohydrate", "Run as planned and check the glucose afterward"], "ans": [0], "why": "Exercise is <b>held when the glucose is above 250 mg/dL with ketones</b>, because exercising in that state pushes the client further toward ketoacidosis. Carrying fast-acting carbohydrate is correct advice for routine exercise but does not make this run safe, and insulin is never skipped before activity."}, {"q": "A nursing student prepares to mix NPH and regular insulin in one syringe. Which action by the student requires the nurse to intervene?", "type": "single", "opts": ["The student draws the cloudy NPH into the syringe before the clear regular insulin", "The student rolls the NPH vial between the palms instead of shaking it", "The student selects an injection site other than the one used for the last dose", "The student confirms that the regular insulin is clear before drawing it up"], "ans": [0], "why": "The rule for mixing is <b>clear before cloudy</b>, so regular insulin is drawn first and the cloudy NPH cannot contaminate the regular vial. Rolling rather than shaking the NPH is correct technique, since shaking foams the protein, so that action needs no correction."}, {"q": "A nurse is teaching a newly diagnosed client with type 1 diabetes who will take lispro with meals and glargine at bedtime. Which instructions does the nurse include? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Inject the lispro immediately before eating rather than 30 minutes ahead", "Rotate injection sites so lipodystrophy does not ruin absorption", "Never mix the glargine with another insulin in the same syringe", "Expect the glargine to peak about 6 hours after the bedtime dose", "Hold the insulin on any day illness keeps you from eating"], "ans": [0, 1, 2], "why": "Lispro starts working in <b>15 minutes</b>, so it is given immediately before the meal, sites are rotated to protect absorption, and glargine is <b>never mixed</b> with another insulin. Glargine has no peak at all, and the sick-day rule is to keep taking insulin even when not eating — holding it is the trap this course guarantees will be tested."}, {"q": "A client who takes glipizide becomes shaky and diaphoretic and reports a headache during morning care. She is awake and able to swallow. Which actions does the nurse take? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Check the blood glucose", "Give 15 g of fast carbohydrate such as 3 glucose tablets", "Recheck the glucose 15 minutes after treating", "Administer IM glucagon because the client is symptomatic", "Withhold anything by mouth until the provider returns the call"], "ans": [0, 1, 2], "why": "Shakiness, sweating and headache in a client on a <b>sulfonylurea</b> point straight to hypoglycemia: check the glucose, treat with 15 g of fast carbohydrate, and recheck in 15 minutes. Glucagon is for the client who is unconscious or cannot swallow, so it is the wrong route here, and withholding oral treatment lets a low glucose keep falling."}, {"q": "The nurse screens clients for metabolic syndrome. Which findings meet one of its criteria? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["A woman with a fasting glucose of 105 mg/dL", "A woman with a waist circumference of 40 inches", "A man with a blood pressure of 135/85 mm Hg", "A man with triglycerides of 140 mg/dL", "A woman with an HDL of 55 mg/dL"], "ans": [0, 1, 2], "why": "Metabolic syndrome is <b>three or more</b> of: fasting glucose <b>100 or higher</b>, waist over <b>35 in (women) / 40 in (men)</b>, BP <b>130/85 or higher</b>, triglycerides <b>150 or higher</b>, and HDL <b>below 50 (women) / 40 (men)</b>. Triglycerides of 140 and an HDL of 55 in a woman are in the healthy range - HDL is the one lipid you want high.", "card": "nur258-module-06-diabetes.html#dx-type-2-diabetes-mellitus", "src": "SimpleNursing review video (Fall 2026, Wk 6): diabetes overview"}, {"q": "A client without diabetes is being treated for urosepsis. Her glucose rises from 150 to 225 mg/dL and sliding-scale insulin is prescribed. Her daughter asks why. What is the nurse's best response?", "type": "single", "opts": ["\"Infection and stress raise blood sugar; insulin keeps it controlled while the infection is treated.\"", "\"Your mother now has type 2 diabetes and will need insulin for life.\"", "\"The insulin is there to prevent low blood sugar.\"", "\"The antibiotics have permanently damaged her pancreas.\""], "ans": [0], "why": "Infection, surgery and stress release cortisol and catecholamines that raise glucose (<b>stress hyperglycemia</b>), and high glucose slows healing and weakens immunity. Insulin controls it until the infection resolves; it does not mean she now has diabetes.", "card": "nur258-module-06-diabetes.html#dx-type-2-diabetes-mellitus", "src": "SimpleNursing review video (Fall 2026, Wk 6): diabetes overview"}, {"q": "A client receives regular insulin at 1200 with lunch but eats only half the tray. When is the client at greatest risk for hypoglycemia?", "type": "single", "opts": ["1400 to 1600", "1215 to 1245", "1800 to 2000", "0200 to 0400"], "ans": [0], "why": "Regular insulin starts working in 30 to 60 minutes and <b>peaks 2 to 4 hours</b> after the dose, so a 1200 dose peaks from <b>1400 to 1600</b> - the time to watch a client who did not finish the meal. <b>Peaks need plates.</b>", "card": "nur258-module-06-diabetes.html#dx-hypoglycemia", "src": "SimpleNursing review video (Fall 2026, Wk 6): diabetes overview"}, {"q": "A client is prescribed insulin aspart before meals. When should the meal be in front of the client?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Within 10 to 15 minutes after the injection", "About 1 hour after the injection", "2 to 4 hours after the injection", "Timing does not matter with aspart"], "ans": [0], "why": "Aspart, lispro and glulisine are <b>rapid-acting</b>: onset about 15 minutes, so the client eats within <b>10 to 15 minutes</b> or risks hypoglycemia. Long-acting glargine has no peak and needs no meal timing.", "card": "nur258-module-06-diabetes.html#dx-type-1-diabetes-mellitus", "src": "SimpleNursing review video (Fall 2026, Wk 6): diabetes overview"}, {"q": "A client who uses an insulin pump has a glucose of 312 mg/dL and the pump is alarming. What does the nurse do first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Assess the client for signs of hyperglycemia and ketoacidosis", "Troubleshoot the pump tubing and infusion site", "Call the pump company", "Increase the basal rate on the pump"], "ans": [0], "why": "<b>Assess the client first, then the machine.</b> A pump delivers only rapid-acting insulin, so an interruption can lead to DKA within hours. The nurse checks the client (ketones, mental status, breathing, hydration) before troubleshooting the equipment, and never changes the rate without a prescription.", "card": "nur258-module-06-diabetes.html#dx-diabetic-ketoacidosis", "src": "SimpleNursing review video (Fall 2026, Wk 6): diabetes overview"}, {"q": "A client taking pioglitazone reports gaining 5 lb (2.3 kg) in a week, ankle swelling and shortness of breath when lying down. What does the nurse do?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Report the findings to the provider: the drug can cause fluid retention and heart failure", "Reassure the client that weight gain means glucose is better controlled", "Tell the client to take an extra dose", "Teach a low-carbohydrate diet and recheck next month"], "ans": [0], "why": "Thiazolidinediones (<b>pioglitazone</b>, rosiglitazone) cause <b>fluid retention</b> and can bring on or worsen <b>heart failure</b>. Rapid weight gain, edema, crackles or orthopnea are reported right away.", "card": "nur258-module-06-diabetes.html#dx-type-2-diabetes-mellitus", "src": "SimpleNursing review video (Fall 2026, Wk 6): diabetes overview"}, {"q": "A client with DKA is receiving IV 0.9% sodium chloride. Which findings show that hydration is improving? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Stable blood pressure", "Capillary refill of 3 seconds or less", "Urine output of 30 mL/hr or more", "Warm skin with normal color", "Apical pulse of 128/min", "Pupils equal and reactive to light"], "ans": [0, 1, 2, 3], "why": "Hydration is judged by <b>perfusion</b>: a steady BP, brisk capillary refill, <b>urine output of at least 30 mL/hr</b> and warm, normally colored skin. A pulse of 128 is still tachycardic, and pupils and lung sounds do not measure hydration - the video's question banks used them as distractors.", "card": "nur258-module-06-diabetes.html#dx-diabetic-ketoacidosis", "src": "SimpleNursing review video (Fall 2026, Wk 6): DKA vs HHS"}, {"q": "IV potassium chloride is added for a client in DKA. Which actions does the nurse take? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Place the client on a cardiac monitor", "Give it on an infusion pump", "Never give it by IV push", "Infuse no faster than about 10 mEq/hr through a peripheral line, per policy", "Confirm adequate urine output before starting", "Give it as a rapid bolus to correct the level quickly"], "ans": [0, 1, 2, 3, 4], "why": "Insulin drives potassium into the cells, so potassium is replaced even when the first level is normal. IV potassium is <b>always diluted, on a pump, with cardiac monitoring</b>, and <b>never IV push</b> - a bolus can stop the heart. The kidneys must be making urine so the excess can be excreted.", "card": "nur258-module-06-diabetes.html#dx-diabetic-ketoacidosis", "src": "SimpleNursing review video (Fall 2026, Wk 6): DKA vs HHS"}], "7": [{"q": "A client with a head injury has BP 82/50, HR 130, and a newly dilated sluggish left pupil. Which does the nurse address first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["The dilated sluggish pupil, because it suggests herniation", "The hypotension and tachycardia", "Obtain a repeat GCS before intervening", "Elevate the head of the bed and recheck in 15 minutes"], "ans": [1], "why": "<b>ABC before D, every time</b> - hypotension with tachycardia outranks the new pupil change, because perfusion failure kills the whole brain, not just one hemisphere. The pupil is the more alarming-looking finding, which is exactly why it is the distractor."}, {"q": "A client with elevated ICP begins decorticate posturing. Mannitol is ordered. What does the nurse do first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Elevate the head of the bed to 30 degrees", "Retrieve and prepare the mannitol", "Place the client in Trendelenburg", "Suction the airway to stimulate a response"], "ans": [0], "why": "<b>Raising the HOB to 30 degrees takes seconds and improves venous drainage</b>, while mannitol has to be fetched and prepared. Trendelenburg is never used with raised ICP, and suctioning transiently raises it further."}, {"q": "A client has an ICP of 22 mmHg and a MAP of 70 mmHg. What is the cerebral perfusion pressure, and how does the nurse interpret it?", "type": "single", "opts": ["92 mmHg, adequate", "48 mmHg, below goal", "48 mmHg, adequate", "22 mmHg, below goal"], "ans": [1], "why": "CPP = MAP - ICP = 70 - 22 = <b>48 mmHg, which is below the goal of greater than 50</b>. Normal ICP is 10 to 15, so this client's rising pressure is already starving the brain of perfusion."}, {"q": "Which findings would exclude a client from receiving t-PA for an ischemic stroke? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Current anticoagulant therapy", "Blood pressure 196/104", "Age 78", "Abdominal surgery 10 days ago", "Symptom onset 2 hours ago"], "ans": [0, 1, 3], "why": "Contraindications include <b>anticoagulant use, SBP over 180, recent bleed or surgery</b>, age under 18 and abnormal glucose. Age 78 is not a barrier, and 2 hours sits inside the 3 to 4.5 hour window - both are there to be mistaken for exclusions."}, {"q": "Which assessment finding is the earliest indicator of rising intracranial pressure?", "type": "single", "opts": ["A subtle change in level of consciousness", "Widening pulse pressure with bradycardia", "Cheyne-Stokes respirations", "Fixed dilated pupils"], "ans": [0], "why": "<b>A change in LOC is the earliest sign</b>; Cushing's triad - rising systolic with widened pulse pressure, bradycardia and irregular respirations - is a <b>late herniation sign</b>. If the triad is present, you are already late."}, {"q": "A client with suspected stroke has a patent airway and adequate breathing. What does the nurse do next?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Administer chewable aspirin", "Perform a rapid focused neurologic assessment", "Start a heparin drip", "Give a bolus of isotonic fluid"], "ans": [1], "why": "After A and B are confirmed the next step is a <b>rapid neuro assessment of about 20 seconds</b> to establish deficits and time of onset. Aspirin cannot be given before the non-contrast CT distinguishes ischemic from hemorrhagic stroke."}, {"type": "sata", "week": "7", "step": "solutions", "topic": "Neurologic Dysfunction…", "q": "A nurse is planning care for a client newly diagnosed with generalized tonic-clonic epilepsy. Which interventions should the nurse include in the plan of care? (Select all that apply.)", "opts": ["Keep oxygen and suction equipment set up at the bedside.", "Pad the side rails and keep the bed in its lowest position.", "Store a padded tongue blade at the bedside to insert if a seizure starts.", "Teach the client to take the antiseizure medication at the same clock time each day.", "Teach the client to avoid swimming alone, ladders, and contact sports.", "Plan to hold the client's arms and legs still if jerking movements begin."], "ans": [0, 1, 3, 4], "why": "A seizure plan protects the airway and prevents injury, so oxygen, suction, padded rails, and a low bed belong in it. Steady daily dosing keeps drug levels therapeutic, and activity limits protect the client if a seizure hits without warning. Nothing is ever placed in the mouth during a seizure, because it can break teeth or block the airway. Restraining a moving limb does not stop the seizure and can tear muscle or fracture bone.", "src": "ATI ch 7 Seizures and Epilepsy", "icon": "🧠", "topicFull": "Neurologic Dysfunction & Cerebrovascular Disorders"}, {"q": "A client arrives with left-sided weakness and slurred speech that began 90 minutes ago. Which action is the priority?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Obtain a non-contrast CT of the head", "Give aspirin 325 mg", "Start a heparin infusion", "Insert a nasogastric tube for medications"], "ans": [0], "why": "Nothing happens until a <b>non-contrast CT</b> separates ischemic from hemorrhagic stroke, because the treatments are opposites: give a thrombolytic in a bleed and you kill the client. The alteplase window is <b>4.5 hours</b>, so at 90 minutes the clock is the priority. Aspirin and heparin both wait for the scan, and nothing goes by mouth until swallowing is screened.", "topic": "Neurologic Dysfunction…", "icon": "🧠", "topicFull": "Neurologic Dysfunction & Cerebrovascular Disorders"}, {"q": "Which findings suggest a stroke affecting the right hemisphere? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Left-sided hemiparesis", "Left visual field neglect", "Impulsive behavior and poor safety judgment", "Expressive aphasia", "Overestimation of one's own abilities"], "ans": [0, 1, 2, 4], "why": "The brain crosses, so a right-hemisphere stroke weakens the <b>left</b> side and neglects the <b>left</b> field. The behavioral signature is the dangerous part: <b>impulsive, unaware of the deficit, overestimating ability</b>, which is why these clients fall. <b>Aphasia is a left-hemisphere</b> problem for almost everyone.", "topic": "Neurologic Dysfunction…", "icon": "🧠", "topicFull": "Neurologic Dysfunction & Cerebrovascular Disorders"}, {"q": "A client with increased intracranial pressure is being cared for. Which nursing actions are appropriate? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Keep the head of the bed at 30 degrees with the head midline", "Cluster nursing care into long uninterrupted sessions", "Keep the environment quiet and dim", "Avoid hip flexion and neck rotation", "Instruct the client to exhale while turning rather than hold the breath"], "ans": [0, 2, 3, 4], "why": "Everything is aimed at <b>letting blood drain out of the head</b>: 30 degrees, head midline, no neck rotation, no hip flexion, and no Valsalva. Care is <b>spaced out, not clustered</b> - stacked stimulation stacks the pressure, which is the one that catches people out.", "topic": "Neurologic Dysfunction…", "icon": "🧠", "topicFull": "Neurologic Dysfunction & Cerebrovascular Disorders", "card": "neuro/NG-308_increased-icp.html", "cardLabel": "Increased Intracranial Pressure"}, {"q": "Which finding in a client with a head injury most concerns the nurse?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Blood pressure 168/56 mm Hg with a heart rate of 48/min", "Headache rated 5 out of 10", "Blood pressure 128/76 mm Hg with a heart rate of 88/min", "Reports of feeling tired"], "ans": [0], "why": "A <b>widening pulse pressure with bradycardia</b> - and irregular respirations - is <b>Cushing's triad</b>, a late and ominous sign of brainstem compression from rising intracranial pressure. It means herniation is close, and it is reported immediately.", "topic": "Neurologic Dysfunction…", "icon": "🧠", "topicFull": "Neurologic Dysfunction & Cerebrovascular Disorders"}, {"q": "A client admitted 4 hours ago with a left-hemisphere ischemic stroke is alert and follows commands. She asks for water and her morning oral medications. Which action does the nurse take first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Offer small sips of water while the client sits fully upright", "Hold all oral intake until a formal swallow screen is completed", "Crush the medications into applesauce to make them easier to swallow", "Have the family feed the client slowly and watch for coughing"], "ans": [1], "why": "Nothing goes by mouth after a stroke — <b>not even water or medications</b> — until swallowing is formally assessed, because aspiration pneumonia is the leading cause of death after stroke and a left-brain stroke brings language and swallowing deficits. Crushing the pills in applesauce is tempting because it feels safer, but it is still oral intake before the swallow screen."}, {"q": "A client admitted for seizure monitoring has been in a generalized tonic-clonic seizure for 7 minutes without regaining consciousness. The airway is protected and she is positioned on her side. Which intervention does the nurse anticipate first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Administering IV lorazepam", "Administering IV fosphenytoin", "Administering IV levetiracetam", "Inserting a padded airway between the teeth"], "ans": [0], "why": "A seizure lasting longer than 5 minutes is status epilepticus, and <b>IV lorazepam is given first</b>; fosphenytoin or levetiracetam follows to keep the seizure from returning. Fosphenytoin is the tempting choice because it is the drug that maintains control, but it is not the first agent — and nothing is ever placed in the mouth during a seizure."}, {"q": "A client with a traumatic brain injury and an ICP of 24 mmHg receives IV mannitol. Two hours later, which finding best tells the nurse the drug is having its intended effect?", "type": "single", "opts": ["A marked increase in urine output", "A heart rate that has fallen to 52 beats per minute", "A systolic blood pressure that has risen with a widened pulse pressure", "Respirations that have become irregular and Cheyne-Stokes"], "ans": [0], "why": "Mannitol pulls fluid off the brain and that fluid is excreted, so <b>increased urine output</b> is how the nurse confirms it is working. The other three findings together are Cushing's triad — a late herniation sign meaning the ICP is still climbing, so they show the opposite of the intended effect."}, {"q": "A client arrives with sudden onset of \"the worst headache of my life,\" vomiting, and photophobia. Non-contrast CT shows blood in the subarachnoid space. Which prescription does the nurse question?", "type": "single", "opts": ["IV t-PA", "A calcium channel blocker to prevent vasospasm", "An IV antihypertensive to lower the blood pressure", "Coagulation studies with reversal of anticoagulation"], "ans": [0], "why": "\"Worst headache of my life\" with blood on CT is a hemorrhagic bleed, and <b>t-PA is never given</b> — it would extend the hemorrhage. The antihypertensive is the tempting one to question because lowering pressure seems risky for perfusion, but lowering the BP, reversing coags, and calcium channel blockers for vasospasm are all correct here."}, {"q": "A client is 6 hours post carotid endarterectomy. Which findings does the nurse report to the surgeon immediately? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Fullness and swelling on the operative side of the neck", "New hoarseness when the client speaks", "Blood pressure of 186/98 mmHg", "Incisional soreness when the client turns her head", "A dry mouth after being NPO for surgery"], "ans": [0, 1, 2], "why": "The three things watched for after endarterectomy are <b>hypertension, neck swelling, and hoarseness</b> — swelling threatens the airway, hoarseness signals nerve injury, and a systolic over 180 threatens the fresh suture line. Incisional soreness and a dry mouth after NPO status are expected findings and do not need the surgeon."}, {"q": "A client admitted with an ischemic stroke is being monitored on the stroke unit. Which findings require the nurse to notify the provider? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Blood pressure of 192/104 mmHg", "Blood pressure of 156/114 mmHg", "SpO2 of 89% on room air", "Blood pressure of 162/98 mmHg", "A Glasgow Coma Scale score that has fallen from 14 to 10"], "ans": [0, 1, 2, 4], "why": "Stroke care calls for notification when the <b>systolic exceeds 180 or the diastolic exceeds 110</b>, when SpO2 drops below 92%, and when the level of consciousness changes — a GCS drop from 14 to 10 is the reportable event. A BP of 162/98 looks alarming but sits under both thresholds, so it is monitored rather than reported."}, {"q": "A client with a seizure disorder says, \"I smell oranges, but there aren't any on my tray.\" What is the nurse's best response?", "type": "single", "opts": ["\"Have you noticed this sensation before?\"", "\"There are no oranges in here.\"", "\"That smell is a side effect of your medication.\"", "\"I will ask the kitchen to check your tray.\""], "ans": [0], "why": "An unusual smell can be an <b>aura</b> that comes before a seizure. The nurse explores it with an open question, stays with the client and gets ready to keep the client safe. Contradicting it or explaining it away misses the warning.", "card": "nur258-module-07-neurologic-cerebrovascular.html#dx-seizures-and-status-epilepticus", "src": "SimpleNursing review video (Fall 2026, Wk 7): CNS and PNS"}, {"q": "A client is starting levetiracetam. Which effect should the nurse teach the client and family to report promptly?", "type": "single", "opts": ["New or worsening depression or thoughts of self-harm", "Mild drowsiness during the first week", "Increased appetite", "Overgrowth of the gums"], "ans": [0], "why": "Antiseizure drugs, including <b>levetiracetam</b>, carry a warning for <b>suicidal thoughts and behavior</b> and mood changes; a new rash is also reported. Early drowsiness is common, and gum overgrowth belongs to phenytoin.", "card": "nur258-module-07-neurologic-cerebrovascular.html#dx-seizures-and-status-epilepticus", "src": "SimpleNursing review video (Fall 2026, Wk 7): CNS and PNS"}, {"q": "A client on continuous tube feedings is prescribed phenytoin suspension through the feeding tube. What does the nurse do?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Hold the feeding 1 to 2 hours before and after the dose and flush the tube", "Add the phenytoin to the feeding bag", "Give it while the feeding runs to reduce stomach upset", "Give it without flushing to avoid extra fluid"], "ans": [0], "why": "Enteral feedings bind phenytoin and <b>lower its absorption</b>, so feedings are held 1 to 2 hours before and after the dose (per policy) and the tube is flushed before and after. Levels are then monitored (10 to 20 mcg/mL).", "card": "nur258-module-07-neurologic-cerebrovascular.html#dx-seizures-and-status-epilepticus", "src": "SimpleNursing review video (Fall 2026, Wk 7): CNS and PNS"}, {"q": "A client with Bell's palsy cannot fully close the right eye. Which teaching is the priority?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Use artificial tears during the day and tape or patch the eye closed at night", "Apply ice to the face several times a day", "Leave the eye uncovered so the lid muscles get stronger", "Avoid chewing on the left side"], "ans": [0], "why": "Facial nerve (CN VII) weakness leaves the cornea exposed, so <b>eye protection</b> comes first: artificial tears, sunglasses, and taping or patching at night. Moist heat (not ice) may ease pain, and the client chews on the <b>unaffected</b> side.", "card": "nur258-module-07-neurologic-cerebrovascular.html#dx-neuro1", "src": "SimpleNursing review video (Fall 2026, Wk 7): CNS and PNS"}, {"q": "A client admitted with a closed head injury had a Glasgow Coma Scale score of 11 on arrival. Two hours later the score is 7 and respirations are shallow. Which action does the nurse anticipate?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Prepare for endotracheal intubation", "Recheck the Glasgow Coma Scale score in 1 hour", "Insert a nasogastric tube to begin feedings", "Prepare the client for a lumbar puncture"], "ans": [0], "why": "A GCS of <b>8 or less</b> means a severe brain injury: the client can no longer protect the airway, so the nurse prepares for <b>intubation</b>. Waiting an hour to recheck delays airway protection while the score is falling, and a lumbar puncture is contraindicated when ICP may be rising.", "topic": "Glasgow Coma Scale", "src": "NUR 258 Week 7 lecture: Neuro part 1 (increased ICP, head injury, stroke), 12 Oct", "icon": "🧠", "topicFull": "Neurologic Dysfunction & Cerebrovascular Disorders"}, {"q": "A client with a severe head injury does not open the eyes, makes no sounds, and has no motor response to a painful stimulus. Which Glasgow Coma Scale score does the nurse document?", "type": "single", "opts": ["0", "3", "6", "8"], "ans": [1], "why": "Each of the three GCS categories (eye opening, verbal, motor) scores <b>at least 1</b>, so the lowest possible total is <b>3</b> and the highest is 15. Zero is not possible on this scale, and 6 or 8 would require some eye, verbal or motor response.", "topic": "Glasgow Coma Scale", "src": "NUR 258 Week 7 lecture: Neuro part 1 (increased ICP, head injury, stroke), 12 Oct", "icon": "🧠", "topicFull": "Neurologic Dysfunction & Cerebrovascular Disorders"}, {"q": "A client with rising intracranial pressure now has a right pupil that is dilated and does not react to the penlight. The nurse recognizes compression of which cranial nerve?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Cranial nerve II (optic)", "Cranial nerve III (oculomotor)", "Cranial nerve V (trigeminal)", "Cranial nerve VII (facial)"], "ans": [1], "why": "Pupil constriction is carried by the <b>oculomotor nerve (CN III)</b>; as the brain swells and herniates it compresses CN III, leaving a <b>fixed, dilated pupil</b> on that side, a late sign of increased ICP. CN II carries vision, not the motor response of the pupil, so it is the tempting but wrong choice.", "topic": "Increased ICP", "src": "NUR 258 Week 7 lecture: Neuro part 1 (increased ICP, head injury, stroke), 12 Oct", "icon": "🧠", "topicFull": "Neurologic Dysfunction & Cerebrovascular Disorders"}, {"q": "The health care team is evaluating a ventilated client with a catastrophic brain injury for brain death. Which findings are consistent with brain death? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Coma with no response to any stimulus", "Absent cough and gag reflexes", "No spontaneous breaths when the ventilator is disconnected during an apnea test", "Decorticate posturing in response to a painful stimulus", "A Glasgow Coma Scale score of 8"], "ans": [0, 1, 2], "why": "Brain death requires <b>coma, absent brainstem reflexes, and apnea</b> (no breaths when taken off the ventilator during an apnea test); an EEG with no activity can confirm it. Posturing is a motor response that comes from a functioning brainstem, so it rules brain death out, and a GCS of 8 still shows responses.", "topic": "Brain death", "src": "NUR 258 Week 7 lecture: Neuro part 1 (increased ICP, head injury, stroke), 12 Oct", "icon": "🧠", "topicFull": "Neurologic Dysfunction & Cerebrovascular Disorders"}, {"q": "A client with increased intracranial pressure is intubated and has secretions in the endotracheal tube. Which approach to suctioning is correct?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Hyperoxygenate first and limit each pass to 10 seconds or less", "Suction every hour on a schedule to keep the airway clear", "Suction for 20 seconds per pass to clear secretions fully", "Lower the head of the bed flat before passing the catheter"], "ans": [0], "why": "Suctioning raises ICP, so it is done <b>only as needed</b>, with <b>hyperoxygenation beforehand and passes of 10 seconds or less</b>. Hourly suctioning on a schedule and long passes both raise ICP further, and lying the client flat slows venous drainage from the head.", "topic": "Increased ICP", "src": "NUR 258 Week 7 lecture: Neuro part 1 (increased ICP, head injury, stroke), 12 Oct", "icon": "🧠", "topicFull": "Neurologic Dysfunction & Cerebrovascular Disorders"}, {"q": "A client with a traumatic brain injury has a temperature of 39.4 C (103 F) and is placed on a cooling blanket. Thirty minutes later the client begins shivering. How does the nurse interpret this?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Shivering shows the cooling is working and should continue unchanged", "Shivering raises metabolic demand and ICP and needs to be stopped", "Shivering is a sign of a seizure and calls for IV lorazepam", "Shivering means the fever has broken and cooling can be removed"], "ans": [1], "why": "<b>Shivering increases the metabolic rate and oxygen consumption, which raises ICP</b>, so the nurse slows the cooling and treats the shivering as prescribed. It is not a sign the treatment is working, and it is not a seizure.", "topic": "Neurogenic fever", "src": "NUR 258 Week 7 lecture: Neuro part 1 (increased ICP, head injury, stroke), 12 Oct", "icon": "🧠", "topicFull": "Neurologic Dysfunction & Cerebrovascular Disorders"}, {"q": "The spouse of a client in a coma after a head injury says, \"There is no point talking to him. He can't hear anything.\" Which response by the nurse is best?", "type": "single", "opts": ["\"He may still be able to hear, so talk to him and tell him what you are doing.\"", "\"You are right, so it is better to save your energy for when he wakes up.\"", "\"Only speak to him when he opens his eyes, so you don't overstimulate him.\"", "\"Keep the television on all day instead, so his brain stays stimulated.\""], "ans": [0], "why": "Hearing may stay intact in a comatose client, so the nurse and family <b>talk to the client, introduce themselves, explain care and orient him</b> to day and time. Agreeing with the spouse is incorrect, and constant TV noise overstimulates a client whose ICP may be raised.", "topic": "Decreased LOC", "src": "NUR 258 Week 7 lecture: Neuro part 1 (increased ICP, head injury, stroke), 12 Oct", "icon": "🧠", "topicFull": "Neurologic Dysfunction & Cerebrovascular Disorders"}, {"q": "A client with a head injury from a motor vehicle crash has clear fluid dripping from the nose. Which actions does the nurse take? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Test the drainage for glucose", "Place a loose sterile pad under the nose to catch the drainage", "Tell the client not to blow the nose", "Pack the nares with gauze to stop the leak", "Insert a cotton-tipped swab into the nostril to collect a sample", "Perform nasotracheal suctioning to clear the drainage"], "ans": [0, 1, 2], "why": "Clear drainage after a head injury may be <b>CSF</b>: the nurse <b>tests it for glucose</b>, lets it drain freely onto a loose pad, and has the client avoid blowing the nose. <b>Never pack the nose or put anything into it</b>: packing blocks the leak and raises ICP, and swabs or nasal suction can carry infection into the brain through the fracture.", "topic": "Basilar skull fracture", "src": "NUR 258 Week 7 lecture: Neuro part 1 (increased ICP, head injury, stroke), 12 Oct", "icon": "🧠", "topicFull": "Neurologic Dysfunction & Cerebrovascular Disorders"}, {"q": "A client has stayed in a coma since a high-speed motorcycle crash 3 days ago. CT and MRI of the head show no hematoma or fracture. Which injury does the nurse suspect?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Concussion", "Diffuse axonal injury", "Epidural hematoma", "Basilar skull fracture"], "ans": [1], "why": "<b>Diffuse axonal injury</b> comes from shearing and rotation of the brain in high-speed crashes; the tearing of axons is microscopic, so <b>imaging may look normal</b> while the client stays in a <b>prolonged coma</b>. A concussion does not cause days of coma, and a hematoma or fracture would show on imaging.", "topic": "Head injury", "src": "NUR 258 Week 7 lecture: Neuro part 1 (increased ICP, head injury, stroke), 12 Oct", "icon": "🧠", "topicFull": "Neurologic Dysfunction & Cerebrovascular Disorders"}, {"q": "A client is being discharged home after a concussion with a normal head CT. Which findings should the family be taught to report right away? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Repeated, forceful vomiting", "Increasing drowsiness or confusion", "A severe headache that keeps getting worse", "New difficulty speaking", "A mild headache relieved by acetaminophen", "Mild sensitivity to light over the next few days"], "ans": [0, 1, 2, 3], "why": "A worsening <b>level of consciousness, severe headache, projectile vomiting, trouble speaking or seizures</b> signal rising ICP or a developing bleed and need emergency care. Mild headache, dizziness and light sensitivity are expected post-concussion symptoms that can last for weeks.", "topic": "Concussion", "src": "NUR 258 Week 7 lecture: Neuro part 1 (increased ICP, head injury, stroke), 12 Oct", "icon": "🧠", "topicFull": "Neurologic Dysfunction & Cerebrovascular Disorders"}, {"q": "Which finding best distinguishes a cerebral contusion from a concussion?", "type": "single", "opts": ["A brief loss of consciousness at the time of injury", "Headaches and dizziness that persist for several weeks", "Bruising of brain tissue that is visible on CT", "Short-term amnesia for the event"], "ans": [2], "why": "In a <b>contusion</b> the brain itself is bruised, and the <b>bruising and bleeding show on imaging</b>; in a concussion the CT is normal. A brief loss of consciousness, post-concussion headaches and amnesia can all occur with a concussion, so they do not tell the two apart.", "topic": "Concussion vs contusion", "src": "NUR 258 Week 7 lecture: Neuro part 1 (increased ICP, head injury, stroke), 12 Oct", "icon": "🧠", "topicFull": "Neurologic Dysfunction & Cerebrovascular Disorders"}, {"q": "A 78-year-old client who takes warfarin falls at home and is diagnosed with a subdural hematoma. The INR is 5.8 (normal about 1.0; the usual goal on warfarin is 2 to 3). Which medication does the nurse anticipate giving?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Protamine sulfate", "Vitamin K (phytonadione)", "Alteplase", "Enoxaparin"], "ans": [1], "why": "An INR well above the 2 to 3 target means the warfarin effect is too high and the bleed will keep expanding; warfarin is reversed with <b>vitamin K</b> (often with prothrombin complex concentrate). Protamine reverses heparin, not warfarin, and alteplase and enoxaparin would make the bleeding worse.", "topic": "Subdural hematoma", "src": "NUR 258 Week 7 lecture: Neuro part 1 (increased ICP, head injury, stroke), 12 Oct", "icon": "🧠", "topicFull": "Neurologic Dysfunction & Cerebrovascular Disorders"}, {"q": "A client returns to the unit after a cerebral angiogram through the right femoral artery. Which actions does the nurse include in the plan of care? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Perform frequent neurologic checks", "Inspect the puncture site for bleeding or a hematoma", "Check the pedal pulses and color of the right leg", "Keep the client on bed rest with the right leg straight as prescribed", "Help the client walk to the bathroom on return to the unit", "Encourage flexing the right hip to prevent stiffness"], "ans": [0, 1, 2, 3], "why": "Moving catheters through cerebral vessels can dislodge a clot, so the nurse does <b>neuro checks</b> to catch a new stroke, and watches the arterial site for <b>bleeding or hematoma</b> and the leg for <b>distal circulation</b>. Bed rest with the leg straight for several hours protects the femoral puncture; walking right away or flexing the hip can start an arterial bleed.", "topic": "Cerebral angiography", "src": "NUR 258 Week 7 lecture: Neuro part 1 (increased ICP, head injury, stroke), 12 Oct", "icon": "🧠", "topicFull": "Neurologic Dysfunction & Cerebrovascular Disorders"}, {"q": "A client is scheduled for a cerebral angiogram. Which laboratory results does the nurse review before the procedure? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["BUN and serum creatinine", "PT/INR and aPTT", "Platelet count", "Serum amylase", "Lipid panel"], "ans": [0, 1, 2], "why": "Iodinated contrast can injure the kidneys, so <b>BUN and creatinine</b> are checked; an arterial puncture also requires <b>coagulation studies and a platelet count</b> to judge the bleeding risk when the sheath is pulled. Amylase and a lipid panel have no bearing on the safety of the procedure.", "topic": "Cerebral angiography", "src": "NUR 258 Week 7 lecture: Neuro part 1 (increased ICP, head injury, stroke), 12 Oct", "icon": "🧠", "topicFull": "Neurologic Dysfunction & Cerebrovascular Disorders"}, {"q": "A hospitalized client is going for an MRI of the brain. Which actions does the nurse take before the client leaves the unit? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Replace the gown that has metal snaps", "Move the client from the portable oxygen cylinder to an MRI-safe oxygen source", "Ask whether the client has an older pacemaker or other implanted metal", "Leave the standard telemetry leads in place during the scan", "Tell the client the scan is quick and usually lasts under 5 minutes"], "ans": [0, 1, 2], "why": "The MRI magnet pulls in <b>ferrous metal</b>: snaps, oxygen cylinders and older pacemakers are hazards, so the nurse completes the <b>MRI safety screening</b> and removes or swaps them. Standard telemetry leads must be changed to MRI-compatible ones, and an MRI takes much longer than a CT, so the client should be told to expect a long time lying still.", "topic": "Neuro diagnostics", "src": "NUR 258 Week 7 lecture: Neuro part 1 (increased ICP, head injury, stroke), 12 Oct", "icon": "🧠", "topicFull": "Neurologic Dysfunction & Cerebrovascular Disorders"}, {"q": "A client with type 2 diabetes who takes metformin is scheduled for a CT of the head with IV contrast. Which action does the nurse take?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Hold the metformin as prescribed and for 48 hours after the scan until renal function is checked", "Give an extra dose of metformin before the scan to prevent hyperglycemia while NPO", "Continue the metformin as usual, since only gadolinium interacts with it", "Hold the metformin only if the client reports an allergy to shellfish"], "ans": [0], "why": "Iodinated contrast can cause acute kidney injury, and metformin with impaired renal function can cause <b>lactic acidosis</b>, so metformin is <b>held around the scan and for 48 hours afterward</b> until the creatinine is rechecked. The shellfish question concerns allergy, not the metformin interaction.", "topic": "Neuro diagnostics", "src": "NUR 258 Week 7 lecture: Neuro part 1 (increased ICP, head injury, stroke), 12 Oct", "icon": "🧠", "topicFull": "Neurologic Dysfunction & Cerebrovascular Disorders"}, {"q": "A client is brought to the emergency department confused, with new right-sided weakness. No history is available. The airway and breathing are adequate. Which bedside test does the nurse perform before the client goes for CT?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Capillary blood glucose", "Urine drug screen", "Carotid duplex ultrasound", "Arterial blood gas"], "ans": [0], "why": "<b>Hypoglycemia can mimic a stroke</b>, and a glucose below 50 mg/dL also rules out alteplase, so a <b>fingerstick glucose</b> is checked right away and does not delay the CT. A carotid ultrasound is done later in the admission, not in the emergency phase.", "topic": "Stroke diagnostics", "src": "NUR 258 Week 7 lecture: Neuro part 1 (increased ICP, head injury, stroke), 12 Oct", "icon": "🧠", "topicFull": "Neurologic Dysfunction & Cerebrovascular Disorders"}, {"q": "A client admitted with an ischemic stroke has a 12-lead ECG. The daughter asks, \"Why are they checking his heart when this is a stroke?\" Which response by the nurse is best?", "type": "single", "opts": ["\"It looks for an irregular rhythm such as atrial fibrillation, which can send a clot to the brain.\"", "\"It is needed to rule out a heart attack as the cause of his weakness.\"", "\"It measures the electrical activity of the brain to find the stroke.\"", "\"Alteplase cannot be given unless the ECG shows a heart attack.\""], "ans": [0], "why": "Undiagnosed <b>atrial fibrillation</b> is a major source of <b>embolic ischemic stroke</b>, so a 12-lead ECG and cardiac monitoring are part of the stroke workup. An EEG, not an ECG, measures brain activity, and alteplase for stroke does not depend on an MI.", "topic": "Stroke diagnostics", "src": "NUR 258 Week 7 lecture: Neuro part 1 (increased ICP, head injury, stroke), 12 Oct", "icon": "🧠", "topicFull": "Neurologic Dysfunction & Cerebrovascular Disorders"}, {"q": "A client went to bed at 2200 feeling well and woke at 0600 with right-sided weakness and slurred speech. The client arrives in the emergency department at 0630, and CT shows no bleeding. How does the nurse understand the alteplase time window?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Onset counts from 0600, when the symptoms were noticed, so the client is within the window", "Onset counts from 2200, when the client was last known well, so the client is outside the window", "Onset counts from 0630, arrival in the emergency department, so the client is within the window", "Onset does not matter once the CT has ruled out bleeding"], "ans": [1], "why": "When nobody saw the symptoms start, onset is counted from the time the client was <b>last known well</b>. Here that is 2200, more than 8 hours ago and past the 3 to 4.5 hour window for IV alteplase. The time the client woke up or reached the ED is not the onset time.", "topic": "Stroke - alteplase", "src": "NUR 258 Week 7 lecture: Neuro part 1 (increased ICP, head injury, stroke), 12 Oct", "icon": "🧠", "topicFull": "Neurologic Dysfunction & Cerebrovascular Disorders"}, {"q": "A client with a traumatic brain injury has cerebral edema and an ICP of 24 mm Hg. Which IV fluid does the nurse anticipate the provider prescribing?", "type": "single", "opts": ["0.45% sodium chloride", "5% dextrose in water", "3% sodium chloride", "Lactated Ringer's solution"], "ans": [2], "why": "<b>Hypertonic (3%) saline</b> pulls water out of swollen brain tissue into the bloodstream and lowers ICP, like the osmotic diuretic mannitol. Hypotonic fluids such as 0.45% saline and D5W, which acts as free water, move fluid into the brain cells and worsen cerebral edema.", "topic": "Increased ICP", "src": "NUR 258 Week 7 lecture: Neuro part 1 (increased ICP, head injury, stroke), 12 Oct", "icon": "🧠", "topicFull": "Neurologic Dysfunction & Cerebrovascular Disorders"}, {"q": "A client has had a ventriculostomy for 4 days. Today the temperature is 38.9 C (102 F), the client reports a severe headache and stiff neck, and the CSF in the drainage bag looks cloudy. Which complication does the nurse suspect?", "type": "single", "opts": ["A neurogenic fever from injury to the hypothalamus", "A central nervous system infection such as meningitis", "A subdural hematoma from draining CSF too fast", "Diabetes insipidus from pituitary injury"], "ans": [1], "why": "A catheter into the ventricle gives bacteria a direct route to the brain, so <b>infection (meningitis or ventriculitis)</b> is the main risk; fever with <b>nuchal rigidity and cloudy CSF</b> points to it. A neurogenic fever does not cloud the CSF or stiffen the neck.", "topic": "ICP monitoring", "src": "NUR 258 Week 7 lecture: Neuro part 1 (increased ICP, head injury, stroke), 12 Oct", "icon": "🧠", "topicFull": "Neurologic Dysfunction & Cerebrovascular Disorders"}, {"q": "A client with left hemiplegia after a stroke is getting out of bed for the first time. Which actions protect the affected arm? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Support the arm in a sling while the client is upright and walking", "Rest the affected arm and hand on a pillow when the client sits", "Inspect the skin of the affected arm, since the client may not feel pressure", "Pull on the affected arm to help the client sit up", "Let the arm hang at the side while sitting to build muscle tone"], "ans": [0, 1, 2], "why": "A flaccid arm is prone to <b>shoulder subluxation, dependent edema and unnoticed injury</b>, so it is <b>supported in a sling when upright, elevated on a pillow</b>, and inspected often. Pulling on the affected arm or letting it hang can dislocate the shoulder.", "topic": "Stroke - care", "src": "NUR 258 Week 7 lecture: Neuro part 1 (increased ICP, head injury, stroke), 12 Oct", "icon": "🧠", "topicFull": "Neurologic Dysfunction & Cerebrovascular Disorders"}, {"q": "A client with dysphagia after a stroke has just finished lunch while sitting upright at 90 degrees. Which action does the nurse take next?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Keep the client sitting upright for at least 30 minutes", "Lower the head of the bed flat so the client can rest", "Turn the client onto the affected side right away", "Offer thin water to rinse food from the mouth"], "ans": [0], "why": "Keeping the client <b>upright for at least 30 minutes after eating</b> lets food clear the throat and lowers the risk of aspiration. Lying flat or side-lying straight after a meal invites aspiration, and thin liquids are the hardest consistency for a client with dysphagia to control.", "topic": "Stroke - dysphagia", "src": "NUR 258 Week 7 lecture: Neuro part 1 (increased ICP, head injury, stroke), 12 Oct", "icon": "🧠", "topicFull": "Neurologic Dysfunction & Cerebrovascular Disorders"}, {"q": "A client arrives in the emergency department after falling down a flight of stairs and striking the head. Cervical spine imaging has not yet been done. Which action is correct?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Keep the cervical collar on and log roll the client until the spine is cleared", "Remove the cervical collar to assess the neck's range of motion", "Raise the head on two pillows to lower the intracranial pressure", "Have the client turn the head side to side to check for neck pain"], "ans": [0], "why": "Every client with a head injury is assumed to have a <b>cervical spine injury</b> until x-ray or CT rules it out, so the <b>collar stays on and the client is log rolled</b>. Pillows under the head flex the neck, which can worsen a spinal injury and blocks venous drainage, raising ICP.", "topic": "Head injury", "src": "NUR 258 Week 7 lecture: Neuro part 1 (increased ICP, head injury, stroke), 12 Oct", "icon": "🧠", "topicFull": "Neurologic Dysfunction & Cerebrovascular Disorders"}, {"q": "A client with a severe head injury is on mechanical ventilation. Which intervention best reduces the risk of ventilator-associated pneumonia?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Routine oral care with chlorhexidine and the head of the bed at 30 degrees or higher", "Changing the ventilator circuit tubing every 24 hours", "Keeping the head of the bed flat to protect the cervical spine", "Instilling normal saline into the endotracheal tube before each suctioning"], "ans": [0], "why": "The VAP bundle includes <b>scheduled oral care with chlorhexidine</b> and keeping the <b>head of the bed elevated at least 30 degrees</b>. Routine circuit changes and saline instillation do not prevent VAP and may raise the risk, and lying flat increases aspiration.", "topic": "Decreased LOC", "src": "NUR 258 Week 7 lecture: Neuro part 1 (increased ICP, head injury, stroke), 12 Oct", "icon": "🧠", "topicFull": "Neurologic Dysfunction & Cerebrovascular Disorders"}, {"q": "A client with a traumatic brain injury and no spinal injury has an ICP of 22 mm Hg. Which actions should the nurse include in the plan of care? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Suction the airway every 2 hours", "Administer the prescribed stool softener", "Check pupil size and reaction to light", "Perform neurologic assessments every hour", "Elevate the head of the bed to 15 degrees", "Have the client cough and deep breathe every hour"], "ans": [1, 2, 3], "why": "Straining raises ICP, so a <b>stool softener</b> prevents the Valsalva maneuver; <b>pupil checks and hourly neuro checks</b> (GCS, cranial nerves) catch deterioration early. Suction only <b>as needed</b>, never on a schedule, because suctioning itself raises ICP. Fifteen degrees is too low (the head of the bed goes to at least 30 degrees), and hourly coughing raises intrathoracic pressure and ICP.", "topic": "Increased ICP care", "src": "NUR 258 Week 8 lecture 1: ICP review and neuro trauma", "icon": "🧠", "topicFull": "Neurologic Dysfunction & Cerebrovascular Disorders"}, {"q": "After a fall, a client's Glasgow Coma Scale score is 10. How does the nurse classify the brain injury?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Mild", "Moderate", "Severe", "Comatose"], "ans": [1], "why": "GCS 13 to 15 is mild, <b>9 to 12 is moderate</b>, and 8 or below is severe (the point at which the airway must be protected). A score of 10 is a moderate injury that still needs frequent reassessment.", "topic": "Glasgow Coma Scale", "src": "NUR 258 Week 8 lecture 1: ICP review and neuro trauma", "icon": "🧠", "topicFull": "Neurologic Dysfunction & Cerebrovascular Disorders"}], "8": [{"q": "A client with a T4 spinal cord injury has BP 208/112, HR 48, a pounding headache and flushing above the injury. What does the nurse do first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Administer the prescribed antihypertensive", "Sit the client upright", "Check for a fecal impaction", "Lay the client flat and elevate the legs"], "ans": [1], "why": "In autonomic dysreflexia the first action is to <b>sit her up</b>, which uses gravity to drop the blood pressure immediately, then find and remove the trigger - check the bladder before the bowel. Antihypertensives are an adjunct, not the first move, and lying flat worsens the hypertension."}, {"q": "On the first day after spinal surgery, which finding does the nurse report immediately?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Blood pressure drifting from 122/78 to 110/70", "Pain rated 4 then 5 then 4", "Progressive left leg weakness now unable to lift the leg", "Requesting a second pillow"], "ans": [2], "why": "<b>Progressive motor loss distal to the surgical site is the concerning trend</b> and suggests cord compromise. Small BP drift and pain oscillating between 4 and 5 are noise - trend items reward spotting the finding that moves in one direction."}, {"q": "A client with myasthenia gravis takes pyridostigmine. Which instruction is correct?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Take it with food to reduce nausea", "Take it 30 to 60 minutes before meals", "Take it only on days when weakness is noticeable", "Take it at bedtime to prevent morning weakness"], "ans": [1], "why": "Pyridostigmine is timed <b>30 to 60 minutes before meals</b> so its 3 to 4 hour peak covers chewing and swallowing. It is never stopped because she feels better - feeling better is the drug working."}, {"q": "A client is admitted with suspected bacterial meningitis. Which nursing actions are appropriate? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Initiate droplet precautions", "Place the client in a negative-pressure room with N95 use", "Elevate the head of the bed 30 degrees", "Assess for a petechial rash on the trunk and extremities", "Keep the room brightly lit for frequent neuro checks"], "ans": [0, 2, 3], "why": "Bacterial (meningococcal) meningitis needs <b>droplet precautions, HOB at 30 degrees, and monitoring for a petechial rash with a falling LOC</b>. Negative pressure and N95 belong to airborne conditions, and photophobia makes a bright room actively unkind."}, {"q": "A client has sudden one-sided facial droop. Which finding points to Bell's palsy rather than a stroke?", "type": "single", "opts": ["The forehead is involved and she cannot raise that eyebrow", "The forehead is spared and the eyebrow raises normally", "She has slurred speech with the droop", "The droop is accompanied by arm weakness"], "ans": [0], "why": "<b>Bell's palsy takes the whole side of the face including the forehead; a stroke spares the forehead.</b> Protect the affected eye with lubricant and a night patch - about 95 percent recover over months."}, {"q": "A client is placed in a halo device. Which nursing action is the priority?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Perform neurovascular checks of all extremities", "Loosen the vest bars slightly for comfort", "Use the vest bars to reposition the client in bed", "Clean the pin sites with an alcohol-based solution"], "ans": [0], "why": "The priority is <b>neurovascular checks of all extremities</b> - the device exists to protect the cord, and circulation and function tell you it is doing so. Never lift or turn a client by the vest bars, never remove the device, and keep a wrench taped to the vest for emergency CPR access."}, {"q": "A client with a spinal cord injury at T4 suddenly develops a pounding headache, flushing above the injury, and a blood pressure of 202/110 mm Hg. Which action does the nurse take first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Sit the client upright", "Administer the prescribed antihypertensive", "Check for a kinked urinary catheter", "Notify the provider"], "ans": [0], "why": "This is <b>autonomic dysreflexia</b>, and it can stroke the client within minutes. <b>Sitting upright is first</b> because gravity alone drops the blood pressure immediately and costs nothing. Then find and remove the trigger - a blocked catheter is the commonest, followed by impaction and skin pressure. Drugs come after position and trigger removal.", "topic": "Traumatic, Infectious,…", "icon": "🧠", "topicFull": "Traumatic, Infectious, Oncologic & Degenerative Neuro"}, {"q": "Which findings would the nurse expect in a client with myasthenia gravis? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Ptosis and diplopia worse at the end of the day", "Muscle weakness that improves with rest", "Symptoms that improve after edrophonium", "Ascending weakness beginning in the legs", "Difficulty chewing toward the end of a meal"], "ans": [0, 1, 2, 4], "why": "Myasthenia gravis is a <b>descending, fatigable</b> weakness: worst with use, best with rest, and it hits the eyes and the muscles of chewing and swallowing first. <b>Ascending weakness from the legs is Guillain-Barré</b>, which is the pairing these questions test.", "topic": "Traumatic, Infectious,…", "icon": "🧠", "topicFull": "Traumatic, Infectious, Oncologic & Degenerative Neuro", "card": "neuro/NG-251_myasthenia-gravis.html", "cardLabel": "Myasthenia Gravis"}, {"q": "A client with Parkinson's disease is prescribed carbidopa-levodopa. Which client statement needs correction?", "type": "single", "opts": ["\"My urine and sweat may darken.\"", "\"I will take it with a high-protein meal so it does not upset my stomach.\"", "\"I should stand up slowly.\"", "\"It may take several weeks before I notice a real difference.\""], "ans": [1], "why": "<b>Dietary protein competes with levodopa for absorption</b>, so a high-protein meal blunts the dose - clients are taught to separate them. Darkened urine and sweat are harmless, orthostatic hypotension is real and common, and the full effect does take weeks.", "pharm": 1, "topic": "Traumatic, Infectious,…", "icon": "🧠", "topicFull": "Traumatic, Infectious, Oncologic & Degenerative Neuro", "card": "neuro/NG-258_parkinsons.html", "cardLabel": "Parkinson's Disease"}, {"q": "A client is admitted with suspected bacterial meningitis. Which actions are priorities? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Place the client on droplet precautions", "Give the prescribed antibiotic as soon as cultures are drawn", "Keep the room bright to help with orientation", "Assess neurological status frequently", "Position for comfort and reduce stimulation"], "ans": [0, 1, 3, 4], "why": "Bacterial meningitis needs <b>droplet precautions</b> and antibiotics <b>within the hour</b> - delay costs lives, so cultures are drawn fast and the drug is not held for results. Photophobia is a hallmark, so the room is kept <b>dark and quiet</b>, not bright.", "topic": "Traumatic, Infectious,…", "icon": "🧠", "topicFull": "Traumatic, Infectious, Oncologic & Degenerative Neuro", "card": "neuro/NG-252_meningitis.html", "cardLabel": "Meningitis"}, {"q": "A client is brought in after a major fall. He was briefly unconscious at the scene, then woke and talked normally with the paramedics for about 30 minutes. He is now difficult to arouse. Which injury does the nurse suspect?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Diffuse axonal injury", "Epidural hematoma", "Subdural hematoma", "Spinal shock"], "ans": [1], "why": "The <b>lucid interval</b> after major trauma — out, awake and fine, then out again — is the signature of an arterial epidural bleed, which moves fast. Subdural is the tempting answer, but it is venous and builds slowly over 24–48 hours to 2 weeks, typically in older adults on anticoagulants or with alcohol use."}, {"q": "A client with a suspected cervical spinal cord injury from a diving accident stops breathing while the nurse is at the bedside. Which action does the nurse take first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Apply a cervical collar before addressing the airway", "Log roll the client onto a backboard", "Open the airway with a jaw thrust", "Open the airway with a head-tilt chin-lift"], "ans": [2], "why": "With a suspected spinal cord injury the airway is opened with a <b>jaw thrust</b>, which does not move the neck. The head-tilt chin-lift is the familiar maneuver and is exactly what must be avoided here, because extending the cervical spine can worsen the cord injury; collar and backboard follow the ABCs, not precede them."}, {"q": "A client admitted with Guillain-Barre syndrome after a recent infection now has weakness that has ascended from the feet to the upper thighs. Which assessment is the priority?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Pulse oximetry readings", "Vital capacity", "Deep tendon reflexes in the legs", "Ability to ambulate safely"], "ans": [1], "why": "Guillain-Barre ascends, so the threat is that the paralysis reaches the diaphragm and ventilation fails; <b>vital capacity</b> is what detects that early. Pulse oximetry looks like the respiratory answer, but oxygen saturation falls late — after respiratory failure is already underway."}, {"q": "An 82-year-old client admitted two days ago for a urinary tract infection becomes confused overnight, with attention that drifts in and out. Her family says her thinking was completely normal last week. Which interpretation by the nurse is correct?", "type": "single", "opts": ["An expected age-related memory change", "Dementia, which is gradual and irreversible", "Delirium, likely from the infection, and reversible", "Early Alzheimer's disease, since memory fails first"], "ans": [2], "why": "Confusion that comes on <b>suddenly</b> over hours to days, fluctuates and impairs attention is delirium until proven otherwise — it is reversible and UTI in an older adult is a classic cause. Dementia is the tempting choice, but it develops gradually over months to years and takes memory first, not attention."}, {"q": "A client is brought to the emergency department after a motorcycle crash. Which findings would lead the nurse to suspect a basilar skull fracture? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Bradycardia with hypotension", "Bruising behind the ear", "A ring of clear fluid around blood on the head dressing", "Pain when the flexed knee is straightened", "Bruising around both eyes"], "ans": [1, 2, 4], "why": "Battle's sign (bruising behind the ear), raccoon eyes and the <b>halo sign</b> — CSF ringing the blood on the dressing — are the three findings of a basilar skull fracture. Bradycardia with hypotension is neurogenic shock from lost sympathetic tone, and pain on straightening the flexed knee is Kernig's sign of meningitis."}, {"q": "A client is admitted to the intensive care unit with an acute cervical spinal cord injury. Which nursing actions are appropriate? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Reposition the client in bed every 2 hours", "Use a head-tilt chin-lift if the airway must be opened", "Maintain the mean arterial pressure at 85 mmHg or higher", "Log roll the client, maintaining spinal alignment", "Reposition every 4 hours once the client is up in a wheelchair"], "ans": [0, 2, 3], "why": "Cord perfusion is protected by keeping <b>MAP at 85 mmHg or higher</b>, skin by turning every 2 hours in bed, and alignment by log rolling. Every 4 hours sounds reasonable but wheelchair repositioning is hourly — pressure injuries can form within 6 hours — and the airway is opened with a jaw thrust, never a head-tilt chin-lift."}, {"q": "A client with multiple sclerosis is having more trouble walking. Which plan best supports the client's independence?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Gait training first, then a cane, a walker and finally a wheelchair as needed", "Request a wheelchair now to prevent falls", "Bed rest until the relapse ends", "Have staff do all activities of daily living"], "ans": [0], "why": "Progressive neuro diseases cannot be cured, only slowed, so the goal is to <b>keep the client independent</b> as long as possible: step up the aids gradually and give the client tools rather than doing everything for them.", "card": "nur258-module-08-neuro-trauma-infection-oncology-degenerative.html#dx-multiple-sclerosis-myasthenia-gravis-and-guillain-barr", "src": "SimpleNursing review video (Fall 2026, Wk 8): CNS and PNS"}, {"q": "Which strategy should the nurse teach a client with multiple sclerosis to limit fatigue?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Plan rest periods in a cool, air-conditioned setting", "Build endurance by lengthening exercise each day without rest breaks", "Take a hot shower before activity to loosen the muscles", "Run all errands in one long trip"], "ans": [0], "why": "Heat worsens MS symptoms, and fatigue is one of the most disabling. Teach <b>cool environments, planned rest and paced activity</b>. \"Lengthen exercise each day\" was the video's most-chosen wrong answer; hot showers and long trips drain energy.", "card": "nur258-module-08-neuro-trauma-infection-oncology-degenerative.html#dx-multiple-sclerosis-myasthenia-gravis-and-guillain-barr", "src": "SimpleNursing review video (Fall 2026, Wk 8): CNS and PNS"}, {"q": "A client with myasthenia gravis has increasing trouble swallowing and a weak cough. What is the nurse's priority?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Maintain respiratory function: assess breathing effort and vital capacity, suction, and be ready for intubation", "Encourage a large meal to keep up nutrition", "Schedule physical therapy", "Teach energy-conservation techniques"], "ans": [0], "why": "Weakness of the swallowing and breathing muscles (including the <b>diaphragm</b>) is the most dangerous complication of myasthenia gravis. Airway and breathing come first: aspiration precautions, suction and an intubation tray at hand.", "card": "nur258-module-08-neuro-trauma-infection-oncology-degenerative.html#dx-multiple-sclerosis-myasthenia-gravis-and-guillain-barr", "src": "SimpleNursing review video (Fall 2026, Wk 8): CNS and PNS"}, {"q": "A client with Guillain-Barre syndrome can no longer lift the head off the pillow and has a weak cough. What does the nurse do?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Notify the provider and prepare for mechanical ventilation", "Let the client rest and reassess in 4 hours", "Begin range-of-motion exercises", "Give a sedative for anxiety"], "ans": [0], "why": "Ascending weakness that reaches the neck and cough muscles signals <b>impending respiratory failure</b>. The nurse monitors vital capacity and prepares for intubation and ventilation before the client tires.", "card": "nur258-module-08-neuro-trauma-infection-oncology-degenerative.html#dx-multiple-sclerosis-myasthenia-gravis-and-guillain-barr", "src": "SimpleNursing review video (Fall 2026, Wk 8): CNS and PNS"}, {"q": "Which finding in a client with amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS) is the priority to report?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Increasing respiratory secretions the client cannot clear", "Weakness in the hands", "Muscle twitching (fasciculations)", "Tiredness in the evening"], "ans": [0], "why": "ALS weakens the breathing and swallowing muscles, so <b>secretions the client cannot clear</b> threaten the airway (aspiration, respiratory failure). Limb weakness and fasciculations are expected findings.", "card": "nur258-module-08-neuro-trauma-infection-oncology-degenerative.html#dx-multiple-sclerosis-myasthenia-gravis-and-guillain-barr", "src": "SimpleNursing review video (Fall 2026, Wk 8): CNS and PNS"}, {"q": "A client with Alzheimer's disease keeps asking for her husband, who died 10 years ago. What is the nurse's best response?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Acknowledge her feelings and redirect her to an activity", "Remind her that her husband died 10 years ago", "Tell her he will visit later today", "Change the subject without responding"], "ans": [0], "why": "Reorienting a client with dementia to a painful fact causes fresh grief and agitation. <b>Validate the feeling, then redirect</b>. Promising a visit is dishonest, and ignoring her dismisses her.", "card": "nur258-module-08-neuro-trauma-infection-oncology-degenerative.html#dx-parkinson-disease-and-alzheimer-disease", "src": "SimpleNursing review video (Fall 2026, Wk 8): CNS and PNS"}, {"q": "A client with Alzheimer's disease who finished lunch 30 minutes ago says, \"I'm hungry. Nobody fed me.\" What does the nurse do?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Offer a small snack, such as half a sandwich, and save the rest for later", "Remind the client that lunch was eaten 30 minutes ago", "Tell the client the next meal is dinner", "Document a delusion and leave the room"], "ans": [0], "why": "Arguing with a client who cannot remember only increases distress. Meeting the need with a <b>small snack</b>, without confrontation, is the safe and respectful choice. \"Gently remind them they already ate\" was the most-chosen wrong answer.", "card": "nur258-module-08-neuro-trauma-infection-oncology-degenerative.html#dx-parkinson-disease-and-alzheimer-disease", "src": "SimpleNursing review video (Fall 2026, Wk 8): CNS and PNS"}, {"q": "A client taking donepezil reports new shortness of breath and wheezing. What does the nurse do?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Report it to the provider: cholinesterase inhibitors can cause bronchoconstriction", "Reassure the client that this is an expected effect", "Give the next dose early", "Encourage extra fluids"], "ans": [0], "why": "Donepezil raises acetylcholine, which can cause <b>bronchoconstriction</b> (caution with asthma or COPD) and bradycardia. New dyspnea is reported promptly.", "card": "nur258-module-08-neuro-trauma-infection-oncology-degenerative.html#dx-parkinson-disease-and-alzheimer-disease", "src": "SimpleNursing review video (Fall 2026, Wk 8): CNS and PNS"}, {"q": "A client with Huntington disease asks about having children. What does the nurse recommend?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Genetic counseling, because each child has a 50% chance of inheriting the gene", "No testing is needed unless both parents carry the gene", "Children are not at risk until they are over 60", "Genetic testing for Huntington disease does not exist"], "ans": [0], "why": "Huntington disease is <b>autosomal dominant</b>: one affected parent gives each child a <b>50% chance</b>. One parent is enough to pass it on, and genetic counseling lets the client weigh testing and family planning.", "card": "nur258-module-08-neuro-trauma-infection-oncology-degenerative.html#dx-neuro2", "src": "SimpleNursing review video (Fall 2026, Wk 8): CNS and PNS"}, {"q": "A client with encephalitis is drowsy and reports a severe headache. Which prescription should the nurse clarify with the provider?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Acetaminophen 650 mg orally every 6 hours as needed", "Morphine 4 mg IV every 2 hours as needed for pain", "Neurologic checks every hour", "Levetiracetam IV twice daily"], "ans": [1], "why": "Opioids and sedatives can <b>mask a falling level of consciousness</b>: the nurse cannot tell whether new drowsiness is the drug or rising ICP. Acetaminophen treats headache and fever without clouding the neuro exam, and hourly neuro checks and seizure prophylaxis are expected care.", "topic": "Sedation masking neuro changes", "src": "NUR 258 Week 8 lecture 2: neuro infections and degenerative disorders", "icon": "🧠", "topicFull": "Traumatic, Infectious, Oncologic & Degenerative Neuro"}, {"q": "A client is scheduled for a bedside lumbar puncture. Which actions should the nurse take before the procedure? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Have the client empty the bladder", "Review the PT/INR and platelet count", "Keep the client NPO for 8 hours", "Check the BUN and creatinine for contrast clearance", "Help the client into a side-lying position with the knees drawn to the chest", "Give IV sedation as for an angiogram"], "ans": [0, 1, 4], "why": "Have the client void first so they do not need to move mid-procedure, check <b>coagulation studies and platelets</b> (bleeding risk; anticoagulation is a contraindication), and position side-lying curled in a C. No contrast is given, so BUN and creatinine are not needed; NPO is not required, and only a local anesthetic is used, not sedation.", "topic": "Lumbar puncture preparation", "src": "NUR 258 Week 8 lecture 2: neuro infections and degenerative disorders", "icon": "🧠", "topicFull": "Traumatic, Infectious, Oncologic & Degenerative Neuro"}, {"q": "A client with suspected bacterial meningitis has a lumbar puncture. Which cerebrospinal fluid findings does the nurse expect? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Cloudy appearance", "Decreased glucose", "Elevated protein", "Elevated white blood cell count", "Red blood cells present", "Elevated D-dimer"], "ans": [0, 1, 2, 3], "why": "Bacterial CSF is <b>cloudy</b> with high white cells, high protein, high pressure and <b>low glucose</b>, because the bacteria consume it. Red blood cells point to bleeding such as a subarachnoid hemorrhage, not infection, and D-dimer is a clotting test used when a pulmonary embolism is suspected.", "topic": "Meningitis CSF findings", "src": "NUR 258 Week 8 lecture 2: neuro infections and degenerative disorders", "icon": "🧠", "topicFull": "Traumatic, Infectious, Oncologic & Degenerative Neuro"}, {"q": "A client's cerebrospinal fluid is clear, with an elevated white blood cell count, elevated protein and a normal glucose. How does the nurse interpret these results?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Most consistent with viral meningitis", "Most consistent with bacterial meningitis", "Normal cerebrospinal fluid", "Most consistent with a subarachnoid hemorrhage"], "ans": [0], "why": "White cells and protein rise in both kinds, so <b>appearance and glucose</b> separate them: viral CSF is clear with a normal glucose, bacterial CSF is cloudy with a low glucose. Normal CSF has no raised white count, and a hemorrhage gives blood-tinged fluid.", "topic": "Meningitis CSF findings", "src": "NUR 258 Week 8 lecture 2: neuro infections and degenerative disorders", "icon": "🧠", "topicFull": "Traumatic, Infectious, Oncologic & Degenerative Neuro"}, {"q": "While assessing a client with fever and headache, the nurse flexes the client's neck toward the chest, and the client involuntarily draws up the hips and knees. How does the nurse document this?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Positive Brudzinski sign", "Positive Kernig sign", "Positive Babinski sign", "Decorticate posturing"], "ans": [0], "why": "<b>Brudzinski sign</b>: flexing the neck causes flexion of the hips and knees. <b>Kernig sign</b> is tested with the hip flexed to 90 degrees, and is positive when extending the knee causes pain or resistance. Both point to meningeal irritation; Babinski is a plantar reflex, and posturing is a response to a painful stimulus.", "topic": "Meningitis assessment", "src": "NUR 258 Week 8 lecture 2: neuro infections and degenerative disorders", "icon": "🧠", "topicFull": "Traumatic, Infectious, Oncologic & Degenerative Neuro"}, {"q": "A college student is admitted with bacterial (meningococcal) meningitis. Her roommate asks what she should do. Which response by the nurse is correct?", "type": "single", "opts": ["You should receive preventive antibiotics because you are a close contact.", "You only need treatment if you develop a stiff neck or fever.", "You need to stay in airborne isolation for 2 weeks.", "Nothing is needed, because meningitis spreads only through blood."], "ans": [0], "why": "Bacterial meningitis spreads by <b>respiratory droplets</b>, so close contacts such as roommates and household members receive <b>prophylactic antibiotics</b>. Waiting for symptoms is too late, airborne isolation is not the precaution, and it is not bloodborne. Meningococcal meningitis is also reported to the public health department.", "topic": "Meningitis contacts", "src": "NUR 258 Week 8 lecture 2: neuro infections and degenerative disorders", "icon": "🧠", "topicFull": "Traumatic, Infectious, Oncologic & Degenerative Neuro"}, {"q": "A client with bacterial meningitis has a serum sodium of 126 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145 mEq/L), concentrated urine and a falling urine output. Which complication does the nurse suspect?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone (SIADH)", "Diabetes insipidus", "Dehydration from fever", "Acute urinary retention"], "ans": [0], "why": "Brain infection or injury can trigger <b>SIADH</b>: retained water causes dilutional hyponatremia with concentrated urine and low output, and the low sodium raises the seizure risk. Diabetes insipidus is the opposite, with large volumes of dilute urine and a <b>high</b> sodium; dehydration also raises sodium.", "topic": "Meningitis complications", "src": "NUR 258 Week 8 lecture 2: neuro infections and degenerative disorders", "icon": "🧠", "topicFull": "Traumatic, Infectious, Oncologic & Degenerative Neuro"}, {"q": "A client with a brain abscess that followed an untreated dental infection asks why the headache is worst on waking and eases after being up for a while. Which explanation by the nurse is accurate?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Lying flat overnight lets the pressure inside the skull rise; sitting up lowers it.", "The antibiotics wear off overnight, so the infection flares each morning.", "Morning headaches come from low blood sugar after fasting overnight.", "The abscess shrinks during the day as you move around."], "ans": [0], "why": "An abscess is a mass that raises ICP. Lying flat all night slows venous drainage, so ICP and the headache <b>peak on waking</b>, and getting upright lowers it: the same reason the head of the bed is raised for raised ICP. The other choices do not explain the positional pattern.", "topic": "Brain abscess", "src": "NUR 258 Week 8 lecture 2: neuro infections and degenerative disorders", "icon": "🧠", "topicFull": "Traumatic, Infectious, Oncologic & Degenerative Neuro"}, {"q": "A client is admitted with West Nile virus encephalitis. Which isolation precautions does the nurse implement?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Standard precautions", "Droplet precautions", "Airborne precautions in a negative-pressure room", "Contact precautions"], "ans": [0], "why": "West Nile encephalitis is carried by <b>mosquitoes</b>, not spread from person to person, so standard precautions are enough. Droplet precautions are for bacterial meningitis, which does spread by respiratory droplets.", "topic": "Encephalitis", "src": "NUR 258 Week 8 lecture 2: neuro infections and degenerative disorders", "icon": "🧠", "topicFull": "Traumatic, Infectious, Oncologic & Degenerative Neuro"}, {"q": "A client is diagnosed with herpes simplex encephalitis. Which medication does the nurse anticipate?", "type": "single", "opts": ["IV acyclovir", "IV ceftriaxone and vancomycin alone", "Pyridostigmine", "IV alteplase"], "ans": [0], "why": "Herpes simplex encephalitis is viral, so treatment is the antiviral <b>acyclovir</b>, started early to shorten the illness and limit brain damage. Ceftriaxone with vancomycin is the empiric regimen for bacterial meningitis; pyridostigmine treats myasthenia gravis and alteplase treats ischemic stroke.", "topic": "Encephalitis", "src": "NUR 258 Week 8 lecture 2: neuro infections and degenerative disorders", "icon": "🧠", "topicFull": "Traumatic, Infectious, Oncologic & Degenerative Neuro"}, {"q": "A 28-year-old woman is being evaluated for multiple sclerosis. Which findings does the nurse expect? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Areas of numbness and tingling", "Blurred or double vision", "Sudden involuntary jerking movements (chorea)", "New urinary incontinence", "Fatigue", "Temporary ascending paralysis after a viral illness"], "ans": [0, 1, 3, 4], "why": "Demyelination in MS causes <b>paresthesia, visual changes, bladder dysfunction and fatigue</b>, typically in a young woman. Chorea belongs to Huntington disease, and temporary ascending paralysis after a viral illness is Guillain-Barre syndrome.", "topic": "Multiple sclerosis", "src": "NUR 258 Week 8 lecture 2: neuro infections and degenerative disorders", "icon": "🧠", "topicFull": "Traumatic, Infectious, Oncologic & Degenerative Neuro"}, {"q": "Which diagnostic finding best supports a diagnosis of multiple sclerosis?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Multiple plaques in the brain and spinal cord on MRI", "Cloudy cerebrospinal fluid with a low glucose", "A positive ice pack test", "Blood in the subarachnoid space on CT"], "ans": [0], "why": "There is no single blood test: MS is diagnosed from the symptoms plus an <b>MRI showing multiple demyelinating plaques</b> in the brain and spinal cord (CSF may show elevated protein). Cloudy, low-glucose CSF is bacterial meningitis, the ice pack test is for myasthenia gravis, and subarachnoid blood is a hemorrhage.", "topic": "Multiple sclerosis", "src": "NUR 258 Week 8 lecture 2: neuro infections and degenerative disorders", "icon": "🧠", "topicFull": "Traumatic, Infectious, Oncologic & Degenerative Neuro"}, {"q": "A client with multiple sclerosis reports double vision. Which intervention does the nurse teach?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Wear a patch over one eye, alternating eyes, and use scanning techniques", "Wear the patch over the same eye at all times", "Dim the room lights to rest the eyes", "Avoid all reading until the double vision resolves"], "ans": [0], "why": "Covering one eye removes the second image; <b>alternating</b> the patch keeps both eyes in use, and scanning techniques plus a clutter-free room reduce falls. Patching the same eye all the time is not the recommendation, and dim lighting raises the fall risk.", "topic": "Multiple sclerosis", "src": "NUR 258 Week 8 lecture 2: neuro infections and degenerative disorders", "icon": "🧠", "topicFull": "Traumatic, Infectious, Oncologic & Degenerative Neuro"}, {"q": "A client with myasthenia gravis is admitted with pneumonia. Which actions should the nurse include in the plan of care? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Keep oral and nasal suction equipment at the bedside", "Plan rest periods between activities", "Reorient the client often, since cognitive decline is expected", "Anticipate plasmapheresis if the weakness worsens", "Encourage long walks late in the day to build strength"], "ans": [0, 1, 3], "why": "An infection can trigger a <b>myasthenic crisis</b>, so the airway must be protectable at any moment with suction at the bedside. Weakness worsens with activity and improves with rest, so activities are spaced out. Plasmapheresis removes the circulating antibodies during an exacerbation. MG does not affect cognition, and late in the day is when the client is weakest.", "topic": "Myasthenia gravis", "src": "NUR 258 Week 8 lecture 2: neuro infections and degenerative disorders", "icon": "🧠", "topicFull": "Traumatic, Infectious, Oncologic & Degenerative Neuro"}, {"q": "A client has a drooping left eyelid and generalized weakness. The nurse applies an ice pack over the closed eyelid for 2 to 5 minutes, and afterward the eyelid lifts noticeably higher. How does the nurse interpret this?", "type": "single", "opts": ["A positive test supporting myasthenia gravis", "A positive test supporting Bell's palsy", "A negative test, because cold should worsen the droop", "A finding typical of a stroke"], "ans": [0], "why": "Cold slows the breakdown of acetylcholine at the neuromuscular junction, so in <b>myasthenia gravis</b> ptosis improves after a few minutes of ice. A droop from a stroke or Bell's palsy does not change with ice.", "topic": "Myasthenia gravis", "src": "NUR 258 Week 8 lecture 2: neuro infections and degenerative disorders", "icon": "🧠", "topicFull": "Traumatic, Infectious, Oncologic & Degenerative Neuro"}, {"q": "A client with myasthenia gravis is scheduled for plasmapheresis. Which statement shows the client understands the treatment?", "type": "single", "opts": ["It filters the antibodies out of my blood, and I still need my medications.", "It will cure my myasthenia gravis, so I can stop my pyridostigmine.", "I will receive healthy antibodies from donors through my IV.", "It does the work of dialysis, removing wastes my kidneys cannot clear."], "ans": [0], "why": "Plasmapheresis removes the plasma carrying the <b>circulating antibodies</b> that block acetylcholine receptors and returns the blood cells. Relief lasts only weeks, so it is <b>not a cure</b> and medications continue. Receiving donor antibodies describes IVIG, and the treatment does not replace kidney function.", "topic": "Myasthenia gravis", "src": "NUR 258 Week 8 lecture 2: neuro infections and degenerative disorders", "icon": "🧠", "topicFull": "Traumatic, Infectious, Oncologic & Degenerative Neuro"}, {"q": "A client arrives with tingling and weakness that began in the feet this morning and has spread to the knees by afternoon. Which question is most important for the nurse to ask?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Have you had a viral illness or a vaccination in the past few weeks?", "Does anyone in your family have a similar movement disorder?", "Is your weakness worse at the end of the day?", "Have you noticed a tremor in your hands at rest?"], "ans": [0], "why": "Rapid <b>ascending</b> weakness suggests Guillain-Barre syndrome, which most often follows a viral or other infection and occasionally a vaccine. Weakness that worsens through the day points to myasthenia gravis, a resting tremor to Parkinson disease, and an inherited movement disorder to Huntington disease.", "topic": "Guillain-Barre syndrome", "src": "NUR 258 Week 8 lecture 2: neuro infections and degenerative disorders", "icon": "🧠", "topicFull": "Traumatic, Infectious, Oncologic & Degenerative Neuro"}, {"q": "Before giving a seasonal influenza vaccine, the nurse reviews the client's history. Which finding requires the nurse to hold the vaccine and consult the provider?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Guillain-Barre syndrome that began within 6 weeks of a previous flu vaccine", "A mild cold without a fever", "Arm soreness at the injection site after last year's vaccine", "Currently taking an oral antibiotic for a sinus infection"], "ans": [0], "why": "A history of <b>Guillain-Barre syndrome within 6 weeks of a prior influenza vaccine</b> is a precaution: the provider weighs the risk before it is given. A mild illness without fever is not a reason to delay, arm soreness is an expected local reaction, and an antibiotic does not interfere with an inactivated vaccine.", "topic": "Guillain-Barre syndrome", "src": "NUR 258 Week 8 lecture 2: neuro infections and degenerative disorders", "icon": "🧠", "topicFull": "Traumatic, Infectious, Oncologic & Degenerative Neuro"}, {"q": "A client is admitted with rapidly progressing Guillain-Barre syndrome. Which treatments does the nurse anticipate? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Plasmapheresis", "IV immunoglobulin (IVIG)", "Pyridostigmine", "Carbidopa-levodopa", "Enoxaparin or sequential compression devices to prevent DVT", "IV alteplase"], "ans": [0, 1, 4], "why": "GBS is treated with <b>plasmapheresis or IVIG</b> to remove or neutralize the attacking antibodies, plus supportive care: airway, nutrition and <b>DVT prevention</b> for an immobile client. Pyridostigmine treats myasthenia gravis, carbidopa-levodopa treats Parkinson disease, and alteplase treats ischemic stroke.", "topic": "Guillain-Barre syndrome", "src": "NUR 258 Week 8 lecture 2: neuro infections and degenerative disorders", "icon": "🧠", "topicFull": "Traumatic, Infectious, Oncologic & Degenerative Neuro"}, {"q": "A client with Guillain-Barre syndrome is on a ventilator and cannot move the arms or head. Which approach to communication is best?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Explain all care to the client and set up a blink code for yes and no", "Speak only to the family, since the client cannot respond", "Use a communication board the client points to", "Limit conversation, since the client's thinking is impaired"], "ans": [0], "why": "GBS paralyzes the muscles but leaves <b>cognition intact</b>: the client hears and understands everything. Talk to the client, explain all care, and use eye blinks for yes and no until movement returns. Pointing needs arm movement the client does not have, and talking past the client or assuming impaired thinking is wrong.", "topic": "Guillain-Barre syndrome", "src": "NUR 258 Week 8 lecture 2: neuro infections and degenerative disorders", "icon": "🧠", "topicFull": "Traumatic, Infectious, Oncologic & Degenerative Neuro"}, {"q": "A client is diagnosed with Bell's palsy. Which medication does the nurse anticipate?", "type": "single", "opts": ["An oral corticosteroid such as prednisone", "IV alteplase", "Pyridostigmine", "Carbidopa-levodopa"], "ans": [0], "why": "Bell's palsy is inflammation of <b>cranial nerve VII</b>, so a corticosteroid started early reduces the swelling; an antiviral may be added when herpes is suspected. Alteplase is for ischemic stroke (a stroke spares the forehead, Bell's palsy does not), pyridostigmine is for myasthenia gravis and carbidopa-levodopa for Parkinson disease.", "topic": "Bell's palsy", "src": "NUR 258 Week 8 lecture 2: neuro infections and degenerative disorders", "icon": "🧠", "topicFull": "Traumatic, Infectious, Oncologic & Degenerative Neuro"}, {"q": "Which technique does the nurse use to assess the trigeminal nerve (cranial nerve V)?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Lightly touch both sides of the face and ask the client to tell sharp from dull with the eyes closed", "Ask the client to smile, raise the eyebrows and puff out the cheeks", "Shine a light into each eye and watch the pupils constrict", "Ask the client to shrug the shoulders against resistance"], "ans": [0], "why": "Cranial nerve <b>V</b> carries facial <b>sensation</b> (and chewing), so compare light touch and sharp versus dull on both sides. Smiling and raising the eyebrows test cranial nerve VII, the pupil response tests cranial nerve III, and a shoulder shrug tests cranial nerve XI.", "topic": "Cranial nerve assessment", "src": "NUR 258 Week 8 lecture 2: neuro infections and degenerative disorders", "icon": "🧠", "topicFull": "Traumatic, Infectious, Oncologic & Degenerative Neuro"}, {"q": "When does the nurse expect the tremor of a client with Parkinson disease to be most noticeable?", "type": "single", "opts": ["While the client is awake and at rest", "While the client is asleep", "Only during purposeful movement, such as reaching for a cup", "Only after the client has exercised"], "ans": [0], "why": "Parkinson tremor is a <b>resting tremor</b> (pill-rolling) that lessens with purposeful movement and disappears during sleep. A tremor that appears only when reaching is an intention tremor, seen with cerebellar disease and MS.", "topic": "Parkinson disease", "src": "NUR 258 Week 8 lecture 2: neuro infections and degenerative disorders", "icon": "🧠", "topicFull": "Traumatic, Infectious, Oncologic & Degenerative Neuro"}, {"q": "A 42-year-old client has sudden, jerky, involuntary movements of the arms and face, grimacing, and new angry outbursts. The client's father had the same problems. Which condition does the nurse suspect?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Huntington disease", "Parkinson disease", "Multiple sclerosis", "Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis"], "ans": [0], "why": "<b>Chorea</b> (sudden, uncontrolled, jerky movements and grimacing) with personality and cognitive changes beginning together in midlife, plus an affected parent, is Huntington disease, which is inherited. Parkinson disease causes a slow resting tremor, rigidity and a shuffling gait; MS causes weakness, numbness and visual changes; ALS causes progressive weakness without chorea.", "topic": "Huntington disease", "src": "NUR 258 Week 8 lecture 2: neuro infections and degenerative disorders", "icon": "🧠", "topicFull": "Traumatic, Infectious, Oncologic & Degenerative Neuro"}, {"q": "A client with Huntington disease says, \"I watched my mother go through this. I don't see the point anymore.\" What is the nurse's priority action?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Ask the client directly about thoughts of suicide", "Reassure the client that new treatments may cure the disease", "Change the subject to the client's upcoming therapy session", "Document the statement and reassess at the next shift"], "ans": [0], "why": "Clients with Huntington disease know the course is progressive and fatal and are at <b>high risk for depression and suicide</b>. A statement of hopelessness calls for a direct suicide-risk assessment now. False reassurance, changing the subject or waiting a shift leaves the risk unaddressed.", "topic": "Huntington disease", "src": "NUR 258 Week 8 lecture 2: neuro infections and degenerative disorders", "icon": "🧠", "topicFull": "Traumatic, Infectious, Oncologic & Degenerative Neuro"}, {"q": "A nurse is planning care for a client in a later stage of Alzheimer disease on a memory-care unit. Which interventions are appropriate? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Weigh the client weekly", "Stay with the client and supervise or assist at meals", "Leave the meal tray and collect it in 45 minutes", "Turn off the television at night", "Offer a simple task such as folding towels when agitation builds", "Insist on the correct date until the client agrees"], "ans": [0, 1, 3, 4], "why": "Clients forget to eat, or forget they have eaten, so <b>supervise meals</b> and <b>weigh weekly</b> to catch loss or gain. A television left on at night overstimulates and escalates agitation, while a simple familiar task such as folding towels redirects it. A tray left alone may go untouched, and arguing about reality increases agitation.", "topic": "Alzheimer disease", "src": "NUR 258 Week 8 lecture 2: neuro infections and degenerative disorders", "icon": "🧠", "topicFull": "Traumatic, Infectious, Oncologic & Degenerative Neuro"}, {"q": "Which finding is an early manifestation of amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS)?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Weakness of the hands or feet", "Memory loss and confusion", "Paralysis of the diaphragm", "Loss of sensation in the legs"], "ans": [0], "why": "ALS destroys motor neurons and begins with <b>weakness in the distal extremities</b> (with fatigue, cramps and twitching), progressing to paralysis. Respiratory paralysis is a late finding, and cognition and sensation are usually spared.", "topic": "ALS", "src": "NUR 258 Week 8 lecture 2: neuro infections and degenerative disorders", "icon": "🧠", "topicFull": "Traumatic, Infectious, Oncologic & Degenerative Neuro"}, {"q": "A client has just been diagnosed with amyotrophic lateral sclerosis. Which topic should the nurse help the client address early, while the client can still communicate?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Advance directives, including wishes about a tracheostomy and mechanical ventilation", "Scheduling a thymectomy", "A ketogenic diet to slow progression", "Plans for returning to full strength after rehabilitation"], "ans": [0], "why": "ALS is progressive and usually fatal within a few years, eventually paralyzing the breathing muscles and speech. The client should state <b>early</b> whether they want a tracheostomy and ventilator or hospice care. Thymectomy is a myasthenia gravis treatment, and a full recovery fits Guillain-Barre syndrome, not ALS.", "topic": "ALS", "src": "NUR 258 Week 8 lecture 2: neuro infections and degenerative disorders", "icon": "🧠", "topicFull": "Traumatic, Infectious, Oncologic & Degenerative Neuro"}, {"q": "A client with a T5 spinal cord injury is suspected of having autonomic dysreflexia. Which finding is a manifestation of the condition itself?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Sweating above the level of the injury", "Flushed, warm skin on the legs", "A distended bladder", "Pressure from sitting on a sharp object"], "ans": [0], "why": "Above the injury the body vasodilates, causing <b>flushing, sweating</b> and a pounding headache with high blood pressure and bradycardia; below the injury the skin is <b>pale and cool</b> from vasoconstriction. A full bladder or a sharp object is the <b>trigger</b>, not a manifestation; the nurse sits the client up, then finds and removes it.", "topic": "Autonomic dysreflexia", "src": "NUR 258 Week 8 lecture 2: neuro infections and degenerative disorders", "icon": "🧠", "topicFull": "Traumatic, Infectious, Oncologic & Degenerative Neuro"}, {"q": "A client arrives in the emergency department after a car crash with a T5 spinal cord injury. Which set of vital signs does the nurse expect?", "type": "single", "opts": ["HR 120, RR 20, BP 100/68", "HR 100, RR 16, BP 120/72", "HR 62, RR 18, BP 110/50", "HR 50, RR 32, BP 78/45"], "ans": [3], "why": "An injury at <b>T6 or above</b> puts the client at risk for <b>neurogenic shock</b>: the sympathetic outflow is cut, so the vessels dilate and blood pools in the veins, while the vagus nerve keeps slowing the heart unopposed. The result is <b>hypotension with bradycardia</b> (HR 50, BP 78/45), and a T5 injury also weakens the intercostal muscles, so breathing becomes fast and labored. HR 120 with a soft blood pressure is the <b>tachycardia of hypovolemic shock</b>, the opposite pattern. The other two sets are normal or close to it.", "topic": "Neurogenic shock", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 practice question (24 Nov), reworded", "icon": "🧠", "topicFull": "Traumatic, Infectious, Oncologic & Degenerative Neuro", "card": "nur258-module-08-neuro-trauma-infection-oncology-degenerative.html#dx-spinal-cord-injury", "cardLabel": "Spinal cord injury"}, {"q": "A client has a spinal cord injury at T4. Which complication causes a sudden, severe rise in blood pressure in response to a stimulus below the level of injury?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Autonomic dysreflexia", "Hemorrhage", "Arrhythmias", "Sepsis"], "ans": [0], "why": "<b>Autonomic dysreflexia</b> is a life-threatening complication of a spinal cord injury <b>at or above T6</b>. A stimulus below the injury, most often a <b>full bladder</b>, a <b>bowel impaction</b> or <b>tight clothing</b>, sets off an exaggerated sympathetic response: a sudden, severe hypertension with a pounding headache, and flushing and sweating above the injury. Do not mix it up with <b>neurogenic shock</b>, which runs the other way: hypotension and bradycardia from lost sympathetic tone. Hemorrhage causes hypovolemic shock, sepsis is a response to infection, and arrhythmias (especially slow ones) can follow a spinal cord injury, but none of them is the sudden hypertensive response described.", "topic": "Autonomic dysreflexia", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 practice question (24 Nov), reworded", "icon": "🧠", "topicFull": "Traumatic, Infectious, Oncologic & Degenerative Neuro", "card": "nur258-module-08-neuro-trauma-infection-oncology-degenerative.html#dx-spinal-cord-injury", "cardLabel": "Spinal cord injury"}, {"q": "A client with a herniated lumbar disc has sciatic pain. Which position should the nurse recommend for rest?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Semi-Fowler's at 15 to 30 degrees with a pillow under or between the knees", "Prone with a pillow under the chest", "High Fowler's with the legs straight", "Flat on the back with the legs straight and no pillows"], "ans": [0], "why": "Raising the head 15 to 30 degrees with the knees flexed on a pillow, or side-lying with a pillow between the knees, <b>takes pressure off the compressed nerve roots</b>. Prone, straight-legged and high Fowler's positions increase strain on the lumbar spine.", "topic": "Disc herniation", "src": "NUR 258 Week 8 lecture 2: neuro infections and degenerative disorders", "icon": "🧠", "topicFull": "Traumatic, Infectious, Oncologic & Degenerative Neuro"}, {"q": "A client returned from lumbar spinal surgery 3 hours ago. The nurse is monitoring for a cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) leak. Which finding is most consistent with a CSF leak?", "type": "single", "opts": ["A small amount of serosanguineous drainage on the dressing", "Clear, colorless fluid seeping from the incision with a new headache", "Incisional pain rated 5 out of 10 when the client is log rolled", "Mild numbness at the skin edges around the incision"], "ans": [1], "why": "Spinal surgery often opens the dural sac, so the nurse watches the wound for <b>clear or pale-yellow drainage</b>; a leak is often paired with a <b>headache</b> from low CSF pressure. Report it to the provider. A small amount of serosanguineous drainage is expected early after surgery, and moderate pain on turning and numbness at the skin edges are expected too. None of those points to a dural leak.", "topic": "Spinal surgery: CSF leak", "src": "LSC exam prep recording (Drs Meyer, Karaguesian and Sollis), Neurological II exam prep", "icon": "🧠", "topicFull": "Traumatic, Infectious, Oncologic & Degenerative Neuro"}], "9": [{"q": "Fifteen minutes into a transfusion a client has chills, fever and flank pain. What does the nurse do first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Notify the provider", "Stop the transfusion", "Slow the rate and reassess in 15 minutes", "Administer the prescribed antipyretic"], "ans": [1], "why": "For any suspected reaction the sequence is <b>stop the transfusion, keep the line open with 0.9 percent NS on new tubing, stay with the client, then notify</b>. Slowing the rate keeps incompatible blood going in, and notifying first delays the one action that stops the exposure."}, {"q": "A client with immune thrombocytopenia has four findings. Which one does the nurse act on first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Scattered petechiae over both shins", "A nosebleed that lasted 10 minutes and stopped", "Severe headache with vomiting", "Bruising on the forearms"], "ans": [2], "why": "<b>Severe headache with vomiting in a thrombocytopenic client means intracranial hemorrhage until proven otherwise.</b> Petechiae and a nosebleed that has already stopped are expected findings that can wait."}, {"q": "Which statement about administering packed red blood cells is correct?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Prime with lactated Ringer's and complete within 6 hours", "Prime with 0.9 percent sodium chloride, stay with the client the first 15 minutes, and infuse within 4 hours", "Prime with D5W and check vital signs every hour", "Stay with the client the first 5 minutes and infuse within 2 hours"], "ans": [1], "why": "Only <b>0.9 percent NS</b> is compatible - dextrose hemolyzes cells and LR clots them - and the nurse stays for the <b>first 15 minutes</b> with the unit completed within <b>4 hours</b>. Two-nurse verification precedes all of it."}, {"q": "On day 7 of heparin therapy a client's platelets drop from 220,000 to 90,000 in 48 hours. Which actions does the nurse take? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Stop the heparin", "Administer vitamin K", "Obtain daily platelet counts", "Start warfarin immediately", "Assess for signs of thrombosis"], "ans": [0, 2, 4], "why": "This is HIT: <b>stop the heparin, follow daily platelet counts, notify, and assess for clot</b>, because HIT clots as well as bleeds. Vitamin K is warfarin's antidote, and starting warfarin now is contraindicated - the falling platelet count makes bleeding look like the only risk, which is the trap."}, {"q": "A client develops respiratory distress during a transfusion. Which finding distinguishes TACO from TRALI?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Fever and chills", "Crackles, jugular venous distention and hypertension", "Urticaria and itching", "Hemoglobinuria and flank pain"], "ans": [1], "why": "<b>TACO shows fluid-overload signs - crackles, JVD, hypertension - while TRALI produces respiratory distress without them.</b> The discriminator is overload, not the presence of dyspnea, which both share."}, {"q": "A client with hemophilia has a swollen, painful knee after a fall. Which intervention does the nurse include?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Apply heat and begin gentle range of motion", "Elevate the joint, apply ice and give factor replacement", "Give aspirin for pain and encourage ambulation", "Wrap the joint and perform passive range of motion hourly"], "ans": [1], "why": "Hemarthrosis is managed with <b>elevation, ice, factor replacement and no range of motion</b>; aspirin and NSAIDs are never given. Heat and movement both increase bleeding into the joint - and joint bleeds are about 75 percent of all bleeds in hemophilia."}, {"q": "A client is receiving a unit of packed red blood cells. Fifteen minutes in, the client reports low back pain and chills, and the urine turns dark. What is the nurse's first action?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Slow the transfusion and continue observing", "Stop the transfusion and keep the line open with normal saline", "Give the prescribed antihistamine", "Obtain a urine specimen for analysis"], "ans": [1], "why": "Back pain, chills and dark urine early in a transfusion mean an <b>acute hemolytic reaction</b>. <b>Stop the blood, keep the vein open with saline through new tubing</b>, then notify and investigate. Slowing it is not enough - every further milliliter adds hemolysis. Antihistamines treat mild allergic reactions, which is a different picture.", "topic": "Hematologic Disorders", "icon": "🩸", "topicFull": "Hematologic Disorders"}, {"q": "Which laboratory findings would the nurse expect in iron-deficiency anemia? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Low ferritin", "Microcytic, hypochromic red cells", "Elevated total iron-binding capacity", "Macrocytic red cells", "Low mean corpuscular volume"], "ans": [0, 1, 2, 4], "why": "Iron deficiency makes cells <b>small and pale</b> - low MCV, low ferritin - and the body raises its iron-binding capacity trying to catch more. <b>Macrocytic</b> cells point the other way, to <b>B12 or folate</b> deficiency, and separating those two anemias is worth a mark on every hematology exam.", "topic": "Hematologic Disorders", "icon": "🩸", "topicFull": "Hematologic Disorders"}, {"q": "A client has disseminated intravascular coagulation. Which findings does the nurse expect? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Prolonged PT and aPTT", "Low platelet count", "Elevated D-dimer", "Elevated fibrinogen", "Bleeding from IV sites with simultaneous clotting"], "ans": [0, 1, 2, 4], "why": "DIC is the paradox: <b>clotting and bleeding at once</b>. Clotting consumes platelets and factors, so counts fall and times lengthen; the clot breakdown raises D-dimer. <b>Fibrinogen falls, not rises</b> - it is being used up - and that is the option most often chosen wrongly.", "topic": "Hematologic Disorders", "icon": "🩸", "topicFull": "Hematologic Disorders"}, {"q": "A client with sickle cell disease is admitted in vaso-occlusive crisis. Which interventions are appropriate? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["IV fluids", "Prescribed opioid analgesia on a schedule", "Supplemental oxygen if hypoxic", "Cold compresses to painful joints", "Assessment for infection"], "ans": [0, 1, 2, 4], "why": "Crisis care is <b>hydration, oxygenation and real pain control</b> - these clients are chronically undertreated for pain, and scheduled dosing beats chasing it. Infection is a common trigger. <b>Cold causes vasoconstriction and worsens sickling</b>; warmth is applied instead.", "topic": "Hematologic Disorders", "icon": "🩸", "topicFull": "Hematologic Disorders", "card": "cardio/NG-344_sickle-cell-hemophilia.html", "cardLabel": "Sickle Cell & Hemophilia"}, {"q": "A client admitted with multiple myeloma has an elevated serum calcium level and reports worsening back pain. Which nursing action is the priority?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Restrict fluids to reduce the workload on the kidneys", "Apply heat to the painful areas of the spine", "Encourage a high fluid intake to protect the kidneys", "Have the client ambulate independently to strengthen the bones"], "ans": [2], "why": "Myeloma follows <b>CRAB</b> (calcium up, renal failure, anemia, bone lesions), and pushing fluids is what protects the kidneys from the elevated calcium. Restricting fluids is tempting once renal failure is mentioned, but it concentrates the calcium and worsens the kidney injury; bone lesions also call for fall and fracture precautions, not independent ambulation."}, {"q": "A client with hemophilia is bleeding briskly from a venipuncture site on the forearm. What does the nurse do first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Administer the prescribed clotting factor concentrate", "Hold firm compression over the site", "Apply a warm pack to the forearm", "Draw a CBC to check the platelet count"], "ans": [1], "why": "When the client is bleeding <b>right now</b>, compression beats a factor injection. Factor replacement is the definitive treatment and is given before and after procedures, but it does not stop active bleeding this minute; heat is wrong (use ice), and a CBC is the wrong lab because platelets are normal in hemophilia — coags are the priority labs."}, {"q": "A client admitted with pernicious anemia reports numbness and tingling in both feet and an unsteady gait. Which teaching point is most important for the nurse to reinforce?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Oral vitamin B12 taken with vitamin C will correct the deficiency", "A folate supplement will resolve the numbness and gait problems", "Vitamin B12 will be needed as lifelong intramuscular injections", "The symptoms will resolve once the red blood cell size normalizes"], "ans": [2], "why": "Pernicious anemia is a lack of <b>intrinsic factor</b>, so oral B12 cannot be absorbed and lifelong IM injections are required. Folate is the tempting choice because B12 and folate deficiency look identical on a blood count, but folate deficiency does not produce neurologic signs — the numbness and gait changes are what identify this as B12."}, {"q": "A client admitted with polycythemia vera has a ruddy face, splenomegaly, and reports intense itching after a warm shower. Which nursing action is the priority?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Institute bleeding precautions because of the high cell counts", "Prepare to transfuse a unit of packed red blood cells", "Administer subcutaneous epoetin alfa as prescribed", "Encourage fluids and ambulation to reduce the risk of clot formation"], "ans": [3], "why": "In polycythemia vera the marrow <b>overproduces</b> red cells, so the blood is thick and the nursing priority is clot prevention while therapeutic phlebotomy remains the mainstay of treatment. Transfusion and epoetin alfa treat anemia — the opposite problem — and this client's risk is clotting, not bleeding."}, {"q": "A client admitted with leukemia has a platelet count of 18,000/mm³. Which interventions does the nurse include in the plan of care? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Give ibuprofen as needed for bone pain", "Provide an electric razor for shaving", "Give scheduled medications by the intramuscular route", "Administer a stool softener to prevent straining", "Provide a soft toothbrush for oral care"], "ans": [1, 3, 4], "why": "Thrombocytopenia precautions are an electric razor, a soft toothbrush, stool softeners to prevent straining, and checking stool for occult blood; a count under 20,000 is a significant bleeding risk. <b>NSAIDs, aspirin, and heparin are avoided</b>, and injections and IV sticks are minimized rather than used routinely — ibuprofen looks reasonable for bone pain but it worsens bleeding."}, {"q": "A client admitted with iron-deficiency anemia is being discharged on oral ferrous sulfate. Which client statements indicate correct understanding of the teaching? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["I will take the iron with a glass of orange juice.", "I will stop the iron and call the clinic if my stools turn black.", "I will take it on an empty stomach unless it upsets my stomach.", "I will sip the liquid form slowly from a cup.", "I should expect my stools to turn black and tarry."], "ans": [0, 2, 4], "why": "Vitamin C doubles iron absorption, iron is best on an empty stomach (with food only if it upsets the gut), and <b>black tarry stools are expected</b>, not a sign of bleeding — so stopping the drug for that reason is wrong. Liquid iron stains the teeth and must be taken through a straw rather than sipped from a cup."}], "10": [{"q": "Which client on the oncology unit does the nurse see first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["A client with ANC 800 and a temperature of 100.8 F", "A client vomiting 3 hours after cisplatin", "A client with new petechiae on both forearms", "A client requesting the next dose of pain medication"], "ans": [0], "why": "<b>Neutropenic fever is the one situation where antibiotics are given without a proven infection</b> - cultures plus IV antibiotics, likely admission. ANC under 1,500 is neutropenic and under 500 is severe; with no neutrophils, a low-grade fever may be the only sign of overwhelming sepsis."}, {"q": "A vesicant chemotherapy agent is infusing peripherally when the site becomes swollen and the client reports burning. What does the nurse do first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Flush the line with saline to check patency", "Stop the infusion", "Remove the catheter and apply pressure", "Apply a warm compress to disperse the drug"], "ans": [1], "why": "<b>Stop the infusion first</b>, then notify the provider, give the antidote subcutaneously around the site and attempt to aspirate - do not remove the catheter and <b>do not flush</b>. Ice is correct because it localizes the drug; heat spreads it."}, {"q": "A client has a brachytherapy implant in place. Which action protects staff correctly?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Remain at least 6 feet away except when providing care, limit time in the room, and rotate caregivers", "Assign the same nurse each shift to maintain continuity", "Wear a shared dosimeter kept at the nurses' station", "Send soiled linens to the laundry immediately after each care episode"], "ans": [0], "why": "Brachytherapy safety is <b>distance of at least 6 feet, a 30-minute maximum exposure, rotating caregivers</b>, a personal dosimeter, a lead apron, and no pregnant staff assigned. Linens and dressings stay in the room, and continuity of assignment - normally a virtue - is exactly backwards here."}, {"q": "Which teaching points does the nurse give a neutropenic client? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Sushi from a reputable restaurant is acceptable", "Discard drinks left sitting out longer than 1 hour", "Avoid gardening and changing the cat litter", "Report any temperature over 100 F", "Fresh raw fruit is encouraged for vitamin intake"], "ans": [1, 2, 3], "why": "Neutropenic teaching means <b>no raw food regardless of the restaurant, discard drinks left out over 1 hour, avoid gardening and cat litter, and report a temp over 100 F</b>. Quality of the kitchen does not change the microbial load on raw fish."}, {"q": "A client receiving curative chemotherapy asks about palliative care. Which response is accurate?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Palliative care can begin now, alongside your treatment", "Palliative care starts only when treatment stops working", "Palliative care requires a prognosis of 6 months or less", "Palliative care and hospice are the same program"], "ans": [0], "why": "<b>Palliative care runs alongside curative treatment at any stage; hospice requires a terminal prognosis of about 6 months or less and a shift away from curative treatment.</b> Conflating the two is asked constantly."}, {"q": "Two days after starting chemotherapy for a bulky lymphoma, which lab pattern indicates tumor lysis syndrome?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Low potassium, low phosphorus, high calcium, low uric acid", "High potassium, high phosphorus, low calcium, high uric acid", "High potassium, low phosphorus, high calcium, normal uric acid", "Normal potassium, high phosphorus, high calcium, high uric acid"], "ans": [1], "why": "Lysed cells dump <b>potassium, phosphorus and uric acid up, with calcium down</b> as phosphate binds it, risking arrhythmias and AKI. Prevention and treatment are aggressive IV fluids plus allopurinol or rasburicase - methotrexate is chemotherapy, not a treatment."}, {"type": "sata", "week": "10", "step": "action", "topic": "Oncologic Disorders…", "q": "A nurse is caring for a client who has an absolute neutrophil count of 400/mm3 following chemotherapy. Which actions should the nurse take? (Select all that apply.)", "opts": ["Place the client in a private room", "Have the client wear a mask when leaving the room for transport", "Assign the client a roommate who is also immunosuppressed", "Perform hand hygiene before and after every client contact", "Take a rectal temperature to obtain the most accurate reading", "Report a temperature above 37.8° C (100° F) to the provider immediately"], "ans": [0, 1, 3, 5], "why": "Neutropenic precautions include a private room, a mask for transport, strict hand hygiene, and avoiding unnecessary invasive procedures, with any fever above 37.8° C (100° F) reported to the provider immediately. Assigning a roommate who is also immunosuppressed increases cross-infection risk rather than reducing it, and a rectal temperature is an invasive procedure that risks mucosal trauma and introducing infection in a neutropenic client, so it should be avoided.", "src": "ATI Adult Med-Surg ch 91 (Cancer Treatment Options)", "icon": "🎗️", "topicFull": "Oncologic Disorders & End-of-Life Care", "card": "core/NG-314_neutropenic-precautions-palliative.html", "cardLabel": "Neutropenic Precautions & Palliative Care"}, {"q": "A client receiving chemotherapy has an absolute neutrophil count of 400/mm³. Which instruction is most important?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Avoid crowds and anyone who is unwell", "Use a soft toothbrush and an electric razor", "Increase dietary iron", "Rest frequently throughout the day"], "ans": [0], "why": "An ANC under 500 is <b>severe neutropenia</b>, and infection is what kills these clients - a fever becomes an emergency. Soft toothbrush and electric razor are the <b>thrombocytopenia</b> precautions, and rest addresses anemia. Matching the precaution to the cell line that is low is the skill being tested.", "topic": "Oncologic Disorders…", "icon": "🎗️", "topicFull": "Oncologic Disorders & End-of-Life Care", "card": "core/NG-314_neutropenic-precautions-palliative.html", "cardLabel": "Neutropenic Precautions & Palliative Care"}, {"q": "Which statements about a client's advance directive are correct? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["It takes effect only when the client cannot make decisions", "The client may revoke it at any time", "A durable power of attorney names a decision maker", "It requires a provider's approval to be valid", "A DNR order means comfort care is withdrawn"], "ans": [0, 1, 2], "why": "An advance directive is the client's voice when they no longer have one, and it is <b>revocable at any time</b> by the client, not the provider. <b>A DNR is not 'do not treat'</b> - pain control, positioning, hygiene and dignity all continue, and that misunderstanding causes real suffering.", "topic": "Oncologic Disorders…", "icon": "🎗️", "topicFull": "Oncologic Disorders & End-of-Life Care", "card": "core/NG-314_neutropenic-precautions-palliative.html", "cardLabel": "Neutropenic Precautions & Palliative Care"}, {"q": "A client near the end of life develops noisy, gurgling respirations. Which nursing action is most appropriate?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Perform deep suctioning every hour", "Reposition to a side-lying position and give the prescribed antimuscarinic", "Increase IV fluids to thin the secretions", "Withhold all sedation so the client stays alert"], "ans": [1], "why": "So-called death rattle distresses the <b>family</b> more than the client. Repositioning plus an antimuscarinic such as glycopyrrolate reduces secretions. <b>Deep suctioning is traumatic and ineffective</b>, and extra fluid makes it worse.", "topic": "Oncologic Disorders…", "icon": "🎗️", "topicFull": "Oncologic Disorders & End-of-Life Care", "card": "core/NG-314_neutropenic-precautions-palliative.html", "cardLabel": "Neutropenic Precautions & Palliative Care"}, {"q": "A client with metastatic breast cancer reports new, persistent mid-back pain that started yesterday. She has full strength in both legs and is walking without difficulty. Which action should the nurse take first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Notify the provider immediately about the new back pain", "Give the ordered acetaminophen and reassess in an hour", "Document the pain and report it if leg weakness develops", "Apply a heating pad and place the client on bed rest"], "ans": [0], "why": "New back pain is the <b>first sign of spinal cord compression</b>, and it appears before any weakness, so reporting it now is what gets steroids and radiation started while she can still walk. Medicating and waiting for weakness is the tempting choice, but by the time weakness or bowel and bladder changes appear the paralysis may be permanent."}, {"q": "A client with lung cancer suddenly has swelling of the face, neck and upper chest with distended neck veins, and reports that his breathing is worse when he lies down. Which nursing action is the priority?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Raise the head of the bed", "Lay the client flat and elevate both legs", "Apply cool compresses to the face to reduce the swelling", "Restrict oral fluids and recheck the client in one hour"], "ans": [0], "why": "This is <b>superior vena cava syndrome</b>, and because it is worse lying flat, elevating the head of the bed is the immediate priority while radiation is arranged. Laying the client flat with the legs up looks like a reasonable positioning measure but increases upper-body congestion and makes the obstruction and dyspnea worse."}, {"q": "A hospice client with end-stage cancer rates his pain 9 out of 10 despite his scheduled morphine. His daughter asks the nurse not to give any more because it may slow his breathing. What is the nurse's best action?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Titrate the opioid to the client's comfort and explain there is no maximum dose at end of life", "Hold the dose until the client's respiratory rate is above 16", "Give acetaminophen instead so respiratory depression is avoided", "Follow the daughter's wishes and document that the family declined pain medication"], "ans": [0], "why": "At end of life there is <b>no maximum opioid dose</b> when it is titrated to comfort, and the risk of respiratory depression is not a reason to under-treat a dying client's pain. Holding the dose or switching to acetaminophen feels safer but leaves severe pain untreated, and the capable client, not the family, decides his own goals of care."}, {"q": "A nurse is giving discharge teaching to a client who has aplastic anemia. Which statement shows that the client understands the instructions?", "type": "single", "opts": ["“I need to stay active to prevent blood clots in my legs.”", "“If I have a bad headache, I can take aspirin for it.”", "“I should cut uncooked foods out of my diet for now.”", "“I should eat more iron-fortified cereal to build up my blood.”"], "ans": [2], "why": "Aplastic anemia leaves the client <b>neutropenic</b>, so food is eaten <b>thoroughly cooked</b> — raw fruit, vegetables, eggs, meat and fish can carry organisms that cooking destroys. Low platelets mean DVT is not the particular risk, and <b>aspirin is avoided</b> because it adds to bleeding. Iron-fortified food is the answer for iron deficiency, not for a marrow that has stopped producing."}, {"q": "A client receiving external beam radiation for breast cancer reports a metallic taste in the mouth. Which dietary suggestion should the nurse give?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Eat with metal utensils", "Limit coffee", "Avoid citrus foods", "Suck on mints or hard candy"], "ans": [3], "why": "<b>Mints or hard candy</b> mask the metallic taste that radiation and chemotherapy can cause. Tart and citrus foods help for the same reason, so avoiding them is backwards; <b>plastic utensils</b>, not metal, cut the taste; and a little coffee added to milk or sweet drinks takes the edge off foods that now taste too sweet."}, {"q": "A nurse is teaching a client who is having external radiation therapy. Which statement shows that the client understands the teaching?", "type": "single", "opts": ["“I should use petroleum-based lotion on the area being treated.”", "“I will dry the area by rubbing it in a circular pattern.”", "“I will put sunscreen on the treated area when I am outside.”", "“I will wash the treated area with my hand instead of a washcloth.”"], "ans": [3], "why": "Washing the radiated skin <b>with the hand</b> is gentler than a washcloth. Nothing goes on the field — no lotions, powders, ointments or creams — <b>unless the radiation team prescribes it</b>; the skin is <b>patted dry, never rubbed</b>; and during treatment the area is protected from sun by <b>clothing and shade, not sunscreen</b>."}, {"q": "A client is midway through a course of external beam radiation to the chest. Which statement by the client tells the nurse that more teaching is needed?", "type": "single", "opts": ["\"I dry the treated skin by rubbing it briskly with a towel.\"", "\"I wear a hat and stay out of the sun in the middle of the day.\"", "\"I do not put lotions or ointments on the area unless the provider orders them.\"", "\"My grandchildren can sit on my lap because I am not radioactive.\""], "ans": [0], "why": "<b>Skin protection is the priority</b> with external beam radiation, so treated skin is patted dry and never rubbed with a towel. The statement about the grandchildren sounds like a dangerous mistake, but it is correct teaching: external beam clients are not radioactive, and only a client with a brachytherapy source in place emits radiation."}, {"q": "A client is being discharged after placement of permanent brachytherapy seeds. Which instructions should the nurse include? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Sleep in a separate bedroom from your partner", "Flush the toilet several times after each use", "If you find a seed, pick it up with tongs and place it in the lead container", "You may stop straining your urine once you get home", "Share the family bathroom as you normally would"], "ans": [0, 1, 2], "why": "With the source in place the client is a radiation source, so home teaching is separate bedrooms and toilets, repeated flushing, and handling a dislodged seed only with <b>tongs into a lead container, never by hand</b>. Stopping the urine strain at discharge is the tempting error, since it must continue <b>7-10 days</b>, and sharing a bathroom repeatedly exposes the family."}, {"q": "A client receiving chemotherapy reports a sore, burning mouth and says he is eating very little because food hurts and tastes strange. Which interventions should the nurse include in the plan of care? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Rinse the mouth with normal saline or a sodium bicarbonate solution", "Apply the ordered topical anesthetic before meals", "Offer cold or room-temperature foods in small, frequent, nutrient-dense portions", "Rinse with an alcohol-based mouthwash after every meal", "Encourage citrus juices and salty broths to stimulate the appetite"], "ans": [0, 1, 2], "why": "Saline or bicarbonate rinses, a topical anesthetic before meals, and small frequent cold or room-temperature meals soothe inflamed mucosa and are the best tolerated. An <b>alcohol-based mouthwash</b> is the classic wrong answer because it dries and burns broken mucosa, and acidic or salty items are avoided for the same reason."}, {"q": "A nurse is planning care for a client who is undergoing brachytherapy with a sealed vaginal implant to treat endometrial cancer. Which action should the nurse include in the plan of care?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Wear a lead apron while providing care to the client", "Collect the client's urine and feces in a biohazard bag", "After the implant is removed, place the linens in a lead container in the client's room", "Limit each visitor to 1 hour per day"], "ans": [0], "why": "A lead apron is part of time, distance and shielding while the source is in. With a <b>sealed</b> implant the radiation stays inside the source, so urine and feces are not radioactive and need no special handling. Linens stay in the room only while the implant is in; once it is removed they are ordinary linens, and a lead container is for a dislodged source, picked up with tongs. Visitors are limited to <b>30 minutes</b> a day, at least 6 feet away, with no pregnant visitors or children.", "topic": "Oncologic Disorders & End-of-Life Care", "src": "Caroline's practice question, brachytherapy (27 Oct)", "icon": "🎗️", "topicFull": "Oncologic Disorders & End-of-Life Care"}, {"q": "A charge nurse is watching a newly licensed nurse give an IV medication through a client's implanted venous access port. Which observation means the charge nurse needs to step in?", "type": "single", "opts": ["No dressing is put over the port site after the needle is removed", "The port is accessed with a 22-gauge non-coring needle", "The nurse checks for a blood return before giving the medication", "The nurse has drawn up 5 mL of heparin 1,000 units/mL to lock the port"], "ans": [3], "why": "<b>Ports are locked with heparin 100 units/mL.</b> A 1,000 units/mL vial is ten times that strength, so this is a medication error to stop before it reaches the client. The rest is correct technique: a <b>non-coring (Huber) needle</b> such as 22-gauge protects the septum, a <b>blood return</b> is checked before anything is given, and once the needle is out a healed port needs <b>no dressing</b>.", "topic": "Oncologic Disorders & End-of-Life Care", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 practice question, implanted port (10 Nov), reworded", "icon": "🎗️", "topicFull": "Oncologic Disorders & End-of-Life Care", "card": "essentials/NG-007_iv-therapy-changes-cautions.html#implanted-port", "cardLabel": "Implanted port: access, flush, lock"}, {"q": "A client is recovering from a urinary diversion done to treat bladder cancer. Which intervention belongs in the plan of care?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Empty the collection pouch when it is two-thirds full", "Expect urine to start draining into the pouch 1 to 2 days after surgery", "Change the collection pouch early in the morning", "Put an aspirin tablet in the pouch to control odor"], "ans": [2], "why": "<b>Change the pouch first thing in the morning</b>, before the client drinks anything, when urine output is lowest and the skin stays dry long enough for the new wafer to seal. Empty the pouch when it is <b>one-third to one-half full</b>: a heavier pouch pulls the wafer loose. Urine drains <b>right away</b>, from the first hours after surgery, so no flow is reported. <b>Aspirin in the pouch can ulcerate the stoma</b>; odor is managed with an odor-proof pouch and good fluid intake.", "topic": "Oncologic Disorders & End-of-Life Care", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 practice question, urinary diversion (10 Nov), reworded", "icon": "🎗️", "topicFull": "Oncologic Disorders & End-of-Life Care", "card": "gi/NG-401_ostomy-care.html#care", "cardLabel": "Ostomy and urostomy care"}], "11": [{"q": "A client with continuous bladder irrigation after a TURP has bright red urine with clots. What does the nurse do first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Decrease the irrigation rate to reduce bladder pressure", "Increase the irrigation rate", "Stop the irrigation and notify the provider", "Deflate the catheter balloon to relieve spasms"], "ans": [1], "why": "Bright red urine with clots means <b>increase the irrigation rate</b>, aiming for a rose color - CBI does for the prostatic bed what fundal massage does after birth. Slowing or stopping it lets the catheter clot off, and then you are manually irrigating."}, {"q": "Six hours after prostate surgery a client has not voided since catheter removal and reports lower abdominal pressure. What does the nurse do first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Encourage 2 L of fluid over the next 2 hours", "Perform a bladder scan", "Insert a straight catheter", "Run warm water and assist him to stand"], "ans": [1], "why": "<b>Bladder scan first</b> - it is noninvasive and tells you whether there is urine to drain. Under 50 to 100 mL residual is normal, 100 to 300 is retention and over 300 is significant; pushing fluids before you know only adds volume to a bladder that cannot empty."}, {"q": "A 34-year-old client asks when to perform breast self-examination. Which instruction is correct?", "type": "single", "opts": ["On the first day of menses each month", "5 to 7 days after menses begins, monthly", "On the same calendar date each month regardless of cycle", "Only at the annual clinical breast exam"], "ans": [1], "why": "BSE is done monthly <b>5 to 7 days after menses starts</b>, when hormonal swelling has settled, and is easiest with soapy skin in the shower using the fingertips. Clinical exams run every 3 years from 20 to 39, then yearly at 40 with annual screening mammography."}, {"q": "On postoperative day 3 after an abdominal hysterectomy the client's temperature has gone 98.6, 99.5, then 101.2 F, the incision is now red and foul-smelling, and pads are saturating every 2 hours. Which actions does the nurse take? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Obtain vital signs and strict intake and output", "Apply warm compresses to the incision", "Administer the prescribed IV antibiotic", "Notify the provider", "Start oral iron supplementation"], "ans": [0, 2, 3], "why": "A rising temperature, a deteriorating incision and accelerating bleeding call for <b>vitals and I&O, IV antibiotics and provider notification</b>. Ambulation and warm compresses are wrong here, and active significant bleeding needs transfusion, not oral iron, which takes weeks."}, {"q": "A nurse is calculating intake and output for a client with continuous bladder irrigation. Which is correct?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Count the irrigant as intake and all drainage as output", "Subtract the irrigant volume so it is not counted in intake or output", "Count only half the irrigant as intake", "Count the irrigant as output only"], "ans": [1], "why": "<b>The irrigant is not counted in I&O</b> - it goes in and comes right back out, so including it invents both intake and urine that never existed. Subtract the irrigation volume from total drainage to get true urine output."}, {"q": "A client taking sildenafil for erectile dysfunction should avoid which medication?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Acetaminophen", "Amoxicillin", "Nitroglycerin", "Ibuprofen"], "ans": [2], "why": "PDE5 inhibitors - recognize the <b>-afil</b> ending - are contraindicated with <b>nitrates</b> such as nitroglycerin and amyl nitrate, and cause additive hypotension with doxazosin. Acetaminophen and amoxicillin were specifically named as safe."}, {"q": "A client is being discharged after a transurethral resection of the prostate with continuous bladder irrigation. Which finding requires immediate action?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Pink-tinged urine", "Bladder spasms relieved by the prescribed medication", "No urine output for one hour with lower abdominal distension", "Small clots in the drainage on the first day"], "ans": [2], "why": "No output with a distended abdomen means the catheter is <b>obstructed by clot</b>, and the bladder is filling behind it - this needs irrigation now. Pink urine, spasms and small early clots are all expected. <b>Bright red bleeding with large clots</b> would be the other finding to report.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "renal/NG-052_urinary-retention-bph.html", "cardLabel": "Urinary Retention & BPH"}, {"q": "Which teaching points are correct for a client after a mastectomy with axillary node dissection? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["No blood pressures or venipunctures in the affected arm", "Report redness, warmth or swelling of the arm", "Keep the arm below heart level", "Wear a glove when gardening", "Begin prescribed arm exercises as directed"], "ans": [0, 1, 3, 4], "why": "Everything protects an arm that can no longer drain lymph: <b>no cuffs, no needles, no cuts</b>, and any break in the skin gets reported. The arm is <b>elevated</b>, not kept low, because dependency worsens lymphedema.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "skin/NG-318_mastectomy.html", "cardLabel": "Mastectomy & Breast Surgery"}, {"q": "A 17-year-old client arrives in the emergency department with sudden severe left scrotal pain, swelling, and nausea, and the left testicle appears high-riding. Symptoms began 2 hours ago. Which action should the nurse take first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Notify the provider immediately and prepare the client for surgical evaluation", "Apply ice, elevate the scrotum, and reassess the client in a few hours", "Teach the client to perform a monthly testicular self-exam after a warm shower", "Collect a urine specimen and anticipate treatment for an infection"], "ans": [0], "why": "Sudden severe unilateral pain with swelling, nausea and a high-riding testicle points to <b>testicular torsion</b>, a true surgical emergency with roughly a <b>6-hour window</b> to save the testicle. Waiting and reassessing is the tempting choice but torsion is never a watch-and-see problem, and self-exam teaching or urine testing only delays the surgery that saves the organ."}, {"q": "A 62-year-old client who completed menopause 10 years ago reports a week of vaginal spotting. She has been taking tamoxifen for 3 years. Which response by the nurse is most appropriate?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Report the bleeding to the provider today, because bleeding after menopause is never normal", "Explain that occasional light spotting is common in the years after menopause", "Reassure her that spotting is an expected side effect of tamoxifen and needs no follow-up", "Arrange a CA-125 blood test to screen her for a reproductive cancer"], "ans": [0], "why": "<b>Postmenopausal bleeding is always abnormal and always referred</b> — it is exactly why endometrial cancer tends to be caught earlier than ovarian cancer. Tamoxifen is a reason for higher suspicion, not reassurance, because it raises endometrial cancer risk by stimulating uterine estrogen receptors, and CA-125 tracks known disease and is <b>not a screening test</b>."}, {"q": "A client with benign prostatic hyperplasia is started on tamsulosin and tells the nurse he already gets up two or three times each night to urinate. Which instruction is the priority?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Sit on the side of the bed for a moment before standing when you get up at night", "Expect this medication to shrink your prostate within about a week", "Take an over-the-counter antihistamine at bedtime to reduce nighttime trips", "Stop taking the medication as soon as your urine stream improves"], "ans": [0], "why": "Tamsulosin relaxes the bladder outlet and causes <b>orthostatic hypotension</b>, which is most dangerous during nighttime voiding, so fall prevention is the priority teaching. Shrinking the prostate is finasteride's effect and takes up to 6 months, and <b>antihistamines worsen retention in BPH</b> rather than helping."}, {"q": "A nurse is caring for a client on the first day after an abdominal hysterectomy. Which finding should the nurse report immediately?", "type": "single", "opts": ["The client has saturated one perineal pad in the past 4 hours", "The indwelling urinary catheter is still in place 18 hours after surgery", "The client is wearing anti-embolism stockings that were applied before surgery", "The client reports cramping abdominal gas pain"], "ans": [0], "why": "The priority postoperative concern after hysterectomy is <b>hemorrhage</b>, and <b>one pad saturated in 4 hours</b> is the stated definition of excessive bleeding. The catheter is the tempting answer, but the Foley is expected to stay in for <b>24 hours</b>, so a catheter still in place at 18 hours is routine, not a reason to call."}, {"q": "A 22-year-old client reports vaginal discharge and pelvic pain that has come and gone for several months and asks to be tested for a sexually transmitted infection. Which nursing actions are appropriate? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Ask how long the symptoms have been going on", "Obtain a cervical swab for testing", "Explain that her sexual partners also need to be treated", "Send a blood sample as the primary test to identify the infection", "Collect a clean-catch urine specimen in place of a cervical swab"], "ans": [0, 1, 2], "why": "The workup begins by <b>asking duration</b>, since months of symptoms suggests PID, and the diagnostic specimen is a <b>cervical swab</b>, with partners treated as well. Blood and urine feel like the familiar answers but are not the specimen used for this workup, and a pelvic ultrasound is added only when PID is suspected."}, {"q": "A 41-year-old client comes in for a routine women's health visit and asks what screening she should be having now. Which statements by the nurse are correct? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Avoid douching, vaginal medications and intercourse for 24 hours before your Pap test", "Pap testing is done every 3 years routinely, and more often only if something indicates it", "Now that you are past 40, you should have a screening mammogram every year", "At your age a clinical breast exam is needed only once every 3 years", "A CA-125 blood test should be added to screen you for ovarian cancer"], "ans": [0, 1, 2], "why": "Routine Pap screening is <b>every 3 years</b> with no douching, vaginal medications or intercourse for <b>24 hours</b> beforehand, and <b>screening mammography is yearly starting at age 40</b>. The every-3-year clinical breast exam is the trap: that interval applies to ages 20-39 and becomes yearly at 40 and older, and CA-125 tracks known disease rather than screening for it."}, {"q": "A nurse is caring for a client who has continuous bladder irrigation after a transurethral resection of the prostate (TURP). Which drainage appearance tells the nurse the irrigation is doing its job?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Dark red drainage that looks like ketchup", "Bright red drainage with several large clots", "Light pink drainage that looks like pink lemonade", "Brown drainage with thick sediment"], "ans": [2], "why": "TURP scrapes away excess prostate tissue, so a little blood is expected. Her card's rule: <b>pink lemonade is good, ketchup is not</b>. Light pink means some blood is tinging the drainage and nothing more. The nurse keeps watching bleeding, <b>catheter patency</b>, irrigation in against output, and bladder distension.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-benign-prostatic-hyperplasia", "cardLabel": "BPH, TURP and bladder irrigation"}, {"q": "A client returns from a TURP with a 3-way catheter and continuous bladder irrigation (CBI). Which nursing actions are correct? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Run the irrigation solution through an IV pump for a steady rate", "Adjust the flow rate to keep the output pink or lighter", "For an obstruction, turn off the CBI and hand irrigate with a 50 mL syringe of irrigation solution", "Slow the irrigation when the drainage turns bright red", "Record intake and output", "Expect that the catheter may be taped tightly to the leg for traction to prevent bleeding"], "ans": [1, 2, 4, 5], "why": "The rate is adjusted to keep drainage <b>pink or lighter</b>, and CBI is <b>never run on an IV pump</b>. Bright red drainage means increase the flow so the clots come out, not slow it. A blocked catheter is handled by turning off the CBI and hand irrigating with a 50 mL syringe. Intake and output are recorded, and traction taping helps prevent bleeding.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-benign-prostatic-hyperplasia", "cardLabel": "BPH, TURP and bladder irrigation"}, {"q": "A client with a 3-way catheter after a TURP says he constantly feels the urge to urinate. The catheter is draining light pink fluid freely. Which response by the nurse is appropriate?", "type": "single", "opts": ["That urge means the catheter is blocked, so I will take it out now.", "Bear down gently and try to pass urine around the catheter to ease the pressure.", "I will deflate the balloon a little so the urge goes away.", "That urge is expected because the catheter and its balloon are large. Do not try to void around it."], "ans": [3], "why": "A continuous urge to urinate is <b>normal</b> with this catheter: it is very large and has a 30 to 40 mL balloon. The client is taught <b>not to void around the catheter</b>. With drainage running freely and light pink, nothing here points to a blockage, and the balloon stays inflated.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-benign-prostatic-hyperplasia", "cardLabel": "BPH, TURP and bladder irrigation"}, {"q": "A nurse is reviewing breast disorders with a student. Which of the following are forms of breast cancer? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Ductal carcinoma in situ", "Fibrocystic breast changes", "Invasive ductal carcinoma", "Mastitis", "Inflammatory breast cancer"], "ans": [0, 2, 4], "why": "The card lists three forms of breast cancer: <b>ductal carcinoma in situ</b>, <b>invasive ductal cancer</b> and <b>inflammatory breast cancer</b>. Fibrocystic breast changes are benign and driven by hormone fluctuations, and mastitis is a breast infection, not a cancer.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-breast-cancer", "cardLabel": "Breast cancer"}, {"q": "A 32-year-old client reports breast tenderness that gets worse before each period and several mobile, tender lumps that change with her menstrual cycle. Which condition does the nurse suspect?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Inflammatory breast cancer", "Fibrocystic breast changes", "Invasive ductal carcinoma", "Ductal carcinoma in situ"], "ans": [1], "why": "<b>Fibrocystic breast changes</b> are benign and caused by hormonal fluctuations. Expected findings are tenderness that worsens before menstruation, <b>multiple mobile, sometimes tender lumps</b>, and changes that follow the cycle. Ultrasound may show fluid-filled cysts. The cancers listed do not come and go with the cycle.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#review-258-m11-fibrocystic", "cardLabel": "Fibrocystic breast changes"}, {"q": "A client with known fibrocystic breast changes finds a new lump that has not gone away and feels different from her usual lumps. Which response by the nurse is correct?", "type": "single", "opts": ["A new, persistent lump still needs to be evaluated, even with fibrocystic changes.", "Fibrocystic lumps are always benign, so no follow-up is needed.", "Wait six months and see whether it shrinks without treatment.", "Stop checking your breasts, because fibrocystic tissue makes lumps meaningless."], "ans": [0], "why": "Fibrocystic changes are benign and fluctuate with the cycle, but her card adds the warning that a <b>new, persistent, suspicious breast mass must still be evaluated</b>. Having fibrocystic breasts does not rule out a separate problem, so waiting or dismissing the lump is unsafe.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#review-258-m11-fibrocystic", "cardLabel": "Fibrocystic breast changes"}, {"q": "Which statement about who develops fibrocystic breast changes is correct?", "type": "single", "opts": ["They appear mainly in men over 60.", "They occur only after menopause in women who have never taken hormones.", "They occur mostly in premenopausal women, often young adults, and can appear when a postmenopausal woman starts hormone therapy.", "They develop only during pregnancy and breastfeeding."], "ans": [2], "why": "Fibrocystic breast disease occurs in <b>premenopausal women, often young adults</b>, because it follows hormone fluctuations. It can also develop when a <b>postmenopausal woman starts hormone replacement therapy</b>.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#review-258-m11-fibrocystic", "cardLabel": "Fibrocystic breast changes"}, {"q": "A client with fibrocystic breast changes is having a fine-needle aspiration of a tender cyst. Which teaching by the nurse is correct?", "type": "single", "opts": ["The aspiration removes the cyst permanently.", "The aspiration is done to keep the cyst from turning into cancer.", "The aspiration will stop new cysts from forming in either breast.", "The aspiration can ease the tender cyst, but cysts refill, so it does not get rid of them."], "ans": [3], "why": "<b>Fine-needle aspiration</b> can be done to reduce tender cysts, but her card notes they <b>will refill</b>, so the procedure relieves the tenderness without getting rid of the condition. It is not a cure and it does not stop new cysts.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#review-258-m11-fibrocystic", "cardLabel": "Fibrocystic breast changes"}, {"q": "Which is the most common female reproductive cancer, and when is it most often diagnosed?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Cervical cancer, during the teenage years", "Endometrial cancer, after menopause", "Ovarian cancer, before the first pregnancy", "Vulvar cancer, during pregnancy"], "ans": [1], "why": "Her card names <b>endometrial cancer</b> as the most common female reproductive cancer, and it is usually <b>diagnosed after menopause</b>. Risk rises with cumulative estrogen exposure, obesity, estrogen replacement, nulliparity, tamoxifen use and early menarche.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-cervical-ovarian-and-endometrial-cancer", "cardLabel": "Cervical, ovarian and endometrial cancer"}, {"q": "A nurse is reviewing a client's history for endometrial cancer risk. Which findings are risk factors? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Obesity", "Several full-term pregnancies", "Never having given birth (nulliparity)", "Use of tamoxifen", "Late onset of the first period", "Early menarche"], "ans": [0, 2, 3, 5], "why": "The common thread is <b>cumulative exposure to estrogen</b>. Obesity, estrogen replacement, <b>nulliparity</b>, tamoxifen use and <b>early menarche</b> are the risk factors on her card. Having several pregnancies and starting periods late are the opposite of two items on that list, so they are not risks.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-cervical-ovarian-and-endometrial-cancer", "cardLabel": "Cervical, ovarian and endometrial cancer"}, {"q": "A nurse is explaining how polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS) is diagnosed. Which statement is correct?", "type": "single", "opts": ["All three criteria must be present: hyperandrogenism, chronic anovulation and polycystic ovaries on ultrasound.", "Polycystic ovaries on ultrasound are enough to make the diagnosis without any other finding.", "Two of three criteria are needed: hyperandrogenism, chronic anovulation, or polycystic ovaries on ultrasound.", "An elevated CA-125 with irregular cycles confirms the diagnosis."], "ans": [2], "why": "PCOS is diagnosed when <b>two of the three</b> criteria are present: <b>hyperandrogenism</b> (such as increased facial hair), <b>chronic anovulation</b> (irregular cycles), and polycystic ovaries on ultrasound. Not all three are required, and one finding alone is not enough.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#review-258-m11-pcos", "cardLabel": "PCOS"}, {"q": "The symptoms of polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS), such as acne, excess body hair and weight gain, come from which two underlying problems?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Low testosterone and high estrogen", "High testosterone and insulin resistance", "Low thyroid hormone and high cortisol", "High progesterone and too little insulin"], "ans": [1], "why": "PCOS symptoms trace back to <b>high testosterone and insulin resistance</b>. The picture includes acne, male-pattern baldness and excess body hair, along with mood changes, fatigue, thirst, weight gain, low sex drive and trouble conceiving.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#review-258-m11-pcos", "cardLabel": "PCOS"}, {"q": "A client with polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS) asks why metformin was prescribed. Which response by the nurse is correct?", "type": "single", "opts": ["It helps regulate your periods and can help with weight loss.", "It replaces the estrogen your ovaries are not making.", "It treats the infection that causes the ovarian cysts.", "It thins the blood to prevent clots while you take hormones."], "ans": [0], "why": "In PCOS, <b>metformin</b> is used to <b>regulate periods and help with weight loss</b>. It sits alongside low-glycemic-index eating, spironolactone for hirsutism, infertility treatment, and surgery to remove ovarian cysts. It is not hormone replacement, an antibiotic or an anticoagulant.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#review-258-m11-pcos", "cardLabel": "PCOS"}, {"q": "A client with PCOS is bothered by excess facial hair. Which medication does the nurse expect to be prescribed to reduce hirsutism?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Tamoxifen", "Nafarelin", "Sildenafil", "Spironolactone"], "ans": [3], "why": "<b>Spironolactone</b> is the PCOS medication that <b>reduces hirsutism</b> (facial hair). Tamoxifen is for estrogen receptor-positive breast cancer, nafarelin is a GnRH agonist for endometriosis, and sildenafil is an erectile dysfunction drug.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#review-258-m11-pcos", "cardLabel": "PCOS"}, {"q": "A client being evaluated for infertility is scheduled for a hysterosalpingogram. What does this test show?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Hormone levels across the menstrual cycle", "Abnormal cervical cells and precancerous changes", "The cervix, uterus and fallopian tubes, including any blockage in the tubes, using contrast dye and x-rays", "Whether a breast lump is fluid-filled or solid, using sound waves"], "ans": [2], "why": "A <b>hysterosalpingogram</b> uses <b>contrast dye and x-rays</b> to visualize the cervix, uterus and fallopian tubes. It is used to evaluate infertility, fibroids or tumors and to find a <b>blockage in the fallopian tubes</b>. Abnormal cervical cells are found by a Pap test.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#review-258-m11-hsg", "cardLabel": "Hysterosalpingogram"}, {"q": "A nurse is preparing a client for a hysterosalpingogram. Which question is most important to ask before the test?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Do you have a pacemaker or other metal implants?", "Are you allergic to iodine or shellfish?", "Have you had anything to eat in the last 12 hours?", "Have you ever had a reaction to a blood transfusion?"], "ans": [1], "why": "The test uses <b>contrast dye</b>, so the card flags <b>iodine and shellfish allergies</b> as the thing to check before the procedure. The other questions belong to MRI, anesthesia or transfusion screening, not to this dye study.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#review-258-m11-hsg", "cardLabel": "Hysterosalpingogram"}, {"q": "A 26-year-old client with a breast lump asks why an ultrasound was ordered instead of a mammogram. Which explanation by the nurse is correct?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Younger breast tissue is dense, and dense tissue shows more clearly on ultrasound than on a mammogram.", "Ultrasound is the only test that can prove a lump is cancer.", "Mammograms are not allowed before age 40.", "Ultrasound removes the lump while it takes the picture."], "ans": [0], "why": "<b>Dense breast tissue</b>, which is typical in younger clients, does not image as well on a mammogram. An <b>ultrasound gives a clearer picture</b>. It is an imaging choice, not a biopsy or a treatment.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-breast-cancer", "cardLabel": "Breast cancer"}, {"q": "A postmenopausal client is diagnosed with a cystocele. Which change best explains why the anterior vaginal wall weakened and let the bladder drop?", "type": "single", "opts": ["High progesterone thickening the vaginal wall", "Low estrogen weakening the muscles of the vaginal wall", "High testosterone enlarging the bladder", "Low insulin causing nerve damage in the pelvis"], "ans": [1], "why": "In a cystocele, <b>low estrogen</b> after menopause leads to vaginal wall atrophy and muscle weakening. The <b>anterior wall</b> gives way and the bladder falls into that space, causing stress incontinence, painful intercourse and pelvic pain. In a rectocele the posterior wall weakens and the rectum falls into the vaginal space.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#review-258-m11-prolapse", "cardLabel": "Cystocele and rectocele"}, {"q": "A client with a cystocele is being fitted for a vaginal pessary. Which description of the device is correct?", "type": "single", "opts": ["A medicated ring that releases estrogen to shrink the bladder", "A surgical mesh stitched into the posterior vaginal wall", "A catheter that drains the bladder continuously", "A device placed in the vaginal opening to keep organs from prolapsing into the canal"], "ans": [3], "why": "A <b>vaginal pessary</b> is a device inserted into the vaginal opening to <b>prevent things from prolapsing into the canal</b>. Other treatments on the card are vaginal estrogen to tone the vaginal wall muscles, bladder training for stress incontinence, Kegels, and surgical repair.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#review-258-m11-prolapse", "cardLabel": "Cystocele and rectocele"}, {"q": "Which pairing of a pelvic organ prolapse with its surgical repair is correct?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Cystocele: posterior colporrhaphy", "Rectocele: anterior colporrhaphy", "Cystocele: anterior colporrhaphy", "Rectocele: myomectomy"], "ans": [2], "why": "A cystocele involves the <b>anterior</b> vaginal wall, so it is repaired with an <b>anterior colporrhaphy</b>. A rectocele involves the posterior wall and is repaired with a posterior colporrhaphy. A myomectomy removes fibroids and has nothing to do with prolapse.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#review-258-m11-prolapse", "cardLabel": "Cystocele and rectocele"}, {"q": "A nurse is teaching a community group about breast cancer. Which are risk factors? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Increasing age", "BRCA1 or BRCA2 pathogenic variant", "Late first period with early menopause", "Previous radiation to the chest", "Alcohol use", "Regular physical activity"], "ans": [0, 1, 3, 4], "why": "Risk factors on her card are <b>increasing age</b>, <b>BRCA1 or BRCA2</b> variants, family or personal history, previous chest radiation, <b>longer lifetime estrogen exposure</b>, obesity after menopause and alcohol use. A late first period with early menopause means shorter estrogen exposure, and exercise is not a risk factor.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-breast-cancer", "cardLabel": "Breast cancer"}, {"q": "A client diagnosed with ductal carcinoma in situ (DCIS) asks what that means. Which explanation by the nurse is correct?", "type": "single", "opts": ["The cancer has spread to the lymph nodes under the arm.", "The malignant cells are confined within the milk ducts and have not invaded through the basement membrane.", "It is a benign cyst caused by hormone changes.", "The cancer has reached the chest muscles and needs a radical mastectomy."], "ans": [1], "why": "In <b>DCIS</b> the malignant cells stay <b>confined within the milk ducts</b> with no invasion through the basement membrane. It is <b>stage 0</b> and does not metastasize while truly noninvasive. It is often treated with breast-conserving surgery and sometimes radiation.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-breast-cancer", "cardLabel": "Breast cancer"}, {"q": "Which assessment finding is typical of invasive ductal carcinoma?", "type": "single", "opts": ["A firm, irregular, often painless mass", "Soft, mobile, tender lumps that change with the menstrual cycle", "A fluid-filled cyst that disappears after the period ends", "Tender swelling in both breasts that begins a few days after giving birth"], "ans": [0], "why": "In <b>invasive ductal carcinoma</b> the cancer extends beyond the duct into surrounding tissue. It may present as a <b>firm, irregular, often painless mass</b> and can spread to lymph nodes and distant organs. Lumps that are mobile, tender and change with the cycle point to fibrocystic changes.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-breast-cancer", "cardLabel": "Breast cancer"}, {"q": "A client reports that one breast became swollen, warm and red over a short time. The skin looks dimpled like an orange peel, and no lump can be felt. Which condition does the nurse suspect?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Fibrocystic breast changes", "Ductal carcinoma in situ", "Lymphedema", "Inflammatory breast cancer"], "ans": [3], "why": "<b>Inflammatory breast cancer</b> is aggressive, with rapid swelling, warmth and erythema and a <b>peau d'orange</b> (orange-peel) skin appearance. It <b>may occur without a distinct palpable lump</b>, so the absence of a mass is not reassuring. It requires prompt diagnostic evaluation.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-breast-cancer", "cardLabel": "Breast cancer"}, {"q": "A client is scheduled for surgery that removes the breast tissue and axillary lymph nodes but preserves the chest muscles. Which procedure is this?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Lumpectomy", "Total (simple) mastectomy", "Modified radical mastectomy", "Radical mastectomy"], "ans": [2], "why": "A <b>modified radical mastectomy</b> removes the breast tissue and axillary lymph nodes and <b>preserves the chest muscles</b>. A total (simple) mastectomy generally leaves out routine axillary node dissection, and a radical mastectomy also takes the pectoral muscles and is rarely performed today.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-breast-cancer", "cardLabel": "Breast cancer"}, {"q": "A client asks what a lumpectomy involves. Which description is accurate?", "type": "single", "opts": ["The breast, axillary nodes and pectoral muscles are removed.", "All of the breast tissue is removed, generally without routine axillary node dissection.", "The breast tissue and axillary lymph nodes are removed, and the chest muscles are preserved.", "The tumor is removed with a margin of surrounding tissue."], "ans": [3], "why": "A <b>lumpectomy</b> removes the tumor with a <b>margin of surrounding tissue</b>. The other descriptions are the radical mastectomy (breast, nodes and pectoral muscles), the total or simple mastectomy (breast tissue), and the modified radical mastectomy (breast tissue and axillary nodes).", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-breast-cancer", "cardLabel": "Breast cancer"}, {"q": "A client with prostate cancer reports new bone pain. What does this finding suggest to the nurse?", "type": "single", "opts": ["An expected effect of benign prostatic hyperplasia", "Metastasis", "Acute bacterial prostatitis", "A urinary obstruction from an enlarged prostate"], "ans": [1], "why": "On her card, <b>bone pain suggests metastasis</b>. Early prostate cancer may have no symptoms at all, and BPH usually causes <b>gradual urinary obstruction</b> rather than bone pain.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-prostate-cancer", "cardLabel": "Prostate cancer"}, {"q": "A client with benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH) is worried about prostate cancer. Which statement by the client shows correct understanding?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Since I have BPH, I already have cancer.", "Prostate cancer always causes pain early, so I would know.", "Having BPH does not automatically mean I have cancer, and early prostate cancer may cause no symptoms.", "BPH comes on suddenly, so my slow urinary changes must be cancer."], "ans": [2], "why": "<b>BPH does not automatically mean cancer</b>; it usually causes gradual urinary obstruction. <b>Early prostate cancer may have no symptoms</b>, which is why absence of pain proves nothing. Bone pain is a later sign that suggests metastasis.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-prostate-cancer", "cardLabel": "Prostate cancer"}, {"q": "A client asks when HPV testing for cervical cancer screening should begin and how often it is done along with a Pap test. Which answer is correct?", "type": "single", "opts": ["At age 25, every 5 years", "At age 16, every year", "At age 40, every 10 years", "At age 50, every 2 years"], "ans": [0], "why": "Her card: <b>HPV testing starts at 25</b>, done along with a Pap <b>every 5 years</b>. If only a Pap test is done, it is repeated every 3 years. Screening stops around 65 when the last 10 years were clear and there was no serious diagnosis in the last 25 years.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-cervical-ovarian-and-endometrial-cancer", "cardLabel": "Cervical, ovarian and endometrial cancer"}, {"q": "A 66-year-old client asks whether she can stop cervical cancer screening. Which answer by the nurse is correct?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Screening continues every year for life.", "Screening stops at menopause for everyone.", "It can stop around 65 if the last 10 years were clear, with no serious diagnosis in the last 25 years.", "It stops as soon as a client has had the HPV vaccine."], "ans": [2], "why": "Cervical screening <b>stops around age 65</b> only when the <b>last 10 years were clear</b> and there has been no serious diagnosis in the last 25 years. Before that, HPV testing with a Pap is done every 5 years, or a Pap alone every 3 years.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-cervical-ovarian-and-endometrial-cancer", "cardLabel": "Cervical, ovarian and endometrial cancer"}, {"q": "A nurse is preparing a client for a Pap test. Which instructions are correct? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Empty your bladder before the test.", "Schedule the test for the heaviest day of your period.", "Do not douche, use vaginal medications or have intercourse before the test.", "You will be positioned in lithotomy, with your legs in stirrups.", "Fast for 8 hours before the test.", "You may have spotting afterward, so a pad may be needed, and some cramping can occur."], "ans": [0, 2, 3, 5], "why": "Before a Pap test the client <b>empties her bladder</b> and avoids <b>douching, vaginal medications and intercourse</b>. The test is done in lithotomy position and is timed after the period, not during it. Afterward, pads or tissues are offered because <b>spotting</b> and cramping may occur. No fasting is needed.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-cervical-ovarian-and-endometrial-cancer", "cardLabel": "Cervical, ovarian and endometrial cancer"}, {"q": "A client is going home after breast surgery with axillary lymph node removal. Which statement by the client shows understanding of lymphedema prevention?", "type": "single", "opts": ["I will keep that arm completely still for the next six weeks.", "Blood pressures should be taken on my surgical arm so swelling is caught early.", "I will watch for swelling or heaviness in that arm or hand and protect the skin from injury.", "Skin changes on that arm are expected and do not need to be reported."], "ans": [2], "why": "The client monitors for <b>arm or hand swelling, heaviness and skin changes</b>, protects the skin from injury and infection, and does gradual prescribed range-of-motion exercises. Blood pressures, injections and venipuncture are <b>avoided in the affected arm</b> when feasible. Current evidence supports individualized precautions rather than an absolute lifelong ban.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-breast-cancer", "cardLabel": "Breast cancer"}, {"q": "True or false: Postmenopausal bleeding is abnormal until it has been evaluated.", "type": "single", "opts": ["True", "False"], "ans": [0], "why": "<b>True.</b> Any bleeding after menopause is treated as abnormal until it has been evaluated, because it <b>could be endometrial cancer</b>. It is not assumed to be a harmless return of periods.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-cervical-ovarian-and-endometrial-cancer", "cardLabel": "Cervical, ovarian and endometrial cancer"}, {"q": "A client has had weeks of vague bloating, feeling full after a few bites, pelvic discomfort and urinary frequency. Which cancer do these findings most suggest?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Cervical cancer", "Endometrial cancer", "Ovarian cancer", "Breast cancer"], "ans": [2], "why": "<b>Ovarian cancer</b> often presents with vague symptoms: <b>bloating, early satiety, pelvic discomfort and urinary frequency</b>. Endometrial cancer typically shows as postmenopausal bleeding, and cervical cancer is often silent early, then causes postcoital bleeding or abnormal discharge.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-cervical-ovarian-and-endometrial-cancer", "cardLabel": "Cervical, ovarian and endometrial cancer"}, {"q": "A client with a positive pregnancy test reports one-sided pelvic pain, vaginal bleeding, shoulder pain and a fainting episode. Which complication needs urgent evaluation?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Ectopic pregnancy", "Pelvic inflammatory disease", "Uterine fibroids", "Endometriosis"], "ans": [0], "why": "Pregnancy with <b>unilateral pelvic pain, vaginal bleeding, syncope or shoulder pain</b> requires urgent evaluation for <b>ectopic pregnancy</b>. The other conditions are not the urgent concern when these findings appear together in a pregnant client.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "more/NG-326_ectopic-molar-pregnancy.html", "cardLabel": "Ectopic and molar pregnancy"}, {"q": "A client is being treated for pelvic inflammatory disease (PID), with cervical motion tenderness, pelvic pain and fever. Which complications should the nurse include in teaching?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Uterine fibroids and heavy periods", "Early menopause and bone loss", "Cystocele and stress incontinence", "Infertility and ectopic pregnancy"], "ans": [3], "why": "PID shows as <b>cervical motion tenderness, pelvic pain and fever</b>. Its complications include <b>infertility and ectopic pregnancy</b>. Fibroids, early menopause and prolapse are not consequences of PID.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-sexually-transmitted-infections", "cardLabel": "Sexually transmitted infections"}, {"q": "A 16-year-old reports sudden, severe pain in one testicle. Which condition does the nurse suspect, and how urgent is it?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Epididymitis, which can wait for a routine clinic visit", "Testicular torsion, a surgical emergency", "Benign prostatic hyperplasia, managed with medication", "Testicular cancer, evaluated at the next annual exam"], "ans": [1], "why": "Her card: <b>sudden, severe unilateral testicular pain</b> is testicular torsion, and this is a <b>surgical emergency</b>. It is never left for a routine visit, and the other conditions listed do not carry this urgency.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-testicular-cancer", "cardLabel": "Testicular cancer"}, {"q": "A client is diagnosed with acute bacterial prostatitis. Which action should be avoided?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Collecting a urine specimen", "Giving the prescribed antibiotic", "Prostatic massage", "Encouraging fluids"], "ans": [2], "why": "Prostatitis causes fever, dysuria, perineal pain and a tender, boggy prostate. In <b>acute bacterial prostatitis</b> the card's rule is to <b>avoid prostatic massage</b>. Urine testing, antibiotics and fluids are ordinary care and are not what the card warns against.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#review-258-m11-prostatitis", "cardLabel": "Prostatitis"}, {"q": "Which findings does the nurse expect in a client with prostatitis? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Fever", "A painless lump in one testicle", "Dysuria", "Perineal pain", "A tender, boggy prostate", "A hard, nontender, nodular prostate"], "ans": [0, 2, 3, 4], "why": "Prostatitis can cause <b>fever, dysuria, perineal pain</b> and a <b>tender, boggy prostate</b>. A painless testicular lump and a hard, nontender, nodular prostate are not part of that picture; they point toward a mass, not an inflamed gland.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#review-258-m11-prostatitis", "cardLabel": "Prostatitis"}, {"q": "A nurse is explaining cervical cancer screening. Which test detects abnormal cervical cells and precancerous changes?", "type": "single", "opts": ["CA-125 blood test", "Hysterosalpingogram", "Endometrial biopsy", "Pap test"], "ans": [3], "why": "The <b>Pap test</b> detects <b>abnormal cervical cells and precancerous changes</b>. CA-125 relates to ovarian cancer, a hysterosalpingogram looks at the uterus and tubes for infertility, and an endometrial biopsy samples the uterine lining.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-cervical-ovarian-and-endometrial-cancer", "cardLabel": "Cervical, ovarian and endometrial cancer"}, {"q": "A client is frightened after learning her HPV test is positive and asks whether she has cancer. Which response by the nurse is correct?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Yes, a positive HPV test confirms cervical cancer.", "No, not automatically. The test shows high-risk HPV is present, which can lead to cervical changes.", "No, HPV has no connection to cervical cancer.", "The result only means you need the HPV vaccine, and no further screening."], "ans": [1], "why": "A positive HPV test does <b>not automatically mean cancer</b>; it shows the presence of <b>high-risk HPV</b>, which can cause cervical dysplasia and cancer. Vaccination prevents many HPV-associated cancers but does not remove the need for cervical screening.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-cervical-ovarian-and-endometrial-cancer", "cardLabel": "Cervical, ovarian and endometrial cancer"}, {"q": "Which HPV types are especially associated with cervical cancer?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Types 16 and 18", "Types 1 and 2", "Types 6 and 11", "Types 3 and 10"], "ans": [0], "why": "<b>HPV types 16 and 18</b> are especially associated with cervical cancer. The HPV test looks for high-risk infection that can cause cervical dysplasia and cancer; a positive result alone is not a cancer diagnosis.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-cervical-ovarian-and-endometrial-cancer", "cardLabel": "Cervical, ovarian and endometrial cancer"}, {"q": "A client's Pap test comes back abnormal. Which diagnostic test does the nurse expect next?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Hysterosalpingogram", "Colposcopy with biopsy", "CA-125 level", "Breast ultrasound"], "ans": [1], "why": "After an abnormal Pap, the next step is a <b>colposcopy</b>, in which a <b>small piece of tissue is removed for biopsy</b>. The Pap only screens; the other tests listed look at the tubes, the ovaries or the breast.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-cervical-ovarian-and-endometrial-cancer", "cardLabel": "Cervical, ovarian and endometrial cancer"}, {"q": "A client has just had a colposcopy with biopsy. Which statement by the client shows understanding of the after-care?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Heavy bleeding that soaks pads is normal for a week.", "I should douche tonight to keep the area clean.", "I can use tampons for any spotting.", "I may have mild cramping and spotting, and I should avoid intercourse, tampons, douching and vaginal medications."], "ans": [3], "why": "After colposcopy with biopsy, <b>mild cramping and spotting</b> may occur. The client <b>avoids intercourse, tampons, douching and vaginal medications</b>. Mild spotting is the expected amount; soaking pads is not described as normal.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-cervical-ovarian-and-endometrial-cancer", "cardLabel": "Cervical, ovarian and endometrial cancer"}, {"q": "A client is going home after a cervical cone biopsy. Which statement by the client shows that discharge teaching was understood?", "type": "single", "opts": ["I will call if I have heavy bleeding, fever, severe pelvic pain or foul-smelling discharge.", "I can use tampons starting tonight.", "Intercourse is fine again as soon as tomorrow.", "Foul-smelling discharge is a normal part of healing."], "ans": [0], "why": "A cone biopsy removes cone-shaped cervical tissue for diagnosis or treatment. The nurse monitors bleeding and infection, and the client follows restrictions on <b>intercourse (2 weeks)</b> and vaginal insertion. She reports <b>heavy bleeding, fever, severe pelvic pain or foul-smelling discharge</b>.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-cervical-ovarian-and-endometrial-cancer", "cardLabel": "Cervical, ovarian and endometrial cancer"}, {"q": "A postmenopausal client has abnormal uterine bleeding and endometrial cancer is suspected. Which test is the definitive tissue-based diagnostic approach?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Pap test", "CA-125 level", "Endometrial biopsy", "Hysterosalpingogram"], "ans": [2], "why": "<b>Endometrial biopsy</b> is the <b>definitive tissue-based diagnostic approach</b> for suspected endometrial cancer. A Pap test screens cervical cells only, CA-125 relates to ovarian cancer, and a hysterosalpingogram checks the uterus and tubes for infertility.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-cervical-ovarian-and-endometrial-cancer", "cardLabel": "Cervical, ovarian and endometrial cancer"}, {"q": "A nurse is caring for a client having an endometrial biopsy. Which nursing actions are appropriate? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Assess for pregnancy before the test", "Keep the client on bed rest for 24 hours afterward", "Tell the client that cramping is expected", "Monitor for excessive bleeding", "Explain that the test screens for ovarian cancer"], "ans": [0, 2, 3], "why": "For an endometrial biopsy the nurse completes a <b>pregnancy assessment before the test</b>, tells the client to <b>expect cramping</b>, and monitors for <b>excessive bleeding</b>. The biopsy is for abnormal uterine bleeding and suspected endometrial cancer; it is not an ovarian test and bed rest is not part of the card.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-cervical-ovarian-and-endometrial-cancer", "cardLabel": "Cervical, ovarian and endometrial cancer"}, {"q": "A client is scheduled for hysterosalpingography (HSG) as part of an infertility evaluation. When in the menstrual cycle is the test typically done?", "type": "single", "opts": ["During the heaviest days of menstrual flow", "After menstruation and before ovulation", "In the week after ovulation", "At any time, because timing does not matter"], "ans": [1], "why": "HSG is contrast imaging of the uterus and fallopian tube patency. It is typically done <b>after menstruation and before ovulation</b>, and <b>pregnancy and active pelvic infection are ruled out</b> first.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#review-258-m11-hsg", "cardLabel": "Hysterosalpingogram"}, {"q": "Which statements about preparing a client for hysterosalpingography (HSG) are correct? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Pregnancy is ruled out before the test.", "Active pelvic infection is ruled out before the test.", "General anesthesia and an overnight stay are required.", "Temporary cramping and spotting afterward are expected.", "The nurse asks about any history of allergy to contrast.", "No radiation is involved, because the test uses sound waves."], "ans": [0, 1, 3, 4], "why": "Before HSG the nurse <b>rules out pregnancy and active pelvic infection</b> and asks about <b>contrast allergy</b>. The client is told to expect temporary cramping and spotting afterward. It is a contrast x-ray study, not an ultrasound, and it is not an overnight surgical procedure.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#review-258-m11-hsg", "cardLabel": "Hysterosalpingogram"}, {"q": "A client has a breast lump, and a breast ultrasound is ordered. What is this test mainly used to show?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Whether cancer has spread to the bones", "The client's lifetime risk based on BRCA status", "Whether the lump is a fluid-filled cyst or a solid lesion", "Whether tumor cells carry estrogen receptors"], "ans": [2], "why": "Breast ultrasound <b>distinguishes fluid-filled cysts from solid lesions</b>. It uses <b>no radiation</b> and is commonly used in younger patients and as adjunct imaging. Receptor status and genetic risk need other tests.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#review-258-m11-fibrocystic", "cardLabel": "Fibrocystic breast changes"}, {"q": "True or false: A Pap test can diagnose ovarian or endometrial cancer.", "type": "single", "opts": ["True", "False"], "ans": [1], "why": "<b>False.</b> A Pap test screens for <b>cervical cellular abnormalities</b>. It does not diagnose ovarian or endometrial cancer, and even a positive Pap needs more tests before a diagnosis is made.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-cervical-ovarian-and-endometrial-cancer", "cardLabel": "Cervical, ovarian and endometrial cancer"}, {"q": "Cystocele and rectocele are pelvic organ prolapse disorders caused by weakened pelvic support structures. Which are common risk factors? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Vaginal childbirth", "Chronic constipation", "Daily pelvic floor (Kegel) exercises", "Menopause", "Obesity", "Never having been pregnant"], "ans": [0, 1, 3, 4], "why": "Common risk factors are <b>vaginal childbirth, aging, menopause, chronic constipation, obesity</b> and repeated increased intra-abdominal pressure. Kegel exercises are a treatment, not a cause, and never having been pregnant is not on the list.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#review-258-m11-prolapse", "cardLabel": "Cystocele and rectocele"}, {"q": "A postmenopausal client reports urinary leakage, frequency and a feeling that her bladder does not empty. The exam shows a bulge in the anterior vaginal wall. Which condition is this?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Rectocele", "Uterine fibroids", "Cystocele", "Endometriosis"], "ans": [2], "why": "A <b>cystocele</b> is the bladder bulging into the <b>anterior vaginal wall</b>, so the primary problem is urinary elimination: leakage, frequency, retention and incomplete emptying. Treatment is pelvic floor exercises, bladder training, or surgery (anterior colporrhaphy). A rectocele bulges into the posterior wall.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#review-258-m11-prolapse", "cardLabel": "Cystocele and rectocele"}, {"q": "A client reports constipation, straining and a feeling of incomplete evacuation. The exam shows a bulge in the posterior vaginal wall. Which condition does the nurse suspect?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Rectocele", "Cystocele", "Pelvic inflammatory disease", "Polycystic ovary syndrome"], "ans": [0], "why": "A <b>rectocele</b> is the rectum bulging into the <b>posterior vaginal wall</b>, so the primary problem is bowel elimination: constipation, incomplete evacuation and straining. Treatment is pelvic floor exercises, constipation management, or surgery (posterior colporrhaphy).", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#review-258-m11-prolapse", "cardLabel": "Cystocele and rectocele"}, {"q": "A client with breast cancer is prescribed tamoxifen. Which description of this medication is correct?", "type": "single", "opts": ["An aromatase inhibitor used only after menopause", "A HER2-targeted therapy that requires cardiac monitoring", "A selective estrogen receptor modulator (SERM) used for estrogen receptor-positive breast cancers", "A GnRH agonist given as a nasal spray"], "ans": [2], "why": "<b>Tamoxifen</b> is a <b>selective estrogen receptor modulator (SERM)</b> used for appropriate estrogen receptor-positive breast cancers. Aromatase inhibitors are anastrozole and letrozole, trastuzumab is the HER2-targeted drug, and nafarelin is the intranasal GnRH agonist.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-breast-cancer", "cardLabel": "Breast cancer"}, {"q": "A client is taking tamoxifen. Which risks does this medication increase? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Venous thromboembolism", "Hyperkalemia", "Endometrial cancer in clients who have a uterus", "Cardiac dysfunction", "Hearing loss"], "ans": [0, 2], "why": "Tamoxifen can increase the risk of <b>venous thromboembolism</b> and may increase <b>endometrial cancer</b> risk in clients with a uterus. So the client reports unilateral leg swelling, chest pain, dyspnea or abnormal uterine bleeding. Hyperkalemia belongs to spironolactone and cardiac dysfunction to trastuzumab.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-breast-cancer", "cardLabel": "Breast cancer"}, {"q": "A postmenopausal client with hormone receptor-positive breast cancer starts anastrozole. Which adverse effects should the nurse teach about?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Blood clots in the legs and uterine bleeding", "Joint pain and bone loss", "Heart failure and a falling ejection fraction", "High potassium and breast tenderness"], "ans": [1], "why": "Aromatase inhibitors (anastrozole, letrozole) <b>lower estrogen production</b>, so they can cause <b>arthralgia and bone loss</b>, and bone health is monitored. Clots and uterine bleeding are the tamoxifen concerns, and cardiac dysfunction is the trastuzumab concern.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-breast-cancer", "cardLabel": "Breast cancer"}, {"q": "A client with HER2-positive breast cancer is receiving trastuzumab. Which assessment is most important to monitor as prescribed?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Hearing", "Bone density", "Serum potassium", "Cardiac function"], "ans": [3], "why": "The important risk with HER2-targeted therapy (<b>trastuzumab</b>) is <b>cardiac dysfunction</b>, so cardiac function is monitored as prescribed. Bone health is the monitoring point for aromatase inhibitors, not for trastuzumab.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-breast-cancer", "cardLabel": "Breast cancer"}, {"q": "Which client has the greatest risk for ovarian cancer?", "type": "single", "opts": ["A 24-year-old with fibrocystic breast changes", "A 30-year-old who has a persistent high-risk HPV infection", "A 68-year-old with a BRCA mutation and a sister who had ovarian cancer", "A 35-year-old with a rectocele after three vaginal births"], "ans": [2], "why": "Ovarian cancer risks are <b>BRCA mutations, family history and increasing age</b>, and this client has all three. Persistent high-risk HPV is the cervical cancer risk, and the other two histories are not on the card's list for ovarian cancer.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-cervical-ovarian-and-endometrial-cancer", "cardLabel": "Cervical, ovarian and endometrial cancer"}, {"q": "A client with suspected ovarian cancer asks what the CA-125 blood test is for. Which answer is correct?", "type": "single", "opts": ["It confirms ovarian cancer without any imaging.", "It helps assess risk and monitor the disease, alongside pelvic imaging.", "It screens for cervical cell changes.", "It measures estrogen receptors on tumor cells."], "ans": [1], "why": "For ovarian cancer the diagnostic focus is <b>pelvic imaging</b>, and <b>CA-125 helps assess risk and monitor disease</b>. It does not confirm cancer by itself, and it has nothing to do with cervical cells or receptor status.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-cervical-ovarian-and-endometrial-cancer", "cardLabel": "Cervical, ovarian and endometrial cancer"}, {"q": "Cervical cancer is often asymptomatic early. Which later finding should make the nurse suspect it?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Persistent bloating and early satiety", "Cyclic breast tenderness before menses", "Hot flashes and night sweats", "Bleeding after intercourse or abnormal vaginal discharge"], "ans": [3], "why": "Cervical cancer is <b>often asymptomatic early</b>; <b>postcoital bleeding or abnormal discharge</b> comes later. The risk is persistent high-risk HPV infection, and diagnosis runs from HPV/Pap screening to colposcopy and biopsy. Bloating with early satiety points to ovarian cancer instead.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-cervical-ovarian-and-endometrial-cancer", "cardLabel": "Cervical, ovarian and endometrial cancer"}, {"q": "True or false: Ovarian cancer is frequently diagnosed after it has spread, because its early symptoms are nonspecific.", "type": "single", "opts": ["True", "False"], "ans": [0], "why": "<b>True.</b> The findings are vague: persistent bloating or increased abdominal size, <b>early satiety</b>, pelvic or abdominal pain, urinary urgency or frequency and unexplained weight changes. Ascites appears in advanced disease. Because these are <b>nonspecific</b>, the cancer is often found late.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-cervical-ovarian-and-endometrial-cancer", "cardLabel": "Cervical, ovarian and endometrial cancer"}, {"q": "True or false: A normal CA-125 level confirms that a client does not have ovarian cancer.", "type": "single", "opts": ["True", "False"], "ans": [1], "why": "<b>False.</b> A normal CA-125 <b>does not exclude ovarian cancer</b>, and CA-125 can be elevated in benign conditions, including endometriosis. It is not an appropriate stand-alone screening test for asymptomatic, average-risk patients.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-cervical-ovarian-and-endometrial-cancer", "cardLabel": "Cervical, ovarian and endometrial cancer"}, {"q": "A client with an intact uterus has prolonged estrogen stimulation without adequate progesterone. Which outcome is the nurse concerned about?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Thinning of the uterine lining with no cancer risk", "Ovarian hyperstimulation syndrome", "Increased endometrial proliferation and a higher risk of endometrial cancer", "Weakening of the posterior vaginal wall"], "ans": [2], "why": "Prolonged <b>estrogen without adequate progesterone</b> causes increased <b>endometrial proliferation and cancer risk</b>. Risk factors include obesity, chronic anovulation, estrogen-only systemic therapy in patients with a uterus, tamoxifen and increasing age.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-cervical-ovarian-and-endometrial-cancer", "cardLabel": "Cervical, ovarian and endometrial cancer"}, {"q": "A client is diagnosed with endometrial cancer after reporting light spotting years after menopause. Which treatment does the nurse expect to be commonly used?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Myomectomy to remove the tumor and keep the uterus", "Hysterectomy with bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy and surgical staging", "A vaginal pessary and pelvic floor exercises", "Methotrexate with serial hCG testing"], "ans": [1], "why": "Even light spotting after menopause warrants evaluation, because postmenopausal bleeding is a warning sign. Treatment is commonly <b>hysterectomy with bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy and surgical staging</b>, with radiation, chemotherapy or other systemic treatment according to <b>disease stage and tumor characteristics</b>.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-cervical-ovarian-and-endometrial-cancer", "cardLabel": "Cervical, ovarian and endometrial cancer"}, {"q": "Most cases of cervical cancer are associated with which factor?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Persistent high-risk HPV infection", "A BRCA1 or BRCA2 variant", "Unopposed estrogen exposure", "Chronic anovulation"], "ans": [0], "why": "Most cervical cancer is associated with <b>persistent high-risk HPV infection</b>. Prevention is HPV vaccination plus cervical screening, and diagnosis requires <b>tissue evaluation</b>, not simply a positive HPV test. BRCA relates to breast and ovarian cancer; unopposed estrogen and anovulation relate to endometrial cancer.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-cervical-ovarian-and-endometrial-cancer", "cardLabel": "Cervical, ovarian and endometrial cancer"}, {"q": "A client with polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS) asks how insulin resistance is connected to her condition. Which explanation is correct?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Insulin resistance lowers androgen levels, which stops ovulation.", "Insulin resistance has no relationship to PCOS.", "Insulin resistance is caused by cysts pressing on the pancreas.", "Insulin resistance can increase androgen production and worsen ovulatory dysfunction."], "ans": [3], "why": "PCOS is a hormonal and metabolic disorder with ovulatory dysfunction, androgen excess and, frequently, insulin resistance. <b>Insulin resistance can increase androgen production</b> and <b>worsen ovulatory dysfunction</b>, so it raises androgens; it does not lower them.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#review-258-m11-pcos", "cardLabel": "PCOS"}, {"q": "True or false: Polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS) always presents with ovarian cysts.", "type": "single", "opts": ["True", "False"], "ans": [1], "why": "<b>False.</b> Ovarian cysts are <b>not required</b> for the diagnosis. PCOS is diagnosed when two of three criteria are met, so hyperandrogenism plus irregular or absent ovulation is enough without cysts on ultrasound.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#review-258-m11-pcos", "cardLabel": "PCOS"}, {"q": "Which findings does the nurse expect in a client with polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS)? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Irregular or absent menstruation", "Hirsutism", "Unintended weight loss", "Acne", "Regular, predictable 28-day cycles", "Acanthosis nigricans"], "ans": [0, 1, 3, 5], "why": "Expected findings are <b>irregular or absent menstruation</b>, infertility from anovulation, <b>hirsutism</b>, acne, androgenic hair thinning, weight gain or trouble managing weight in some patients, and acanthosis nigricans. Regular cycles and weight loss do not fit the picture.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#review-258-m11-pcos", "cardLabel": "PCOS"}, {"q": "A nurse notes dark skin patches (acanthosis nigricans) on the neck of a client with PCOS. What does this finding suggest?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Insulin resistance", "Iron-deficiency anemia", "Low estrogen from menopause", "A medication allergy"], "ans": [0], "why": "<b>Acanthosis nigricans</b>, the dark skin patches seen in some clients with PCOS, <b>suggests insulin resistance</b>. It sits on the card's list of expected PCOS findings beside hirsutism, acne and irregular or absent periods.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#review-258-m11-pcos", "cardLabel": "PCOS"}, {"q": "A client with PCOS wants to become pregnant. Which medication does the nurse expect to be used to induce ovulation?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Combined oral contraceptive", "Spironolactone", "Letrozole", "Nafarelin"], "ans": [2], "why": "<b>Letrozole</b> is used to <b>induce ovulation when pregnancy is desired</b>; clomiphene is the alternative ovulation-inducing medication. Combined oral contraceptives regulate bleeding and reduce androgen symptoms, and spironolactone reduces hirsutism and acne.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#review-258-m11-pcos", "cardLabel": "PCOS"}, {"q": "A client is starting clomiphene for ovarian stimulation as part of infertility treatment. Which risk should the nurse discuss?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Multiple gestation", "Permanent menopause", "Hyperkalemia", "Cardiac dysfunction"], "ans": [0], "why": "<b>Letrozole and clomiphene</b> are the two ovarian stimulation medications used to support infertility treatment, and the card attaches a <b>risk of multiple gestation</b> to them. Permanent menopause, hyperkalemia and cardiac dysfunction are not the concern here.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#review-258-m11-pcos", "cardLabel": "PCOS"}, {"q": "A client taking an ovarian stimulation medication reports severe abdominal distention, rapid weight gain, reduced urine output and shortness of breath. Which complication does the nurse suspect?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Pelvic inflammatory disease", "Uterine fibroids", "Endometriosis", "Ovarian hyperstimulation syndrome"], "ans": [3], "why": "<b>Ovarian hyperstimulation syndrome</b> is a potentially serious complication of ovarian stimulation. Its red flags are <b>severe abdominal distention, rapid weight gain, reduced urine output, dyspnea</b> and significant abdominal pain.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#review-258-m11-pcos", "cardLabel": "PCOS"}, {"q": "A nurse suspects a ruptured ectopic pregnancy with internal hemorrhage. Which findings support this? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Sudden, severe pelvic or abdominal pain", "Painless bleeding with a soft, nontender abdomen", "Referred shoulder pain", "Dizziness or syncope", "Tachycardia and hypotension", "Hypertension with a slow, bounding pulse"], "ans": [0, 2, 3, 4], "why": "With rupture, suspect <b>internal hemorrhage</b>: sudden severe pelvic or abdominal pain, <b>referred shoulder pain</b> from diaphragmatic irritation, dizziness, syncope, tachycardia, hypotension and a rigid or tender abdomen. Vaginal bleeding may be present. A soft, painless abdomen and a rising blood pressure do not fit.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "more/NG-326_ectopic-molar-pregnancy.html", "cardLabel": "Ectopic and molar pregnancy"}, {"q": "A client with a suspected ruptured ectopic pregnancy arrives in the emergency department. Which is the priority?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Give methotrexate and schedule serial hCG tests", "Give an oral analgesic and reassess in 4 hours", "Rapidly assess hemodynamic stability, establish IV access, resuscitate, and arrange urgent surgical evaluation", "Schedule an outpatient pelvic ultrasound for the next day"], "ans": [2], "why": "For a ruptured ectopic pregnancy the priority is <b>rapid assessment of hemodynamic stability, IV access, resuscitation and urgent surgical evaluation</b>. <b>Methotrexate is not appropriate</b> when rupture or hemodynamic instability is suspected.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "more/NG-326_ectopic-molar-pregnancy.html", "cardLabel": "Ectopic and molar pregnancy"}, {"q": "For which client with an ectopic pregnancy is methotrexate an appropriate treatment?", "type": "single", "opts": ["A client with an unruptured ectopic pregnancy who is hemodynamically stable", "A client with shoulder pain, syncope and a rigid abdomen", "A client with suspected rupture whose blood pressure is falling", "Any client with an ectopic pregnancy, whatever her condition"], "ans": [0], "why": "Methotrexate is used for a <b>non-ruptured</b> ectopic pregnancy. It is <b>not appropriate with suspected rupture or hemodynamic instability</b>. Serial hCG testing is essential after it is given.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "more/NG-326_ectopic-molar-pregnancy.html", "cardLabel": "Ectopic and molar pregnancy"}, {"q": "A client is treated with methotrexate for an unruptured ectopic pregnancy. Which teaching points are correct? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Avoid alcohol.", "Take a folic acid supplement every day while the medication works.", "Avoid becoming pregnant during the recommended interval.", "Return for serial hCG blood tests.", "No follow-up is needed once the pain stops.", "Avoid folic acid supplements unless directed."], "ans": [0, 2, 3, 5], "why": "After methotrexate the client <b>avoids pregnancy</b> for the recommended interval, <b>alcohol, folic acid supplements</b> unless directed, and medications that interfere with treatment. <b>Serial hCG testing is essential</b>, so follow-up cannot be skipped.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "more/NG-326_ectopic-molar-pregnancy.html", "cardLabel": "Ectopic and molar pregnancy"}, {"q": "A client has heavy menstrual bleeding, pelvic pressure, an enlarged uterus and iron-deficiency anemia. Imaging shows benign smooth muscle tumors of the uterus. What are these called?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Ovarian cysts", "Uterine fibroids", "Endometriosis implants", "Cervical dysplasia"], "ans": [1], "why": "<b>Uterine fibroids</b> are <b>benign smooth muscle tumors of the uterus</b>. They cause heavy bleeding, pelvic pressure, an enlarged uterus and iron-deficiency anemia, and are treated with medication, myomectomy, embolization or hysterectomy.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#review-258-m11-fibroids", "cardLabel": "Uterine fibroids"}, {"q": "A client with symptomatic fibroids wants surgery but wishes to keep her uterus. Which procedure does the nurse expect?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Hysterectomy", "Bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy", "Anterior colporrhaphy", "Myomectomy"], "ans": [3], "why": "A <b>myomectomy removes the fibroids while preserving the uterus</b>. A hysterectomy removes the uterus and is the definitive treatment. The other two procedures remove the tubes and ovaries or repair a cystocele.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#review-258-m11-fibroids", "cardLabel": "Uterine fibroids"}, {"q": "A client with uterine fibroids has persistent heavy menstrual bleeding. Which laboratory value is most important for the nurse to monitor?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Hemoglobin", "Serum potassium", "CA-125", "Blood glucose"], "ans": [0], "why": "Fibroids are benign, but <b>persistent heavy bleeding can cause significant anemia</b> and hemodynamic instability, so the nurse monitors <b>hemoglobin</b> and symptoms of anemia. NSAIDs or tranexamic acid may reduce bleeding in appropriate patients.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#review-258-m11-fibroids", "cardLabel": "Uterine fibroids"}, {"q": "A client with endometriosis is prescribed nafarelin. Which description of this medication is correct?", "type": "single", "opts": ["An aromatase inhibitor taken by mouth once a week", "A GnRH agonist given intranasally", "A selective estrogen receptor modulator given by injection", "An estrogen cream applied vaginally"], "ans": [1], "why": "<b>Nafarelin</b> is a <b>gonadotropin-releasing hormone (GnRH) agonist given intranasally</b>. With continuous use it suppresses pituitary gonadotropins and reduces ovarian estrogen production. It is not an aromatase inhibitor, a SERM or an estrogen product.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "more/NG-415_endometriosis.html", "cardLabel": "Endometriosis"}, {"q": "A client has been using nafarelin for endometriosis for a prolonged period. Which adverse effect of prolonged suppression should the nurse monitor for?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Heavier menstrual bleeding", "Excess facial hair growth", "Reduced bone mineral density", "Rapid weight gain with fluid in the abdomen"], "ans": [2], "why": "Nafarelin reduces ovarian estrogen production, so expected adverse effects are hot flashes, vaginal dryness and mood changes, plus <b>reduced bone mineral density with prolonged suppression</b>. The other options are not on the card's list for this drug.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "more/NG-415_endometriosis.html", "cardLabel": "Endometriosis"}, {"q": "A client with endometriosis reports recurring pain during and after sexual intercourse. Which term does the nurse use to document this?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Dyschezia", "Dysmenorrhea", "Amenorrhea", "Dyspareunia"], "ans": [3], "why": "<b>Dyspareunia</b> is persistent or recurring pain experienced <b>before, during or after sexual intercourse</b>. Dysmenorrhea is painful menstruation, amenorrhea is absence of menstruation, and dyschezia refers to painful bowel movements.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "more/NG-415_endometriosis.html", "cardLabel": "Endometriosis"}, {"q": "Which statement correctly describes a menstrual disorder?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Amenorrhea is excessive menstrual blood loss.", "Dysmenorrhea is the absence of menstruation.", "Dysmenorrhea is painful menstruation, often prostaglandin-mediated.", "Heavy menstrual bleeding means pain with intercourse."], "ans": [2], "why": "<b>Dysmenorrhea</b> is painful menstruation, often <b>prostaglandin-mediated</b>. Heavy menstrual bleeding is excessive menstrual blood loss from various causes, and <b>amenorrhea</b> is the absence of menstruation. Pain with intercourse is dyspareunia.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#alt-reproductive-physiologic-processes", "cardLabel": "Reproductive physiologic processes"}, {"q": "A client using nafarelin for endometriosis asks why she is having hot flashes and vaginal dryness. Which explanation is correct?", "type": "single", "opts": ["The medication creates a temporary low-estrogen state similar to menopause.", "The medication raises estrogen to very high levels.", "The medication has caused permanent menopause.", "These are signs of an allergic reaction to the nasal spray."], "ans": [0], "why": "GnRH agonist therapy such as <b>nafarelin</b> produces a <b>temporary low-estrogen state similar to menopause</b>, which explains the menopause-like symptoms. It is temporary, not permanent, and it lowers estrogen; it does not raise it.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "more/NG-415_endometriosis.html", "cardLabel": "Endometriosis"}, {"q": "A client is scheduled for a total hysterectomy. Which organs are removed?", "type": "single", "opts": ["The uterus only, with the cervix retained", "The uterus and cervix", "Both fallopian tubes and ovaries only", "The uterus, cervix, both ovaries and the bladder"], "ans": [1], "why": "A <b>total hysterectomy</b> removes the <b>uterus and cervix</b>. A supracervical (subtotal) hysterectomy keeps the cervix, and a bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy is the separate procedure that removes both fallopian tubes and ovaries.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#alt-reproductive-physiologic-processes", "cardLabel": "Reproductive physiologic processes"}, {"q": "Which procedure removes the uterus, cervix, surrounding tissues and sometimes the upper vagina, generally for selected cancers?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Supracervical hysterectomy", "Bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy", "Total hysterectomy", "Radical hysterectomy"], "ans": [3], "why": "A <b>radical hysterectomy</b> removes the uterus, cervix, surrounding tissues and sometimes the upper vagina, and is generally used for <b>selected cancers</b>. A supracervical hysterectomy leaves the cervix; a bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy removes both fallopian tubes and ovaries.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#alt-reproductive-physiologic-processes", "cardLabel": "Reproductive physiologic processes"}, {"q": "True or false: A hysterectomy always includes removal of the ovaries.", "type": "single", "opts": ["True", "False"], "ans": [1], "why": "A hysterectomy does <b>not automatically</b> mean the ovaries were removed. If the ovaries are left in place and keep working, the client does <b>not necessarily enter menopause</b> straight away.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#alt-reproductive-physiologic-processes", "cardLabel": "Reproductive physiologic processes"}, {"q": "A nurse is planning care for a client who had a hysterectomy yesterday. Which action addresses the risk of thromboembolism?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Keep the client on bed rest until the abdominal incision has fully healed", "Limit oral fluids to reduce vaginal drainage", "Encourage early ambulation and give the ordered prophylaxis", "Remove the urinary catheter as early as possible"], "ans": [2], "why": "After a hysterectomy, <b>thromboembolism</b> is prevented with <b>early ambulation and ordered prophylaxis</b>. Bed rest does the opposite. The other post-op watch points are hemorrhage, urinary function, infection, bowel function and pain.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#alt-reproductive-physiologic-processes", "cardLabel": "Reproductive physiologic processes"}, {"q": "A client's urinary catheter has just been removed after a hysterectomy. Which problem does the nurse evaluate for?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Urinary retention", "Dilutional hyponatremia", "Bladder stones", "Hydronephrosis"], "ans": [0], "why": "For urinary function after a hysterectomy, the nurse monitors catheter output and then <b>evaluates for retention after the catheter is removed</b>. A client who cannot empty the bladder needs to be picked up early.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#alt-reproductive-physiologic-processes", "cardLabel": "Reproductive physiologic processes"}, {"q": "A nurse is explaining how perimenopause differs from menopause. Which statement describes perimenopause?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Ovarian function fluctuates, periods are irregular, and pregnancy is still possible", "Ovarian function is markedly decreased, menstruation has stopped, and pregnancy does not happen", "Ovarian function is unchanged and periods stay regular", "Menstruation has stopped, but pregnancy remains likely"], "ans": [0], "why": "In <b>perimenopause</b> ovarian function fluctuates, menstruation is irregular and <b>pregnancy is still possible</b>. In <b>menopause</b> ovarian function is markedly decreased, menstruation stops and pregnancy does not happen.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#alt-reproductive-physiologic-processes", "cardLabel": "Reproductive physiologic processes"}, {"q": "A 52-year-old client asks how she will know she has reached menopause. Which response by the nurse is correct?", "type": "single", "opts": ["When your periods first become irregular", "After 12 consecutive months with no period and no other cause", "After 6 months without a period", "As soon as hot flashes and night sweats begin to disturb your sleep"], "ans": [1], "why": "Menopause is usually defined as <b>12 consecutive months without menstruation</b> when no other cause exists. Irregular periods describe perimenopause. Vaginal dryness and genitourinary changes are among the symptoms.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#alt-reproductive-physiologic-processes", "cardLabel": "Reproductive physiologic processes"}, {"q": "True or false: Once periods become irregular in perimenopause, pregnancy is impossible.", "type": "single", "opts": ["True", "False"], "ans": [1], "why": "Pregnancy can still happen in perimenopause because the client is <b>still ovulating occasionally</b>, even though her periods have become irregular. Irregular periods are not a sign that contraception is no longer needed.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#alt-reproductive-physiologic-processes", "cardLabel": "Reproductive physiologic processes"}, {"q": "A client who still has her uterus is prescribed systemic estrogen for menopausal symptoms. Why does the nurse expect a progestogen to be prescribed with it?", "type": "single", "opts": ["To protect against endometrial hyperplasia", "To make the estrogen relieve hot flashes sooner", "To prevent blood clots in the legs", "To restore regular ovulation"], "ans": [0], "why": "With a <b>uterus present</b>, systemic estrogen generally requires a progestogen to <b>protect against endometrial hyperplasia</b>. With the uterus absent, estrogen-only therapy may be appropriate if no contraindications exist.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#alt-reproductive-physiologic-processes", "cardLabel": "Reproductive physiologic processes"}, {"q": "A client asks about hormone therapy for menopausal symptoms. Which findings in her history are contraindications? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Unexplained vaginal bleeding", "Hot flashes that disrupt sleep", "Previous deep vein thrombosis", "Active liver disease", "Previous hysterectomy", "An estrogen-sensitive cancer"], "ans": [0, 2, 3, 5], "why": "Hormone therapy is contraindicated with <b>unexplained vaginal bleeding</b>, certain estrogen-sensitive cancers, <b>active or previous thromboembolic disease</b>, active liver disease, or major cardiovascular contraindications without specialist evaluation. Hot flashes are a reason to consider it, and a client without a uterus may take estrogen alone.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#alt-reproductive-physiologic-processes", "cardLabel": "Reproductive physiologic processes"}, {"q": "Which statement correctly compares human papillomavirus (HPV) infection and genital herpes (HSV)?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Both are bacterial and are cured with penicillin", "HPV causes painful grouped vesicles; herpes causes painless warts", "Both are viral; a vaccine is available for HPV but not for herpes", "Acyclovir cures herpes; HPV has no link to cancer"], "ans": [2], "why": "Both are <b>viral</b>. HPV shows as genital warts or no symptoms, carries cervical cancer risk and has a <b>vaccine</b>. Herpes causes <b>painful grouped vesicles or ulcers</b>; acyclovir or valacyclovir treat it but are not curative, and there is no vaccine.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-sexually-transmitted-infections", "cardLabel": "Sexually transmitted infections"}, {"q": "A client had a painless genital chancre and now has a rash that includes the palms and soles. Which infection and treatment does the nurse anticipate?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Genital herpes, treated with acyclovir", "Syphilis, treated with penicillin", "Trichomoniasis, treated with metronidazole", "Gonorrhea, treated with ceftriaxone"], "ans": [1], "why": "Syphilis begins with a <b>painless chancre</b>; a later rash can include the <b>palms and soles</b>. Treatment is <b>penicillin</b>. Because the first sore does not hurt, it is often missed and the infection is passed on.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-sexually-transmitted-infections", "cardLabel": "Sexually transmitted infections"}, {"q": "A nonpregnant adult tests positive for chlamydia and says, 'But I feel fine.' Which response by the nurse is correct?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Chlamydia often causes no symptoms; it is treated with doxycycline.", "Without symptoms, the result is most likely a false positive and should be repeated.", "Treatment can wait until burning or discharge develops.", "Chlamydia is viral, so no medication will clear it."], "ans": [0], "why": "Chlamydia is <b>frequently asymptomatic</b> at first, which makes spread to others more likely; when symptoms appear they are dysuria or discharge. <b>Doxycycline</b> is the treatment for nonpregnant adults.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-sexually-transmitted-infections", "cardLabel": "Sexually transmitted infections"}, {"q": "A client with purulent discharge and dysuria is diagnosed with gonorrhea. Chlamydia has not been ruled out. Which treatment does the nurse anticipate?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Oral metronidazole alone for both client and partner", "Oral acyclovir", "Ceftriaxone, plus coverage for chlamydia", "Penicillin alone"], "ans": [2], "why": "Gonorrhea causes purulent discharge, dysuria and pelvic pain. It is treated with <b>ceftriaxone</b>, and <b>chlamydia coverage is added</b> when chlamydia has not been excluded. Metronidazole treats trichomoniasis, acyclovir herpes, and penicillin syphilis.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-sexually-transmitted-infections", "cardLabel": "Sexually transmitted infections"}, {"q": "A nurse is teaching a client newly diagnosed with genital herpes. Which statements should the nurse include? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["The infection is lifelong.", "A full course of antivirals removes the virus from the body.", "The virus can be passed on even when no lesions are visible.", "Antivirals reduce the severity and duration of outbreaks.", "A vaccine will prevent future outbreaks.", "Antivirals lower the risk of transmission."], "ans": [0, 2, 3, 5], "why": "Herpes causes painful lesions and is <b>lifelong</b>. <b>Transmission can occur without visible lesions</b>. Antivirals reduce severity, duration and transmission risk but <b>do not eliminate the virus</b>, and there is no vaccine.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-sexually-transmitted-infections", "cardLabel": "Sexually transmitted infections"}, {"q": "A client with syphilis asks why treatment matters when the sore does not hurt. Which long-term result of untreated syphilis does the nurse describe?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Serious cardiovascular and neurologic complications", "Cervical cancer and genital warts", "Bladder stones, hydronephrosis and eventual renal impairment", "Ovarian cysts and excess hair growth"], "ans": [0], "why": "Long-term untreated syphilis can cause <b>serious cardiovascular and neurologic complications</b>. Cervical cancer and warts belong to HPV, bladder stones and hydronephrosis to prostate obstruction, and cysts with excess hair to PCOS.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-sexually-transmitted-infections", "cardLabel": "Sexually transmitted infections"}, {"q": "A client is diagnosed with trichomoniasis. Which teaching points should the nurse include? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["It is caused by a protozoan, not a bacterium.", "Metronidazole is the standard treatment.", "Your partner needs treatment only if symptoms develop.", "Avoid intercourse until treatment is finished and symptoms have resolved.", "Intercourse is safe again after the first dose.", "Your sexual partners need to be treated."], "ans": [0, 1, 3, 5], "why": "Trichomoniasis is <b>protozoal, not bacterial</b>. <b>Metronidazole</b> is the standard treatment, <b>sexual partners require treatment</b> whether or not they have symptoms, and intercourse is avoided until treatment is completed and symptoms have resolved.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-sexually-transmitted-infections", "cardLabel": "Sexually transmitted infections"}, {"q": "A client asks what can happen if chlamydia or gonorrhea is left untreated. Which complications does the nurse include? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Pelvic inflammatory disease", "Tubal scarring", "Cervical cancer", "Infertility", "Ectopic pregnancy", "Polycystic ovary syndrome"], "ans": [0, 1, 3, 4], "why": "Untreated chlamydia and gonorrhea can <b>ascend into the upper reproductive tract and cause PID</b>. Complications include <b>tubal scarring, infertility and ectopic pregnancy</b>. Cervical cancer is linked to HPV, and PCOS is a hormonal disorder, not an infection.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-sexually-transmitted-infections", "cardLabel": "Sexually transmitted infections"}, {"q": "A client has finished treatment for chlamydia. Which teaching best helps prevent reinfection?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Take a second course of antibiotics in one month", "Make sure sexual partners are treated as well", "Douche after intercourse", "Get the HPV vaccine"], "ans": [1], "why": "<b>Reinfection is common without partner treatment</b>. Treating only the client leaves the source in place, and untreated chlamydia or gonorrhea can ascend to cause PID, with tubal scarring, infertility and ectopic pregnancy.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-sexually-transmitted-infections", "cardLabel": "Sexually transmitted infections"}, {"q": "A nurse is assessing a client with suspected pelvic inflammatory disease (PID). Which findings are classic for PID? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Cervical motion tenderness", "Painless genital chancre", "Lower abdominal or pelvic pain", "Uterine or adnexal tenderness", "Abnormal vaginal discharge", "Painful grouped vesicles on the vulva"], "ans": [0, 2, 3, 4], "why": "PID is infection and inflammation of the upper female reproductive tract (uterus, tubes, ovaries). Classic findings are <b>lower abdominal or pelvic pain, cervical motion tenderness</b>, uterine or adnexal tenderness and abnormal discharge; fever, dyspareunia or postcoital bleeding may be present. A painless chancre is syphilis; grouped vesicles are herpes.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-sexually-transmitted-infections", "cardLabel": "Sexually transmitted infections"}, {"q": "Which medication regimen does the nurse anticipate for a client being treated for pelvic inflammatory disease?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Intramuscular penicillin as a single dose", "Oral acyclovir for 14 days", "Oral metronidazole as a single dose for the client and for every sexual partner", "Intramuscular ceftriaxone plus 14 days of oral doxycycline and metronidazole"], "ans": [3], "why": "PID is treated with <b>intramuscular ceftriaxone</b> plus <b>oral doxycycline for 14 days</b> and <b>oral metronidazole for 14 days</b>. A single drug or a single dose does not cover it.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-sexually-transmitted-infections", "cardLabel": "Sexually transmitted infections"}, {"q": "Which client with pelvic inflammatory disease does the nurse expect to need hospitalization?", "type": "single", "opts": ["A client who is tolerating oral antibiotics at home", "A client with a tubo-ovarian abscess", "A client whose partner has not yet been treated", "A client with pelvic pain and abnormal vaginal discharge"], "ans": [1], "why": "Hospitalization may be necessary for <b>severe illness, pregnancy, a tubo-ovarian abscess</b>, inability to tolerate oral treatment, or when a surgical emergency cannot be excluded. Pelvic pain and discharge alone are the classic findings, not a reason for admission.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-sexually-transmitted-infections", "cardLabel": "Sexually transmitted infections"}, {"q": "A client asks what besides cancer can raise a prostate-specific antigen (PSA) level. Which does the nurse include? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Benign prostatic hyperplasia", "Prostatitis", "Taking finasteride", "Urinary tract infection", "Recent urinary retention or instrumentation", "Erectile dysfunction"], "ans": [0, 1, 3, 4], "why": "PSA is a protein made by prostate tissue. It is useful but <b>not specific for cancer</b>: it may be elevated with prostate cancer, <b>BPH, prostatitis, a urinary tract infection</b>, or recent urinary retention or instrumentation. Finasteride lowers PSA rather than raising it.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-benign-prostatic-hyperplasia", "cardLabel": "BPH, TURP and bladder irrigation"}, {"q": "True or false: An elevated PSA level is diagnostic of prostate cancer.", "type": "single", "opts": ["True", "False"], "ans": [1], "why": "Not necessarily. PSA can also be elevated with a <b>urinary tract infection, BPH or prostatitis</b>, so a raised result calls for further evaluation and does <b>not diagnose cancer</b> on its own.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-benign-prostatic-hyperplasia", "cardLabel": "BPH, TURP and bladder irrigation"}, {"q": "A client's PSA result is 5.1 ng/mL and he asks what that means. Which response by the nurse is correct?", "type": "single", "opts": ["A PSA above 4 ng/mL confirms prostate cancer, so treatment starts now.", "Above 4 ng/mL is a general threshold for further evaluation.", "The number is not useful; only the rectal exam matters.", "PSA is meaningful only after the prostate has been removed."], "ans": [1], "why": "A PSA <b>above 4 ng/mL</b> is used as a threshold for <b>further evaluation</b>. In practice it is interpreted with age, PSA trend, risk factors, medications and clinical findings rather than as a universal cutoff.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-benign-prostatic-hyperplasia", "cardLabel": "BPH, TURP and bladder irrigation"}, {"q": "During a digital rectal exam, which prostate finding is most suspicious for malignancy?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Smooth, enlarged and rubbery", "Boggy and very tender", "Hard, irregular and nodular", "Small, soft and nontender"], "ans": [2], "why": "A <b>hard, irregular, nodular</b> prostate is suspicious for malignancy. A smooth, enlarged, rubbery prostate is BPH. A boggy, very tender prostate with systemic symptoms is acute prostatitis.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-benign-prostatic-hyperplasia", "cardLabel": "BPH, TURP and bladder irrigation"}, {"q": "A client with erectile dysfunction is scheduled for a test that evaluates penile blood flow. Which test does the nurse prepare him for?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Transrectal ultrasound (TRUS)", "Prostate biopsy", "Prostate-specific antigen (PSA) level", "Penile Doppler ultrasound"], "ans": [3], "why": "A <b>penile Doppler ultrasound</b> evaluates blood flow in erectile dysfunction. TRUS helps visualize the prostate and may guide a biopsy, and a prostate biopsy obtains tissue to confirm cancer.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#review-258-m11-male-tests", "cardLabel": "Male diagnostic tests"}, {"q": "Which procedure obtains tissue to confirm prostate cancer?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Prostate biopsy", "Penile Doppler ultrasound", "Transrectal ultrasound on its own", "PSA blood test"], "ans": [0], "why": "A <b>prostate biopsy obtains tissue to confirm cancer</b>. Transrectal ultrasound helps visualize the prostate and may guide the biopsy, PSA is not specific for cancer, and penile Doppler looks at blood flow.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#review-258-m11-male-tests", "cardLabel": "Male diagnostic tests"}, {"q": "A client asks why benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH) makes it hard to urinate. Which explanation is correct?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Cancer cells from the prostate block the bladder.", "The kidneys make less urine with age.", "The enlarged prostate compresses the urethra.", "An infection has scarred the bladder wall."], "ans": [2], "why": "BPH is a <b>nonmalignant enlargement</b> of the prostate, commonly associated with aging. As the gland enlarges it can <b>compress the urethra</b>, which produces lower urinary tract symptoms.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-benign-prostatic-hyperplasia", "cardLabel": "BPH, TURP and bladder irrigation"}, {"q": "A nurse is assessing a client with benign prostatic hyperplasia. Which findings does the nurse expect? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Hesitancy starting urination", "Weak urinary stream", "Unexplained weight loss", "Nocturia", "Dribbling after voiding", "Bone pain in the hips"], "ans": [0, 1, 3, 4], "why": "BPH brings <b>hesitancy, a weak stream, dribbling after voiding and nocturia</b>, along with urgency, frequency, incomplete emptying and retention. Unexplained weight loss and bone pain point to advanced prostate cancer.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-benign-prostatic-hyperplasia", "cardLabel": "BPH, TURP and bladder irrigation"}, {"q": "A client has put off treatment for benign prostatic hyperplasia. Which complications can develop? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Bone metastases", "Acute urinary retention", "Bladder stones", "Hydronephrosis", "Spinal cord compression", "Renal impairment from prolonged obstruction"], "ans": [1, 2, 3, 5], "why": "BPH can lead to <b>acute urinary retention</b>, recurrent urinary tract infections, bladder stones, <b>hydronephrosis and renal impairment</b> from prolonged obstruction. BPH is benign and does not metastasize, so bone metastases and cord compression belong to prostate cancer.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-benign-prostatic-hyperplasia", "cardLabel": "BPH, TURP and bladder irrigation"}, {"q": "Which statement describes how tamsulosin helps a client with benign prostatic hyperplasia?", "type": "single", "opts": ["It relaxes smooth muscle in the prostate and bladder neck", "It increases blood flow to the penis", "It treats infection in the prostate", "It is a diuretic that increases the volume of urine produced"], "ans": [0], "why": "Tamsulosin is an <b>alpha-1 blocker</b> that <b>relaxes smooth muscle in the prostate and bladder neck</b>. Its adverse effects are orthostatic hypotension and dizziness, so fall precautions apply.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-benign-prostatic-hyperplasia", "cardLabel": "BPH, TURP and bladder irrigation"}, {"q": "A client starting finasteride for benign prostatic hyperplasia asks how the drug works. Which response is correct?", "type": "single", "opts": ["It is an alpha-1 blocker that relaxes smooth muscle in the bladder neck and prostate.", "It is an antibiotic that clears prostate infection.", "It is a diuretic that increases urine flow.", "It is a 5-alpha-reductase inhibitor that lowers dihydrotestosterone (DHT)."], "ans": [3], "why": "Finasteride is a <b>5-alpha-reductase inhibitor</b> that <b>lowers dihydrotestosterone (DHT)</b>. It takes months to work, can cause sexual adverse effects and lowers PSA. Dutasteride is similar. The alpha-1 blocker is tamsulosin.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-benign-prostatic-hyperplasia", "cardLabel": "BPH, TURP and bladder irrigation"}, {"q": "A client who began finasteride 3 weeks ago says his urinary stream is no better. Which response by the nurse is best?", "type": "single", "opts": ["The drug has failed, so you will need surgery.", "It shrinks the prostate gradually; full benefit may take 3 to 6 months or longer.", "Take two tablets a day until the stream improves.", "It normally works within a few days, so something else must be causing the problem."], "ans": [1], "why": "<b>Finasteride gradually reduces prostate volume</b> and may need about <b>3 to 6 months or longer</b> for maximal benefit. Tamsulosin is the drug that improves urine flow relatively quickly, by relaxing smooth muscle.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-benign-prostatic-hyperplasia", "cardLabel": "BPH, TURP and bladder irrigation"}, {"q": "A client has taken finasteride for a year and is due for a PSA test. What should the nurse know about the result?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Finasteride can lower measured PSA by about 50%; the prescriber must account for it", "Finasteride falsely raises PSA results, so a high value can be ignored while he takes it", "Finasteride has no effect on PSA", "PSA testing is no longer needed while taking finasteride"], "ans": [0], "why": "After sustained use, finasteride can <b>lower measured PSA by approximately 50%</b>. The prescribing clinician <b>must account for this</b> when interpreting the result, so the nurse makes sure the prescriber knows the client takes it.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-benign-prostatic-hyperplasia", "cardLabel": "BPH, TURP and bladder irrigation"}, {"q": "A client with symptomatic benign prostatic hyperplasia is scheduled for a transurethral resection of the prostate (TURP). Which description is accurate?", "type": "single", "opts": ["The whole prostate is removed through an abdominal incision", "Radioactive seeds are placed in the prostate", "Obstructing prostate tissue is removed through the urethra", "A needle takes a tissue sample through the rectum"], "ans": [2], "why": "A TURP <b>removes obstructing prostate tissue through the urethra</b>. It is commonly performed for <b>symptomatic BPH</b>, so there is no abdominal incision and the whole gland is not taken out.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-benign-prostatic-hyperplasia", "cardLabel": "BPH, TURP and bladder irrigation"}, {"q": "After a TURP a client has a three-way catheter with continuous bladder irrigation. What is the purpose of the irrigation?", "type": "single", "opts": ["To prevent blood clots from obstructing drainage", "To deliver antibiotics directly into the bladder lining", "To make urine output easier to measure", "To keep the bladder full while it heals"], "ans": [0], "why": "Continuous bladder irrigation helps <b>prevent blood clots from obstructing drainage</b>. The three-way catheter gives it one channel for fluid going in, one for urine and irrigation draining out, and one for the balloon.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-benign-prostatic-hyperplasia", "cardLabel": "BPH, TURP and bladder irrigation"}, {"q": "Which describes the three channels of a three-way urinary catheter used for continuous bladder irrigation?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Irrigation inflow, a medication port for antibiotics, and an air vent to prevent suction", "Urine drainage, blood sampling, and retention balloon", "Irrigation inflow, drainage of urine and irrigation fluid, and retention balloon", "Two drainage channels and a temperature probe"], "ans": [2], "why": "A three-way catheter has a channel for <b>irrigation fluid to enter</b> the bladder, a channel for <b>urine and irrigation fluid to drain</b>, and a channel for the <b>retention balloon</b>.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-benign-prostatic-hyperplasia", "cardLabel": "BPH, TURP and bladder irrigation"}, {"q": "A client is receiving continuous bladder irrigation 6 hours after a TURP. Which findings does the nurse recognize as expected? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Light pink drainage that is becoming clearer", "Large clots in the tubing", "Mild bladder discomfort or spasms", "Bright red drainage that is increasing", "Adequate catheter drainage", "Increasing suprapubic pressure"], "ans": [0, 2, 4], "why": "Expected: <b>light pink drainage that clears over time</b>, mild bladder discomfort or spasms, and adequate drainage. Unexpected: <b>bright red drainage that persists or increases</b>, large clots, decreased or absent drainage, increasing suprapubic pressure or distention, and signs of hemorrhage such as tachycardia, hypotension and pallor.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-benign-prostatic-hyperplasia", "cardLabel": "BPH, TURP and bladder irrigation"}, {"q": "A client receiving continuous bladder irrigation after a TURP reports severe suprapubic pain, and the bladder is distended. What does the nurse suspect?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Expected mild bladder spasms", "A urinary tract infection", "The irrigation is running too slowly", "Catheter obstruction with retention"], "ans": [3], "why": "Severe suprapubic pain with distention signals possible <b>retention or obstruction</b> and needs prompt assessment and intervention. <b>Do not simply increase the irrigation</b> into a bladder that is not draining; that can worsen distention and injury.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-benign-prostatic-hyperplasia", "cardLabel": "BPH, TURP and bladder irrigation"}, {"q": "A nurse suspects a clot is blocking a client's bladder irrigation catheter after a TURP. Which statement about manual irrigation is correct?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Push 50 mL of fluid through firmly until the resistance in the catheter gives way", "Only per an order or protocol, using the prescribed solution and technique", "Use any sterile fluid on hand, because speed matters most", "Manual irrigation is never allowed after prostate surgery"], "ans": [1], "why": "Manual irrigation is performed <b>only according to an order or established protocol</b>, using the prescribed solution and technique. The nurse does <b>not automatically force 50 mL against resistance</b>.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-benign-prostatic-hyperplasia", "cardLabel": "BPH, TURP and bladder irrigation"}, {"q": "During a shift, a client with continuous bladder irrigation after a TURP received 2,400 mL of irrigation fluid. The drainage bag holds 3,050 mL. What is the client's actual urine output?", "type": "single", "opts": ["5,450 mL", "3,050 mL", "650 mL", "2,400 mL"], "ans": [2], "why": "<b>Actual urine output = total drainage − irrigation infused</b>. Here 3,050 − 2,400 = <b>650 mL</b>. Charting the whole bag would count the irrigation fluid as urine, and adding the two numbers counts it twice.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-benign-prostatic-hyperplasia", "cardLabel": "BPH, TURP and bladder irrigation"}, {"q": "During some monopolar TURP procedures the client absorbs hypotonic irrigation fluid. Which problem does the nurse monitor for?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Fluid volume deficit with hypernatremia", "Fluid overload with dilutional hyponatremia", "Hypocalcemia with tetany and a positive Chvostek sign", "Hypoglycemia"], "ans": [1], "why": "TURP syndrome: absorbed hypotonic irrigation fluid may cause <b>fluid overload and dilutional hyponatremia</b>. Modern bipolar techniques often use normal saline and greatly reduce the classic hyponatremia risk, although fluid absorption and overload can still occur.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-benign-prostatic-hyperplasia", "cardLabel": "BPH, TURP and bladder irrigation"}, {"q": "A client becomes unwell soon after a TURP and the nurse suspects TURP syndrome. Which findings support this? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Confusion and restlessness", "Fever with flank pain", "Headache with nausea and vomiting", "Bradycardia", "Dry mucous membranes with poor skin turgor", "Hypertension"], "ans": [0, 2, 3, 5], "why": "TURP syndrome shows as <b>confusion or restlessness</b>, headache, nausea and vomiting, <b>hypertension and bradycardia</b>, with seizures in severe cases. It is a fluid-overload picture, so findings of dehydration do not fit.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-benign-prostatic-hyperplasia", "cardLabel": "BPH, TURP and bladder irrigation"}, {"q": "Which factors increase a client's risk for prostate cancer? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Increasing age", "Undescended testicle", "Family history", "An inherited BRCA2 variant", "Age 15 to 35", "African ancestry"], "ans": [0, 2, 3, 5], "why": "Risk factors for prostate cancer are <b>increasing age, family history</b>, certain inherited variants including <b>BRCA2</b>, and African ancestry. It commonly develops slowly and is often asymptomatic early. An undescended testicle and young adulthood go with testicular cancer.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-prostate-cancer", "cardLabel": "Prostate cancer"}, {"q": "A client with prostate cancer reports new severe back pain, leg weakness and loss of bladder control. What does the nurse suspect?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Metastatic spinal cord compression, an oncologic emergency", "An expected adverse effect of his androgen deprivation therapy", "Acute prostatitis", "A strained back muscle"], "ans": [0], "why": "New severe back pain, leg weakness, sensory changes, or bowel or bladder dysfunction in a client with prostate cancer may indicate <b>metastatic spinal cord compression</b>, which is an <b>oncologic emergency</b>.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-prostate-cancer", "cardLabel": "Prostate cancer"}, {"q": "True or false: Benign prostatic hyperplasia is malignant, similar to prostate cancer.", "type": "single", "opts": ["True", "False"], "ans": [1], "why": "<b>BPH is benign</b> and does not metastasize; it causes urinary symptoms and the prostate feels smooth on exam. Prostate cancer is often asymptomatic, <b>can metastasize</b>, and makes the prostate hard or nodular.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-benign-prostatic-hyperplasia", "cardLabel": "BPH, TURP and bladder irrigation"}, {"q": "For which client with prostate cancer is active surveillance an appropriate option?", "type": "single", "opts": ["A client whose cancer has spread to bone", "An appropriately selected client with localized, lower-risk cancer", "A client with new back pain and leg weakness", "A client with advanced disease who needs chemotherapy or newer systemic therapy"], "ans": [1], "why": "<b>Active surveillance</b> is for appropriately selected <b>localized, lower-risk</b> cancers. Other options are radical prostatectomy, radiation (external beam or brachytherapy), androgen deprivation therapy, and chemotherapy or newer systemic therapies for selected advanced disease.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-prostate-cancer", "cardLabel": "Prostate cancer"}, {"q": "How does androgen deprivation therapy (ADT) treat prostate cancer?", "type": "single", "opts": ["It suppresses testosterone signaling", "It removes the prostate surgically", "It places radioactive seeds in the gland", "It relaxes the bladder neck muscle"], "ans": [0], "why": "<b>Androgen deprivation therapy suppresses testosterone signaling</b>. Surgical removal of the prostate is a radical prostatectomy, and radioactive seeds are brachytherapy, a form of radiation therapy.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-prostate-cancer", "cardLabel": "Prostate cancer"}, {"q": "A nurse is preparing a client for a radical prostatectomy. Which statements about recovery are accurate? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Bladder control always returns the day the catheter is removed.", "Urinary incontinence may occur during recovery.", "Erectile dysfunction may occur, depending on surgical and patient factors.", "The surgery cannot affect erections.", "Bleeding, infection and blood clots are risks the team watches for."], "ans": [1, 2, 4], "why": "After radical prostatectomy the concerns are hemorrhage and hypovolemic shock, catheter obstruction, infection and venous thromboembolism. <b>Urinary incontinence may occur during recovery</b>, and <b>erectile dysfunction may occur</b> depending on surgical and patient factors.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-prostate-cancer", "cardLabel": "Prostate cancer"}, {"q": "After a radical prostatectomy a client has a urethral catheter in place. Which instruction about the catheter is correct?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Irrigate it with 50 mL of saline every shift, whether or not it is draining, to keep it patent", "Remove it once the client is walking", "Reposition it if the client finds it uncomfortable", "Do not remove, manipulate or irrigate it unless the surgical team authorizes it"], "ans": [3], "why": "The catheter <b>protects the new surgical connection between the bladder and urethra</b>. It is not removed, manipulated or irrigated <b>unless the surgical team specifically authorizes it</b>.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-prostate-cancer", "cardLabel": "Prostate cancer"}, {"q": "Which statement by a client prescribed sildenafil shows correct understanding?", "type": "single", "opts": ["It will cause an erection whether or not I am sexually stimulated.", "A headache or flushed face means I am allergic and must stop it.", "It promotes blood flow to the penis during sexual stimulation.", "I can still use my nitroglycerin with it if I get chest pain."], "ans": [2], "why": "Sildenafil <b>promotes penile blood flow during sexual stimulation</b>. Headache, flushing, dyspepsia and nasal congestion are common adverse effects, and it may cause hypotension. <b>Nitrates, including nitroglycerin, are the major contraindication</b> because the combination can cause profound hypotension.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#review-258-m11-ed", "cardLabel": "Erectile dysfunction"}, {"q": "A client taking sildenafil calls the clinic to report an erection that has lasted 4 hours. What should the nurse tell him?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Apply ice and call back tomorrow if it continues.", "This is priapism; go for emergency treatment now.", "This is an expected effect and will pass.", "Skip the next dose and mention it at your next visit."], "ans": [1], "why": "<b>Priapism</b> is a prolonged erection lasting <b>four hours or more</b>, and it <b>requires emergency treatment</b>. Waiting until tomorrow or the next visit is not safe.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#review-258-m11-ed", "cardLabel": "Erectile dysfunction"}, {"q": "Which assessment findings does the nurse expect in a client with epididymitis?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Sudden severe testicular pain with nausea and vomiting, and a high-riding testicle", "A painless testicular mass", "Gradual one-sided scrotal pain with swelling and tenderness behind the testicle", "A boggy, very tender prostate on rectal exam"], "ans": [2], "why": "Epididymitis is inflammation of the epididymis, often from urinary pathogens or STIs. Expect <b>gradually developing unilateral scrotal pain</b> with swelling and tenderness, often <b>posterior to the testicle</b>, and possibly dysuria or fever.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#review-258-m11-epididymitis", "cardLabel": "Epididymitis"}, {"q": "A nurse is triaging two clients with scrotal pain. Which finding points to testicular torsion rather than epididymitis?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Scrotal pain and swelling that have built up gradually over several days", "Tenderness mostly behind the testicle", "Burning with urination", "Sudden, severe one-sided testicular pain with nausea and vomiting"], "ans": [3], "why": "Torsion has a <b>sudden onset</b> of severe, intense unilateral testicular pain, and <b>nausea and vomiting are common</b>. Epididymitis is usually gradual, with pain that is mostly tenderness posteriorly.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-testicular-cancer", "cardLabel": "Testicular cancer"}, {"q": "True or false: Testicular cancer is the most common solid malignancy in young adult men, particularly ages 15 to 35.", "type": "single", "opts": ["True", "False"], "ans": [0], "why": "Testicular cancer is the <b>most common solid malignancy in young adult men, ages 15 to 35</b>. Risks are an undescended testicle (cryptorchidism), family history, a previous testicular cancer, and certain genetic and developmental factors.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-testicular-cancer", "cardLabel": "Testicular cancer"}, {"q": "Which history finding places a young man at increased risk for testicular cancer?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Recurrent urinary tract infections in childhood", "Taking tamsulosin", "Undescended testicle (cryptorchidism)", "Benign prostatic hyperplasia"], "ans": [2], "why": "An <b>undescended testicle (cryptorchidism)</b> is a risk for testicular cancer, along with family history, a <b>previous testicular cancer</b>, and certain genetic and developmental factors. Urinary infections, BPH and tamsulosin are not among them.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-testicular-cancer", "cardLabel": "Testicular cancer"}, {"q": "Which finding is most characteristic of testicular cancer?", "type": "single", "opts": ["A painless testicular mass or enlargement", "Sudden severe scrotal pain with vomiting", "Fever with a tender, boggy prostate", "Gradual pain and tenderness behind the testicle"], "ans": [0], "why": "The common finding is a <b>painless testicular mass or enlargement</b>. Heaviness in the scrotum, a dull ache, or back pain with advanced disease are also possible. Sudden severe pain is torsion; gradual posterior tenderness is epididymitis.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-testicular-cancer", "cardLabel": "Testicular cancer"}, {"q": "A 24-year-old client with testicular cancer is about to start treatment that may impair fertility. Which topic should the nurse raise beforehand?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Starting finasteride", "PSA testing every 6 months", "Avoiding all sexual activity for a year", "Sperm banking"], "ans": [3], "why": "<b>Sperm banking</b> is discussed <b>before</b> treatments that may impair fertility, so the conversation belongs at the start, with the other teaching about a painless testicular mass and its treatment.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-testicular-cancer", "cardLabel": "Testicular cancer"}, {"q": "True or false: A transscrotal needle biopsy should not be performed as routine evaluation of a suspected testicular malignancy.", "type": "single", "opts": ["True", "False"], "ans": [0], "why": "The risk is <b>tumor seeding</b>: a needle passed through the scrotal wall into the tumor can <b>track malignant cells along the needle pathway</b>, potentially contaminating the scrotal skin and tissue.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-testicular-cancer", "cardLabel": "Testicular cancer"}, {"q": "A transgender man who still has his cervix asks whether he needs cervical cancer screening. Which response by the nurse is correct?", "type": "single", "opts": ["No. Testosterone therapy removes the need for cervical screening altogether.", "Only if you develop symptoms such as bleeding.", "Yes. Screening is based on the organs present, so you may still need it.", "No. Screening applies only to clients who identify as women."], "ans": [2], "why": "Screening is based on <b>organs present</b>, exposures, age and individual risk factors. A transgender man who <b>retains a cervix</b> may still need cervical cancer screening, just as a transgender woman who retains a prostate may still need prostate-related evaluation.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#alt-reproductive-physiologic-processes", "cardLabel": "Reproductive physiologic processes"}, {"q": "A client taking testosterone as gender-affirming therapy says he no longer needs contraception or STI protection. Which response by the nurse is correct?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Correct. Testosterone reliably prevents pregnancy and also lowers infection risk.", "Testosterone is not reliable contraception, and STI risk remains.", "Testosterone prevents pregnancy but not infection.", "Protection is needed only during the first year of therapy."], "ans": [1], "why": "<b>Testosterone therapy is not reliable contraception</b>, and gender-affirming hormone therapy <b>does not automatically eliminate STI risk</b>. The nurse also assesses reproductive goals, contraception needs and fertility preservation when relevant.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#alt-reproductive-physiologic-processes", "cardLabel": "Reproductive physiologic processes"}, {"q": "A 63-year-old client reports vaginal bleeding several years after menopause. Which condition does the nurse expect the provider to evaluate for?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Polycystic ovary syndrome", "Endometrial cancer", "Endometriosis", "Ovarian cancer"], "ans": [1], "why": "<b>Postmenopausal bleeding</b> is the clue to <b>endometrial cancer</b>. Persistent bloating with vague pelvic or gastrointestinal symptoms suggests ovarian cancer, and postcoital bleeding with HPV suggests cervical cancer.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-cervical-ovarian-and-endometrial-cancer", "cardLabel": "Cervical, ovarian and endometrial cancer"}, {"q": "Four hours after a TURP, a client's continuous bladder irrigation is infusing, but only 50 mL has drained in the past hour and he reports growing suprapubic pressure. What is the nurse's priority action?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Increase the irrigation rate.", "Stop the irrigation inflow and assess catheter patency.", "Administer prescribed analgesics.", "Encourage the client to bear down."], "ans": [1], "why": "Fluid going in with little coming out, plus suprapubic pressure, means the catheter may be obstructed. <b>Stop the inflow and assess patency</b>. <b>Increasing the irrigation</b> into a bladder that is not draining can worsen distention and injury.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov), practice item reworded", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-benign-prostatic-hyperplasia", "cardLabel": "BPH, TURP and bladder irrigation"}, {"q": "A client with PCOS is prescribed spironolactone for excess hair growth. Which teaching is correct?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Use salt substitutes and add extra potassium-rich foods to every meal.", "It is taken as a nasal spray.", "Avoid pregnancy while taking it; it can raise your potassium level.", "It induces ovulation, so pregnancy becomes more likely."], "ans": [2], "why": "<b>Spironolactone</b> is used for excess hair in PCOS. It carries a <b>hyperkalemia risk</b>, and the client must <b>avoid pregnancy</b> while taking it. The intranasal drug on this list is nafarelin, for endometriosis.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#review-258-m11-pcos", "cardLabel": "PCOS"}, {"q": "Several hours after a hysterectomy, a client becomes hypotensive and tachycardic. What does the nurse suspect first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["An expected response to pain medication", "Urinary retention", "Blood loss", "Early menopause"], "ans": [2], "why": "<b>Hysterectomy plus hypotension and tachycardia = suspect blood loss</b>. In the same way, heavy bleeding or unstable vital signs after a TURP points to hemorrhage.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#alt-reproductive-physiologic-processes", "cardLabel": "Reproductive physiologic processes"}, {"q": "A nurse is caring for a client after a mastectomy. Which does the nurse assess for? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Bleeding", "Dilutional hyponatremia from irrigation fluid", "Drain function and output", "Signs of infection", "Urinary retention from clots", "Lymphedema"], "ans": [0, 2, 3, 5], "why": "After a mastectomy the nurse assesses <b>bleeding, drains, infection and lymphedema</b>. Dilutional hyponatremia and clot retention are concerns after a TURP with bladder irrigation, not after breast surgery.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-breast-cancer", "cardLabel": "Breast cancer"}, {"q": "A nurse is teaching a client who is scheduled for a transrectal ultrasound (TRUS). Which statement should the nurse include?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Avoid any bowel movements for 2 hours prior to the test.", "This test will determine if prostate cancer is present.", "An image of your prostate gland will be produced with this test.", "This procedure is contraindicated with an IV contrast allergy."], "ans": [2], "why": "A TRUS uses an <b>ultrasound wand inserted into the rectum to visualize the prostate</b>. It uses no contrast dye and does not determine whether cancer is present. Clients are encouraged to have a bowel movement first and may be given an enema.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov), practice item reworded", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#review-258-m11-male-tests", "cardLabel": "Male diagnostic tests"}, {"q": "A nurse in a provider's office is taking a history from a client being evaluated for benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH). Which findings are consistent with this condition? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Hematuria", "Backache", "Urinary incontinence", "Frequent urinary tract infections", "Weight loss"], "ans": [0, 2, 3], "why": "<b>Hematuria, urinary incontinence and frequent urinary tract infections</b> go with BPH; recurrent urinary tract infection is a recognized complication of the obstruction. <b>Backache and weight loss</b> fit advanced prostate cancer rather than BPH.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov), practice item reworded", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-benign-prostatic-hyperplasia", "cardLabel": "BPH, TURP and bladder irrigation"}, {"q": "A nurse is reviewing the record of a client who is menopausal. Which findings does the nurse expect? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Decreased follicle-stimulating hormone level", "Increased HDL level", "Decreased bone density", "Increased vaginal secretions", "Increased pubic hair growth", "Decreased skin elasticity"], "ans": [2, 5], "why": "Estrogen falls at menopause, so expect <b>decreased bone density</b> and <b>decreased skin elasticity</b>. FSH rises rather than falls, HDL goes down, vaginal secretions decrease, and pubic hair thins rather than grows. (The practice key listed increased pubic hair growth, but its own feedback says pubic hair decreases.)", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov), practice item reworded", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#alt-reproductive-physiologic-processes", "cardLabel": "Reproductive physiologic processes"}, {"q": "A nurse is giving discharge teaching to a client going home with an indwelling urinary catheter after an open radical prostatectomy. Which client statement shows the teaching was understood?", "type": "single", "opts": ["I will change the catheter drainage bag once each week.", "I will regain my bladder control once the catheter is removed.", "I will use suppositories to prevent constipation.", "I will be able to take a tub bath in 1 week."], "ans": [0], "why": "The <b>drainage bag is changed once each week</b>. No soaking in water such as a tub bath for 4 to 6 weeks, and <b>nothing is inserted rectally</b>. After the catheter comes out, some clients leak urine for a long time and benefit from bladder training.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov), practice item reworded", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-prostate-cancer", "cardLabel": "Prostate cancer"}, {"q": "A nurse is assessing a client 4 hours after a transurethral resection of the prostate who has an indwelling urinary catheter. Which finding does the nurse expect?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Client report of severe bladder spasms", "Blood-tinged urine in the drainage bag", "Catheter tubing coiled at the client's side", "Urinary output of 20 mL/hr"], "ans": [1], "why": "<b>Blood-tinged urine is expected</b> 4 hours after a TURP. Spasms are not uncommon but should not be severe. Coiled tubing can stop urine draining and let it flow back to the bladder. <b>20 mL/hr is not adequate</b> and suggests a possible obstruction.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov), practice item reworded", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-benign-prostatic-hyperplasia", "cardLabel": "BPH, TURP and bladder irrigation"}, {"q": "A nurse is supporting a client newly diagnosed with endometriosis. Which condition should the nurse explain is a possible complication?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Vaginitis", "Pelvic inflammatory disease", "Insulin resistance", "Infertility"], "ans": [3], "why": "Endometriosis is often a cause of <b>difficulty conceiving and infertility</b>. It does not increase the risk of vaginitis, of insulin resistance (that is PCOS), or of pelvic inflammatory disease (that follows STIs).", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov), practice item reworded", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "more/NG-415_endometriosis.html", "cardLabel": "Endometriosis"}, {"q": "A nurse is teaching a client after a vaginal hysterectomy with bilateral oophorectomy. Which information should the nurse include?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Expect bloody vaginal drainage to increase during the first 10 days after surgery.", "Use a water-based lubricant during sexual intercourse.", "Plan to start aerobic exercise such as swimming within a week after surgery.", "Plan to use some type of birth control for up to 6 weeks after surgery."], "ans": [1], "why": "Removing both ovaries puts the client into <b>medically induced menopause</b>. Menopause symptoms include <b>vaginal dryness and atrophy</b>, so a water-based lubricant is appropriate to use during sexual intercourse.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov), practice item reworded", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#alt-reproductive-physiologic-processes", "cardLabel": "Reproductive physiologic processes"}, {"q": "A nurse is teaching a client about testicular self-examination. Which statement by the client shows the teaching was understood?", "type": "single", "opts": ["\"I will palpate the testicles with my palms.\"", "\"I will examine prior to taking a warm bath.\"", "\"I will examine one testicle at a time.\"", "\"I will only report tender lumps to my doctor.\""], "ans": [2], "why": "The client examines <b>one testicle at a time</b>. The best time is <b>after</b> a warm bath or shower, not before, and the fingertips are used, not the palms. Testicular cancer is usually <b>not painful</b>, so every nodule is reported, not only tender ones.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov), practice item reworded", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-testicular-cancer", "cardLabel": "Testicular cancer"}, {"q": "A nurse is teaching a client who is about to have a cervical biopsy. Which teaching points should the nurse include? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Avoid the use of tampons for at least 2 weeks.", "Plan to rest at home for the first 72 hours.", "Avoid heavy lifting for at least 2 weeks.", "Heavy bleeding is expected for 12 hours.", "This procedure is usually painless."], "ans": [0, 2], "why": "Nothing goes in the vagina, including <b>tampons</b>, and there is <b>no heavy lifting</b> for at least 2 weeks. The biopsy can be uncomfortable, with some pain and cramping expected. Heavy bleeding is not expected and is reported right away, and rest is for 24 hours, not 72.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov), practice item reworded", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-cervical-ovarian-and-endometrial-cancer", "cardLabel": "Cervical, ovarian and endometrial cancer"}, {"q": "A client is scheduled for a panhysterectomy. Which structures does the nurse explain will be removed?", "type": "single", "opts": ["The uterus only; the cervix stays", "The uterus and cervix only", "The ovaries and fallopian tubes only; the uterus stays", "The uterus, cervix, ovaries and fallopian tubes"], "ans": [3], "why": "A <b>panhysterectomy</b> removes the uterus, cervix, ovaries and tubes. Total removes the uterus and cervix; subtotal leaves the cervix; bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy removes only the ovaries and tubes; radical also takes the upper vaginal wall, adjacent tissue and lymph nodes.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#alt-reproductive-physiologic-processes", "cardLabel": "Reproductive physiologic processes"}, {"q": "A client reports bleeding between her menstrual periods. Which term does the nurse use to document this?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Menorrhagia", "Metrorrhagia", "Dysmenorrhea", "Amenorrhea"], "ans": [1], "why": "<b>Metrorrhagia</b> is bleeding between periods. Menorrhagia is excessive bleeding with clots, dysmenorrhea is painful bleeding, and amenorrhea is the absence of menses. The names sound alike, so match each one to its own bleeding pattern.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#alt-reproductive-physiologic-processes", "cardLabel": "Reproductive physiologic processes"}, {"q": "A client reports excessive menstrual bleeding with clots. Which term describes this?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Amenorrhea", "Dysmenorrhea", "Metrorrhagia", "Menorrhagia"], "ans": [3], "why": "<b>Menorrhagia</b> is excessive menstrual bleeding, often with clots. Metrorrhagia, by contrast, is bleeding between periods; dysmenorrhea is painful bleeding; and amenorrhea is the absence of menses.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#alt-reproductive-physiologic-processes", "cardLabel": "Reproductive physiologic processes"}, {"q": "A client's premenstrual symptoms are severe. Which term names the severe form of premenstrual syndrome (PMS)?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Premenstrual dysphoric disorder (PMDD)", "Primary dysmenorrhea", "Perimenopause", "Polycystic ovary syndrome"], "ans": [0], "why": "The severe form of PMS is <b>premenstrual dysphoric disorder (PMDD)</b>. Dysmenorrhea is painful menstruation, perimenopause is the transition when ovarian function fluctuates, and polycystic ovary syndrome is a hormonal and metabolic disorder.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#alt-reproductive-physiologic-processes", "cardLabel": "Reproductive physiologic processes"}, {"q": "True or false: Pain from endometriosis is often unresponsive to NSAIDs.", "type": "single", "opts": ["True", "False"], "ans": [0], "why": "True. Endometriosis pain often does <b>not respond to NSAIDs</b>, which is why treatment also includes hormonal suppression, such as a GnRH agonist like nafarelin, and sometimes surgery.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "more/NG-415_endometriosis.html", "cardLabel": "Endometriosis"}, {"q": "A client's PSA is elevated. He was treated for a urinary tract infection three weeks ago. Which statement by the nurse is correct?", "type": "single", "opts": ["\"An elevated PSA confirms prostate cancer.\"", "\"A urinary tract infection lowers the PSA, so the true value is higher.\"", "\"A urinary tract infection can raise the PSA for up to 6 weeks; the result is not specific for cancer.\"", "\"PSA rises only with cancer, never with benign prostatic hyperplasia.\""], "ans": [2], "why": "PSA is <b>sensitive but not specific</b> for cancer. It can rise with cancer, BPH, acute urine retention and acute prostatitis, and a urinary tract infection can keep it <b>elevated for up to 6 weeks</b>.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-benign-prostatic-hyperplasia", "cardLabel": "BPH, TURP and bladder irrigation"}, {"q": "A client prescribed finasteride for benign prostatic hyperplasia lives with his pregnant partner. Which teaching point is most important?", "type": "single", "opts": ["He should expect a stronger urine stream within a day or two.", "He should take it only on days when his symptoms are worse.", "He should rise slowly because finasteride is an alpha blocker.", "His pregnant partner should not handle the tablets because the drug can harm a male fetus."], "ans": [3], "why": "Finasteride lowers DHT and shrinks the prostate but takes about <b>6 months</b> to work. It is <b>teratogenic to male fetuses</b>, so a pregnant woman should not handle it. Tamsulosin is the fast-acting alpha blocker with the orthostatic hypotension risk.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#dx-benign-prostatic-hyperplasia", "cardLabel": "BPH, TURP and bladder irrigation"}, {"q": "A client with a cystocele is being fitted with a vaginal pessary. Which statement by the client shows understanding?", "type": "single", "opts": ["\"Once I have the pessary I can stop my Kegel exercises.\"", "\"My health care provider will insert the pessary, and I will keep doing my Kegel exercises.\"", "\"The pessary will permanently repair my bladder prolapse.\"", "\"The pessary is placed during surgery under general anesthesia.\""], "ans": [1], "why": "A pessary is <b>inserted by a health care provider</b>, not by the client. It is a support tool to be used <b>together with Kegel exercises</b>; it is not the only thing the client does to support a cystocele.", "topic": "Reproductive Disorders", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🌸", "topicFull": "Reproductive Disorders", "card": "nur258-module-11-reproductive-disorders.html#review-258-m11-prolapse", "cardLabel": "Cystocele and rectocele"}], "12": [{"q": "At a mass casualty scene, which client does the nurse tag red and treat first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["An unresponsive client with agonal respirations and massive head trauma", "A client with chest burns, hoarseness and stridor", "A client with an open femur fracture and stable vital signs", "A client walking around with a superficial forearm burn"], "ans": [1], "why": "<b>Chest burns with hoarseness and stridor are red</b>: life-threatening but survivable with quick intervention. Massive head trauma with agonal respirations is black-tagged, the open femur fracture is yellow, and the ambulatory burn is green."}, {"q": "A client rescued from a house fire has headache and dizziness, and SpO2 reads 98 percent on room air. What does the nurse do?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Document the normal saturation and continue monitoring", "Apply 100 percent oxygen by non-rebreather and obtain a carboxyhemoglobin level", "Apply 2 L by nasal cannula and recheck in an hour", "Prepare for immediate intubation"], "ans": [1], "why": "In carbon monoxide poisoning the <b>pulse oximeter is worthless</b> - the probe cannot distinguish oxyhemoglobin from carboxyhemoglobin, so it reads 98 percent on a poisoned client. Treatment is <b>100 percent oxygen by non-rebreather</b> plus a blood carboxyhemoglobin level; symptoms are mostly neurologic."}, {"q": "A client swallowed a non-corrosive medication 45 minutes ago and is alert with a protected airway. Which intervention does the nurse anticipate?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Activated charcoal", "Induced vomiting", "Immediate endoscopy", "Supportive care only"], "ans": [0], "why": "<b>Activated charcoal works for medications within about 1 to 2 hours</b>, since the stomach empties in roughly 2 hours, and requires a protected airway. Corrosives get endoscopy with no charcoal and no lavage, and we never induce vomiting."}, {"q": "Using field triage criteria, which findings assign a red tag? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Respiratory rate of 34", "Capillary refill of 4 seconds", "Respiratory rate of 22", "Unable to follow simple commands", "Ambulatory with a deep laceration to the forearm"], "ans": [0, 1, 3], "why": "The field cutoffs are <b>RR under 10 or over 30, capillary refill over 2 seconds or pulse over 120, and inability to follow commands</b> - blood pressures are not taken, there is no time. RR 22 sits inside the acceptable 10 to 29 range, and a walking client with a laceration is green or yellow."}, {"q": "Three clients are admitted after a suspected anthrax exposure. Which precautions does the nurse implement?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Airborne precautions with negative pressure", "Droplet precautions with a private room", "Standard precautions; anthrax is not spread person to person", "Contact precautions with dedicated equipment"], "ans": [2], "why": "<b>Anthrax is bacterial and is not transmitted person to person, so no isolation is required</b>; treatment is ciprofloxacin or penicillin. Smallpox (airborne) and plague (droplet) are transmissible - grouping all bioterrorism agents together is the trap."}, {"q": "A client is brought in after a rattlesnake bite to the right forearm. Which action is correct?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Apply a tourniquet above the bite", "Immobilize the limb at or below heart level and remove jewelry", "Apply ice to slow venom absorption", "Elevate the arm above the heart on pillows"], "ans": [1], "why": "Keep her still with the <b>limb at or below heart level, remove jewelry before swelling</b>, and assess edema every 15 to 30 minutes; antivenom is given within 4 to 12 hours. Ice, tourniquets, cutting and elevation are all contraindicated."}, {"type": "sata", "week": "12", "step": "recognize", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty…", "q": "A nurse in the emergency department is assessing a client. Which findings should prompt the nurse to further screen for possible human trafficking? (Select all that apply.)", "opts": ["Multiple bruises and burns in various stages of healing", "The client appears frightened and avoids eye contact while a companion answers all the questions", "Unexplained abdominal pain and a frightened demeanor", "The client arrives with a well-documented, consistent medical history", "Blunt abdominal trauma and periorbital bruising", "The client requests a routine follow-up appointment in 2 weeks"], "ans": [0, 1, 2, 4], "why": "Trafficking red flags include bruises, burns, or fractures in various stages of healing, a frightened or agitated demeanor with unexplained abdominal pain or headache, blunt abdominal trauma with black eyes, and a controlling companion who answers all questions for the client. A well-documented, consistent history and a routine follow-up request are unremarkable, reassuring findings that do not raise trafficking concern.", "src": "ATI Adult Med-Surg ch 3 (Emergency Nursing Principles and Management)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing"}, {"q": "During a mass casualty incident using START triage, a client is breathing at 34 breaths per minute with a capillary refill of 4 seconds and cannot follow simple commands. Which category applies?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Green - minor", "Yellow - delayed", "Red - immediate", "Black - expectant"], "ans": [2], "why": "START tags <b>red</b> for a respiratory rate over 30, capillary refill over 2 seconds or absent radial pulse, or inability to follow commands - and this client fails all three. Red means <b>treatable now and will die without it</b>. Black is reserved for those without spontaneous respiration after the airway is opened.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty…", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing"}, {"q": "Which clients would be triaged as green (minor) in a mass casualty event? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["A client with a closed forearm fracture who is walking", "A client with superficial abrasions who is anxious but ambulatory", "A client with a tension pneumothorax", "A client with a sprained ankle who can hop with assistance", "A client in cardiac arrest"], "ans": [0, 1, 3], "why": "Green is the <b>walking wounded</b>: they can move themselves and will survive a wait. A tension pneumothorax is <b>red</b> - it is fixable in seconds with decompression. In a mass casualty, cardiac arrest is <b>black</b>, because resuscitating one person consumes the resources of many. That reversal of everyday priorities is the whole point of disaster triage.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty…", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing"}, {"q": "A client admitted after a motor vehicle crash suddenly becomes severely short of breath. The nurse notes a deviated trachea, absent breath sounds on the right, and unequal chest rise. Which action does the nurse anticipate first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Prepare for immediate needle decompression", "Send the client for a stat chest x-ray to confirm the diagnosis", "Set up for chest tube insertion and wait for the provider", "Place the client in high-Fowler's position and apply a nonrebreather mask"], "ans": [0], "why": "Tension pneumothorax is diagnosed by <b>assessment</b>, not imaging, and needle decompression comes first, with the chest tube after. Waiting for the x-ray or for the chest tube setup delays the one intervention that relieves the pressure now."}, {"q": "A client with a chest tube placed after blunt chest trauma is being repositioned when the tube is accidentally pulled out of the chest wall. Which action does the nurse take first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Clamp the tube close to the insertion site", "Cover the site with an occlusive dressing taped on three sides", "Apply dry sterile gauze and tape it down on all four sides", "Submerge the end of the tube in sterile water"], "ans": [1], "why": "A tube pulled <b>out</b> of the chest is covered with an occlusive dressing taped on three sides so air can escape but not re-enter. The sterile water seal is the fix for a <b>disconnected</b> system, not a dislodged tube, and clamping is not the answer here."}, {"q": "A client is freed after several hours pinned beneath a collapsed wall. The left leg is swollen but intact and vital signs are currently stable. Which intervention is the priority once the force has been released?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Restrict IV fluids to avoid overloading the kidneys", "Give tetanus prophylaxis before any other intervention", "Begin aggressive IV fluid resuscitation", "Apply a tourniquet proximal to the crushed area"], "ans": [2], "why": "In a crush injury, myoglobin released once the force is lifted causes <b>acute kidney injury</b>, so aggressive fluids are the priority. Restricting fluids is the opposite of what protects the kidneys, and tetanus prophylaxis matters only if the last dose was 5-10 years ago and is not the first action."}, {"q": "A client with a long history of heavy alcohol use arrives intoxicated and drowsy. The nurse has placed her side-lying with a patent airway. Point-of-care glucose is low. Which action does the nurse take next?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Give IV dextrose immediately and follow it with thiamine", "Administer flumazenil to reverse the sedation", "Withhold both until confirmatory laboratory results return", "Administer thiamine before giving glucose"], "ans": [3], "why": "In chronic drinkers, <b>thiamine comes before glucose</b>. Giving the dextrose first feels right because the glucose is low, but the order is the point of the rule; flumazenil is the benzodiazepine antidote and can precipitate seizures."}, {"q": "Several clients arrive at the ED entrance after a chemical release at a nearby plant. Which actions does the nurse take before these clients enter the treatment area? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Bring the most critically injured straight into the ED and decontaminate after stabilization", "Don personal protective equipment before any client contact", "Direct the runoff water into the ambulance bay drain", "Remove all of each client's clothing and jewelry", "Irrigate head-to-toe with copious soap and water in a dedicated decon zone"], "ans": [1, 3, 4], "why": "PPE protects the nurse first, removing clothing and jewelry alone removes most of the contaminant, and copious head-to-toe irrigation happens in a dedicated decon zone. Taking the sickest client in undecontaminated is the tempting <b>everyday</b> priority but it contaminates the ED, and the runoff must be contained."}, {"q": "A hospital-wide disaster alert is announced and the charge nurse must free medical-surgical beds. Which clients does the nurse identify as appropriate for early discharge? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["A client who required needle decompression two hours ago", "A client already awaiting discharge paperwork", "A client whose chest tube water seal is bubbling continuously", "A day-surgery client who is awake and ambulating", "A stable client with a well-controlled chronic condition"], "ans": [1, 3, 4], "why": "The clients discharged first are the <b>most stable</b> - those already awaiting discharge, day-surgery clients, and stable chronic clients. Continuous water-seal bubbling signals an air leak and the post-decompression client is still unstable, so neither is stable enough to leave despite having a plan of care that looks routine."}, {"q": "Using the three-level triage system, a nurse sees a client who is not in immediate danger but needs treatment promptly, within about 2 hours. Which category applies?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Emergent", "Urgent", "Nonurgent", "Expectant"], "ans": [1], "why": "The three basic levels are <b>emergent</b> (immediate, life-threatening), <b>urgent</b> (needs prompt treatment, within 2 hours) and <b>nonurgent</b> (stable, treatment can wait). A client who must be treated soon but has no immediate threat to life is urgent.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#dx-triage-and-mass-casualty-response", "cardLabel": "Triage and mass casualty response"}, {"q": "In the five-level Emergency Severity Index (ESI), what separates stable clients into levels 3, 4 and 5?", "type": "single", "opts": ["How long the client has already waited", "The client's age", "Whether the client arrived by ambulance or was brought in by family", "The number of resources the client is expected to need"], "ans": [3], "why": "ESI 1 is resuscitation and ESI 2 is high risk, such as an altered mental state or severe pain or distress. Stable clients are then sorted by <b>resources</b>: level 3 needs <b>two or more</b>, level 4 needs <b>one</b>, and level 5 needs none, an exam only.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#dx-triage-and-mass-casualty-response", "cardLabel": "Triage and mass casualty response"}, {"q": "A triage nurse is using the Emergency Severity Index (ESI). Which client is assigned ESI level 1?", "type": "single", "opts": ["A client who is unresponsive and apneic", "A client with a suspected acute stroke who is protecting the airway", "A client with stable chest pain suspicious for acute coronary syndrome", "A client with a simple laceration that needs sutures"], "ans": [0], "why": "<b>ESI 1 is resuscitation</b>: an unresponsive, apneic client, or severe respiratory distress needing immediate assisted ventilation. A suspected stroke protecting the airway and stable chest pain suspicious for acute coronary syndrome are <b>ESI 2</b>. A simple laceration needing sutures is ESI 4.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#dx-triage-and-mass-casualty-response", "cardLabel": "Triage and mass casualty response"}, {"q": "A client has stable abdominal pain and will need laboratory tests, IV fluids and a CT scan. Which Emergency Severity Index (ESI) level does the triage nurse assign?", "type": "single", "opts": ["ESI 2", "ESI 3", "ESI 4", "ESI 5"], "ans": [1], "why": "The client is stable but needs several resources (labs, IV fluids and CT), which is <b>ESI 3</b>. ESI 4 is one resource, such as sutures for a simple laceration. ESI 5 needs no resources, such as a prescription refill without symptoms. ESI 2 is a high-risk client.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#dx-triage-and-mass-casualty-response", "cardLabel": "Triage and mass casualty response"}, {"q": "A trauma client arrives with catastrophic external bleeding from a leg wound, and the team follows the XABCDE sequence. What does this approach put first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Finishing the full airway and breathing assessment before any team member touches the bleeding wound", "Obtaining the AMPLE history before any intervention", "Immediate control of the exsanguinating bleeding while the team manages airway and breathing", "Removing all clothing to look for other injuries"], "ans": [2], "why": "With <b>catastrophic external hemorrhage</b>, trauma protocols may use <b>XABCDE</b> or C-ABC. These put immediate control of exsanguinating bleeding first, while the rest of the team manages airway and breathing at the same time.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#review-258-m12-trauma", "cardLabel": "Primary survey and trauma"}, {"q": "A nurse hears gurgling as an unresponsive client breathes. What does this sound indicate, and what should the nurse do?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Bronchospasm; give a bronchodilator and recheck in 30 minutes", "Normal deep sleep; let the client rest", "Air in the stomach; insert a nasogastric tube", "Secretions or fluid in the airway; suction"], "ans": [3], "why": "<b>Gurgling</b> means secretions or fluid in the airway, so the nurse <b>suctions</b>. The other airway red flags: stridor is upper airway obstruction, snoring in an unconscious client suggests tongue obstruction, and decreased consciousness can mean loss of the protective airway reflexes.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#review-258-m12-trauma", "cardLabel": "Primary survey and trauma"}, {"q": "An unconscious client in the emergency department has snoring respirations. Which problem does the nurse suspect?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Fluid in the lower airways", "Possible obstruction of the airway by the tongue", "Narrowing of the small bronchioles", "An expected finding that needs no action"], "ans": [1], "why": "<b>Snoring in an unconscious client</b> points to possible <b>tongue obstruction</b> of the airway. Gurgling is the sound of secretions or fluid, and stridor signals upper airway obstruction. Decreased consciousness itself can mean the protective airway reflexes are lost.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#review-258-m12-trauma", "cardLabel": "Primary survey and trauma"}, {"q": "A client with a head injury has a Glasgow Coma Scale (GCS) score of 8. What does this score prompt the nurse to do?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Recheck the score in 4 hours, since it shows mild impairment", "Prepare for intubation in every case, whatever ventilation, oxygenation and the other findings show", "Assess the airway urgently, because the score shows severe neurological impairment", "Continue routine monitoring, since a score of 8 is expected after trauma"], "ans": [2], "why": "A GCS of <b>8 or lower</b> indicates severe neurological impairment and prompts <b>urgent airway assessment</b>. Intubation is not automatic: that decision also depends on ventilation, oxygenation and whether the client can protect the airway.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#review-258-m12-trauma", "cardLabel": "Primary survey and trauma"}, {"q": "A nurse is monitoring a trauma client with ongoing blood loss. Which finding is often a late sign of hemorrhagic shock?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Hypotension", "Tachycardia", "Cool, clammy skin", "Delayed capillary refill"], "ans": [0], "why": "<b>Hypotension is often a late sign</b> of hemorrhagic shock. Tachycardia, cool clammy skin, delayed capillary refill and altered mental status may appear <b>earlier</b>, so those are the changes to catch before the blood pressure falls.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#review-258-m12-trauma", "cardLabel": "Primary survey and trauma"}, {"q": "During the Disability step of the ABCDE primary survey, which assessments does the nurse perform? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Level of consciousness using AVPU", "Pupil response", "Last oral intake", "Blood glucose", "Movement", "Breath sounds"], "ans": [0, 1, 3, 4], "why": "Disability uses <b>AVPU</b>: Alert, responds to Verbal stimulation, responds to Painful stimulation, Unresponsive. The nurse also assesses <b>pupil response, movement and blood glucose</b>, and GCS when appropriate. Last oral intake belongs to the AMPLE history, and breath sounds belong to the Breathing step.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#review-258-m12-trauma", "cardLabel": "Primary survey and trauma"}, {"q": "The nurse is triaging five clients in the emergency department. Using the components of the primary survey, in which order does the nurse assess them, first to last? 1) arterial laceration with external hemorrhage after a car crash, 2) facial trauma from a fistfight, 3) COPD exacerbation, 4) altered mental status, 5) ankle fracture from soccer practice", "type": "single", "opts": ["1 (hemorrhage), 2 (facial trauma), 3 (COPD), 4 (altered mental status), 5 (ankle fracture)", "2 (facial trauma), 3 (COPD), 1 (hemorrhage), 4 (altered mental status), 5 (ankle fracture)", "2 (facial trauma), 1 (hemorrhage), 3 (COPD), 5 (ankle fracture), 4 (altered mental status)", "4 (altered mental status), 1 (hemorrhage), 2 (facial trauma), 3 (COPD), 5 (ankle fracture)"], "ans": [0], "why": "The order follows the primary survey, with <b>catastrophic bleeding first</b> (XABCDE): <b>1</b> an arterial bleed can empty the circulation in minutes, so it is stopped before anything else; <b>2</b> facial trauma threatens the <b>Airway</b>; <b>3</b> a COPD exacerbation is a <b>Breathing</b> problem; <b>4</b> altered mental status is <b>Disability</b>; <b>5</b> an ankle fracture is found on <b>Exposure</b> and is stable, so it comes last. Without exsanguinating bleeding the classic order is plain ABCDE, airway first.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 practice question (24 Nov), reworded from drag-and-drop ordering", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#review-258-m12-trauma", "cardLabel": "Primary survey and trauma"}, {"q": "A trauma client's Glasgow Coma Scale score suddenly falls from 14 to 9. How should the nurse respond?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Document the change and recheck in 1 hour", "Treat it as an emergency and immediately reassess airway, oxygenation and perfusion", "Finish the secondary survey before reassessing", "Assume it is from pain medication and continue monitoring"], "ans": [1], "why": "A <b>sudden decrease in GCS is an emergency</b>. It requires immediate reassessment of airway, oxygenation and perfusion, and it raises concern for possible <b>intracranial pathology</b>. Waiting an hour or finishing other tasks first delays that reassessment.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#review-258-m12-trauma", "cardLabel": "Primary survey and trauma"}, {"q": "Which three problems make up the lethal triad that worsens bleeding and mortality in trauma clients?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Hypotension, jugular venous distention and muffled heart sounds", "Hyperthermia, alkalosis and thrombosis", "Hypothermia, acidosis and coagulopathy", "Pallor, paralysis and pulselessness"], "ans": [2], "why": "Trauma clients are vulnerable to the <b>lethal triad</b> of <b>hypothermia, acidosis and coagulopathy</b>, which worsens bleeding and mortality. Hypotension, JVD and muffled heart sounds are the Beck triad of cardiac tamponade; pallor, paralysis and pulselessness are late findings of compartment syndrome.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#review-258-m12-trauma", "cardLabel": "Primary survey and trauma"}, {"q": "When does the nurse perform the secondary survey on a trauma client?", "type": "single", "opts": ["As the very first step on arrival", "Only if the primary survey finds no injuries", "After immediate life threats have been addressed", "Only after the client has been transferred to an inpatient unit"], "ans": [2], "why": "The <b>secondary survey</b> is a comprehensive head-to-toe assessment performed <b>after immediate life threats have been addressed</b>. It includes the AMPLE history: Allergies, Medications, Past medical history and pregnancy, Last oral intake, and Events leading to the illness or injury.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#review-258-m12-trauma", "cardLabel": "Primary survey and trauma"}, {"q": "A nurse is collecting an AMPLE history from a trauma client. What does the L stand for?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Level of consciousness", "Last oral intake", "Location of pain", "Last tetanus immunization"], "ans": [1], "why": "AMPLE is Allergies, Medications, Past medical history and pregnancy, <b>Last oral intake</b>, and Events leading to the illness or injury. It is gathered during the <b>secondary survey</b>, the head-to-toe assessment done after immediate life threats are addressed.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#review-258-m12-trauma", "cardLabel": "Primary survey and trauma"}, {"q": "After a motor vehicle crash a client has BP 78/50, HR 150, RR 40, abdominal bruising, open bilateral femur fractures and a GCS of 6. Which interpretations are correct? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["BP 78/50 shows severe hypotension, likely shock", "HR 150 is compensatory tachycardia", "GCS 6 means mild impairment with a secure airway", "Abdominal bruising suggests possible internal hemorrhage", "Open bilateral femur fractures carry catastrophic hemorrhage and infection risk", "RR 40 is an expected response that needs no follow-up"], "ans": [0, 1, 3, 4], "why": "BP 78/50 is <b>severe hypotension, likely shock</b>, and HR 150 is compensatory tachycardia. Abdominal bruising suggests internal hemorrhage, and open bilateral femur fractures mean catastrophic hemorrhage and infection risk. <b>GCS 6</b> is severe neurological dysfunction with airway risk, and RR 40 signals respiratory distress, shock, acidosis or chest injury.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#review-258-m12-trauma", "cardLabel": "Primary survey and trauma"}, {"q": "A client with chest trauma has diminished breath sounds on one side and pleuritic pain. Which additional findings suggest a tension pneumothorax rather than a simple pneumothorax?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Paradoxical movement of part of the chest wall", "Dullness to percussion over the affected side", "Crackles with slowly worsening oxygen levels", "Severe distress, hypotension and absent breath sounds on one side, possibly with JVD"], "ans": [3], "why": "A <b>simple pneumothorax</b> causes unilateral diminished breath sounds and pleuritic pain. A <b>tension pneumothorax</b> adds severe distress, hypotension, absent unilateral breath sounds and possible JVD or tracheal deviation, and needs immediate decompression, then a chest tube. Paradoxical movement is flail chest; dullness is hemothorax.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#review-258-m12-crush-chest", "cardLabel": "Crush injury and chest trauma"}, {"q": "A stable client is diagnosed with a simple pneumothorax. Which treatment plan does the nurse anticipate?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Oxygen as needed, with observation or a chest tube depending on severity", "Urgent drainage of the pericardium", "Blood resuscitation and immediate surgery", "Immediate surgical and vascular evaluation for a suspected injury to the aorta"], "ans": [0], "why": "A simple pneumothorax is treated with <b>oxygen as needed and observation or a chest tube</b>, depending on severity. Immediate decompression followed by a chest tube is for a <b>tension</b> pneumothorax, which shows severe distress and hypotension. Drainage of the pericardium is for tamponade.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#review-258-m12-crush-chest", "cardLabel": "Crush injury and chest trauma"}, {"q": "After blunt chest trauma a client has diminished breath sounds, dullness to percussion on the left and signs of shock. Which treatment does the nurse prepare for?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Analgesia and observation only", "Chest tube insertion and blood resuscitation, with surgery if needed", "Urgent drainage of the pericardium", "Supportive oxygen only, with no drainage"], "ans": [1], "why": "Diminished breath sounds, <b>dullness to percussion</b> and shock point to <b>hemothorax</b>. Treatment is a chest tube and blood resuscitation, with surgery if needed. Flail chest, by contrast, shows paradoxical chest movement and is treated with oxygen, analgesia and ventilatory support.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#review-258-m12-trauma", "cardLabel": "Primary survey and trauma"}, {"q": "A client with multiple rib fractures has a section of chest wall that moves paradoxically, with pain and hypoxemia. Which interventions does the nurse anticipate?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Immediate needle decompression", "Urgent drainage of the pericardium", "Oxygen, analgesia and ventilatory support", "Withholding analgesia so breathing is not depressed"], "ans": [2], "why": "<b>Paradoxical chest movement</b> with pain and hypoxemia is <b>flail chest</b>. It is treated with oxygen, analgesia and ventilatory support. Decompression is for tension pneumothorax, and drainage of the pericardium is for cardiac tamponade.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#review-258-m12-trauma", "cardLabel": "Primary survey and trauma"}, {"q": "A client with chest trauma has hypotension, jugular venous distention and muffled heart sounds. Which condition and treatment does the nurse anticipate?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Hemothorax; chest tube and blood resuscitation", "Pulmonary contusion; supportive oxygenation", "Cardiac tamponade; urgent drainage", "Flail chest; analgesia and ventilatory support"], "ans": [2], "why": "Hypotension, JVD and muffled heart sounds are the classic <b>Beck triad</b> of <b>cardiac tamponade</b>, which needs urgent drainage and definitive treatment. Hemothorax shows dullness to percussion, pulmonary contusion shows crackles and hypoxemia, and flail chest shows paradoxical chest movement.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#review-258-m12-crush-chest", "cardLabel": "Crush injury and chest trauma"}, {"q": "After blunt chest trauma a client develops hypoxemia, crackles and a worsening respiratory status. Which injury does the nurse suspect, and how is it managed?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Aortic injury; immediate evaluation by the surgical or vascular team", "Cardiac tamponade; urgent drainage", "Simple pneumothorax; observation only", "Pulmonary contusion; supportive oxygenation and ventilation"], "ans": [3], "why": "<b>Pulmonary contusion</b> causes hypoxemia, crackles and worsening respiratory status, and is managed with <b>supportive oxygenation and ventilation</b>. Aortic injury follows high-energy trauma with chest or back pain and shock and needs immediate surgical or vascular evaluation. Tamponade shows muffled heart sounds.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#review-258-m12-trauma", "cardLabel": "Primary survey and trauma"}, {"q": "A trauma client is hypotensive and tachycardic with jugular venous distention. Which finding points to cardiac tamponade rather than tension pneumothorax?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Absent breath sounds on one side", "Muffled heart sounds", "Tracheal deviation", "Dullness to percussion on one side"], "ans": [1], "why": "Both cause <b>obstructive shock</b> with hypotension, tachycardia and JVD. <b>Tension pneumothorax</b> adds unilateral absent breath sounds, respiratory distress and possible tracheal deviation. <b>Cardiac tamponade</b> has muffled heart sounds and may show pulsus paradoxus.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#review-258-m12-crush-chest", "cardLabel": "Crush injury and chest trauma"}, {"q": "A client with blunt abdominal trauma and no shoulder injury reports new shoulder pain. How does the nurse interpret this finding?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Referred pain from irritation of the diaphragm", "Muscle strain from lying on the stretcher", "Anxiety about the injury", "An expected finding that rules out internal injury"], "ans": [0], "why": "<b>Referred shoulder pain</b> in abdominal trauma comes from <b>diaphragmatic irritation</b>. Other common findings are abdominal tenderness or rigidity, distention, bruising or penetrating wounds, and tachycardia, hypotension or other signs of shock.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#review-258-m12-trauma", "cardLabel": "Primary survey and trauma"}, {"q": "A nurse is caring for a client with blunt abdominal trauma. Which interventions are appropriate? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Maintain airway, breathing and circulation, with spinal precautions as indicated", "Offer clear liquids to maintain hydration", "Establish IV access and monitor hemodynamics", "Keep the client NPO", "Send an unstable client for CT before any surgical evaluation", "Prepare for a FAST exam"], "ans": [0, 2, 3, 5], "why": "Care is <b>ABCs with spinal precautions</b>, <b>NPO</b>, IV access and hemodynamic monitoring. The nurse prepares for focused assessment with sonography for trauma (FAST), <b>CT if the client is stable, or urgent surgery if unstable</b>, documents wounds and gives prescribed prophylaxis. Oral fluids break NPO status.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#review-258-m12-trauma", "cardLabel": "Primary survey and trauma"}, {"q": "A client arrives with abdominal organs protruding through a wound. Which action should the nurse take?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Gently push the organs back into the abdomen", "Cover the organs with a dry gauze dressing", "Cover the organs with sterile saline-moistened dressings", "Leave the organs uncovered and untouched until the surgeon arrives"], "ans": [2], "why": "For <b>evisceration</b>, the exposed abdominal organs are covered with <b>sterile saline-moistened dressings</b>. The nurse does <b>not</b> attempt to replace the organs into the abdomen. The dressing is moist, not dry.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#review-258-m12-trauma", "cardLabel": "Primary survey and trauma"}, {"q": "A client is freed after a prolonged crush injury. Which substances released from the damaged muscle cells create the main dangers of rhabdomyolysis?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Sodium, which causes cerebral edema, and bicarbonate, which causes alkalosis", "Myoglobin, which injures the renal tubules, and potassium, which can cause fatal dysrhythmias", "Bilirubin, which causes jaundice, and ammonia, which causes confusion", "Calcium, which causes kidney stones, and glucose, which causes hyperglycemia"], "ans": [1], "why": "Prolonged or severe compression damages muscle, and the damaged cells release <b>myoglobin and potassium</b>. Myoglobin injures the renal tubules, leading to <b>acute kidney injury</b>. Potassium causes hyperkalemia, which can cause <b>fatal dysrhythmias</b>.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#review-258-m12-crush-chest", "cardLabel": "Crush injury and chest trauma"}, {"q": "Which findings does the nurse expect in a client with rhabdomyolysis? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Dark, tea-colored urine", "Large amounts of pale, dilute urine", "Elevated creatine kinase (CK)", "Hyperkalemia", "Jaundice with clay-colored stools", "Muscle pain, swelling and weakness"], "ans": [0, 2, 3, 5], "why": "Key findings are <b>dark, tea-colored urine</b> from myoglobinuria, <b>elevated CK</b>, hyperkalemia, and muscle pain, swelling and weakness. Acute kidney injury and compartment syndrome can follow. Pale dilute urine and jaundice with clay-colored stools are not part of this picture.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "musc/NG-392_rhabdomyolysis.html", "cardLabel": "Rhabdomyolysis"}, {"q": "A client has rhabdomyolysis after a crush injury to the leg, which is now in a cast. Once airway, breathing, hemorrhage and shock are addressed, which actions are nursing priorities? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Initiate ECG monitoring", "Restrict IV fluids to rest the kidneys", "Give isotonic IV fluids as prescribed, individualized to volume status", "Monitor potassium, creatinine, CK, urine output and acid-base status", "Wait for the next scheduled labs before treating dangerous hyperkalemia", "Assess for compartment syndrome"], "ans": [0, 2, 3, 5], "why": "Priorities are <b>ECG monitoring</b>, <b>isotonic IV fluids</b> as prescribed and individualized to volume status, monitoring potassium, creatinine, CK, urine output and acid-base status, and assessing for compartment syndrome, which matters more with a <b>cast</b> on: the cast can hide swelling and squeeze the leg, so check for pain on passive stretch and tense swelling, and if they appear, notify the provider and loosen or bivalve the cast as ordered. Dangerous <b>hyperkalemia is treated immediately</b>, not at the next lab draw, and fluids are given, not restricted.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#review-258-m12-crush-chest", "cardLabel": "Crush injury and chest trauma"}, {"q": "A client trapped beneath a car has neck swelling, decreased right-sided air entry, muffled heart sounds, GCS 8, BP 82/55, HR 143, RR 36 and SpO2 82%. What is the priority?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Complete the head-to-toe secondary survey and AMPLE history first", "Immediate trauma resuscitation: cervical spine protection, airway, oxygenation and ventilation, and treatment of shock", "Send the client to CT before any intervention", "Collect a urine sample to check for myoglobin first"], "ans": [1], "why": "The priority is <b>immediate trauma resuscitation</b> with cervical spine protection, airway management, oxygenation and ventilation, evaluation for <b>tension pneumothorax and tamponade</b>, and treatment of shock. Dark urine and elevated potassium would suggest rhabdomyolysis, but the life threats are managed first.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#review-258-m12-crush-chest", "cardLabel": "Crush injury and chest trauma"}, {"q": "A landscaper working outdoors has cool, clammy, sweaty skin, tachycardia and a core temperature of 102.6°F, and is alert and oriented. Which condition does the nurse suspect?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Heat stroke", "Mild hypothermia", "Heat exhaustion", "Carbon monoxide poisoning"], "ans": [2], "why": "<b>Heat exhaustion</b>: core temperature below 104°F, cool, clammy, sweaty skin, tachycardia and a normal or decreased blood pressure. <b>Heat stroke</b> is above 104°F with central nervous system dysfunction, so an alert, oriented client below 104°F fits heat exhaustion.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#dx-environmental-emergencies", "cardLabel": "Environmental emergencies"}, {"q": "A client with heat stroke arrives in the emergency department. Which interventions are appropriate? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Begin rapid active cooling, preferably cold-water immersion when feasible and safe", "Give acetaminophen to bring the temperature down", "Monitor core temperature continuously, preferably with a rectal probe", "Give IV isotonic fluids when indicated and watch for fluid overload", "Hold cooling until laboratory results are back", "Use evaporative cooling, cooling blankets and ice packs when immersion is not possible"], "ans": [0, 2, 3, 5], "why": "The client is moved from the heat and <b>rapid active cooling</b> begins, preferably cold-water immersion, or evaporative cooling, cooling blankets and ice packs. Core temperature is monitored continuously, preferably by <b>rectal probe</b>, with isotonic IV fluids when indicated. Antipyretics are ineffective, and cooling is never delayed for labs.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#dx-environmental-emergencies", "cardLabel": "Environmental emergencies"}, {"q": "During active cooling for heat stroke, at about which core temperature is cooling commonly stopped, and why?", "type": "single", "opts": ["About 36°C (96.8°F), so there is no chance that the temperature rebounds afterward", "About 38.6 to 39°C (101.5 to 102.2°F), to prevent overshoot hypothermia", "40.5°C (105°F), as soon as the client begins to sweat", "35°C (95°F), to leave a safety margin"], "ans": [1], "why": "The temperature is brought down rapidly to about <b>39.2°C (102°F)</b>. Current cooling protocols commonly stop active cooling around 38.6 to 39°C (101.5 to 102.2°F) to prevent <b>overshoot hypothermia</b>, which is the risk of cooling any further.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#dx-environmental-emergencies", "cardLabel": "Environmental emergencies"}, {"q": "Why is acetaminophen not used to lower the temperature of a client with heat stroke?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Heat stroke is not caused by a raised hypothalamic set point, so antipyretics are ineffective", "It is toxic to the kidneys at any dose", "It works too slowly by mouth but works well intravenously", "It causes shivering that raises the temperature"], "ans": [0], "why": "<b>Antipyretics are ineffective</b> because an elevated hypothalamic fever set point does not cause heat stroke. The treatment is <b>cooling</b>, which is never delayed while waiting for laboratory results. A client with altered consciousness also should not receive oral fluids.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#dx-environmental-emergencies", "cardLabel": "Environmental emergencies"}, {"q": "A confused client has a core temperature of 105.4°F after working in the heat and is still sweating. How does the nurse interpret the sweating?", "type": "single", "opts": ["It rules out heat stroke", "It means the client is cooling adequately without help", "It does not rule out heat stroke", "It shows the client only needs oral fluids"], "ans": [2], "why": "<b>Sweating does not rule out heat stroke.</b> A temperature above 104°F with a change in level of consciousness is heat stroke, so cooling is not delayed. Because consciousness is altered, the client should <b>not receive oral fluids</b>.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#dx-environmental-emergencies", "cardLabel": "Environmental emergencies"}, {"q": "A client with frostbite of the fingers has hemorrhagic blisters and deeper tissue damage. Which degree of frostbite is this?", "type": "single", "opts": ["First degree", "Second degree", "Third degree", "Fourth degree"], "ans": [2], "why": "First degree: superficial numbness, pallor, erythema and mild edema with <b>no blistering</b>. Second: <b>clear blisters</b> and swelling. Third: <b>hemorrhagic blisters</b> and deeper tissue damage. Fourth: full-thickness injury with tissue necrosis and possible gangrene.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "skin/NG-116_frostbite.html", "cardLabel": "Frostbite"}, {"q": "Shortly after arrival, a client with frostbite asks how severe the injury is. Which response by the nurse is accurate?", "type": "single", "opts": ["\"If there are no blisters now, the injury is only superficial.\"", "\"The skin color right now tells us the final depth.\"", "\"All frostbite heals fully once the area is warm.\"", "\"The depth of the injury may not be fully apparent for 24 hours or longer.\""], "ans": [3], "why": "Frostbite depth <b>may not be fully apparent for 24 hours or longer</b>, so an early look cannot give the final answer. The degrees run from superficial numbness and pallor without blisters to full-thickness injury with necrosis and possible gangrene.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "skin/NG-116_frostbite.html", "cardLabel": "Frostbite"}, {"q": "Refreezing can be prevented for a client with frostbitten feet. Which rewarming method does the nurse use?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Rapid rewarming in circulating water at about 37 to 39°C (98.6 to 102.2°F)", "Dry heat from a heating pad", "Brisk massage of the feet with warm hands", "Slow thawing at room temperature over several hours"], "ans": [0], "why": "When refreezing can be prevented, the area is <b>rapidly rewarmed in circulating water</b> at about 37 to 39°C. Rewarming is painful, so <b>analgesia</b> is given. Rubbing or massaging and direct dry heat such as heating pads are never used on frozen tissue.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "skin/NG-116_frostbite.html", "cardLabel": "Frostbite"}, {"q": "Which actions belong in the care of a client with frostbite of the hands? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Address hypothermia and life threats first", "Keep the hands hanging down after thawing", "Remove constricting jewelry and wet clothing", "Give analgesia because rewarming is painful", "Wrap the hands tightly to limit swelling", "Assess tetanus immunization status"], "ans": [0, 2, 3, 5], "why": "<b>Hypothermia and life threats come first.</b> Then remove constricting jewelry and wet clothing, protect the area, rewarm, give analgesia, <b>elevate after thawing</b>, monitor perfusion and compartment syndrome, and check tetanus status. Hanging the hands down and tight wraps work against elevation and perfusion.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "skin/NG-116_frostbite.html", "cardLabel": "Frostbite"}, {"q": "A hiker with frostbitten toes is still several hours from shelter, and the toes are likely to refreeze on the way. Which instruction is correct?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Rub the toes briskly to restore circulation", "Warm the toes with a heating pad or other dry heat", "Thaw the toes now, then keep walking", "Do not thaw the toes while refreezing is likely"], "ans": [3], "why": "Tissue is <b>not thawed when refreezing is likely</b>. Frozen tissue is never rubbed or massaged, and direct dry heat such as heating pads is not applied. Walking on frostbitten feet is avoided unless it is necessary for safety.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "skin/NG-116_frostbite.html", "cardLabel": "Frostbite"}, {"q": "A client found outdoors has a core temperature of 30°C (86°F), a decreased level of consciousness, bradycardia and reduced shivering. How does the nurse classify this hypothermia?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Mild", "Moderate", "Severe", "Not hypothermia"], "ans": [1], "why": "Hypothermia is a core temperature <b>below 35°C (95°F)</b>. Mild is 32 to 35°C with shivering, tachycardia and impaired coordination. <b>Moderate is 28 to 32°C</b> with decreased consciousness, bradycardia and reduced shivering. Severe is below 28°C with profound unconsciousness and serious dysrhythmias.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "core/NG-041_hypothermia.html", "cardLabel": "Hypothermia"}, {"q": "Which findings does the nurse expect in a client with mild hypothermia?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Shivering, tachycardia and impaired coordination", "Decreased consciousness, bradycardia and reduced shivering", "Profound unconsciousness and serious dysrhythmias", "Hot, dry skin and confusion"], "ans": [0], "why": "<b>Mild hypothermia</b> (32 to 35°C) shows shivering, tachycardia and impaired coordination. As it worsens to moderate, consciousness decreases, the heart slows and <b>shivering lessens</b>. Severe hypothermia brings profound unconsciousness, serious dysrhythmias and possible cardiac arrest.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "core/NG-041_hypothermia.html", "cardLabel": "Hypothermia"}, {"q": "A client with mild hypothermia is fully alert, shivering and able to swallow. Which care does the nurse provide?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Extracorporeal rewarming", "Vigorous rubbing of the arms and legs", "Removal of wet clothing, dry blankets, a warm environment and warm oral fluids", "A warm alcoholic drink to speed rewarming"], "ans": [2], "why": "Mild hypothermia is managed by removing wet clothing and <b>passive external warming</b> with dry blankets and a warm environment. <b>Warm oral fluids</b> are given only if the client is fully alert and able to swallow. Extracorporeal rewarming is considered for severe instability or arrest.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "core/NG-041_hypothermia.html", "cardLabel": "Hypothermia"}, {"q": "A client has moderate to severe hypothermia. Which interventions are appropriate? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Monitor the cardiac rhythm continuously", "Give warmed IV fluids and warmed, humidified oxygen", "Rub the arms and legs briskly to speed warming", "Use active external or internal rewarming based on severity", "Have the client walk the hallway to generate body heat", "Handle the client gently"], "ans": [0, 1, 3, 5], "why": "The client is <b>handled gently</b> to minimize dysrhythmia risk, with continuous cardiac monitoring, warmed IV fluids and warmed humidified oxygen, and <b>active external or internal rewarming</b> based on severity. Extracorporeal rewarming is considered for severe instability or arrest. Rough movement can precipitate ventricular dysrhythmias.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "core/NG-041_hypothermia.html", "cardLabel": "Hypothermia"}, {"q": "A nurse cannot easily find a pulse on a severely hypothermic client. Which action is correct?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Follow hypothermic resuscitation protocols rather than assuming death", "Stop care, because no pulse felt quickly means death", "Shake the client firmly to check responsiveness", "Wait for the client to warm unaided for an hour before assessing for a pulse again"], "ans": [0], "why": "Severely hypothermic clients may have <b>very slow, difficult-to-detect pulses</b>, and bradycardia is expected as hypothermia worsens. The nurse follows <b>hypothermic resuscitation protocols</b> rather than prematurely assuming death. Rough movement can precipitate ventricular dysrhythmias.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "core/NG-041_hypothermia.html", "cardLabel": "Hypothermia"}, {"q": "A client has swallowed a poison. After airway, breathing and circulation are supported and the substance is identified, which action should the nurse avoid?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Considering an antidote", "Considering activated charcoal when indicated", "Inducing vomiting", "Finding out when the substance was taken"], "ans": [2], "why": "Priorities are ABCs, identifying the substance, and considering an antidote or activated charcoal when indicated. <b>Inducing vomiting is avoided</b>: the substance can harm the linings again <b>on its way back up</b>.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#dx-poisoning-and-overdose", "cardLabel": "Poisoning and overdose"}, {"q": "A worker has collapsed in a room filled with toxic fumes. Which sequence of actions is correct?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Enter at once without protection to begin care at the worker's side", "Remove the worker from the exposure safely, then give oxygen and respiratory support", "Give activated charcoal as soon as the worker can swallow", "Induce vomiting to clear the toxin"], "ans": [1], "why": "For <b>inhaled poisons</b>, the client is removed from the exposure <b>safely</b> and given oxygen and respiratory support. Rescuers avoid entering a contaminated area without protection. Charcoal acts in the gastrointestinal tract, so it does not help with an inhaled toxin.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#dx-poisoning-and-overdose", "cardLabel": "Poisoning and overdose"}, {"q": "A client arrives after a chemical splashed into both eyes. What does the nurse do first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Complete a full eye examination, then irrigate", "Patch both eyes and wait for the provider", "Apply an antibiotic ointment", "Begin copious irrigation immediately"], "ans": [3], "why": "<b>Eye exposure</b> calls for <b>immediate copious irrigation</b>, and the nurse avoids delaying irrigation for an examination. On the same card: foodborne toxins need hydration, electrolytes and monitoring of respiratory status, and antimotility drugs are not used automatically.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#dx-poisoning-and-overdose", "cardLabel": "Poisoning and overdose"}, {"q": "A nurse is reviewing when activated charcoal will help. Which ingested substances does activated charcoal NOT bind effectively? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Iron", "Acetaminophen", "Lithium", "Alcohols", "Aspirin"], "ans": [0, 2, 3], "why": "Activated charcoal binds certain toxins in the gastrointestinal tract and reduces absorption, but it does <b>not effectively bind alcohols, iron, lithium</b> or many corrosive substances. It works best early, often within 1 hour, and needs an <b>intact or protected airway</b>.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#dx-poisoning-and-overdose", "cardLabel": "Poisoning and overdose"}, {"q": "A client has swallowed a corrosive cleaning product. Which actions are appropriate? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Give a neutralizing substance by mouth", "Assess for drooling, dysphagia, burns, stridor and respiratory distress", "Keep the client NPO until evaluated", "Induce vomiting", "Prepare for airway intervention if swelling threatens ventilation", "Give activated charcoal routinely"], "ans": [1, 2, 4], "why": "Acids and alkalis damage tissue <b>on contact</b>. The nurse assesses for drooling, dysphagia, burns, stridor and respiratory distress, keeps the client <b>NPO</b>, and prepares for airway intervention if swelling threatens ventilation. No induced vomiting, <b>no chemical neutralization</b>, and no routine activated charcoal.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#dx-poisoning-and-overdose", "cardLabel": "Poisoning and overdose"}, {"q": "Which manifestations are consistent with carbon monoxide poisoning? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Headache and dizziness", "Nausea and weakness", "Pinpoint pupils with slow breathing", "Confusion", "Chest pain", "Descending flaccid paralysis"], "ans": [0, 1, 3, 4], "why": "Carbon monoxide poisoning causes <b>headache and dizziness</b>, nausea and weakness, <b>confusion and altered mental status</b>, chest pain and cardiac ischemia, and seizures or coma with severe exposure. Pinpoint pupils with respiratory depression are the opioid picture, and descending flaccid paralysis is botulism.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#dx-poisoning-and-overdose", "cardLabel": "Poisoning and overdose"}, {"q": "A nurse is explaining carbon monoxide poisoning to a family. Which explanation of how it causes harm is accurate?", "type": "single", "opts": ["It destroys the alveoli so no gas can cross", "It binds to hemoglobin, forming carboxyhemoglobin and impairing oxygen delivery", "It paralyzes the respiratory muscles", "It makes the airways swell shut"], "ans": [1], "why": "<b>Carbon monoxide binds to hemoglobin</b>, forming <b>carboxyhemoglobin</b> and impairing oxygen delivery. That is why the manifestations are headache, dizziness, confusion, chest pain and cardiac ischemia, with seizures or coma in severe exposure.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#dx-poisoning-and-overdose", "cardLabel": "Poisoning and overdose"}, {"q": "For which client with carbon monoxide poisoning would hyperbaric oxygen be considered?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Every client with any exposure, whatever the symptoms", "Only a client whose pulse oximeter reading is low", "A client who refuses oxygen by mask", "A client who is pregnant or has significant neurological or cardiac involvement"], "ans": [3], "why": "Treatment is removing the client from the source safely, <b>100% oxygen promptly</b>, cardiac and neurological monitoring, and carboxyhemoglobin testing. <b>Hyperbaric oxygen</b> is considered for selected severe cases, including significant neurological or cardiac involvement and pregnancy.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#dx-poisoning-and-overdose", "cardLabel": "Poisoning and overdose"}, {"q": "True or false: Cherry-red skin is a classic textbook description of carbon monoxide poisoning, but it is uncommon and unreliable.", "type": "single", "opts": ["True", "False"], "ans": [0], "why": "True. Cherry-red skin is the textbook picture, but it is <b>uncommon and unreliable</b>, so it is <b>not the best way</b> to identify carbon monoxide poisoning. Its absence does not mean the client was not exposed.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#dx-poisoning-and-overdose", "cardLabel": "Poisoning and overdose"}, {"q": "A nurse is reviewing clients with food poisoning. For which client should an antimotility (antidiarrheal) drug be avoided?", "type": "single", "opts": ["An adult with uncomplicated watery diarrhea for one day", "An adult with watery diarrhea and no fever who is drinking well", "An adult with bloody diarrhea and a high fever", "An adult with watery diarrhea that is improving"], "ans": [2], "why": "Antimotility drugs are avoided with <b>bloody diarrhea, high fever or suspected invasive infection</b>. They may be appropriate for selected adults with <b>uncomplicated watery diarrhea</b>, which describes the other three clients.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#review-258-m12-foodpoison", "cardLabel": "Food poisoning"}, {"q": "A client with a suspected foodborne illness has diplopia, ptosis, dysphagia, dysarthria and muscle weakness that is moving downward. What is the nursing priority?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Give an antimotility drug", "Monitor respiratory function and prepare for ventilation", "Encourage oral fluids and a regular diet", "Schedule outpatient neurology follow-up for the weakness within a week"], "ans": [1], "why": "This is <b>botulism</b>, a neurotoxin-mediated illness causing <b>descending flaccid paralysis</b> that can reach the respiratory muscles. The priority is to monitor respiratory function, prepare for ventilation and give antitoxin when indicated.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#review-258-m12-foodpoison", "cardLabel": "Food poisoning"}, {"q": "A nurse is teaching the parents of an infant about infant botulism. Which food is associated with exposure to Clostridium botulinum spores?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Honey", "Rice cereal", "Pureed carrots", "Iron-fortified formula"], "ans": [0], "why": "<b>Infant botulism</b> is associated with exposure to Clostridium botulinum spores, including from <b>honey</b>. Botulism causes descending flaccid paralysis with diplopia, ptosis, dysphagia, dysarthria and respiratory muscle paralysis, so respiratory function is the priority.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#review-258-m12-foodpoison", "cardLabel": "Food poisoning"}, {"q": "A client is brought in with respiratory depression, a decreased level of consciousness and pinpoint pupils. Which overdose does the nurse suspect, and what is the treatment?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Benzodiazepine; flumazenil for every client", "Stimulant; benzodiazepines and cooling", "Opioid; ventilation support and naloxone", "Salicylate; sodium bicarbonate"], "ans": [2], "why": "<b>Respiratory depression, decreased level of consciousness and pinpoint pupils</b> are the classic opioid picture. Treatment is <b>ventilation support and naloxone</b>. Benzodiazepine overdose causes sedation and ataxia, stimulants cause agitation and hyperthermia, and salicylates cause tinnitus and tachypnea.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#dx-poisoning-and-overdose", "cardLabel": "Poisoning and overdose"}, {"q": "A client has sedation, ataxia and a decreased level of consciousness after a benzodiazepine overdose. Which treatment does the nurse anticipate?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Supportive care, with flumazenil only in carefully selected cases", "Naloxone", "N-acetylcysteine", "Sodium bicarbonate"], "ans": [0], "why": "Benzodiazepine overdose is managed with <b>supportive care</b>; <b>flumazenil</b> is used only in carefully selected cases. Naloxone reverses opioids, N-acetylcysteine treats acetaminophen overdose, and sodium bicarbonate is used for salicylate overdose and tricyclic cardiotoxicity.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#dx-poisoning-and-overdose", "cardLabel": "Poisoning and overdose"}, {"q": "A client took a large amount of acetaminophen several hours ago and has few symptoms. Which statement guides the nurse's care?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Few symptoms mean the dose was harmless", "Liver injury can be delayed, and the treatment is N-acetylcysteine", "The antidote is naloxone", "The treatment is sodium bicarbonate, with dialysis possibly needed as well"], "ans": [1], "why": "Acetaminophen overdose <b>may cause few symptoms at first</b>, with <b>delayed liver injury</b>. The treatment is <b>N-acetylcysteine</b>. Sodium bicarbonate and possible dialysis are for salicylate overdose, and naloxone is for opioids.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#dx-poisoning-and-overdose", "cardLabel": "Poisoning and overdose"}, {"q": "A client has tinnitus, tachypnea and a mixed acid-base disturbance after an overdose. Which substance and treatment does the nurse expect?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Acetaminophen; N-acetylcysteine", "Opioid; naloxone", "Benzodiazepine; supportive care", "Salicylate; sodium bicarbonate and possibly dialysis"], "ans": [3], "why": "<b>Tinnitus, tachypnea and a mixed acid-base disturbance</b> are the classic picture of <b>salicylate</b> overdose. Treatment is sodium bicarbonate, with possible dialysis. Acetaminophen may have few early symptoms, and opioids cause pinpoint pupils with respiratory depression.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#dx-poisoning-and-overdose", "cardLabel": "Poisoning and overdose"}, {"q": "A client who overdosed on a tricyclic antidepressant has a wide QRS complex, hypotension and dysrhythmias. Which medication does the nurse anticipate for the cardiotoxicity?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Naloxone", "Flumazenil", "Sodium bicarbonate", "N-acetylcysteine"], "ans": [2], "why": "Tricyclic antidepressant overdose causes a <b>wide QRS</b>, dysrhythmias, hypotension and seizures. <b>Sodium bicarbonate</b> is given for the cardiotoxicity. Naloxone is for opioids, flumazenil is for carefully selected benzodiazepine cases, and N-acetylcysteine is for acetaminophen.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#dx-poisoning-and-overdose", "cardLabel": "Poisoning and overdose"}, {"q": "A client is agitated with hyperthermia, hypertension and tachycardia after taking a stimulant. Which treatment does the nurse anticipate?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Naloxone with ventilation support until the client is alert", "Benzodiazepines, cooling and supportive care", "N-acetylcysteine", "Sodium bicarbonate and dialysis"], "ans": [1], "why": "Stimulant overdose causes <b>agitation, hyperthermia, hypertension and tachycardia</b>. Treatment is <b>benzodiazepines, cooling and supportive care</b>. Naloxone treats opioid overdose, N-acetylcysteine treats acetaminophen, and bicarbonate with possible dialysis is for salicylates.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#dx-poisoning-and-overdose", "cardLabel": "Poisoning and overdose"}, {"q": "A client with an opioid overdose is barely breathing while naloxone is being prepared. Which principle guides the nurse?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Naloxone makes ventilatory support unnecessary", "Ventilatory support waits until the naloxone has taken effect", "Ventilation is started only if a second dose fails", "Naloxone does not replace immediate ventilatory support"], "ans": [3], "why": "<b>Naloxone reverses opioid effects, but it does not replace immediate ventilatory support.</b> A client who is not breathing adequately is supported with ventilation right away, while the naloxone is given.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#dx-poisoning-and-overdose", "cardLabel": "Poisoning and overdose"}, {"q": "A client's last alcoholic drink was about 72 hours ago. The client now has severe agitation, confusion, hallucinations, fever and unstable vital signs. What does the nurse suspect?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Early, mild withdrawal", "Delirium tremens", "Opioid overdose", "Benzodiazepine overdose"], "ans": [1], "why": "<b>Delirium tremens</b> may occur about <b>48 to 96 hours</b> after the last drink, sometimes later. Findings are severe agitation, confusion, hallucinations, autonomic instability, fever and possible cardiovascular collapse. Early withdrawal at 6 to 24 hours is tremor, anxiety, nausea, sweating and tachycardia.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#dx-poisoning-and-overdose", "cardLabel": "Poisoning and overdose"}, {"q": "About when after the last drink do alcohol withdrawal seizures usually occur?", "type": "single", "opts": ["About 6 to 48 hours", "Within the first 30 minutes", "About 5 to 7 days", "Only after 2 weeks"], "ans": [0], "why": "The common progression: at about 6 to 24 hours, tremor, anxiety, nausea, sweating and tachycardia; at about <b>6 to 48 hours, withdrawal seizures</b>; at about 48 to 96 hours, <b>delirium tremens</b> may occur, sometimes later.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#dx-poisoning-and-overdose", "cardLabel": "Poisoning and overdose"}, {"q": "A client is in alcohol withdrawal. Which interventions does the nurse expect? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Benzodiazepines as prescribed", "Seizure precautions", "Withholding all sedating medication so neurological checks stay accurate", "Thiamine replacement when indicated", "Fluid and electrolyte correction", "Vital signs once a shift"], "ans": [0, 1, 3, 4], "why": "Care includes <b>benzodiazepines as prescribed</b>, <b>seizure precautions</b>, frequent vital signs and neurological assessments, thiamine replacement when indicated, fluid and electrolyte correction, and escalation for severe withdrawal. Once a shift is not frequent, and benzodiazepines are the treatment, not something to withhold.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#dx-poisoning-and-overdose", "cardLabel": "Poisoning and overdose"}, {"q": "A client arrives after a bite from a poisonous snake. Which nursing actions are appropriate? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Keep the client calm and limit movement", "Elevate the bitten limb above heart level", "Mark the progression of swelling and reassess frequently", "Monitor for an allergic reaction to antivenom", "Have the client walk around to stay alert", "Monitor for compartment syndrome"], "ans": [0, 2, 3, 5], "why": "After ABCs, the client is kept <b>calm and still</b>, rings and tight clothing come off, and the limb is immobilized in a neutral position <b>below heart level</b>. Swelling is marked and reassessed, antivenom is given when indicated, and the nurse watches for allergic reactions to it and for compartment syndrome.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#dx-environmental-emergencies", "cardLabel": "Environmental emergencies"}, {"q": "Which actions should be avoided when caring for a client with a snakebite? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Applying a tourniquet", "Removing rings and watches", "Cutting or suctioning the wound", "Applying ice packs", "Immobilizing the bitten limb", "Attempting to capture the snake"], "ans": [0, 2, 3, 5], "why": "Avoid <b>tourniquets, cutting or suctioning the wound, ice packs</b> and attempting to capture the snake. Corticosteroids and prophylactic antibiotics are not given routinely without an indication. Removing rings and watches and immobilizing the limb are part of correct care.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#dx-environmental-emergencies", "cardLabel": "Environmental emergencies"}, {"q": "A nurse is collecting the clothing of a client who was sexually assaulted. How should the clothing be packaged?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Together in one plastic bag", "In separate paper bags", "In a sealed biohazard container with the linens", "Returned to the client to take home"], "ans": [1], "why": "Clothing is generally placed in <b>separate paper bags</b>. The nurse documents objective findings and the client's statements accurately and maintains the <b>chain of custody</b>. Avoiding bathing or changing clothes before collection is encouraged only if the client wishes to preserve evidence.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#review-258-m12-personsafety", "cardLabel": "Sexual assault, trafficking and psychiatric emergencies"}, {"q": "A client who was sexually assaulted says, \"I already showered and changed, so there is no point in an exam.\" Which response by the nurse is best?", "type": "single", "opts": ["\"You are right; the evidence is gone now.\"", "\"You should not have showered before coming in.\"", "\"We can only examine you if you bring the clothes you were wearing.\"", "\"There may still be valuable evidence, and an evaluation is still available to you.\""], "ans": [3], "why": "A client who has already showered or changed clothes <b>may still have valuable forensic evidence</b>. The nurse does <b>not discourage evaluation</b>, and does not blame the client for bathing.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#review-258-m12-personsafety", "cardLabel": "Sexual assault, trafficking and psychiatric emergencies"}, {"q": "True or false: Forensic evidence collection can occur up to six days after a sexual assault.", "type": "single", "opts": ["True", "False"], "ans": [0], "why": "True. Collection can occur up to <b>six days</b> after an assault. Actual windows depend on jurisdiction, evidence type and protocol, and clients should be <b>offered evaluation</b> even when they are outside a typical collection window.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#review-258-m12-personsafety", "cardLabel": "Sexual assault, trafficking and psychiatric emergencies"}, {"q": "A client who was sexually assaulted says she does not want to report the assault to the police. Which response by the nurse is appropriate?", "type": "single", "opts": ["\"We cannot treat you until a police report is filed.\"", "\"I promise nothing you tell me will ever be shared with anyone.\"", "\"You can receive care here whether or not you report to the police.\"", "\"Everyone who comes in after an assault must have the forensic exam.\""], "ans": [2], "why": "Police reporting is <b>never a condition of receiving care</b>, and a forensic examination is never forced. The nurse does not promise absolute confidentiality when mandatory reporting laws apply, and lets a client with capacity make <b>informed decisions</b>.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#review-258-m12-personsafety", "cardLabel": "Sexual assault, trafficking and psychiatric emergencies"}, {"q": "A nurse suspects that a client is being trafficked. The person accompanying the client answers every question. What is the nurse's priority?", "type": "single", "opts": ["The client's safety and autonomy, without confronting the companion", "Confronting the companion about the suspicion", "Telling the client she must leave the companion today", "Having the companion removed at once, whatever the risk to the client"], "ans": [0], "why": "The priority is the client's <b>safety and autonomy</b>. The nurse does <b>not confront a suspected trafficker</b> and does not automatically separate the client in a manner that increases danger.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#review-258-m12-personsafety", "cardLabel": "Sexual assault, trafficking and psychiatric emergencies"}, {"q": "True or false: A client who expresses suicidal intent with a specific plan and access to the means requires immediate safety intervention.", "type": "single", "opts": ["True", "False"], "ans": [0], "why": "True. Intent, a <b>specific plan</b> and <b>access to means</b> together call for immediate safety intervention, not simply routine counseling or discharge education. The client is not sent home with teaching alone.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#review-258-m12-personsafety", "cardLabel": "Sexual assault, trafficking and psychiatric emergencies"}, {"q": "A client with type 2 diabetes who drinks at least six beers daily has severe epigastric pain rated 10/10, temperature 101.8°F, HR 128, BP 90/60, abdominal distention, periumbilical bruising and jaundice. Which condition does the nurse suspect?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Acute appendicitis", "Gastroesophageal reflux disease", "Acute pancreatitis, possibly severe or hemorrhagic, with hypovolemia or evolving shock", "Uncomplicated gastroenteritis"], "ans": [2], "why": "Severe epigastric pain, fever, tachycardia, low blood pressure, distention, <b>periumbilical bruising</b> and jaundice in a daily drinker point to <b>acute pancreatitis</b>, potentially severe or hemorrhagic, with hypovolemia or evolving shock. Alcohol-associated and gallstone-related pancreatitis are both considerations.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "gi/NG-159_pancreatitis.html", "cardLabel": "Pancreatitis"}, {"q": "Which laboratory test is more specific than amylase for acute pancreatitis?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Serum calcium", "Blood urea nitrogen", "Lactate", "Serum lipase"], "ans": [3], "why": "<b>Serum lipase is more specific than amylase</b> for pancreatitis. The workup also includes a CBC, comprehensive metabolic panel, glucose and calcium, liver enzymes and bilirubin, BUN and creatinine, and <b>lactate when shock is suspected</b>.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "gi/NG-159_pancreatitis.html", "cardLabel": "Pancreatitis"}, {"q": "A client with suspected acute pancreatitis is scheduled for a right upper quadrant ultrasound. What is the purpose of this test?", "type": "single", "opts": ["To measure the lipase level", "To look for biliary disease", "To check the size of the kidneys", "To confirm a bowel perforation"], "ans": [1], "why": "A <b>right upper quadrant ultrasound</b> looks for <b>biliary disease</b>, since gallstone-related pancreatitis is a consideration. A CT of the abdomen is used when the diagnosis is unclear or complications are suspected. Lipase is a blood test.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "gi/NG-159_pancreatitis.html", "cardLabel": "Pancreatitis"}, {"q": "A nurse notes ecchymosis around a client's umbilicus. What is this finding called, and what does it suggest?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Grey Turner sign; a normal variant that needs no further assessment", "Cullen sign; intra-abdominal or retroperitoneal hemorrhage", "Murphy sign; gallbladder inflammation", "Beck triad; cardiac tamponade"], "ans": [1], "why": "<b>Cullen sign</b> is periumbilical ecchymosis, associated with intra-abdominal or retroperitoneal hemorrhage. <b>Grey Turner sign</b> is flank ecchymosis, also associated with retroperitoneal bleeding. Neither is specific to pancreatitis; both warrant investigation for hemorrhage.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "gi/NG-159_pancreatitis.html", "cardLabel": "Pancreatitis"}, {"q": "A client has flank ecchymosis (Grey Turner sign). Which statement about this finding is correct?", "type": "single", "opts": ["It is specific to pancreatitis and confirms the diagnosis", "It is a harmless finding that needs no follow-up", "It is associated with retroperitoneal bleeding and warrants investigation for hemorrhage", "It appears only with gallstones"], "ans": [2], "why": "<b>Grey Turner sign</b>, flank ecchymosis, is associated with <b>retroperitoneal bleeding</b>. Like Cullen sign, it is <b>not specific to pancreatitis</b>, so it does not confirm that diagnosis; it warrants investigation for hemorrhage.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "gi/NG-159_pancreatitis.html", "cardLabel": "Pancreatitis"}, {"q": "A nurse gives IV calcium gluconate to a client with severe hyperkalemia. What is the purpose of this medication?", "type": "single", "opts": ["It stabilizes the cardiac membranes and protects the heart", "It shifts potassium into the cells", "It removes potassium from the body through the kidneys and bowel", "It corrects metabolic acidosis"], "ans": [0], "why": "IV calcium gluconate <b>stabilizes cardiac membranes</b> and <b>protects the heart</b> in emergencies. Shifting potassium into the cells is the job of insulin with dextrose and nebulized albuterol; sodium bicarbonate may help when significant acidosis is present.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#review-258-m12-crush-chest", "cardLabel": "Crush injury and chest trauma"}, {"q": "Which therapies shift potassium into the cells for a client with hyperkalemia? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Regular insulin IV with dextrose as indicated", "IV calcium gluconate", "Nebulized albuterol", "An oral potassium binder", "Sodium bicarbonate in selected clients with metabolic acidosis"], "ans": [0, 2, 4], "why": "<b>Regular insulin IV with dextrose</b> temporarily lowers serum potassium by moving it into cells, <b>nebulized albuterol</b> adds a further shift, and sodium bicarbonate may help when significant acidosis is present. Calcium gluconate stabilizes cardiac membranes instead, and a binder removes potassium rather than shifting it.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#review-258-m12-crush-chest", "cardLabel": "Crush injury and chest trauma"}, {"q": "Which are key principles of the Incident Command System? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["A clear chain of command", "Defined responsibilities", "Each department sets its own priorities independently", "Coordinated communication", "Resource allocation", "Interagency cooperation"], "ans": [0, 1, 3, 4, 5], "why": "The Incident Command System establishes a <b>standardized organizational structure</b> for emergency response. Its key principles are a clear chain of command, defined responsibilities, <b>coordinated communication</b>, resource allocation and interagency cooperation. Departments working independently is the opposite of that structure.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#dx-triage-and-mass-casualty-response", "cardLabel": "Triage and mass casualty response"}, {"q": "True or false: During a disaster, the nurse should follow the assigned role and the chain of command rather than improvising independently outside established responsibilities.", "type": "single", "opts": ["True", "False"], "ans": [0], "why": "True. The incident command system gives every responder a <b>defined role</b> inside a <b>clear chain of command</b>. Staying in that role keeps communication coordinated and resources allocated properly; improvising outside it undermines the response.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#dx-emergency-preparedness-and-the-nurse-s-role", "cardLabel": "Emergency preparedness and the nurse's role"}, {"q": "A nurse who usually works emergency department triage is assigned to triage at a mass casualty incident. How does the priority change?", "type": "single", "opts": ["The most critically injured client is always treated first, as in the emergency department.", "Clients most likely to benefit from the limited resources receive priority.", "Clients are treated strictly in the order they arrive.", "Clients with minor injuries are sent home without being assessed."], "ans": [1], "why": "Emergency department triage centers on the <b>individual</b>: the most critical client often gets immediate care. Mass casualty triage centers on <b>population-level survival</b>, so those most likely to benefit from limited resources receive priority.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#dx-triage-and-mass-casualty-response", "cardLabel": "Triage and mass casualty response"}, {"q": "In the color-coded disaster triage system, which tag is correctly paired with its meaning?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Green: significant injury, but treatment can be briefly delayed", "Black: minor injuries, walking wounded", "Yellow: expectant or deceased", "Red: immediate, life-threatening but potentially survivable with prompt intervention"], "ans": [3], "why": "<b>Red</b> is immediate: life-threatening but potentially survivable with prompt intervention. Yellow is delayed: a significant injury whose treatment can wait briefly. Green is minor, the walking wounded. <b>Black</b> is expectant or deceased.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#dx-triage-and-mass-casualty-response", "cardLabel": "Triage and mass casualty response"}, {"q": "True or false: In disaster triage, tagging a client as expectant does not mean the client is abandoned.", "type": "single", "opts": ["True", "False"], "ans": [0], "why": "True. An expectant (black-tagged) client is not abandoned: <b>comfort care and reassessment</b> remain appropriate. The specific color assignments vary with the disaster triage system being used.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#dx-triage-and-mass-casualty-response", "cardLabel": "Triage and mass casualty response"}, {"q": "Using START triage, a nurse assesses a client who cannot walk. Respirations are 24/min, the radial pulse is present with capillary refill under 2 seconds, and the client follows simple commands. Which tag applies?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Green", "Black", "Yellow", "Red"], "ans": [2], "why": "In START, a client who can walk is green. This client cannot, so the nurse checks respirations, perfusion and mental status. A rate of 30/min or less, adequate perfusion and following commands is <b>yellow (delayed)</b>. Failing any one of those checks would make the client red.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#dx-triage-and-mass-casualty-response", "cardLabel": "Triage and mass casualty response"}, {"q": "Using START triage, a nurse finds a client who is not breathing. The nurse opens the airway and the client remains apneic. Which tag does the nurse assign?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Black", "Red", "Yellow", "Green"], "ans": [0], "why": "Under START, a client who is not breathing has the airway opened. If the client is <b>still apneic, the tag is black</b>. If breathing resumes once the airway is open, the tag is red.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#dx-triage-and-mass-casualty-response", "cardLabel": "Triage and mass casualty response"}, {"q": "Which level of personal protective equipment is a self-contained breathing apparatus (SCBA) worn with chemical-resistant clothing, chosen when the respiratory hazard is high but the skin hazard is lower?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Level A", "Level B", "Level C", "Level D"], "ans": [1], "why": "<b>Level B</b> is SCBA with chemical-resistant clothing: high respiratory hazard, lower skin hazard. Level A adds a fully encapsulating vapor-tight suit. Level C uses an air-purifying respirator for a known contaminant, and Level D is standard work clothing for minimal hazard.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#dx-bioterrorism-agents", "cardLabel": "Bioterrorism, chemical and radiation agents"}, {"q": "Which level of personal protective equipment gives the greatest combined respiratory and skin protection?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Level D", "Level C", "Level B", "Level A"], "ans": [3], "why": "<b>Level A</b> is a fully encapsulating, vapor-tight suit with self-contained breathing apparatus, giving the greatest combined respiratory and skin protection. Level B keeps the SCBA with less skin protection, Level C relies on an air-purifying respirator, and Level D is standard work clothing.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#dx-bioterrorism-agents", "cardLabel": "Bioterrorism, chemical and radiation agents"}, {"q": "A response team must enter an area where the oxygen level is deficient. Which statement about respiratory protection is correct?", "type": "single", "opts": ["An air-purifying respirator is the right choice because it makes its own oxygen.", "Standard work clothing is acceptable if the exposure is brief.", "An air-purifying respirator is not appropriate in an oxygen-deficient environment.", "A surgical mask with a face shield gives enough protection."], "ans": [2], "why": "An <b>air-purifying respirator</b> only filters the air, so it is <b>not appropriate where oxygen is deficient</b>. Level C equipment is for a known contaminant with suitable oxygen levels and a suitable filter. Levels A and B use a self-contained breathing apparatus instead.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#dx-bioterrorism-agents", "cardLabel": "Bioterrorism, chemical and radiation agents"}, {"q": "After an explosion, a client has a ruptured tympanic membrane and blast lung caused by the direct pressure wave. How is this type of blast injury classified?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Primary", "Secondary", "Tertiary", "Quaternary"], "ans": [0], "why": "<b>Primary</b> blast injury comes from the direct pressure wave: blast lung, tympanic membrane rupture, bowel injury. Secondary is flying debris, tertiary is the body thrown against objects, and quaternary covers other effects such as burns, smoke inhalation and crush injury.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#dx-bioterrorism-agents", "cardLabel": "Bioterrorism, chemical and radiation agents"}, {"q": "A client was thrown against a wall by an explosion and has fractures and a traumatic brain injury. Which blast injury category describes these injuries?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Primary", "Secondary", "Tertiary", "Quaternary"], "ans": [2], "why": "<b>Tertiary</b> blast injuries happen when the body is thrown against objects, causing fractures and traumatic brain injury. Primary injuries come from the pressure wave itself, secondary from flying debris, and quaternary from other effects such as burns or smoke inhalation.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#dx-bioterrorism-agents", "cardLabel": "Bioterrorism, chemical and radiation agents"}, {"q": "A client was standing close to an explosion. Which findings should make the nurse suspect blast lung? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Dyspnea", "Hemoptysis", "Pinpoint pupils with heavy salivation", "Hypoxemia", "Chest pain", "A painless black eschar on the skin"], "ans": [0, 1, 3, 4], "why": "In blast lung the pressure wave damages lung tissue, causing <b>hemorrhage, edema and impaired gas exchange</b>: dyspnea, hypoxemia, chest pain and hemoptysis, possibly pneumothorax or ARDS. Pinpoint pupils with salivation point to a nerve agent; a painless black eschar is cutaneous anthrax.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#dx-bioterrorism-agents", "cardLabel": "Bioterrorism, chemical and radiation agents"}, {"q": "A client is admitted with suspected smallpox. Which precautions does the nurse implement?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Standard precautions only", "Droplet precautions only", "Airborne and contact precautions", "Protective (reverse) isolation"], "ans": [2], "why": "Smallpox is <b>viral</b> and spreads person to person by the respiratory route and from lesions, so it needs <b>airborne and contact precautions</b>. Anthrax is bacterial, spread by spores rather than between people, and is managed with standard precautions.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#dx-bioterrorism-agents", "cardLabel": "Bioterrorism, chemical and radiation agents"}, {"q": "Which skin finding points to smallpox rather than cutaneous anthrax?", "type": "single", "opts": ["A painless black eschar", "Painful grouped vesicles on the genitals", "A rash on the palms and soles after a painless chancre", "Deep, firm lesions that are all in a similar stage of development"], "ans": [3], "why": "Smallpox produces <b>deep, firm lesions that are all in a similar stage</b> of development. Cutaneous anthrax produces a painless black eschar. Painful grouped vesicles are herpes, and a palm-and-sole rash after a painless chancre is syphilis.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#dx-bioterrorism-agents", "cardLabel": "Bioterrorism, chemical and radiation agents"}, {"q": "A client seen after a suspected bioterrorism exposure has a painless black eschar with surrounding edema on the forearm. Which condition does the nurse suspect?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Smallpox", "Cutaneous anthrax", "Sulfur mustard exposure", "Botulism"], "ans": [1], "why": "A <b>painless black eschar with surrounding edema</b> is the characteristic lesion of cutaneous anthrax. Smallpox causes deep, firm lesions in the same stage, sulfur mustard causes burning and blisters, and botulism causes descending paralysis.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#dx-bioterrorism-agents", "cardLabel": "Bioterrorism, chemical and radiation agents"}, {"q": "The family of a client with inhalation anthrax asks whether they could catch it from the client. Which response by the nurse is correct?", "type": "single", "opts": ["\"Inhalation anthrax is not typically passed from person to person.\"", "\"Yes, it spreads easily through the air from one person to another.\"", "\"Yes, anyone who has touched the client must go into airborne isolation.\"", "\"Only if the client develops a rash.\""], "ans": [0], "why": "Inhalation anthrax is <b>not typically transmitted person to person</b>; it comes from spores. Smallpox is the agent that needs strict airborne and contact precautions because of its potential for person-to-person spread.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#dx-bioterrorism-agents", "cardLabel": "Bioterrorism, chemical and radiation agents"}, {"q": "A disease that began as an epidemic in one region is now spreading across multiple countries and continents. Which term applies?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Outbreak", "Endemic disease", "Sporadic case", "Pandemic"], "ans": [3], "why": "A <b>pandemic</b> is an epidemic spreading across multiple countries or continents. An outbreak is more cases than expected in a defined population or area; an epidemic is a significant increase in disease within a region or population.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#review-258-m12-outbreaks", "cardLabel": "Outbreak, epidemic and pandemic"}, {"q": "A public health nurse notes more cases of an illness than expected within one defined community. Which term describes this?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Pandemic", "Outbreak", "Endemic disease", "Sporadic case"], "ans": [1], "why": "An <b>outbreak</b> is more cases than expected within a defined population or area. An epidemic is a significant increase across a region or population, and a pandemic is an epidemic that has spread across multiple countries or continents.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#review-258-m12-outbreaks", "cardLabel": "Outbreak, epidemic and pandemic"}, {"q": "Sulfur mustard and lewisite belong to which class of chemical agent, and what injury do they cause?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Nerve agents; they inhibit acetylcholinesterase and cause heavy secretions", "Biological agents; they cause a painless black eschar", "Vesicants; they injure tissue and cause burning, erythema and blisters", "Radiological agents; they cause internal contamination"], "ans": [2], "why": "Sulfur mustard and lewisite are <b>vesicants</b>: they injure tissue and cause burning, erythema and <b>blisters</b>, plus airway irritation and pulmonary injury. Treatment is decontamination, supportive care and agent-specific treatment if available.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#review-258-m12-vesicants", "cardLabel": "Vesicants"}, {"q": "Sarin, soman and VX are nerve agents. How do they cause harm?", "type": "single", "opts": ["They inhibit acetylcholinesterase, so acetylcholine is not broken down.", "They bind to hemoglobin and impair oxygen delivery.", "They blister the skin and airway on contact.", "They are bacterial spores that form a black eschar."], "ans": [0], "why": "Nerve agents such as sarin, soman and VX <b>inhibit acetylcholinesterase</b>, so acetylcholine is not broken down and cholinergic stimulation becomes excessive. Binding hemoglobin describes carbon monoxide, blistering describes vesicants, and a black eschar is anthrax.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#dx-bioterrorism-agents", "cardLabel": "Bioterrorism, chemical and radiation agents"}, {"q": "A client was exposed to a nerve agent. Which manifestations does the nurse expect? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Miosis (pinpoint pupils)", "Dry mouth and dry skin", "Bradycardia", "Bronchospasm", "Urinary retention", "Lacrimation and salivation"], "ans": [0, 2, 3, 5], "why": "Excess acetylcholine produces <b>DUMBBELSS</b>: diarrhea, urination, miosis, bronchospasm, bradycardia, emesis, lacrimation, salivation, sweating. Also watch for fasciculations, weakness, seizures and respiratory failure. Dry mouth and urinary retention are the opposite of this picture.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#dx-bioterrorism-agents", "cardLabel": "Bioterrorism, chemical and radiation agents"}, {"q": "A client with nerve agent exposure is prescribed pralidoxime (2-PAM). What does this medication do?", "type": "single", "opts": ["It blocks muscarinic effects such as bronchorrhea and bradycardia.", "It reactivates acetylcholinesterase when given early enough.", "It treats the seizures.", "It binds the agent in the stomach to reduce absorption."], "ans": [1], "why": "<b>Pralidoxime (2-PAM)</b> reactivates acetylcholinesterase when given appropriately and early enough, helping reverse neuromuscular effects. Atropine is the drug that blocks muscarinic effects such as bronchorrhea, bronchospasm and bradycardia; benzodiazepines treat seizures.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#dx-bioterrorism-agents", "cardLabel": "Bioterrorism, chemical and radiation agents"}, {"q": "Which complication is a leading cause of death after nerve agent exposure?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Liver failure", "Hemorrhage from coagulopathy", "Acute kidney injury", "Respiratory compromise from secretions, bronchospasm and muscle paralysis"], "ans": [3], "why": "In a nerve agent exposure, <b>respiratory compromise</b> from secretions, bronchospasm and muscle paralysis is a leading cause of death. That is why airway support sits beside decontamination and antidotes among the first priorities.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#dx-bioterrorism-agents", "cardLabel": "Bioterrorism, chemical and radiation agents"}, {"q": "A client was exposed to a radiation source but has no radioactive material on the skin, on clothing or inside the body. Which term describes this?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Irradiation", "External contamination", "Internal contamination (incorporation)", "Decontamination"], "ans": [0], "why": "<b>Irradiation</b> is exposure to radiation without necessarily becoming radioactive or contaminated. Contamination means radioactive material is on skin, clothing or surfaces; internal contamination means it has entered the body.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#dx-bioterrorism-agents", "cardLabel": "Bioterrorism, chemical and radiation agents"}, {"q": "A client inhaled radioactive dust during an incident. Which term describes radioactive material that has entered the body through inhalation, ingestion or wounds?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Irradiation", "External contamination", "Internal contamination (incorporation)", "Acute radiation syndrome"], "ans": [2], "why": "<b>Internal contamination (incorporation)</b> means radioactive material has entered the body through inhalation, ingestion or wounds. External contamination is material on skin, clothing or surfaces, and irradiation is exposure without necessarily becoming contaminated.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#dx-bioterrorism-agents", "cardLabel": "Bioterrorism, chemical and radiation agents"}, {"q": "A nurse is decontaminating the skin of a client with external radioactive contamination. Which technique is correct?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Scrub the skin vigorously with a stiff brush", "Gently wash exposed skin with soap and lukewarm water", "Rinse with very hot water to open the pores", "Leave the skin unwashed and cover it with a dry dressing"], "ans": [1], "why": "Exposed skin is <b>gently washed with soap and lukewarm water</b>. Aggressive scrubbing damages the skin and is avoided. Staff wear appropriate PPE, contaminated clothing is removed, bagged and isolated, and the client is monitored for residual contamination.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#review-258-m12-decon", "cardLabel": "Decontamination"}, {"q": "Which statement about removing a client's outer clothing after external radioactive contamination is correct?", "type": "single", "opts": ["It can eliminate a large proportion of the external contamination.", "It makes little difference; only a shower removes contamination.", "It should wait until the client reaches an inpatient unit.", "The clothing can go into the regular linen hamper."], "ans": [0], "why": "Removing outer clothing alone can eliminate a <b>large proportion</b> of external radioactive contamination. The clothing is then <b>bagged and isolated</b> according to protocol, and the skin is washed gently with soap and lukewarm water.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#review-258-m12-decon", "cardLabel": "Decontamination"}, {"q": "True or false: Life-saving treatment should not be delayed solely to finish radiological decontamination.", "type": "single", "opts": ["True", "False"], "ans": [0], "why": "True. <b>Stabilization is coordinated with contamination-control precautions</b>: the team treats life threats while limiting spread of the contaminant, rather than making a critically ill client wait until decontamination is complete.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#review-258-m12-decon", "cardLabel": "Decontamination"}, {"q": "Clients exposed to a nerve agent arrive at the hospital. Which set of actions is the first priority?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Obtain a full health history and allergy list", "Give activated charcoal and oral fluids", "Put on PPE, decontaminate the clients and support the airway", "Seat the clients together in the waiting room until antidotes arrive"], "ans": [2], "why": "The first priority is <b>PPE, decontamination and airway support</b>. Ongoing treatment is atropine, pralidoxime and seizure treatment. Respiratory compromise is a leading cause of death, so the airway is supported from the start.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#dx-bioterrorism-agents", "cardLabel": "Bioterrorism, chemical and radiation agents"}, {"q": "True or false: All trauma clients should receive large volumes of normal saline.", "type": "single", "opts": ["True", "False"], "ans": [1], "why": "False. Large volumes of saline are not right for every trauma client. Significant hemorrhage often requires <b>early blood products</b> and <b>bleeding control</b>. Trauma clients are also vulnerable to hypothermia, acidosis and coagulopathy, which worsen bleeding.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#review-258-m12-trauma", "cardLabel": "Primary survey and trauma"}, {"q": "True or false: Tracheal deviation must be present before a tension pneumothorax can be diagnosed.", "type": "single", "opts": ["True", "False"], "ans": [1], "why": "False. Tracheal deviation is a <b>late and unreliable</b> finding. Severe distress, hypotension and absent breath sounds on one side are enough to suspect a tension pneumothorax, which needs immediate decompression without waiting for a chest x-ray.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#review-258-m12-crush-chest", "cardLabel": "Crush injury and chest trauma"}, {"q": "True or false: IV calcium lowers the serum potassium level.", "type": "single", "opts": ["True", "False"], "ans": [1], "why": "False. IV calcium <b>stabilizes the heart</b> in severe hyperkalemia but does <b>not remove or shift potassium</b>. Insulin with dextrose shifts potassium into the cells, and definitive potassium removal must follow.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#review-258-m12-crush-chest", "cardLabel": "Crush injury and chest trauma"}, {"q": "True or false: A client with no urine output will respond well to furosemide.", "type": "single", "opts": ["True", "False"], "ans": [1], "why": "False. A client with end-stage renal disease who makes no urine often needs <b>dialysis</b> to remove both <b>volume and potassium</b>; a diuretic cannot be relied on when there is no urine output.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#review-258-m12-crush-chest", "cardLabel": "Crush injury and chest trauma"}, {"q": "A client arrives after a motor vehicle collision with BP 76/42 mm Hg, heart rate 148/min, respirations 34/min and a Glasgow Coma Scale score of 7. Which intervention has the highest priority?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Complete the secondary head-to-toe assessment.", "Obtain a detailed medication history.", "Administer pain medication.", "Assess and secure the airway while protecting the cervical spine."], "ans": [3], "why": "Airway comes first in the primary survey. A GCS of 8 or lower signals severe neurological impairment and prompts <b>urgent airway assessment</b>, with the cervical spine protected. The secondary head-to-toe survey and history come only after immediate life threats are addressed.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov), practice item reworded", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#review-258-m12-trauma", "cardLabel": "Primary survey and trauma"}, {"q": "A nurse is deciding whether a client has heat stroke or heat exhaustion. Which finding most strongly points to heat stroke?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Tachycardia", "Excessive sweating", "Confusion and altered mental status", "Dehydration"], "ans": [2], "why": "<b>Central nervous system dysfunction</b> is the hallmark of heat stroke, so confusion and altered mental status set it apart. Sweating, tachycardia and dehydration can occur with either heat stroke or heat exhaustion.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov), practice item reworded", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#dx-environmental-emergencies", "cardLabel": "Environmental emergencies"}, {"q": "A client has a frostbitten foot. Which planned action should the nurse question?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Remove constrictive jewelry.", "Rub the foot vigorously to restore circulation.", "Administer prescribed analgesia.", "Use controlled warm-water rewarming when refreezing can be prevented."], "ans": [1], "why": "<b>Rubbing frostbitten tissue causes additional injury</b>, so the nurse questions it. Protect the area and rewarm appropriately: analgesia, removing constrictive jewelry and controlled warm-water rewarming when refreezing can be prevented are all correct.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov), practice item reworded", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "skin/NG-116_frostbite.html", "cardLabel": "Frostbite"}, {"q": "A client who was exposed to a faulty furnace has a headache and is confused. The SpO2 reads 99%. What should the nurse do?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Give activated charcoal.", "Discharge the client after symptoms improve.", "Administer 100% oxygen and obtain co-oximetry testing.", "Exclude carbon monoxide poisoning because the oxygen saturation is normal."], "ans": [2], "why": "Standard pulse oximetry cannot tell oxyhemoglobin from carboxyhemoglobin, so a <b>normal SpO2 does not exclude</b> carbon monoxide poisoning. Give <b>100% oxygen</b> promptly and confirm the exposure with blood co-oximetry.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov), practice item reworded", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#dx-poisoning-and-overdose", "cardLabel": "Poisoning and overdose"}, {"q": "A client on dialysis has a potassium of 7.0 mEq/L and a widening QRS complex. Which prescribed medication is the immediate priority to stabilize the heart? <span class=\"normals\" style=\"display:block;margin:8px 0;color:#ffe9a8;font-size:.92em\">Normal: potassium 3.5 to 5.0 mEq/L</span>", "type": "single", "opts": ["Furosemide", "Oral potassium binder", "Sodium polystyrene sulfonate", "IV calcium gluconate"], "ans": [3], "why": "<b>IV calcium gluconate</b> stabilizes the myocardium in severe hyperkalemia with ECG changes, so it comes first. It does not itself lower potassium: insulin with dextrose and definitive potassium removal must follow.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov), practice item reworded", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#review-258-m12-crush-chest", "cardLabel": "Crush injury and chest trauma"}, {"q": "A client taking warfarin arrives vomiting blood, with BP 78/40 mm Hg and an INR of 7.2. Which intervention should the nurse anticipate? <span class=\"normals\" style=\"display:block;margin:8px 0;color:#ffe9a8;font-size:.92em\">Normal: INR 0.8 to 1.1 (2 to 3 on warfarin)</span>", "type": "single", "opts": ["Urgent resuscitation, blood products as indicated, and rapid anticoagulation reversal", "Continue warfarin", "Administer vitamin K alone and discharge", "Encourage oral hydration"], "ans": [0], "why": "Hematemesis with hypotension and an INR of 7.2 is life-threatening bleeding on an anticoagulant. The client needs <b>urgent resuscitation</b>, blood products as indicated and <b>rapid reversal</b> of the anticoagulation. Continuing warfarin, vitamin K alone with discharge, or oral fluids would not address active hemorrhage.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov), practice item reworded", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#review-258-m12-trauma", "cardLabel": "Primary survey and trauma"}, {"q": "A client is admitted after a crush injury. Which finding is most suggestive of rhabdomyolysis?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Clear urine and low CK", "Low potassium and low creatinine", "Dark urine and elevated CK", "Isolated bruising without muscle injury"], "ans": [2], "why": "Muscle breakdown releases <b>myoglobin</b>, which causes dark urine and possible acute kidney injury, and <b>CK rises substantially</b>. Hyperkalemia, not low potassium, is the major danger.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov), practice item reworded", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "musc/NG-392_rhabdomyolysis.html", "cardLabel": "Rhabdomyolysis"}, {"q": "A client exposed to a nerve agent has copious secretions, pinpoint pupils, wheezing and muscle fasciculations. Which medications does the nurse anticipate?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Atropine and pralidoxime", "Naloxone and vitamin K", "N-acetylcysteine and calcium gluconate", "Flumazenil and insulin"], "ans": [0], "why": "Nerve agents inhibit acetylcholinesterase. <b>Atropine</b> treats the muscarinic effects and <b>pralidoxime</b> helps restore enzyme activity when appropriate. Airway support and decontamination remain essential. The other pairs are antidotes for different problems, not for a nerve agent.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov), practice item reworded", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#dx-bioterrorism-agents", "cardLabel": "Bioterrorism, chemical and radiation agents"}, {"q": "After blunt chest trauma a client has hypotension, absent breath sounds on the right and severe respiratory distress. What should the nurse anticipate?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Wait for chest radiography before notifying the provider.", "Immediate treatment for suspected tension pneumothorax.", "Encourage deep breathing only.", "Administer oral fluids."], "ans": [1], "why": "Hypotension, absent breath sounds on one side and severe distress point to a tension pneumothorax. With instability it is a <b>clinical emergency requiring immediate decompression</b>; treatment is <b>not delayed for imaging</b>.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov), practice item reworded", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#review-258-m12-crush-chest", "cardLabel": "Crush injury and chest trauma"}, {"q": "A client has a cast on the leg after a crush injury and now has severe pain with passive stretch and tense swelling. Pedal pulses are still palpable. What is the priority?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Reassess in four hours.", "Apply a tight compression bandage.", "Immediately escalate suspected compartment syndrome.", "Massage the extremity."], "ans": [2], "why": "Severe pain with passive stretch and tense swelling are <b>early warning signs</b> of compartment syndrome. <b>Pulses can remain present until late</b>, so palpable pedal pulses do not rule it out. Suspected compartment syndrome is escalated immediately: notify the provider, loosen or bivalve (split) the cast as ordered, and keep the leg at <b>heart level</b>, not raised above it.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov), practice item reworded", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "musc/NG-388_compartment-syndrome.html", "cardLabel": "Compartment syndrome"}, {"q": "An emergency department nurse is triaging clients during a shift. What is the priority in the treatment of any client in the emergency department?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Obtaining consent for treatment", "Establishing an airway", "Controlling hemorrhage", "Restoring cardiac output"], "ans": [1], "why": "The <b>ABCDE framework</b> guides priority-setting for every client as it applies to their situation, and <b>airway</b> is first. Hemorrhage control and cardiac output belong to circulation, which comes after airway and breathing.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov), practice item reworded", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#review-258-m12-trauma", "cardLabel": "Primary survey and trauma"}, {"q": "Using the disaster triage system, which tag does the nurse place on a client who is unresponsive with third-degree burns over 75% of the body?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Yellow", "Black", "Red", "Green"], "ans": [1], "why": "This client is tagged <b>black</b>. Black tags go to clients whose injuries are so extensive that there is a <b>minimal chance of survival</b>. Expectant clients still receive comfort care and reassessment.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov), practice item reworded", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#dx-triage-and-mass-casualty-response", "cardLabel": "Triage and mass casualty response"}, {"q": "A client with suicidal ideation is being transferred to the mental health unit. Which intervention should the nurse include in the plan of care?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Keep the door to the client's room closed.", "Assign the client to a private room near the nurses' station.", "Search the client and his belongings upon arrival.", "Instruct an assistive personnel to check on the client every 15 minutes."], "ans": [2], "why": "The nurse <b>searches the client and belongings</b> for potentially harmful objects. A semi-private room lowers suicide risk, observation is <b>one-to-one</b> (the client is not left alone, so 15-minute checks are not enough), and the door stays open.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov), practice item reworded", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#review-258-m12-personsafety", "cardLabel": "Sexual assault, trafficking and psychiatric emergencies"}, {"q": "A charge nurse in the emergency department learns that a tornado has touched down in a nearby town and mass casualties are on the way. Which action should the nurse take first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Call in off-duty staff members.", "Prepare the triage rooms.", "Obtain additional supplies.", "Follow facility policy to activate the disaster plan."], "ans": [3], "why": "The nurse has little information yet, so the first step is to follow facility policy to <b>activate the disaster plan</b>. The plan sets out every responder's role and makes sure clients are treated safely and in order by enough caregivers.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov), practice item reworded", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#dx-emergency-preparedness-and-the-nurse-s-role", "cardLabel": "Emergency preparedness and the nurse's role"}, {"q": "A triage nurse in the emergency department assesses four clients who arrive for treatment. Which client needs treatment first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["A client who has epigastric pain, left arm pain, and is diaphoretic", "A client who has nasal and orbital ecchymosis and a respiratory rate of 16/min", "A client who has abdominal pain and is 2 months pregnant", "A client who has neck pain and was transported to the facility on a backboard"], "ans": [0], "why": "Epigastric pain, left arm pain and diaphoresis are <b>classic manifestations of a myocardial infarction</b>, which is life-threatening, so this client is treated ahead of the other three.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov), practice item reworded", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#dx-triage-and-mass-casualty-response", "cardLabel": "Triage and mass casualty response"}, {"q": "A triage nurse in the emergency department determines that a client with dyspnea and dehydration is not in a life-threatening situation. Which triage category does the nurse choose?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Yellow stable", "Orange unstable", "Green stable", "Red unstable"], "ans": [0], "why": "The nurse has judged this to be a <b>non-life-threatening</b> situation. The client still needs care but is considered <b>stable</b> and can wait to receive treatment, which is the yellow category.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov), practice item reworded", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#dx-triage-and-mass-casualty-response", "cardLabel": "Triage and mass casualty response"}, {"q": "At the start of a shift on an oncology unit, a nurse receives report on four postoperative clients. Which client should the nurse see first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["2 days after excision of an abdominal mass, with a portable wound suction device draining 20 mL/hr of serosanguineous fluid", "1 day after a lobectomy for small-cell carcinoma, with a chest tube draining 35 mL/hr of bright red, bloody drainage", "1 day after excision of a bladder wall tumor and prostate, with continuous bladder irrigation returning 300 mL/hr of reddish pink urine", "2 days after a colectomy for colorectal cancer, with an ostomy bag full of bright red, bloody drainage"], "ans": [3], "why": "Think stable versus unstable. An ostomy bag <b>full of blood means hemorrhage</b>, an immediate threat to circulation that must be reported to the surgeon at once; the client may need IV fluids, blood or more surgery.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov), practice item reworded", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#dx-triage-and-mass-casualty-response", "cardLabel": "Triage and mass casualty response"}, {"q": "A nurse is planning care for four clients receiving oxygen therapy. Which client should the nurse plan to see first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["A client who has COPD and is receiving oxygen at 2 L/min via nasal cannula", "A client who has heart failure and is receiving 100% oxygen via partial rebreather mask", "A client who has an old tracheostomy and is receiving 40% humidified oxygen via tracheostomy collar", "A client who has emphysema and is receiving oxygen at 3 L/min via transtracheal oxygen cannula"], "ans": [1], "why": "A client needing <b>100% oxygen</b> is the most unstable, judged by the amount of oxygen required. The nurse also checks that the bag on the rebreather mask inflates properly so the client does not rebreathe carbon dioxide.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov), practice item reworded", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#dx-triage-and-mass-casualty-response", "cardLabel": "Triage and mass casualty response"}, {"q": "True or false: While cooling a client who has heat stroke, the nurse wants to prevent shivering.", "type": "single", "opts": ["True", "False"], "ans": [0], "why": "True. Shivering uses <b>too much metabolic effort</b>, which the client with heat stroke cannot afford. Medication, such as a benzodiazepine, may be prescribed to prevent shivering while rapid cooling continues.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#dx-environmental-emergencies", "cardLabel": "Environmental emergencies"}, {"q": "A nurse is rewarming a client who has hypothermia. Which electrolyte complication of rewarming should the nurse monitor for?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Hypernatremia with seizures", "Hypocalcemia with tetany", "Hyperkalemia with dysrhythmias", "Hypomagnesemia with tremors"], "ans": [2], "why": "As the body rewarms, potassium that was trapped in the cells is released into the bloodstream. The result is <b>hyperkalemia</b>, which can cause <b>dysrhythmias</b>, so the ECG is monitored during rewarming.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "core/NG-041_hypothermia.html", "cardLabel": "Hypothermia"}, {"q": "Which interventions are examples of active core rewarming for a client with hypothermia? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Warm IV fluids", "Dry blankets", "Warm, humidified oxygen", "Warming the room", "Irrigating the bladder with warm water", "Cardiopulmonary bypass that warms the blood"], "ans": [0, 2, 4, 5], "why": "<b>Active core rewarming</b> warms from the inside: cardiopulmonary bypass, warm IV fluids, warm humidified oxygen and warm bladder irrigation. Blankets and a warm room are <b>passive external</b> rewarming. ECG and ABG monitoring track the electrolyte release that follows.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "core/NG-041_hypothermia.html", "cardLabel": "Hypothermia"}, {"q": "A client is being treated after exposure to anthrax. Which medication does the nurse expect to be prescribed?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Acyclovir", "Atropine", "Ciprofloxacin", "No medication; care is supportive only"], "ans": [2], "why": "Anthrax is caused by a <b>bacterium</b>, so it is treated with antibiotics such as <b>penicillin or ciprofloxacin</b>. Acyclovir is an antiviral, atropine is for nerve agents, and supportive care alone is the approach for smallpox, a virus.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#dx-bioterrorism-agents", "cardLabel": "Bioterrorism, chemical and radiation agents"}, {"q": "A client has high fever, malaise, headache, backache and prostration. After 1 to 2 days a maculopapular rash appears on the face, mouth, forearms and pharynx. Which highly contagious viral infection does the nurse suspect?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Cutaneous anthrax", "Smallpox", "Botulism", "Sulfur mustard exposure"], "ans": [1], "why": "<b>Smallpox</b> is an extremely contagious viral infection: high fever, malaise, headache, backache and prostration, then after 1 to 2 days a maculopapular rash on the face, mouth, forearms and pharynx.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#dx-bioterrorism-agents", "cardLabel": "Bioterrorism, chemical and radiation agents"}, {"q": "Which statement about the management of smallpox is correct?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Treatment is supportive care, and the vaccine can be given after a known exposure.", "Penicillin or ciprofloxacin cures the infection.", "Every adult receives the vaccine routinely.", "Standard precautions alone are enough."], "ans": [0], "why": "Smallpox is viral, so treatment is <b>supportive care only</b>. The <b>vaccine can be given after a known exposure</b>; it is given preventively only to the military. Penicillin and ciprofloxacin treat anthrax, which is bacterial.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#dx-bioterrorism-agents", "cardLabel": "Bioterrorism, chemical and radiation agents"}, {"q": "Several clients were exposed to a vesicant such as sulfur mustard. What is the key intervention?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Give atropine and pralidoxime", "Have them rinse in a decontamination shower with soap and water, and wash exposed eyes", "Start oral antibiotics", "Cover the skin with dry dressings and leave it unwashed"], "ans": [1], "why": "The key for a vesicant is to <b>rinse it off</b>: a long decontamination shower with soap and water, plus an eye wash if the eyes were exposed. Vesicants such as lewisite and sulfur mustard cause blisters, burning and respiratory effects.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#review-258-m12-vesicants", "cardLabel": "Vesicants"}, {"q": "Which sequence describes the two steps of client decontamination?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Wash with soap and water inside the treatment room, then remove the clothing", "Rinse with water only; removing clothing and jewelry is not needed", "Remove clothing and jewelry and rinse with water, then wash thoroughly with soap and water, before entering the building", "Wrap the client in blankets, then wash only the visibly soiled areas"], "ans": [2], "why": "Step 1: <b>remove clothing and jewelry and rinse with water</b>. Step 2: <b>wash thoroughly with soap and water</b>. Both are done before the client enters the building, so the contaminant is not spread to staff or other areas.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#review-258-m12-decon", "cardLabel": "Decontamination"}, {"q": "A client exposed to a chemical nerve agent has received atropine and pralidoxime and now begins to have seizures. Which medication class does the nurse anticipate?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Opioid antagonists", "Benzodiazepines", "Beta blockers", "Loop diuretics"], "ans": [1], "why": "Atropine is the classic acetylcholine blocker for a cholinergic crisis from a chemical agent, and pralidoxime removes the poison from acetylcholinesterase. <b>Seizures are treated with benzodiazepines</b>.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "nur258-module-12-disaster-emergency-nursing.html#dx-bioterrorism-agents", "cardLabel": "Bioterrorism, chemical and radiation agents"}, {"q": "A client's frostbitten toes are black and necrotic. Which degree of frostbite is this, and what treatment does the nurse anticipate?", "type": "single", "opts": ["First degree; no treatment is needed", "Second degree; a warm water bath is the only treatment", "Fourth degree; surgical removal of the dead tissue", "Third degree; massage to restore circulation"], "ans": [2], "why": "Black, necrotic tissue is <b>fourth-degree</b> frostbite and needs <b>surgical removal</b> of the dead tissue. Blisters mark second degree and deeper whitening marks third. Care also includes elevation to limit edema, a tetanus shot and watching for compartment syndrome.", "topic": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 Module 11-12 study cards (24 Nov)", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Disaster, Mass Casualty & Emergency Nursing", "card": "skin/NG-116_frostbite.html", "cardLabel": "Frostbite"}], "13": [{"q": "A client meets sepsis criteria in the ED. Which action does the nurse perform first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Administer the broad-spectrum antibiotic", "Obtain two sets of blood cultures", "Start a norepinephrine infusion", "Infuse a 30 mL/kg fluid bolus"], "ans": [1], "why": "Order matters within the first hour: labs including lactate, then <b>blood cultures before antibiotics</b>, then antibiotics, then fluids, with vasopressors only if fluids fail. Giving the antibiotic first feels lifesaving but sterilizes the cultures and blinds the whole admission."}, {"q": "A client in cardiogenic shock has troponin I 2.9, BNP 157, crackles, SpO2 88 percent on 4 L and urine output 15 mL/hr. Which intervention improves cardiac output?", "type": "single", "opts": ["A 2 L isotonic fluid bolus", "An inotropic infusion", "Sublingual nitroglycerin", "A unit of packed red blood cells"], "ans": [1], "why": "Cardiogenic shock is pump failure, so the answer is <b>inotropes</b>, not volume - large boluses flood lungs that are already crackling. Nitroglycerin while hypotensive turns an 80 into a 60."}, {"q": "A client is admitted with cardiogenic shock after a myocardial infarction. Which medications does the nurse anticipate preparing once the provider's orders arrive? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Dopamine", "Digoxin", "Diphenhydramine", "Daunorubicin", "Dexamethasone"], "ans": [0, 1], "why": "Cardiogenic shock is <b>pump failure</b>, most often after an MI, so treatment aims to restore heart function and blood pressure quickly with drugs that make the heart squeeze harder. <b>Dopamine</b> raises contractility and blood pressure, the choice for a hypotensive client, and <b>digoxin</b> is a cardiac glycoside that strengthens each contraction. Diphenhydramine is an antihistamine used in anaphylaxis, and only after epinephrine. Daunorubicin is a chemotherapy drug whose adverse effect is heart damage. Dexamethasone is a steroid for swelling and inflammation, not a priority in cardiogenic shock. Side note: in acute care the usual inotropes are dobutamine and milrinone, and digoxin acts slowly; this item keys digoxin, so choose it here.", "topic": "Shock & MODS", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 practice question (24 Nov), reworded", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Shock & MODS", "pharm": 1, "card": "cardio/NG-050_shock.html", "cardLabel": "Shock"}, {"q": "A client with suspected shock has profound bradycardia. Based on the pathophysiology of each type of shock, which type does the nurse suspect?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Septic shock", "Neurogenic shock", "Hypovolemic shock", "Anaphylactic shock"], "ans": [1], "why": "<b>Profound bradycardia belongs to neurogenic shock alone.</b> An injury at <b>T6 or higher</b> cuts off the sympathetic nervous system, which normally speeds the vital signs and constricts vessels. With only the <b>parasympathetic</b> side left, the heart rate drops and the vessels dilate everywhere. Every other shock drives the heart rate <b>up</b>: septic shock to fight cytokine-driven vasodilation, hypovolemic shock to make up for lost volume, and anaphylactic shock in response to histamine.", "topic": "Shock & MODS", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 practice question (24 Nov), reworded", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Shock & MODS", "card": "cardio/NG-050_shock.html", "cardLabel": "Shock"}, {"q": "The nurse is caring for a client with septic shock. Which findings indicate the progressive stage? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Urine output of 10 mL/hr", "Temperature of 101.4°F (38.6°C)", "Capillary refill under 2 seconds", "Pale, cool extremities", "Confusion"], "ans": [0, 3, 4], "why": "Every type of shock moves through the same stages: <b>initial, compensatory, progressive, irreversible</b>. In the <b>progressive</b> stage compensation has failed, and the body shunts blood from the limbs to keep the core perfused. That gives <b>a urine output of 10 mL/hr</b> (the kidneys are starved), <b>pale, cool, clammy extremities</b>, and <b>confusion</b> as the brain runs short of oxygen, along with sluggish capillary refill, severe shortness of breath, hypotension and tachycardia. A fever fits early sepsis; as shock progresses the temperature <b>falls</b>, to 96°F (35.6°C) or lower. A capillary refill under 2 seconds is normal.", "topic": "Shock & MODS", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 practice question (24 Nov), reworded", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Shock & MODS", "card": "cardio/NG-050_shock.html", "cardLabel": "Shock"}, {"q": "Which are stages of shock? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Irreversible stage", "Compensatory stage", "Intermediate stage", "Initial stage", "Progressive stage"], "ans": [0, 1, 3, 4], "why": "Shock has four stages, in this order: <b>initial, compensatory, progressive, irreversible</b>. There is no \"intermediate\" stage. <b>Initial</b>: too little oxygen reaches the cells, so they switch to anaerobic metabolism, but there are no outward signs yet. <b>Compensatory</b>: the sympathetic nervous system speeds the heart and breathing, and renin-angiotensin holds the blood pressure up to keep the organs perfused. <b>Progressive</b>: compensation fails and the skin turns cold and clammy. <b>Irreversible</b>: the damage cannot be undone and death is imminent.", "topic": "Shock & MODS", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 practice question (24 Nov), reworded", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Shock & MODS", "card": "cardio/NG-050_shock.html", "cardLabel": "Shock"}, {"q": "Which statement correctly defines multiple organ dysfunction syndrome (MODS)?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Progressive dysfunction of two or more organ systems, where homeostasis cannot be maintained without intervention, set off by an uncontrolled inflammatory response", "Failure of any single organ that recovers once the original injury is treated", "Progressive dysfunction of three or more organs caused by a localized allergic reaction", "An autoimmune attack on one organ that slowly spreads to the organs next to it"], "ans": [0], "why": "MODS means <b>two or more</b> organ systems failing, one after another, to the point that the body can no longer keep <b>homeostasis</b>, its internal balance, without support. The trigger is a major insult such as sepsis, severe trauma or critical illness that sets off an <b>uncontrolled inflammatory response</b>: the normal protective inflammation spreads through the whole body, the vessels leak and the microcirculation clots, and organ after organ is starved of oxygen. Blood pressure and consciousness can fall too, but they are signs of it, not the definition.", "topic": "Shock & MODS", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 practice question (24 Nov), reworded", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Shock & MODS", "card": "nur258-module-13-shock-mods.html#dx-multiple-organ-dysfunction-syndrome", "cardLabel": "Multiple organ dysfunction syndrome"}, {"q": "An ICU client has MODS secondary to sepsis. Which steps in the development of MODS belong to secondary MODS? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["The insult prompts immune cells to set off a cytokine storm", "An exaggerated stress response raises oxygen use and energy demand, leading to cellular dysfunction and acidosis", "The inflammatory response leaves the immune system primed for a further injury", "A direct insult to an organ impairs its perfusion and causes organ dysfunction", "Clots in the microvasculature lead to tissue hypoxia"], "ans": [1, 4], "why": "<b>Primary MODS</b> is the first hit: a <b>direct insult</b> to an organ impairs its perfusion and function, the injury sets off a <b>cytokine storm</b>, and that inflammation leaves the immune system <b>primed</b>, so a later hit provokes a much bigger response. <b>Secondary MODS</b> is that bigger response arriving, often with sepsis as the second hit: an <b>exaggerated stress response</b> drives up oxygen use and energy demand (hypermetabolism), cells fail and acidosis builds, and <b>clots in the microvasculature</b> starve distant organs of oxygen. The \"primed\" row is the one most often put on the wrong side: priming happens in the primary phase; it is what makes the secondary phase so severe.", "topic": "Shock & MODS", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 practice question (24 Nov), reworded", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Shock & MODS", "card": "nur258-module-13-shock-mods.html#fig-primary-secondary-mods", "cardLabel": "Primary vs secondary MODS"}, {"q": "Which clients are at higher risk of developing MODS? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["A client with sepsis", "A client with acute respiratory distress syndrome", "A client with stable angina", "A client with severe pancreatitis", "A client with a migraine"], "ans": [0, 1, 3], "why": "MODS grows out of <b>uncontrolled inflammation</b>. The most common source is <b>sepsis and septic shock</b>; others include <b>ARDS</b>, acute kidney injury, acute liver failure, pancreatitis, DIC, burns, major surgery and trauma. Each one floods the body with inflammatory mediators that injure organs far from where the problem began. Stable angina, type 2 diabetes, migraine and osteoarthritis are chronic or local problems that do not set off that system-wide response.", "topic": "Shock & MODS", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 practice question (24 Nov), reworded", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Shock & MODS", "card": "nur258-module-13-shock-mods.html#dx-multiple-organ-dysfunction-syndrome", "cardLabel": "Multiple organ dysfunction syndrome"}, {"q": "An ICU student nurse reviews the normal inflammatory response to better understand care for a client with multiple organ dysfunction syndrome (MODS). Which statements about the normal inflammatory response are accurate? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Increased vascular constriction lets more blood collect at the injury site", "Adhesion molecules help immune cells reach the injury", "Clots form and start the healing process", "Inflammatory mediators draw more immune cells to the injury and stimulate endothelial cells to change the vessels", "Stress hormones help regulate the inflammatory response and keep it from becoming excessive"], "ans": [1, 2, 3, 4], "why": "In a normal inflammatory response, mediators released at the injury <b>draw in immune cells</b> and act on the endothelium; <b>adhesion molecules</b> let those cells stick and slip through the vessel wall; <b>clots</b> wall off the area and start healing; and <b>stress hormones</b> such as cortisol keep the response in check. The vessels <b>dilate</b> and become leaky, they do not constrict, which is why the area turns red, warm and swollen. MODS is this same response gone systemic and uncontrolled. Side note: the source of this item also had \"immediately after the injury, cells of the injured tissue release inflammatory mediators\" keyed as wrong, with no explanation; that is how the response normally begins, so it was left out.", "topic": "Shock & MODS", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 practice question (24 Nov), reworded", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Shock & MODS", "card": "nur258-module-13-shock-mods.html#dx-multiple-organ-dysfunction-syndrome", "cardLabel": "Multiple organ dysfunction syndrome"}, {"q": "An ICU client with an acute kidney injury is being watched for multiple organ dysfunction syndrome (MODS). Which findings could be associated with MODS? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Vesicular lung sounds", "Reflexes 2+ bilaterally", "Normoactive bowel sounds", "Pupils equal, round and reactive to light and accommodation", "New-onset agitation", "Jaundice", "Emesis", "Alert and oriented", "Respiratory rate 26/min", "Dyspnea", "Peripheral pulses 2+ bilaterally"], "ans": [4, 5, 6, 8, 9], "why": "In MODS, organs fail one after another, so look for a <b>new</b> sign of a <b>second</b> system going wrong. <b>Brain:</b> new-onset agitation. <b>Liver:</b> jaundice. <b>GI tract:</b> emesis. <b>Lungs:</b> a respiratory rate of 26 and dyspnea, often the first organ to fail. The rest are <b>normal</b> findings: vesicular lung sounds, 2+ reflexes, normoactive bowel sounds, PERRLA, alert and oriented, and 2+ peripheral pulses. Side note: the source of this item called a pulse of 107 \"not associated\"; your Module 13 notes treat a rising heart rate as an early warning sign (SIRS counts anything over 90), so that finding was left out here.", "topic": "Shock & MODS", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 practice question (24 Nov), reworded from a matrix grid", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Shock & MODS", "card": "nur258-module-13-shock-mods.html#dx-multiple-organ-dysfunction-syndrome", "cardLabel": "Multiple organ dysfunction syndrome"}, {"q": "The nurse is caring for a client with suspected hypovolemic shock. Which action does the nurse take first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Ensure patent IV access", "Place suction equipment at the bedside", "Assess bilateral pedal pulses", "Lower the head of the bed"], "ans": [3], "why": "Hypovolemic shock is a huge loss of blood or fluid (trauma, surgery, burns), with hypotension, tachycardia, a low CVP and falling urine output. The first action is to <b>lower the head of the bed</b>, so blood flows back toward the brain and the body core; this costs seconds and needs no equipment. Your shock page's position is <b>flat with the legs raised 20 to 30 degrees</b> (modified Trendelenburg); full head-down Trendelenburg is not used. <b>Then</b> IV access and normal saline, with vasopressors only once volume is going in. Nothing suggests suction is needed, and checking pedal pulses delays action when the nurse already has enough information.", "topic": "Shock & MODS", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 practice question (24 Nov), reworded", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Shock & MODS", "card": "cardio/NG-050_shock.html", "cardLabel": "Shock"}, {"q": "Which scenario correctly matches a type of shock with a cause of that type?", "type": "single", "opts": ["A client with neurogenic shock from a bloodborne infection", "A client with anaphylactic shock from a chemical burn", "A client with hypovolemic shock from gastrointestinal bleeding", "A client with cardiogenic shock from a spinal cord injury"], "ans": [2], "why": "A <b>GI bleed</b> drains blood volume, which is <b>hypovolemic</b> (hemorrhagic) shock; trauma, surgery and burns (from massive fluid shifts) cause it too, with hypotension, tachycardia, a low CVP and low urine output. Each of the five types has its own cause: <b>septic</b>, an infection overwhelming the body; <b>neurogenic</b>, a spinal cord injury at <b>T6 or higher</b>; <b>cardiogenic</b>, a failing pump (MI, end-stage heart failure); <b>anaphylactic</b>, an allergen. So a bloodborne infection gives septic shock, a burn gives hypovolemic shock, and a spinal cord injury gives neurogenic shock.", "topic": "Shock & MODS", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 practice question (24 Nov), reworded", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Shock & MODS", "card": "cardio/NG-050_shock.html", "cardLabel": "Shock"}, {"q": "Which situations put a client at risk of hypovolemic shock? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Surgery", "Severe burns", "A gunshot wound", "A gastrointestinal bleed", "Severe nausea, vomiting and diarrhea"], "ans": [0, 1, 2, 3, 4], "why": "<b>All five</b>: hypovolemic shock follows any large loss of <b>blood or fluid</b>, and each of these causes one. <b>Blood</b> is lost in surgery, a gunshot wound (trauma) and a GI bleed. <b>Fluid</b> is lost in severe burns, where plasma shifts out of the vessels in huge amounts, and in severe vomiting and diarrhea. Watch for hypotension, tachycardia, a low CVP and falling urine output.", "topic": "Shock & MODS", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 practice question (24 Nov), reworded", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Shock & MODS", "card": "cardio/NG-050_shock.html", "cardLabel": "Shock"}, {"q": "Which statement describes the pathophysiology of neurogenic shock?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Dangerous hypertension caused by excessive vasodilation", "Bradycardia and hypotension from the loss of sympathetic outflow", "Loss of all sensation below the level of a spinal cord injury", "Impaired reflexes caused by overstimulation of the parasympathetic nervous system"], "ans": [1], "why": "Neurogenic shock usually follows a <b>high spinal cord injury</b> (T6 or above), which cuts off <b>sympathetic outflow</b> and with it the catecholamines. Without epinephrine's push the heart slows into <b>bradycardia</b>; without norepinephrine's squeeze the vessels dilate everywhere, giving <b>hypotension</b>. Dangerous <b>hypertension</b> after a high spinal injury is <b>autonomic dysreflexia</b>, set off by a trigger such as a full bladder. Loss of all sensation and reflexes below the injury is <b>spinal shock</b>, a different condition. The parasympathetic side is not overstimulated; it is simply left unopposed.", "topic": "Shock & MODS", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 practice question (24 Nov), reworded", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Shock & MODS", "card": "cardio/NG-050_shock.html", "cardLabel": "Shock"}, {"q": "Which clients are at risk of developing cardiogenic shock? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["A client having a myocardial infarction", "A client with a bee sting", "A client with a peanut allergy who ate cake containing nuts", "A client with an exacerbation of heart failure", "A client with end-stage heart failure"], "ans": [0, 3, 4], "why": "Cardiogenic shock is <b>pump failure</b>: the heart muscle is too weak or damaged to push out enough blood. That makes a <b>myocardial infarction</b>, a <b>heart failure exacerbation</b> and <b>end-stage heart failure</b> the classic set-ups. A bee sting and a peanut allergy meeting nuts are allergen exposures, and they put a client at risk of <b>anaphylactic</b> shock, not cardiogenic.", "topic": "Shock & MODS", "src": "Caroline's NUR 258 practice question (24 Nov), reworded", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Shock & MODS", "card": "cardio/NG-050_shock.html", "cardLabel": "Shock"}, {"q": "Which set of values indicates septic shock rather than SIRS alone?", "type": "single", "opts": ["BP 110/68, WBC 18,000, lactate 1.8", "BP 78/46, HR 132, lactate 4.2, requiring vasopressors", "Temp 38.5 C, HR 96, RR 22, WBC 13,000", "BP 96/60, HR 104, lactate 1.2"], "ans": [1], "why": "Septic shock requires <b>SBP under 90 and lactate over 2 - with 4 as the key marker - plus a vasopressor requirement</b>. SIRS needs only 2 of 4 criteria and meeting them is not automatically sepsis, which is why the normal-lactate options are distractors."}, {"q": "A client with a C5 spinal cord injury has BP 74/42 and HR 48. Which findings support neurogenic shock? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Bradycardia with hypotension", "Cool clammy skin with capillary refill over 4 seconds", "Warm skin with capillary refill under 2 seconds", "Compensatory tachycardia", "Loss of sympathetic tone below the level of injury"], "ans": [0, 2, 4], "why": "Neurogenic shock is the one shock <b>without tachycardia</b> - the vagus is intact and the sympathetic chain is lost, giving <b>hypotension with bradycardia, warm skin and brisk capillary refill</b>. Treatment is norepinephrine; high-dose corticosteroids are never used in spinal cord injury."}, {"q": "A client has BP 78/50, jugular venous distention, muffled heart sounds and a history of chest trauma. What does the nurse anticipate?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Inotropic support for cardiogenic shock", "Pericardiocentesis", "Rapid isotonic fluid boluses for hypovolemia", "Needle decompression at the second intercostal space"], "ans": [1], "why": "Beck's triad - <b>muffled heart sounds, JVD and hypotension</b> - is obstructive shock from tamponade, and the definitive answer is <b>pericardiocentesis</b> at the 5th intercostal space, after which BP can jump from 78/50 to 140/80 in minutes. It involves the heart, so it is repeatedly mistaken for cardiogenic shock."}, {"q": "A deteriorating client in shock has PaO2 58, PaCO2 80 and pH 7.31. Which action does the nurse anticipate?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Increase the nasal cannula to 15 L/min", "Prepare for intubation and mechanical ventilation", "Give sodium bicarbonate", "Begin incentive spirometry every hour"], "ans": [1], "why": "Hypoxemia with a PaCO2 of 80 and falling pH means ventilatory failure, so the answer is <b>intubate</b> - preoxygenate at 100 percent, sedate before paralyzing, and continue sedation after. Cranking a nasal cannula to 15 L/min is not an answer; it cannot fix a ventilation problem."}, {"q": "A client has a blood pressure of 84/50 mm Hg, heart rate 124/min, warm flushed skin, and a temperature of 38.9° C. Which type of shock does the nurse suspect?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Hypovolemic", "Cardiogenic", "Septic (distributive)", "Obstructive"], "ans": [2], "why": "<b>Warm, flushed skin with fever and a wide pulse pressure</b> is early distributive shock from sepsis - the vessels have dilated, so the problem is the container, not the volume or the pump. Hypovolemic and cardiogenic shock both present <b>cold, clammy and vasoconstricted</b>.", "topic": "Shock & MODS", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Shock & MODS", "card": "cardio/NG-050_shock.html", "cardLabel": "Shock"}, {"q": "Which interventions are priorities in the first hour of septic shock? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Obtain blood cultures before antibiotics", "Give broad-spectrum antibiotics", "Begin a 30 mL/kg crystalloid bolus", "Measure a serum lactate", "Start vasopressors before any fluid is given"], "ans": [0, 1, 2, 3], "why": "The sepsis bundle is <b>cultures, lactate, antibiotics, fluid</b> - and cultures go first only because drawing them takes moments, never as a reason to delay the antibiotic. <b>Fluid comes before vasopressors</b>: squeezing an empty tank does not perfuse anything.", "topic": "Shock & MODS", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Shock & MODS", "card": "cardio/NG-050_shock.html", "cardLabel": "Shock"}, {"q": "A client in shock has a urine output of 12 mL/hr, a lactate of 4.8 mmol/L, and a mean arterial pressure of 58 mm Hg. What do these findings together indicate?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Adequate compensation", "Tissue hypoperfusion requiring escalation", "An expected response to fluid therapy", "Resolving shock"], "ans": [1], "why": "All three say the same thing in different languages: <b>the tissues are not being perfused</b>. A MAP under 65 will not perfuse the kidneys, the urine output proves it, and the lactate is the cells' own report of anaerobic metabolism. Rising lactate is the earliest reliable sign that the treatment is not working.", "topic": "Shock & MODS", "icon": "🚨", "topicFull": "Shock & MODS", "card": "cardio/NG-050_shock.html", "cardLabel": "Shock"}, {"q": "A client admitted with an upper GI hemorrhage has BP 78/50 mm Hg, HR 132/min, RR 28/min, urine output 10 mL/hr, Hgb 8.2 g/dL and Hct 25%. Which intervention does the nurse anticipate first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Rapid isotonic crystalloid, with blood products because bleeding is ongoing", "A norepinephrine infusion titrated to a MAP above 65 mm Hg", "An inotrope to improve cardiac output", "High-flow oxygen by nonrebreather as the primary treatment"], "ans": [0], "why": "A <b>low</b> H&H signals active bleeding, so the treatment is fixing the cause plus fluids, with blood products for the hemorrhage. Norepinephrine is tempting because the MAP is low, but pressors come only <b>after</b> fluids fail, and oxygen alone does not treat the underlying volume loss."}, {"q": "A client receiving an IV antibiotic suddenly develops hives, warm flushed skin, wheezing and BP 82/48 mm Hg. Which action does the nurse take first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Administer intramuscular epinephrine", "Apply oxygen by nonrebreather mask", "Begin a rapid IV crystalloid bolus", "Elevate the client's legs to shift volume back to the heart"], "ans": [0], "why": "In anaphylactic shock the order is <b>epinephrine first</b>, then oxygen, then fluids. A bolus looks right because the client is hypotensive, but <b>fluids are not the priority</b> here — only epinephrine reverses the histamine release causing the vasodilation."}, {"q": "A client in septic shock is receiving norepinephrine through a peripheral IV while central access is being arranged. The nurse finds the site pale, swollen and cool with the infusion running. Which action does the nurse anticipate?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Administering phentolamine to reverse the extravasation", "Applying warm compresses and continuing the infusion at that site", "Flushing the line with saline and resuming the norepinephrine", "Increasing the norepinephrine rate to maintain the MAP"], "ans": [0], "why": "<b>Phentolamine reverses norepinephrine extravasation</b>, and the drug belongs in a central line titrated to MAP. Continuing or restarting the infusion at that site is the trap: norepinephrine causes tissue injury, and prolonged use is also associated with <b>digital necrosis</b>."}, {"q": "A client being treated for shock has BP 86/64 mm Hg with a MAP of 71 mm Hg, HR 96/min, is alert and oriented, and has a urine output of 45 mL/hr. A new nurse wants to bolus fluids for the low systolic pressure. Which response by the charge nurse is correct?", "type": "single", "opts": ["The MAP is above 65, so perfusion is adequate; continue therapy and vital signs every 15 minutes", "Any systolic pressure below 90 requires an immediate fluid bolus", "Start norepinephrine now because the systolic pressure is in the 80s", "Place the client in modified Trendelenburg until the systolic pressure exceeds 90"], "ans": [0], "why": "<b>MAP is the perfusion indicator, with a goal above 65</b> — an SBP in the 80s to 90s is not alarming when the MAP is adequate. Bolusing or starting a pressor for the systolic number alone treats a number rather than perfusion, and the urine output above 30 mL/hr and clear mental status confirm the client is perfusing."}, {"q": "A client is 4 hours out from a motor vehicle crash with a suspected splenic injury. Which findings indicate the compensatory stage of shock rather than a later stage? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Heart rate 118/min", "Cool, clammy skin", "Restlessness", "Blood pressure 76/44 mm Hg", "Confusion and disorientation"], "ans": [0, 1, 2], "why": "<b>Tachycardia is the earliest response</b>, and restlessness from cerebral hypoxia with cool clammy skin rounds out the compensatory picture while the <b>blood pressure is still normal</b>. A falling BP marks the progressive stage, and confusion or altered mental status is a <b>late</b> sign, not an early one."}, {"q": "A client in progressive shock is being intubated at the bedside. Which actions by the nurse are appropriate? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Preoxygenate with 100% oxygen before the attempt", "Give the sedative before the paralytic agent", "Auscultate for bilateral breath sounds and check for symmetric chest rise after insertion", "Stop sedation once the paralytic has taken effect", "Accept a purple color change on the ETCO2 detector as confirmation of tracheal placement"], "ans": [0, 1, 2], "why": "Intubation means <b>preoxygenate at 100%, sedate before you paralyze, and keep sedation going afterward</b>, then confirm with bilateral breath sounds and symmetric chest rise. Stopping sedation leaves a paralyzed client awake, and on ETCO2 <b>purple means esophagus</b> — pull and retry — while yellow or green indicates the trachea, with CXR as the gold standard."}], "14": [{"q": "A nurse receives report on four clients. Which does she assess first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["A burn client with facial burns who is now hoarse", "A client with a hemoglobin of 7.4 scheduled for transfusion", "A client with a fasting glucose of 168", "A client with a new prescription for allopurinol"], "ans": [0], "why": "The cross-cutting rule is <b>ABCs first</b>, then urgent over non-urgent, then assess before intervene, then Maslow, then least restrictive. A hoarse voice after facial burns is an airway about to close; the anemia, hyperglycemia and new prescription are all real but none is an airway."}, {"q": "A client is short of breath and also asks for help to the bathroom and reports 6/10 incisional pain. Which priority rule directs the nurse's first action?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Maslow, so the physiologic elimination need comes first", "ABCs, so breathing is addressed first", "Least restrictive, so the client's request is honored first", "Assess before intervene, so pain is rated first"], "ans": [1], "why": "<b>ABCs sit above Maslow, above assess-before-intervene and above least restrictive</b> in the hierarchy - breathing is addressed before elimination or pain. The other rules are real, but they only decide the question once airway, breathing and circulation are secure."}, {"q": "A drip is ordered at 5 mcg/kg/min for a client weighing 154 lb. The bag contains 400 mg in 250 mL. What rate does the nurse program?", "type": "single", "opts": ["8.2 mL/hr", "13.1 mL/hr", "21 mL/hr", "26.3 mL/hr"], "ans": [1], "why": "154 divided by 2.2 = 70 kg; 70 x 5 = 350 mcg/min = 21,000 mcg/hr = 21 mg/hr; the bag is 1.6 mg/mL, so 21 divided by 1.6 = <b>13.1 mL/hr</b>. Answering 21 means stopping at mg/hr and never converting to volume - round only at the very end."}, {"q": "Which are steps of the Clinical Judgment Measurement Model? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Recognize cues", "Chart by exception", "Analyze cues", "Prioritize hypotheses", "Delegate to unlicensed personnel"], "ans": [0, 2, 3], "why": "The six steps are <b>recognize cues, analyze cues, prioritize hypotheses, generate solutions, take action and evaluate outcomes</b>, and they head every module review list. Charting and delegation are nursing activities, not steps of the model."}, {"q": "A client's labs return: sodium 138, potassium 5.9, WBC 8,200, hemoglobin 13.4. Which value does the nurse report?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Sodium 138", "Potassium 5.9", "WBC 8,200", "Hemoglobin 13.4"], "ans": [1], "why": "Only the <b>potassium is outside the reference range</b>, and hyperkalemia risks arrhythmia. Values inside normal limits are almost always distractors in select-all and highlight items - the skill being tested is knowing the ranges cold."}, {"q": "With one week left before the cumulative final, which review target gives the highest yield per hour?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Module 13 Shock and MODS", "Module 1 Sensory Disorders", "Module 6 Diabetes", "Module 10 Oncologic Disorders"], "ans": [0], "why": "The final is cumulative, and <b>Module 13 is the only content that has not already appeared on an exam</b>, making it the highest-value target per hour. Note also that the ATI CMS Medical-Surgical Proctored Assessment is scheduled on a separate date from the final - check the calendar, they are days apart."}, {"q": "A nurse receives report on four clients. Which one does the nurse assess first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["A client with diabetes whose morning glucose is 244 mg/dL", "A client 1 day post-op reporting incisional pain of 6 out of 10", "A client with a new tracheostomy whose oxygen saturation has fallen from 96% to 88%", "A client with cellulitis awaiting the first dose of antibiotic"], "ans": [2], "why": "<b>Airway and breathing outrank everything.</b> A falling saturation in a new tracheostomy suggests obstruction or a displaced tube, and that becomes unsurvivable in minutes. The glucose, the pain and the pending antibiotic all matter, and all of them can wait the few minutes this takes.", "topic": "Final Exam Review", "icon": "🎯", "topicFull": "Final Exam Review"}, {"q": "Which findings across body systems require the nurse to act immediately? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Potassium of 6.8 mEq/L", "Widening pulse pressure with bradycardia after a head injury", "Urine output of 12 mL/hr in a burn client", "Hemoglobin of 10.9 g/dL in a client with chronic anemia", "New-onset stridor after extubation"], "ans": [0, 1, 2, 4], "why": "Four of these kill quickly: potassium at 6.8 stops the heart, Cushing's triad means herniation is near, oliguria in a burn means failing resuscitation, and stridor is an airway closing. A hemoglobin of 10.9 in someone with <b>chronic</b> anemia is a stable abnormal - which is exactly the distinction these questions are built to test.", "topic": "Final Exam Review", "icon": "🎯", "topicFull": "Final Exam Review"}, {"q": "Fifteen minutes into a unit of packed red blood cells, a client develops chills, flank pain, and a rising temperature. Which action does the nurse take first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Stop the transfusion and infuse normal saline through new tubing", "Slow the transfusion and continue to monitor the client closely", "Flush the existing tubing with saline and restart the unit", "Leave the line running and notify the provider and blood bank"], "ans": [0], "why": "The module rule for a transfusion reaction is <b>STOP, saline, new tubing</b>. Slowing the rate or flushing the old tubing is tempting because it seems to buy time, but both keep blood reaching the client, which is exactly what must stop first."}, {"q": "Minutes after a new IV medication is started, a client develops lip and tongue swelling, wheezing, and widespread hives. Which action does the nurse take first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Recheck the blood pressure before acting", "Apply oxygen and raise the head of the bed", "Administer epinephrine IM", "Notify the provider and obtain a full allergy history"], "ans": [2], "why": "For anaphylaxis the module states <b>epinephrine IM first</b>. Rechecking the blood pressure looks right to a student applying assess-before-intervene, but that rule is set aside when it is an emergency with an obvious action."}, {"q": "A client admitted after a head injury was alert on arrival but now needs repeated prompting to answer questions. Vital signs are unchanged from baseline. Which action does the nurse take first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["Document the finding and reassess later in the shift", "Report the change in level of consciousness to the provider", "Recheck the vital signs and act only if they change", "Let the client rest and attribute the change to fatigue"], "ans": [1], "why": "A change in <b>level of consciousness is the earliest sign of rising ICP</b>, so it is reported now. Waiting for the vital signs to change is the trap: unchanged values inside the normal range are reassuring-looking distractors, not evidence that nothing is wrong."}, {"q": "A client is admitted with a markedly elevated blood glucose. Orders include an IV fluid bolus and an insulin infusion. Which does the nurse start first?", "type": "single", "opts": ["The IV fluid bolus", "The insulin infusion", "Both at the same time through separate lines", "Neither, until a repeat glucose is drawn"], "ans": [0], "why": "The module rule is <b>fluids before insulin</b>. Starting insulin first is tempting because the glucose is the most obvious abnormality on the chart, but volume is replaced before the glucose is driven down."}, {"q": "A client with extensive burns is receiving Parkland-based fluid resuscitation. Which statements about the nurse's management are correct? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Half of the calculated 24-hour volume is given in the first 8 hours", "Urine output is the gauge of adequate resuscitation", "The client's weight in pounds is divided by 2.2 to obtain kilograms", "The remaining volume is withheld until the second 24-hour period", "A blood pressure within the normal range confirms adequate resuscitation"], "ans": [0, 1, 2], "why": "Parkland gives <b>half the total in the first 8 hours</b>, uses weight in kilograms (lb / 2.2), and is judged by <b>urine output</b>. A normal blood pressure is the tempting choice, but values inside the normal reference range are usually distractors and blood pressure falls last."}, {"q": "A client with a spinal cord injury becomes hypotensive and bradycardic. Which statements about the nurse's reasoning are correct? Select all that apply.", "type": "sata", "opts": ["Bradycardia with hypotension points to neurogenic shock", "A blood pressure still within the normal range would rule out shock", "Blood pressure is a late finding, so earlier cues must be recognized first", "Airway and breathing are addressed before circulation", "A fast rather than slow heart rate would be expected in neurogenic shock"], "ans": [0, 2, 3], "why": "<b>Neurogenic is the bradycardic shock</b>, <b>blood pressure falls last</b>, and priority still runs airway, breathing, then circulation. Expecting tachycardia is the half-knowledge trap carried over from other shock states, and a normal blood pressure never rules shock out."}]}
